{"parentUuid":"59892acd-68d7-4547-9f60-1cb826e3e57b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Write","toolUseID":"toolu_015u9XdXZxpxYZnnHtbNzzHV","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Write\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.1.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":58},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8c18e16c-1e7f-4dc5-b376-814e65f4c16b","timestamp":"2026-07-23T00:23:22.879Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.217","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"8c18e16c-1e7f-4dc5-b376-814e65f4c16b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"output_style","style":"Explanatory"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6a8271bf-6b60-4b19-80de-2de2ded12cec","timestamp":"2026-07-23T00:23:22.887Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.217","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"6a8271bf-6b60-4b19-80de-2de2ded12cec","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdJ33WSqWDyi9uHqTqriz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QxUWmXDTPMF9qWBw62UVey","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/adr/0001-oxc-over-typescript-compiler.md","content":"# 0001. Parse with OXC instead of the TypeScript compiler\n\n**Status:** Accepted\n**Date:** 2026-07-22 (retroactive — true since the project started)\n\n## Context\n\n`react-docgen-typescript` extracts props by spinning up a full TypeScript `Program` — real type-checking, not just parsing. On a mid-size design system that takes seconds, which shows up as slow Storybook startup and sluggish HMR. We wanted prop extraction fast enough to run on every file save without anyone noticing.\n\n## Decision\n\nParse TypeScript/TSX with OXC — a Rust parser with no type-checking pass — and resolve prop types structurally from the AST instead of asking a type checker. Cold extraction on a 15-component fixture set (shadcn, MUI, Chakra, Mantine, React Aria, Radix) takes 32ms.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Parsing runs in parallel per file with no shared `Program` state to build or invalidate — this is most of the speed win, not just OXC being a fast parser.\n- We give up real type inference. Conditional types, mapped types over an unbound generic, and a few `@emotion/styled`-specific call shapes can't be resolved without one — see `docs/type-checker-integration.md` for the full list.\n- Every one of those gaps degrades to a `Diagnostic` plus an `Opaque` prop type, never a silent wrong answer (`crates/core/CLAUDE.md` non-negotiable #6) — the speed trade-off costs completeness, never correctness.\n- `typescript-go`'s Corsa API may eventually let us add real type inference back in without giving up the parallel-parse architecture — tracked in `docs/type-checker-integration.md`.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1041,"cache_read_input_tokens":421624,"output_tokens":687,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1041,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":687,"cache_read_input_tokens":421624,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1041,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1041},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdJ33Sxz4VBgsAPC1nLmj","type":"assistant","uuid":"c6a7bf06-963a-43e8-8e60-4ad0c31fcc35","timestamp":"2026-07-23T00:23:30.504Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.217","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"c6a7bf06-963a-43e8-8e60-4ad0c31fcc35","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2767ee7d-d768-4fc5-b864-ecc99fbdd4cb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QxUWmXDTPMF9qWBw62UVey","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/adr/0001-oxc-over-typescript-compiler.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"e0c7dda3-a9c7-4297-8bba-c7e11177ffa8","timestamp":"2026-07-23T00:23:30.597Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/adr/0001-oxc-over-typescript-compiler.md","content":"# 0001. Parse with OXC instead of the TypeScript compiler\n\n**Status:** Accepted\n**Date:** 2026-07-22 (retroactive — true since the project started)\n\n## Context\n\n`react-docgen-typescript` extracts props by spinning up a full TypeScript `Program` — real type-checking, not just parsing. On a mid-size design system that takes seconds, which shows up as slow Storybook startup and sluggish HMR. We wanted prop extraction fast enough to run on every file save without anyone noticing.\n\n## Decision\n\nParse TypeScript/TSX with OXC — a Rust parser with no type-checking pass — and resolve prop types structurally from the AST instead of asking a type checker. Cold extraction on a 15-component fixture set (shadcn, MUI, Chakra, Mantine, React Aria, Radix) takes 32ms.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Parsing runs in parallel per file with no shared `Program` state to build or invalidate — this is most of the speed win, not just OXC being a fast parser.\n- We give up real type inference. Conditional types, mapped types over an unbound generic, and a few `@emotion/styled`-specific call shapes can't be resolved without one — see `docs/type-checker-integration.md` for the full list.\n- Every one of those gaps degrades to a `Diagnostic` plus an `Opaque` prop type, never a silent wrong answer (`crates/core/CLAUDE.md` non-negotiable #6) — the speed trade-off costs completeness, never correctness.\n- `typescript-go`'s Corsa API may eventually let us add real type inference back in without giving up the parallel-parse architecture — tracked in `docs/type-checker-integration.md`.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":5,"oldLines":31,"newStart":5,"newLines":15,"lines":[" "," ## Context"," ","-`react-docgen-typescript` extracts props by spinning up a full TypeScript","-`Program` — real type-checking, not just parsing. On a mid-size design system","-that takes seconds, which shows up as slow Storybook startup and sluggish","-HMR. We wanted prop extraction fast enough to run on every file save without","-anyone noticing.","+`react-docgen-typescript` extracts props by spinning up a full TypeScript `Program` — real type-checking, not just parsing. On a mid-size design system that takes seconds, which shows up as slow Storybook startup and sluggish HMR. We wanted prop extraction fast enough to run on every file save without anyone noticing."," "," ## Decision"," ","-Parse TypeScript/TSX with OXC — a Rust parser with no type-checking pass —","-and resolve prop types structurally from the AST instead of asking a type","-checker. Cold extraction on a 15-component fixture set (shadcn, MUI, Chakra,","-Mantine, React Aria, Radix) takes 32ms.","+Parse TypeScript/TSX with OXC — a Rust parser with no type-checking pass — and resolve prop types structurally from the AST instead of asking a type checker. Cold extraction on a 15-component fixture set (shadcn, MUI, Chakra, Mantine, React Aria, Radix) takes 32ms."," "," ## Consequences"," ","-- Parsing runs in parallel per file with no shared `Program` state to build","-  or invalidate — this is most of the speed win, not just OXC being a fast","-  parser.","-- We give up real type inference. Conditional types, mapped types over an","-  unbound generic, and a few `@emotion/styled`-specific call shapes can't be","-  resolved without one — see `docs/type-checker-integration.md` for the full","-  list.","-- Every one of those gaps degrades to a `Diagnostic` plus an `Opaque` prop","-  type, never a silent wrong answer (`crates/core/CLAUDE.md` non-negotiable","-  #6) — the speed trade-off costs completeness, never correctness.","-- `typescript-go`'s Corsa API may eventually let us add real type inference","-  back in without giving up the parallel-parse architecture — tracked in","-  `docs/type-checker-integration.md`.","+- Parsing runs in parallel per file with no shared `Program` state to build or invalidate — this is most of the speed win, not just OXC being a fast parser.","+- We give up real type inference. Conditional types, mapped types over an unbound generic, and a few `@emotion/styled`-specific call shapes can't be resolved without one — see `docs/type-checker-integration.md` for the full list.","+- Every one of those gaps degrades to a `Diagnostic` plus an `Opaque` prop type, never a silent wrong answer (`crates/core/CLAUDE.md` non-negotiable #6) — the speed trade-off costs completeness, never correctness.","+- `typescript-go`'s Corsa API may eventually let us add real type inference back in without giving up the parallel-parse architecture — tracked in `docs/type-checker-integration.md`."]}],"originalFile":"# 0001. Parse with OXC instead of the TypeScript compiler\n\n**Status:** Accepted\n**Date:** 2026-07-22 (retroactive — true since the project started)\n\n## Context\n\n`react-docgen-typescript` extracts props by spinning up a full TypeScript\n`Program` — real type-checking, not just parsing. On a mid-size design system\nthat takes seconds, which shows up as slow Storybook startup and sluggish\nHMR. We wanted prop extraction fast enough to run on every file save without\nanyone noticing.\n\n## Decision\n\nParse TypeScript/TSX with OXC — a Rust parser with no type-checking pass —\nand resolve prop types structurally from the AST instead of asking a type\nchecker. Cold extraction on a 15-component fixture set (shadcn, MUI, Chakra,\nMantine, React Aria, Radix) takes 32ms.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Parsing runs in parallel per file with no shared `Program` state to build\n  or invalidate — this is most of the speed win, not just OXC being a fast\n  parser.\n- We give up real type inference. Conditional types, mapped types over an\n  unbound generic, and a few `@emotion/styled`-specific call shapes can't be\n  resolved without one — see `docs/type-checker-integration.md` for the full\n  list.\n- Every one of those gaps degrades to a `Diagnostic` plus an `Opaque` prop\n  type, never a silent wrong answer (`crates/core/CLAUDE.md` non-negotiable\n  #6) — the speed trade-off costs completeness, never correctness.\n- `typescript-go`'s Corsa API may eventually let us add real type inference\n  back in without giving up the parallel-parse architecture — tracked in\n  `docs/type-checker-integration.md`.\n","userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c6a7bf06-963a-43e8-8e60-4ad0c31fcc35","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.217","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"e0c7dda3-a9c7-4297-8bba-c7e11177ffa8","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Write","toolUseID":"toolu_01QxUWmXDTPMF9qWBw62UVey","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Write\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.1.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":59},"type":"attachment","uuid":"761062bd-06d9-415d-ac80-4cda2fff3788","timestamp":"2026-07-23T00:23:30.597Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.217","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"761062bd-06d9-415d-ac80-4cda2fff3788","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"output_style","style":"Explanatory"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"176e4d36-4563-4fe3-a486-dfb1f43aa8e7","timestamp":"2026-07-23T00:23:30.602Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.217","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"176e4d36-4563-4fe3-a486-dfb1f43aa8e7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdJ344SvkoaDt7hwidDyZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TiAU4sSz7kwKtx51MKYna9","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/adr/0002-manual-serialize-for-prop-type.md","content":"# 0002. Hand-write Serialize/Deserialize for PropType instead of deriving it\n\n**Status:** Accepted\n**Date:** 2026-07-22 (retroactive)\n\n## Context\n\n`PropType` and `CollectedType` are recursive enums — variants nest through `Vec`/`Box`/`Option` back into the same type (`Union`, `Object`, `Array`, ...). At some point this project derived `Serialize`/`Deserialize` directly on a tagged `PropType` (`#[serde(tag = \"kind\")]`) and had to raise `#![recursion_limit]` to 2048 to get it to compile — each nesting level wraps the generated serializer in another layer during codegen, and the default limit (128) isn't enough for a real component tree.\n\n## Decision\n\n`PropType`, `CollectedType`, and `OpaqueReason` don't derive `Serialize`/`Deserialize`. Each has a hand-written impl (`to_json_value`/`from_json_value` for the first two) that builds a `serde_json::Value` directly instead of going through serde's derive machinery.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Adding a new variant means updating the manual match in both directions by hand — the compiler won't remind you the way a derive would. `resolver/mod.rs`'s \"no wildcard matches\" convention is what catches a missed arm here instead.\n- `CollectedObjectField` (a struct nested inside `CollectedType::Object`, not an enum) still derives normally — the recursion problem is specific to the recursive *enums*, not every type that touches them.\n- The `#![recursion_limit = \"2048\"]` attribute in `crates/core/src/lib.rs` is left over from the old derive-based design. Verified 2026-07-22: the full workspace builds and every test passes with it removed — nothing in the current manual-impl code path needs it. Worth deleting as a follow-up; left as-is here since confirming that wasn't the point of this decision.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n`serde(remote)`, or just keeping the recursion-limit bump — both keep the derive, so you inherit the compile-time cost and a cryptic \"recursion limit exceeded\" the moment someone adds one more level of nesting. Manual impls trade a bit of hand-maintenance for a predictable compile time.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":810,"cache_read_input_tokens":422665,"output_tokens":866,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":810,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":866,"cache_read_input_tokens":422665,"cache_creation_input_tokens":810,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":810},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdJ341rQowLSfN2Cs71Yr","type":"assistant","uuid":"1736963f-c10e-4d06-8057-24649ea0689a","timestamp":"2026-07-23T00:23:38.533Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.217","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"1736963f-c10e-4d06-8057-24649ea0689a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2767ee7d-d768-4fc5-b864-ecc99fbdd4cb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TiAU4sSz7kwKtx51MKYna9","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/adr/0002-manual-serialize-for-prop-type.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"70c0425c-f22a-4209-bff4-74f44410fe6c","timestamp":"2026-07-23T00:23:38.621Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/adr/0002-manual-serialize-for-prop-type.md","content":"# 0002. Hand-write Serialize/Deserialize for PropType instead of deriving it\n\n**Status:** Accepted\n**Date:** 2026-07-22 (retroactive)\n\n## Context\n\n`PropType` and `CollectedType` are recursive enums — variants nest through `Vec`/`Box`/`Option` back into the same type (`Union`, `Object`, `Array`, ...). At some point this project derived `Serialize`/`Deserialize` directly on a tagged `PropType` (`#[serde(tag = \"kind\")]`) and had to raise `#![recursion_limit]` to 2048 to get it to compile — each nesting level wraps the generated serializer in another layer during codegen, and the default limit (128) isn't enough for a real component tree.\n\n## Decision\n\n`PropType`, `CollectedType`, and `OpaqueReason` don't derive `Serialize`/`Deserialize`. Each has a hand-written impl (`to_json_value`/`from_json_value` for the first two) that builds a `serde_json::Value` directly instead of going through serde's derive machinery.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Adding a new variant means updating the manual match in both directions by hand — the compiler won't remind you the way a derive would. `resolver/mod.rs`'s \"no wildcard matches\" convention is what catches a missed arm here instead.\n- `CollectedObjectField` (a struct nested inside `CollectedType::Object`, not an enum) still derives normally — the recursion problem is specific to the recursive *enums*, not every type that touches them.\n- The `#![recursion_limit = \"2048\"]` attribute in `crates/core/src/lib.rs` is left over from the old derive-based design. Verified 2026-07-22: the full workspace builds and every test passes with it removed — nothing in the current manual-impl code path needs it. Worth deleting as a follow-up; left as-is here since confirming that wasn't the point of this decision.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n`serde(remote)`, or just keeping the recursion-limit bump — both keep the derive, so you inherit the compile-time cost and a cryptic \"recursion limit exceeded\" the moment someone adds one more level of nesting. Manual impls trade a bit of hand-maintenance for a predictable compile time.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":5,"oldLines":39,"newStart":5,"newLines":18,"lines":[" "," ## Context"," ","-`PropType` and `CollectedType` are recursive enums — variants nest through","-`Vec`/`Box`/`Option` back into the same type (`Union`, `Object`, `Array`,","-...). At some point this project derived `Serialize`/`Deserialize` directly","-on a tagged `PropType` (`#[serde(tag = \"kind\")]`) and had to raise","-`#![recursion_limit]` to 2048 to get it to compile — each nesting level wraps","-the generated serializer in another layer during codegen, and the default","-limit (128) isn't enough for a real component tree.","+`PropType` and `CollectedType` are recursive enums — variants nest through `Vec`/`Box`/`Option` back into the same type (`Union`, `Object`, `Array`, ...). At some point this project derived `Serialize`/`Deserialize` directly on a tagged `PropType` (`#[serde(tag = \"kind\")]`) and had to raise `#![recursion_limit]` to 2048 to get it to compile — each nesting level wraps the generated serializer in another layer during codegen, and the default limit (128) isn't enough for a real component tree."," "," ## Decision"," ","-`PropType`, `CollectedType`, and `OpaqueReason` don't derive","-`Serialize`/`Deserialize`. Each has a hand-written impl (`to_json_value`/","-`from_json_value` for the first two) that builds a `serde_json::Value`","-directly instead of going through serde's derive machinery.","+`PropType`, `CollectedType`, and `OpaqueReason` don't derive `Serialize`/`Deserialize`. Each has a hand-written impl (`to_json_value`/`from_json_value` for the first two) that builds a `serde_json::Value` directly instead of going through serde's derive machinery."," "," ## Consequences"," ","-- Adding a new variant means updating the manual match in both directions by","-  hand — the compiler won't remind you the way a derive would.","-  `resolver/mod.rs`'s \"no wildcard matches\" convention is what catches a","-  missed arm here instead.","-- `CollectedObjectField` (a struct nested inside `CollectedType::Object`, not","-  an enum) still derives normally — the recursion problem is specific to the","-  recursive *enums*, not every type that touches them.","-- The `#![recursion_limit = \"2048\"]` attribute in `crates/core/src/lib.rs` is","-  left over from the old derive-based design. Verified 2026-07-22: the full","-  workspace builds and every test passes with it removed — nothing in the","-  current manual-impl code path needs it. Worth deleting as a follow-up;","-  left as-is here since confirming that wasn't the point of this decision.","+- Adding a new variant means updating the manual match in both directions by hand — the compiler won't remind you the way a derive would. `resolver/mod.rs`'s \"no wildcard matches\" convention is what catches a missed arm here instead.","+- `CollectedObjectField` (a struct nested inside `CollectedType::Object`, not an enum) still derives normally — the recursion problem is specific to the recursive *enums*, not every type that touches them.","+- The `#![recursion_limit = \"2048\"]` attribute in `crates/core/src/lib.rs` is left over from the old derive-based design. Verified 2026-07-22: the full workspace builds and every test passes with it removed — nothing in the current manual-impl code path needs it. Worth deleting as a follow-up; left as-is here since confirming that wasn't the point of this decision."," "," ## Alternatives considered"," ","-`serde(remote)`, or just keeping the recursion-limit bump — both keep the","-derive, so you inherit the compile-time cost and a cryptic \"recursion limit","-exceeded\" the moment someone adds one more level of nesting. Manual impls","-trade a bit of hand-maintenance for a predictable compile time.","+`serde(remote)`, or just keeping the recursion-limit bump — both keep the derive, so you inherit the compile-time cost and a cryptic \"recursion limit exceeded\" the moment someone adds one more level of nesting. Manual impls trade a bit of hand-maintenance for a predictable compile time."]}],"originalFile":"# 0002. Hand-write Serialize/Deserialize for PropType instead of deriving it\n\n**Status:** Accepted\n**Date:** 2026-07-22 (retroactive)\n\n## Context\n\n`PropType` and `CollectedType` are recursive enums — variants nest through\n`Vec`/`Box`/`Option` back into the same type (`Union`, `Object`, `Array`,\n...). At some point this project derived `Serialize`/`Deserialize` directly\non a tagged `PropType` (`#[serde(tag = \"kind\")]`) and had to raise\n`#![recursion_limit]` to 2048 to get it to compile — each nesting level wraps\nthe generated serializer in another layer during codegen, and the default\nlimit (128) isn't enough for a real component tree.\n\n## Decision\n\n`PropType`, `CollectedType`, and `OpaqueReason` don't derive\n`Serialize`/`Deserialize`. Each has a hand-written impl (`to_json_value`/\n`from_json_value` for the first two) that builds a `serde_json::Value`\ndirectly instead of going through serde's derive machinery.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Adding a new variant means updating the manual match in both directions by\n  hand — the compiler won't remind you the way a derive would.\n  `resolver/mod.rs`'s \"no wildcard matches\" convention is what catches a\n  missed arm here instead.\n- `CollectedObjectField` (a struct nested inside `CollectedType::Object`, not\n  an enum) still derives normally — the recursion problem is specific to the\n  recursive *enums*, not every type that touches them.\n- The `#![recursion_limit = \"2048\"]` attribute in `crates/core/src/lib.rs` is\n  left over from the old derive-based design. Verified 2026-07-22: the full\n  workspace builds and every test passes with it removed — nothing in the\n  current manual-impl code path needs it. Worth deleting as a follow-up;\n  left as-is here since confirming that wasn't the point of this decision.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n`serde(remote)`, or just keeping the recursion-limit bump — both keep the\nderive, so you inherit the compile-time cost and a cryptic \"recursion limit\nexceeded\" the moment someone adds one more level of nesting. Manual impls\ntrade a bit of hand-maintenance for a predictable compile time.\n","userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1736963f-c10e-4d06-8057-24649ea0689a","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.217","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
