{"parentUuid":"bff85756-8a07-4682-8a01-62804ac868d9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdMjDzbhKdxd8Yh7JApc7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2403,"cache_read_input_tokens":809472,"output_tokens":7501,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2403,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7501,"cache_read_input_tokens":809472,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2403,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2403},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdMjDtYKb8NKWXQctHL8z","type":"assistant","uuid":"876e63c4-da75-4b3b-b189-b2690915edc5","timestamp":"2026-07-24T23:12:42.393Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"876e63c4-da75-4b3b-b189-b2690915edc5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdMjDzbhKdxd8Yh7JApc7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UydN8kv3Ud7GKJYTaqGLXj","name":"Workflow","input":{"script":"export const meta = {\n  name: 'post-session-adversarial-review',\n  description: 'Multi-model adversarial review of everything since cc2ff97 — bugs, perf, competitive-fit vs react-docgen-typescript',\n  phases: [\n    { title: 'Find', detail: 'Sonnet 5 scans 7 areas for bugs/perf/DX/infra issues' },\n    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'Opus 5 + Fable 5 independently judge each finding' },\n    { title: 'Synthesize', detail: 'Opus 5 (high effort) produces the prioritized report' },\n  ],\n}\n\nconst PROJECT_CONTEXT = `\noxc-react-docgen: a 10-100x faster drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript (RDT).\nOXC parses TypeScript/TSX in Rust with no type-checking pass; props are resolved structurally.\nThe ENTIRE value proposition is speed (cold extraction ~32ms vs RDT's multi-second Program spin-up)\nAND accuracy (must match RDT's real output on real component libraries — tracked in docs/rdt-coverage.md).\n\nTwo categories of bug are competitively fatal, more than any generic code-quality issue:\n1. PERFORMANCE REGRESSIONS in hot paths (resolver/*, extractor/*, cache.rs) — speed is why anyone\n   would choose this over RDT. An accidental clone, allocation, or repeated computation on a path\n   that runs per-type or per-file is a real competitive threat, not just a style nit.\n2. ACCURACY REGRESSIONS — anything that silently produces a wrong prop type, drops a prop, or\n   misclassifies a component. crates/core/CLAUDE.md non-negotiable #6: always emit a Diagnostic\n   when degrading, never fail silently.\n\nNon-negotiables: no unwrap() outside #[cfg(test)]; FxHashMap internal maps, BTreeMap JSON-output\nmaps; CompactString for hot-path names; no AST refs escape parse_file(); no terminal code in\ncrates/core; no wildcard matches on project-local enums (exhaustive match is deliberate — it's\nalready caught several real bugs this session by forcing every call site to handle new variants).\n\nThis review covers everything from commit cc2ff97 to HEAD on main: resolver diagnostic-quality\nfixes, a newly-wired barrel/re-export resolution feature (recursive, runs on every type lookup),\nconsumer-surface (NAPI binding + vite-plugin) fixes, and new infrastructure (moon task graph, CI\naffected-detection gating, oxfmt formatting, oxlint linting, AGPL license adoption). The infra\nchanges are ONLY locally verified — nobody has actually run the GitHub Actions workflow yet.\n`.trim()\n\nconst FINDINGS_SCHEMA = {\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    findings: {\n      type: 'array',\n      maxItems: 5,\n      items: {\n        type: 'object',\n        properties: {\n          file: { type: 'string' },\n          line: { type: 'number' },\n          dimension: { type: 'string', enum: ['correctness', 'performance', 'competitive-accuracy', 'dx-adoption', 'infra-correctness'] },\n          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['critical', 'high', 'medium', 'low'] },\n          summary: { type: 'string' },\n          detail: { type: 'string' },\n          suggested_fix: { type: 'string' },\n        },\n        required: ['file', 'dimension', 'severity', 'summary', 'detail'],\n      },\n    },\n  },\n  required: ['findings'],\n}\n\nconst VERDICT_SCHEMA = {\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    refuted: { type: 'boolean' },\n    reasoning: { type: 'string' },\n  },\n  required: ['refuted', 'reasoning'],\n}\n\nconst AREAS = [\n  {\n    key: 'resolver-diagnostics',\n    prompt: `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nReview the diagnostic-quality fixes in this range: run \\`git log --oneline cc2ff97..HEAD -- crates/core/src/resolver crates/core/src/types/output.rs crates/core/src/types/diagnostic.rs crates/core/src/known.rs\\` then \\`git diff cc2ff97..HEAD -- crates/core/src/resolver crates/core/src/types/output.rs crates/core/src/types/diagnostic.rs crates/core/src/known.rs\\`. Focus: corrected OpaqueReason tags, the new max_depth_diagnostic/io_read_error shared constructors, the UnresolvableImport message rewrite in chain.rs, group_diagnostics' comparison change. Look for: a wrong OpaqueReason mapping that slipped through, a message that leaks internal state again, a missed call site still using the old inline diagnostic construction, any correctness OR performance issue. Return up to 5 real findings, most severe first.`,\n  },\n  {\n    key: 'barrel-resolution',\n    prompt: `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nReview the new barrel/re-export resolution feature: \\`git show c11130a\\` and \\`git diff cc2ff97..HEAD -- crates/core/src/resolver/import.rs crates/core/src/import_map.rs\\`. This is NEW recursive logic (follow_reexports, MAX_REEXPORT_DEPTH=8) that runs on every named-type resolution — a hot path. Look hard for: infinite-loop risk in cyclic re-export graphs, unnecessary allocation per call (String/Vec churn), whether the depth guard is actually reachable/tested, whether wildcard-source iteration could be quadratic for large barrels, and any correctness gap (does it handle a barrel re-exporting from another barrel re-exporting from a third file — 3+ hops). Return up to 5 real findings, most severe first.`,\n  },\n  {\n    key: 'cleanup-and-dedup',\n    prompt: `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nReview \\`git show b50fa0b\\` (TS-utility-type dedup, dead branch removal, CollectedObjectField derive change, canonicalize_first_src_dir extraction). Look for: did the is_ts_utility_type consolidation actually preserve identical behavior at both call sites (chain.rs vs named.rs had a slightly different name list historically — verify nothing was dropped), does the CollectedObjectField derive change actually stay wire-compatible with anything that might depend on the old manual JSON shape, is the cva-call-detection allocation fix actually correct (still matches the same set of callee names). Return up to 5 real findings, most severe first.`,\n  },\n  {\n    key: 'consumer-surface',\n    prompt: `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nReview \\`git show bd6a8f9\\` (crates/binding/src/lib.rs's TryFrom rewrite, packages/napi/index.d.ts fixes, packages/vite-plugin/src/index.ts's buildStart + skipHtmlProps). This is the actual code real npm consumers run — a bug here is a first-impression bug. Look for: does the TryFrom<JsExtractOptions> rewrite handle every field correctly (compare against the old hand-built version for a dropped field), does buildStart's cold-extraction path have a race with configureServer in any dev-server-then-rebuild scenario, is skipHtmlProps's mapping to htmlAttributes:'none' actually correct for every options combination, does the Diagnostic field type fix (file/line/column/help as T|null) match what's actually serialized today. Return up to 5 real findings, most severe first.`,\n  },\n  {\n    key: 'infra-moon-ci',\n    prompt: `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nReview \\`git show 611d215\\` and \\`git show b06e303\\` (moon.yml task definitions across all 6 projects, and .github/workflows/ci.yml's new detect-changes affected-gating job). NOBODY has run this CI workflow yet — only local \\`moon query\\`/\\`moon run\\` testing happened. Look hard for: a project dependency edge that's still missing from the moon graph (would cause a real regression to be silently skipped by CI), a bash edge case in the detect-changes step (empty AFFECTED, a base ref that resolves but to a weird state, the python3 one-liner failing silently), whether the fail-open default actually triggers correctly in every case it's supposed to, whether gating cargo-deny would have been more correct than not gating it. Return up to 5 real findings, most severe first.`,\n  },\n  {\n    key: 'infra-format-lint',\n    prompt: `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nReview \\`git show 4c79e73\\` and \\`git show 31fd9f5\\` (.oxfmtrc.json, .oxlintrc.json, the justfile/CI wiring). Look for: source files that exist today but aren't covered by any of the explicit globs (check apps/validate, packages/napi, packages/vite-plugin, packages/cli for anything missed), whether any of the ~19 disabled oxlint rules are actually hiding a real bug class rather than pure noise, whether the two-invocation \"strict src / relaxed tests\" split could silently pass a file that matches neither glob, whether the config will visibly rot (e.g. a new file added later that nobody remembers to add to the globs). Return up to 5 real findings, most severe first.`,\n  },\n  {\n    key: 'license-and-docs',\n    prompt: `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nReview \\`git show 6c411b9\\` (AGPL license adoption across 13 package.json + Cargo.toml) and the docs/adr/*.md files added this range. Look for: any package.json or Cargo.toml that still claims MIT or has no license field that should have one, whether cargo-deny's allow-list change is scoped correctly (does it accidentally allow AGPL for a THIRD-PARTY dependency where that would be wrong, vs. just this workspace's own crates), whether any ADR's factual claim (the recursion_limit finding, the 32ms benchmark citation) is unverifiable or overstated as written. Return up to 5 real findings, most severe first.`,\n  },\n]\n\nphase('Find')\nconst found = await Promise.all(AREAS.map((a) => agent(a.prompt, { label: `find:${a.key}`, phase: 'Find', schema: FINDINGS_SCHEMA })))\n\nconst allFindings = []\nfound.forEach((result, i) => {\n  const list = (result && result.findings) || []\n  list.forEach((f) => allFindings.push({ ...f, area: AREAS[i].key }))\n})\nlog(`Found ${allFindings.length} candidate findings across ${AREAS.length} areas.`)\n\nphase('Verify')\nconst verified = await pipeline(\n  allFindings,\n  (f) =>\n    Promise.all([\n      agent(\n        `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nAdversarially verify this finding — try to REFUTE it. Read the actual current code at the cited file/line yourself before judging; don't take the summary on faith. Default to refuted=true if you can't confirm the issue is real and matters.\\n\\nFinding (area: ${f.area}, dimension: ${f.dimension}, severity: ${f.severity}):\\n${f.summary}\\n\\n${f.detail}`,\n        { label: `verify-opus:${f.area}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA, model: 'opus' },\n      ),\n      agent(\n        `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nAdversarially verify this finding — try to REFUTE it. Read the actual current code at the cited file/line yourself before judging; don't take the summary on faith. Default to refuted=true if you can't confirm the issue is real and matters.\\n\\nFinding (area: ${f.area}, dimension: ${f.dimension}, severity: ${f.severity}):\\n${f.summary}\\n\\n${f.detail}`,\n        { label: `verify-fable:${f.area}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA, model: 'fable' },\n      ),\n    ]).then((votes) => ({ ...f, votes: votes.filter(Boolean) })),\n)\n\nconst confirmed = verified.filter(Boolean).filter((f) => {\n  const refutes = f.votes.filter((v) => v.refuted).length\n  return refutes < f.votes.length // survives unless ALL available votes refute it\n})\nlog(`${confirmed.length} of ${allFindings.length} findings survived adversarial verification.`)\n\nphase('Synthesize')\nconst report = await agent(\n  `${PROJECT_CONTEXT}\\n\\nYou are producing the final report for this adversarial review. Below are confirmed findings (JSON), each with the verifying models' reasoning attached. Write a prioritized report: group by severity, lead with anything that's a real performance regression on a hot path or a real accuracy regression — those are the competitively fatal categories per the project context above. For each finding give: what it is, why it matters given this project's actual goal (beating RDT on speed+accuracy), and a concrete suggested fix. End with a short \"what's NOT a real problem\" section for anything notable that got refuted, so the reasoning isn't lost. Keep it tight and actionable, not padded.\\n\\nConfirmed findings:\\n${JSON.stringify(confirmed, null, 2)}`,\n  { label: 'synthesis', phase: 'Synthesize', model: 'opus', effort: 'high' },\n)\n\nreturn { totalFindings: allFindings.length, confirmedCount: confirmed.length, confirmed, report }\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2403,"cache_read_input_tokens":809472,"output_tokens":7501,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2403,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7501,"cache_read_input_tokens":809472,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2403,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2403},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdMjDtYKb8NKWXQctHL8z","type":"assistant","uuid":"d7716544-238f-4572-a243-ac54732a0222","timestamp":"2026-07-24T23:13:27.340Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
