{"parentUuid":"47113b20-8923-4b2a-8232-857185b78d0a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdMkYYh7XNDVVjLTbr1gB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013cwep8iMnh2WPHebwAeqYh","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport json\nd = json.load(open('/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-oxc-react-codegen/6e77fa42-b39d-4d85-9370-fe0b0fa9b104/tasks/w3830vi6c.output'))\nr = d['result']\nprint(r['report'])\n\" > /tmp/full_report.txt\nwc -l /tmp/full_report.txt\ncat /tmp/full_report.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":368,"cache_read_input_tokens":825275,"output_tokens":201,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":368},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":201,"cache_read_input_tokens":825275,"cache_creation_input_tokens":368,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":368,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdMkYUCVU833W2LLh2NSb","type":"assistant","uuid":"b25bb3cd-bfd9-4305-8c50-718240a2b5b0","timestamp":"2026-07-24T23:29:27.287Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"b25bb3cd-bfd9-4305-8c50-718240a2b5b0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a93efbbf-d554-43f4-8215-0c7aba37141b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013cwep8iMnh2WPHebwAeqYh","type":"tool_result","content":"     121 /tmp/full_report.txt\n# Adversarial Review Report — cc2ff97..HEAD\n\nTwo of the eleven confirmed findings touch the value proposition directly. One is a genuine hot-path performance regression introduced by this range's new feature; the rest is infra that silently doesn't do what it claims.\n\n---\n\n## P0 — Performance regression in a hot path (competitively fatal)\n\n### 1. `follow_reexports` has no visited-set — barrel graphs blow up exponentially\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/crates/core/src/resolver/import.rs:51-82`\n\n**What it is.** The newly wired barrel/re-export resolution recurses through every `export * from` specifier with only `depth >= MAX_REEXPORT_DEPTH` (8) as a bound. No `(file, name)` visited tracking, no memoization anywhere upstream — `resolve_to_canonical` is called fresh from `chain.rs:143`, `named.rs:47`, `primitives.rs:138`, `substitute.rs:93`, `template.rs:116`, and `ResolutionContext` (`resolver/mod.rs:96-121`) holds no cache. Cyclic or diamond-shaped barrel graphs therefore re-walk the same subtree once per path, `branching_factor^depth`.\n\nReproduced empirically with N mutually-re-exporting barrel files plus one component importing a name that doesn't exist (the unhappy path, which forces full traversal every call):\n\n| N barrels | `pipeline::extract` wall |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | 156 ms |\n| 4 | 188 ms |\n| 5 | ~550 ms |\n| 6 | 2.52 s |\n| 7 | 9.70 s |\n\n**Why it matters here.** Seven files turn a ~150 ms extraction into 9.7 s. The entire pitch is \"32 ms cold vs RDT's multi-second spin-up\"; this hands that back on a graph shape (`index.ts` wildcarding into sub-barrels that share an internal `types.ts`) that is *the exact motivating case* for the feature per the doc comment at `import.rs:44-50`. The non-cyclic diamond case degrades polynomially with no cycle needed at all. Every other recursive resolver walk in this codebase already guards this way (`chain.rs:39` `state.visited.insert(visit_key)`, `alias.rs:46-52` branch-local visited clone) — `follow_reexports` is the lone exception.\n\n**Secondary accuracy consequence (non-negotiable #6 adjacent):** `resolve_import_specifier` pushes an `UnresolvableImport` diagnostic per failure (`import.rs:196-204`), and nothing dedupes diagnostics in `pipeline/mod.rs`. Repeated traversal of the same failing edge emits N duplicate user-visible diagnostics for one real problem.\n\n**Fix.** Thread `visited: &mut FxHashSet<(Utf8PathBuf, CompactString)>` through `follow_reexports` and skip already-seen pairs; this subsumes the depth cap and collapses the redundant work. Fold in the memoization case below with the same change — a per-`ResolutionContext` memo of `(file, name) -> (canonical_file, canonical_name)` measured ~4x redundant `resolve_import_specifier` calls (4080 → 1014 on a 40-component fixture, `follow_reexports` CPU 14.5 ms → 6.6 ms, ~5-6% wall) even with a crude contended mutex. Two constraints on that memo: it must not swallow the `UnresolvableImport` diagnostics `resolve_import_specifier` emits, and `ResolutionContext` is shared by `&` across rayon, so it needs interior mutability that doesn't reintroduce lock contention (per-resolution-call memo in `ResolveState` avoids the problem entirely if cross-component reuse isn't worth the sharding work).\n\nWhile in here: `is_declared_at` (`import.rs:87-92`) does up to four `format!` allocations per visit, and `ImportResolutionMap::resolve_reexport_chain` (`import_map.rs:94-115`) rebuilds a `Vec` of cloned specifier `String`s on every call. Both are cheap to fix once the traversal count is bounded.\n\n---\n\n## P1 — Infra that reports success while doing nothing\n\n### 2. `moon query projects` doesn't accept `--base`/`--head` — affected-detection is 100% dead code\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/.github/workflows/ci.yml:52`\n\n**What it is.** On the pinned toolchain (`moon = \"2.3.3\"` in `.prototools`, installed by `moonrepo/setup-toolchain@v0`), `moon query projects --help` lists only `--affected`, `--downstream`, `--upstream` and filter flags. The literal CI command exits 2 with `error: unexpected argument '--base' found` — clap rejects it before moon does any work. Both fallback conditions then fire independently (`$?` is 2 *and* `AFFECTED` is empty, since the error went to stderr), so the step sets `rust=true`/`typescript=true`, prints \"moon query failed — running everything.\", and exits 0.\n\n**Why it matters.** Commit b06e303's entire stated benefit — skipping untouched language groups — has never engaged and never will. Every run pays full Rust+TS cost while showing green, with the only symptom a buried clap parse error and CI wall-clock that never improves. It fails open, so no check is *wrongly skipped* today and no perf/accuracy regression can slip past — that's why this is P1 and not P0 — but the commit asserts a verified feature that has literally never run.\n\n**Fix.** moon 2.3.3 reads base/head from the environment. Verified working:\n```yaml\nenv:\n  MOON_BASE: ${{ env.BASE }}\n  MOON_HEAD: ${{ github.sha }}\nrun: moon query projects --affected --downstream deep\n```\nNote that simply *dropping* the flags is not a fix: bare `--affected` compares local working-tree state, which is clean in a fresh checkout, yielding `{\"projects\":[]}` and tripping the `-z \"$AFFECTED\"` branch anyway. Add a `moon --version` assertion or a smoke step so a future proto bump can't silently re-break this.\n\n### 3. Fixing #2 without touching the case arms opens a real gating hole\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/.github/workflows/ci.yml:65-72`\n\n**What it is.** `.moon/workspace.yml` registers six projects; the loop maps only `core|cli|crates-binding → rust` and `vite-plugin|napi → typescript`, with no default arm. `validate` (`apps/validate`) matches neither. `apps/validate/moon.yml` depends on `cli:build`, i.e. validate is *downstream* of cli with no dependents of its own, so `--downstream deep` on a validate-only change yields exactly `IDS=\"validate\"` → both outputs false. The `format` job — the only thing in the repo that runs oxfmt/oxlint, and which explicitly targets `apps/validate/src/**/*.ts` (line 166) and `apps/validate/src` (line 169) — is gated on `typescript == 'true'`. Separately, `packages/cli` has no `moon.yml` and no `projects:` entry, so a change to `packages/cli/bin/cli.js` can never surface as any project id at all, even though the format job lints it.\n\nThis is currently masked by #2's fail-open. The moment #2 is fixed, a PR touching only `apps/validate/src` (the exact tree 31fd9f5 hand-edited for lint fixes) or only `packages/cli/bin/cli.js` gets zero CI coverage of the files it changed — the *silently skipped check* failure mode the workflow's own comment at lines 21-23 calls \"a much worse failure mode than an unnecessary one.\"\n\n**Fix.** Same PR as #2. Add `validate) TS=true ;;` (or better, a `*) RUST=true; TS=true ;;` default arm so the next unmapped project fails open instead of silently ungated), and either give `packages/cli` a `moon.yml` + workspace entry or drop it from the format job's path list so coverage isn't claimed where it can't be triggered.\n\n---\n\n## P2 — Medium\n\n### 4. `vcs.defaultBranch: master`, repo default is `main`\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/.moon/workspace.yml:14`\n\nNo `master` ref exists anywhere. `moon query projects --affected true` and, worse, `CI=true moon ci core:build` — moon's canonical CI entry point — hard-fail with `fatal: ambiguous argument 'master': unknown revision`. Proved causal in an isolated worktree: changing only that one word makes both commands work. It's latent for the current workflow (which passes explicit refs), but it's a trap sitting directly in front of the #2 fix, and moon also uses `defaultBranch` for remote-cache write gating, which is being silently misjudged. One-word fix: `defaultBranch: main`. Note bare `--affected` (no value) doesn't consult `defaultBranch` at all — it uses working-tree status — so the finding's \"any operation that falls back\" is narrower than stated, but `moon ci` alone justifies the fix.\n\n### 5. Format/lint allowlist uses hard-coded filenames; the gap has already been realized\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/justfile:40-41`, mirrored verbatim at `ci.yml:163-171`\n\n`ts_src`/`ts_src_only` name `packages/napi/index.d.ts` and `packages/cli/bin/cli.js` as exact files while every other entry is a directory glob. Nothing enumerates the filesystem to assert coverage, so an unlisted file produces silence, not a failure. This isn't hypothetical — `packages/vite-plugin/vitest.config.ts` is hand-written, committed, behavior-relevant (`pool: 'threads'`, test `include`), declared as a moon `test` input, and matched by no entry: it sits at package root between the `src` and `tests` globs. It happens to be clean today (both tools exit 0 on it), so this is drift exposure rather than an existing violation.\n\nThe obvious defense of the literal filenames — \"those packages contain generated output\" — doesn't hold: `.oxfmtrc.json` and `.oxlintrc.json` already exclude `**/binding.d.ts`, `packages/napi/index.js`, and `dist/**` by pattern, and `packages/napi/index.d.ts` line 1 says \"Hand-maintained — NOT regenerated by `napi build`\". Directory globs are safe.\n\n**Fix.** Widen to `packages/napi/*.d.ts`, `packages/cli/**/*.js`, add `packages/vite-plugin/vitest.config.ts` (or `packages/vite-plugin/*.ts`), and have `ci.yml`'s format job call `just lint`/`just fmt-check` instead of duplicating the lists — the duplication is a second drift surface that already needs fixing twice.\n\n---\n\n## P3 — Low (doc/consistency, no runtime impact)\n\n### 6. `named.rs` unresolvable diagnostic didn't get chain.rs's barrel-location note\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/crates/core/src/resolver/named.rs:149-160`\n\n`chain.rs:161-181` gained `resolved_elsewhere` / `(resolved to 'N' in 'F')` in this range specifically because barrel following can move `canonical_file`/`canonical_name` away from the consuming file. `named.rs` step 7 goes through the same `resolve_to_canonical` and is reachable in the identical redirect state, but still emits the flat message. Both variables are in scope at the call site, so it's a four-line copy — better, factor the note out into a shared helper alongside `max_depth_diagnostic` so the two can't drift again. Diagnostic text only; the `UnresolvableImport` warning still fires, so #6 is satisfied.\n\n### 7. Stale `recursion_limit` rationale contradicts the project's own ADR\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/crates/core/src/pipeline/mod.rs:174-178`\n\nThe comment says serde monomorphization for `PropType` \"requires `#![recursion_limit = \"2048\"]`\". `PropType` has a hand-written `impl serde::Serialize` (`types/output.rs:280`, plus a manual `Deserialize` at 286) — no derive, no recursive expansion. ADR 0002 line 18 says the attribute is \"left over from the old derive-based design. Verified 2026-07-22: the full workspace builds and every test passes with it removed\", and `ARCHITECTURE.md:160` agrees it's \"no longer load-bearing\". The comment now deters the ADR's own recommended cleanup. Drop the parenthetical; the rest of the sentence (centralize the generic `serde_json` instantiation so downstream crates don't re-monomorphize) is still valid.\n\n### 8. `deny.toml` AGPL allow is graph-wide, comment implies it isn't\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/deny.toml:7`\n\n`\"AGPL-3.0-or-later\", # this workspace's own crates` is a plain `[licenses] allow` member; no `[[licenses.exceptions]]` or `clarify` block exists, so it permits AGPL from any transitive dep too. The entry *is* required as written (all three member crates are publishable with `license.workspace = true`), so the fix is cosmetic scoping:\n```toml\n[[licenses.exceptions]]\nname = \"oxc-react-docgen-core\"   # + -cli, -binding\nallow = [\"AGPL-3.0-or-later\"]\n```\nFiled as high; it isn't. See below.\n\n---\n\n## What's NOT a real problem\n\n**The README's \"32ms cold extraction on a 15-component fixture set\" is reproducible.** Filed as a stale/invented number on the grounds that `crates/core/benches/extraction.rs` only covers 3 libraries. But the claim describes whole-pipeline cold extraction, which the CLI itself reports as `stats.durationMs`, not the divan bench. Running the set the docs actually name:\n```\n./target/release/oxc-react-docgen extract --src fixtures/shadcn,fixtures/mui,fixtures/chakra,fixtures/mantine,fixtures/react-aria,fixtures/radix\n→ components: 15  durationMs: 24-29   (27/26/26 with a fresh --cache-dir, dtsCacheHits: 0)\n```\nExactly 15 components, all six named libraries represented. The residual is reproducibility hygiene only — no committed task pins the number, so it can drift as fixtures change. Worth a `just bench-cold` recipe naming the invocation; not a correctness issue, and not medium severity.\n\n**`deny.toml` as high severity.** The harm channel is \"an unnoticed transitive AGPL dep,\" but this workspace *is* AGPL-3.0-or-later, so an inbound AGPL dep creates no incompatibility or obligation the project doesn't already carry. Also, the comment follows the file's existing convention — every sibling entry annotates *provenance* (`# unicode-ident`, `# foldhash (via simd-json → oxc_resolver)`), not scoping — so \"will mislead the next reviewer\" is weaker than argued. Real tidy-up, low priority.\n\n**Missing memoization as a standalone medium perf finding.** Measured impact on its own is ~5-6% wall time; oxc_resolver already caches path resolution internally (~1.3 µs/hop), the depth cap is 8, and `is_declared_at`'s early return skips the walk entirely for direct imports. It only becomes serious when combined with the missing visited-set, which is why it's folded into P0 rather than tracked separately — and the visited-set fix captures most of the win.\n\n**\"apps/validate / packages/cli changes silently skip CI today.\"** Not today — #2's fail-open runs everything unconditionally, so a `packages/cli`-only PR currently gets full lint and format coverage. The gating hole is purely latent and only activates when #2 is fixed. That's a reason to fix both in the same PR, not a reason to file it as critical current behavior.\n\n**Out-of-scope observation worth a separate look:** in the extract run above, `fixtures/mui/Button.d.ts` and `fixtures/mantine/Button.d.ts` yield no component while their `TextField.d.ts`/`TextInput.d.ts` siblings do. Both libraries are still represented so it doesn't affect the benchmark claim, but a silently-dropped component in a fixture is exactly the accuracy category this project cares about.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"fe227f0f-78ad-48ea-b167-80aefab7c547","timestamp":"2026-07-24T23:29:30.225Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"     121 /tmp/full_report.txt\n# Adversarial Review Report — cc2ff97..HEAD\n\nTwo of the eleven confirmed findings touch the value proposition directly. One is a genuine hot-path performance regression introduced by this range's new feature; the rest is infra that silently doesn't do what it claims.\n\n---\n\n## P0 — Performance regression in a hot path (competitively fatal)\n\n### 1. `follow_reexports` has no visited-set — barrel graphs blow up exponentially\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/crates/core/src/resolver/import.rs:51-82`\n\n**What it is.** The newly wired barrel/re-export resolution recurses through every `export * from` specifier with only `depth >= MAX_REEXPORT_DEPTH` (8) as a bound. No `(file, name)` visited tracking, no memoization anywhere upstream — `resolve_to_canonical` is called fresh from `chain.rs:143`, `named.rs:47`, `primitives.rs:138`, `substitute.rs:93`, `template.rs:116`, and `ResolutionContext` (`resolver/mod.rs:96-121`) holds no cache. Cyclic or diamond-shaped barrel graphs therefore re-walk the same subtree once per path, `branching_factor^depth`.\n\nReproduced empirically with N mutually-re-exporting barrel files plus one component importing a name that doesn't exist (the unhappy path, which forces full traversal every call):\n\n| N barrels | `pipeline::extract` wall |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | 156 ms |\n| 4 | 188 ms |\n| 5 | ~550 ms |\n| 6 | 2.52 s |\n| 7 | 9.70 s |\n\n**Why it matters here.** Seven files turn a ~150 ms extraction into 9.7 s. The entire pitch is \"32 ms cold vs RDT's multi-second spin-up\"; this hands that back on a graph shape (`index.ts` wildcarding into sub-barrels that share an internal `types.ts`) that is *the exact motivating case* for the feature per the doc comment at `import.rs:44-50`. The non-cyclic diamond case degrades polynomially with no cycle needed at all. Every other recursive resolver walk in this codebase already guards this way (`chain.rs:39` `state.visited.insert(visit_key)`, `alias.rs:46-52` branch-local visited clone) — `follow_reexports` is the lone exception.\n\n**Secondary accuracy consequence (non-negotiable #6 adjacent):** `resolve_import_specifier` pushes an `UnresolvableImport` diagnostic per failure (`import.rs:196-204`), and nothing dedupes diagnostics in `pipeline/mod.rs`. Repeated traversal of the same failing edge emits N duplicate user-visible diagnostics for one real problem.\n\n**Fix.** Thread `visited: &mut FxHashSet<(Utf8PathBuf, CompactString)>` through `follow_reexports` and skip already-seen pairs; this subsumes the depth cap and collapses the redundant work. Fold in the memoization case below with the same change — a per-`ResolutionContext` memo of `(file, name) -> (canonical_file, canonical_name)` measured ~4x redundant `resolve_import_specifier` calls (4080 → 1014 on a 40-component fixture, `follow_reexports` CPU 14.5 ms → 6.6 ms, ~5-6% wall) even with a crude contended mutex. Two constraints on that memo: it must not swallow the `UnresolvableImport` diagnostics `resolve_import_specifier` emits, and `ResolutionContext` is shared by `&` across rayon, so it needs interior mutability that doesn't reintroduce lock contention (per-resolution-call memo in `ResolveState` avoids the problem entirely if cross-component reuse isn't worth the sharding work).\n\nWhile in here: `is_declared_at` (`import.rs:87-92`) does up to four `format!` allocations per visit, and `ImportResolutionMap::resolve_reexport_chain` (`import_map.rs:94-115`) rebuilds a `Vec` of cloned specifier `String`s on every call. Both are cheap to fix once the traversal count is bounded.\n\n---\n\n## P1 — Infra that reports success while doing nothing\n\n### 2. `moon query projects` doesn't accept `--base`/`--head` — affected-detection is 100% dead code\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/.github/workflows/ci.yml:52`\n\n**What it is.** On the pinned toolchain (`moon = \"2.3.3\"` in `.prototools`, installed by `moonrepo/setup-toolchain@v0`), `moon query projects --help` lists only `--affected`, `--downstream`, `--upstream` and filter flags. The literal CI command exits 2 with `error: unexpected argument '--base' found` — clap rejects it before moon does any work. Both fallback conditions then fire independently (`$?` is 2 *and* `AFFECTED` is empty, since the error went to stderr), so the step sets `rust=true`/`typescript=true`, prints \"moon query failed — running everything.\", and exits 0.\n\n**Why it matters.** Commit b06e303's entire stated benefit — skipping untouched language groups — has never engaged and never will. Every run pays full Rust+TS cost while showing green, with the only symptom a buried clap parse error and CI wall-clock that never improves. It fails open, so no check is *wrongly skipped* today and no perf/accuracy regression can slip past — that's why this is P1 and not P0 — but the commit asserts a verified feature that has literally never run.\n\n**Fix.** moon 2.3.3 reads base/head from the environment. Verified working:\n```yaml\nenv:\n  MOON_BASE: ${{ env.BASE }}\n  MOON_HEAD: ${{ github.sha }}\nrun: moon query projects --affected --downstream deep\n```\nNote that simply *dropping* the flags is not a fix: bare `--affected` compares local working-tree state, which is clean in a fresh checkout, yielding `{\"projects\":[]}` and tripping the `-z \"$AFFECTED\"` branch anyway. Add a `moon --version` assertion or a smoke step so a future proto bump can't silently re-break this.\n\n### 3. Fixing #2 without touching the case arms opens a real gating hole\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/.github/workflows/ci.yml:65-72`\n\n**What it is.** `.moon/workspace.yml` registers six projects; the loop maps only `core|cli|crates-binding → rust` and `vite-plugin|napi → typescript`, with no default arm. `validate` (`apps/validate`) matches neither. `apps/validate/moon.yml` depends on `cli:build`, i.e. validate is *downstream* of cli with no dependents of its own, so `--downstream deep` on a validate-only change yields exactly `IDS=\"validate\"` → both outputs false. The `format` job — the only thing in the repo that runs oxfmt/oxlint, and which explicitly targets `apps/validate/src/**/*.ts` (line 166) and `apps/validate/src` (line 169) — is gated on `typescript == 'true'`. Separately, `packages/cli` has no `moon.yml` and no `projects:` entry, so a change to `packages/cli/bin/cli.js` can never surface as any project id at all, even though the format job lints it.\n\nThis is currently masked by #2's fail-open. The moment #2 is fixed, a PR touching only `apps/validate/src` (the exact tree 31fd9f5 hand-edited for lint fixes) or only `packages/cli/bin/cli.js` gets zero CI coverage of the files it changed — the *silently skipped check* failure mode the workflow's own comment at lines 21-23 calls \"a much worse failure mode than an unnecessary one.\"\n\n**Fix.** Same PR as #2. Add `validate) TS=true ;;` (or better, a `*) RUST=true; TS=true ;;` default arm so the next unmapped project fails open instead of silently ungated), and either give `packages/cli` a `moon.yml` + workspace entry or drop it from the format job's path list so coverage isn't claimed where it can't be triggered.\n\n---\n\n## P2 — Medium\n\n### 4. `vcs.defaultBranch: master`, repo default is `main`\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/.moon/workspace.yml:14`\n\nNo `master` ref exists anywhere. `moon query projects --affected true` and, worse, `CI=true moon ci core:build` — moon's canonical CI entry point — hard-fail with `fatal: ambiguous argument 'master': unknown revision`. Proved causal in an isolated worktree: changing only that one word makes both commands work. It's latent for the current workflow (which passes explicit refs), but it's a trap sitting directly in front of the #2 fix, and moon also uses `defaultBranch` for remote-cache write gating, which is being silently misjudged. One-word fix: `defaultBranch: main`. Note bare `--affected` (no value) doesn't consult `defaultBranch` at all — it uses working-tree status — so the finding's \"any operation that falls back\" is narrower than stated, but `moon ci` alone justifies the fix.\n\n### 5. Format/lint allowlist uses hard-coded filenames; the gap has already been realized\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/justfile:40-41`, mirrored verbatim at `ci.yml:163-171`\n\n`ts_src`/`ts_src_only` name `packages/napi/index.d.ts` and `packages/cli/bin/cli.js` as exact files while every other entry is a directory glob. Nothing enumerates the filesystem to assert coverage, so an unlisted file produces silence, not a failure. This isn't hypothetical — `packages/vite-plugin/vitest.config.ts` is hand-written, committed, behavior-relevant (`pool: 'threads'`, test `include`), declared as a moon `test` input, and matched by no entry: it sits at package root between the `src` and `tests` globs. It happens to be clean today (both tools exit 0 on it), so this is drift exposure rather than an existing violation.\n\nThe obvious defense of the literal filenames — \"those packages contain generated output\" — doesn't hold: `.oxfmtrc.json` and `.oxlintrc.json` already exclude `**/binding.d.ts`, `packages/napi/index.js`, and `dist/**` by pattern, and `packages/napi/index.d.ts` line 1 says \"Hand-maintained — NOT regenerated by `napi build`\". Directory globs are safe.\n\n**Fix.** Widen to `packages/napi/*.d.ts`, `packages/cli/**/*.js`, add `packages/vite-plugin/vitest.config.ts` (or `packages/vite-plugin/*.ts`), and have `ci.yml`'s format job call `just lint`/`just fmt-check` instead of duplicating the lists — the duplication is a second drift surface that already needs fixing twice.\n\n---\n\n## P3 — Low (doc/consistency, no runtime impact)\n\n### 6. `named.rs` unresolvable diagnostic didn't get chain.rs's barrel-location note\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/crates/core/src/resolver/named.rs:149-160`\n\n`chain.rs:161-181` gained `resolved_elsewhere` / `(resolved to 'N' in 'F')` in this range specifically because barrel following can move `canonical_file`/`canonical_name` away from the consuming file. `named.rs` step 7 goes through the same `resolve_to_canonical` and is reachable in the identical redirect state, but still emits the flat message. Both variables are in scope at the call site, so it's a four-line copy — better, factor the note out into a shared helper alongside `max_depth_diagnostic` so the two can't drift again. Diagnostic text only; the `UnresolvableImport` warning still fires, so #6 is satisfied.\n\n### 7. Stale `recursion_limit` rationale contradicts the project's own ADR\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/crates/core/src/pipeline/mod.rs:174-178`\n\nThe comment says serde monomorphization for `PropType` \"requires `#![recursion_limit = \"2048\"]`\". `PropType` has a hand-written `impl serde::Serialize` (`types/output.rs:280`, plus a manual `Deserialize` at 286) — no derive, no recursive expansion. ADR 0002 line 18 says the attribute is \"left over from the old derive-based design. Verified 2026-07-22: the full workspace builds and every test passes with it removed\", and `ARCHITECTURE.md:160` agrees it's \"no longer load-bearing\". The comment now deters the ADR's own recommended cleanup. Drop the parenthetical; the rest of the sentence (centralize the generic `serde_json` instantiation so downstream crates don't re-monomorphize) is still valid.\n\n### 8. `deny.toml` AGPL allow is graph-wide, comment implies it isn't\n`/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/deny.toml:7`\n\n`\"AGPL-3.0-or-later\", # this workspace's own crates` is a plain `[licenses] allow` member; no `[[licenses.exceptions]]` or `clarify` block exists, so it permits AGPL from any transitive dep too. The entry *is* required as written (all three member crates are publishable with `license.workspace = true`), so the fix is cosmetic scoping:\n```toml\n[[licenses.exceptions]]\nname = \"oxc-react-docgen-core\"   # + -cli, -binding\nallow = [\"AGPL-3.0-or-later\"]\n```\nFiled as high; it isn't. See below.\n\n---\n\n## What's NOT a real problem\n\n**The README's \"32ms cold extraction on a 15-component fixture set\" is reproducible.** Filed as a stale/invented number on the grounds that `crates/core/benches/extraction.rs` only covers 3 libraries. But the claim describes whole-pipeline cold extraction, which the CLI itself reports as `stats.durationMs`, not the divan bench. Running the set the docs actually name:\n```\n./target/release/oxc-react-docgen extract --src fixtures/shadcn,fixtures/mui,fixtures/chakra,fixtures/mantine,fixtures/react-aria,fixtures/radix\n→ components: 15  durationMs: 24-29   (27/26/26 with a fresh --cache-dir, dtsCacheHits: 0)\n```\nExactly 15 components, all six named libraries represented. The residual is reproducibility hygiene only — no committed task pins the number, so it can drift as fixtures change. Worth a `just bench-cold` recipe naming the invocation; not a correctness issue, and not medium severity.\n\n**`deny.toml` as high severity.** The harm channel is \"an unnoticed transitive AGPL dep,\" but this workspace *is* AGPL-3.0-or-later, so an inbound AGPL dep creates no incompatibility or obligation the project doesn't already carry. Also, the comment follows the file's existing convention — every sibling entry annotates *provenance* (`# unicode-ident`, `# foldhash (via simd-json → oxc_resolver)`), not scoping — so \"will mislead the next reviewer\" is weaker than argued. Real tidy-up, low priority.\n\n**Missing memoization as a standalone medium perf finding.** Measured impact on its own is ~5-6% wall time; oxc_resolver already caches path resolution internally (~1.3 µs/hop), the depth cap is 8, and `is_declared_at`'s early return skips the walk entirely for direct imports. It only becomes serious when combined with the missing visited-set, which is why it's folded into P0 rather than tracked separately — and the visited-set fix captures most of the win.\n\n**\"apps/validate / packages/cli changes silently skip CI today.\"** Not today — #2's fail-open runs everything unconditionally, so a `packages/cli`-only PR currently gets full lint and format coverage. The gating hole is purely latent and only activates when #2 is fixed. That's a reason to fix both in the same PR, not a reason to file it as critical current behavior.\n\n**Out-of-scope observation worth a separate look:** in the extract run above, `fixtures/mui/Button.d.ts` and `fixtures/mantine/Button.d.ts` yield no component while their `TextField.d.ts`/`TextInput.d.ts` siblings do. Both libraries are still represented so it doesn't affect the benchmark claim, but a silently-dropped component in a fixture is exactly the accuracy category this project cares about.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b25bb3cd-bfd9-4305-8c50-718240a2b5b0","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
