{"parentUuid":"f3cb46ea-9fcb-4842-993a-7ef0b6fdd6a0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdPGu3jAfGwEK5GiS3eQC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Let me look at what benchmark/comparison infrastructure already exists before building on top of it."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1461,"cache_read_input_tokens":213429,"output_tokens":1461,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1461,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1461,"cache_read_input_tokens":213429,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1461},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdPGtzjb9pCQ4sJpG9H9G","type":"assistant","uuid":"ac76b2dc-ef25-4abb-834d-4b9567355b71","timestamp":"2026-07-25T18:48:06.955Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"ac76b2dc-ef25-4abb-834d-4b9567355b71","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdPGu3jAfGwEK5GiS3eQC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0169JGzWKNNfcc2MbEoEHqcP","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Survey existing benchmark and comparison infra","prompt":"In the repo at /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen, I need a thorough inventory of existing benchmarking and RDT/react-docgen comparison infrastructure before I build a new \"proper benchmarks\" report. Investigate and report back (this is research only — do not write or edit any files):\n\n1. `crates/core/benches/extraction.rs` (or wherever Rust benchmarks live) — what does it measure, what harness (criterion?), does it produce reproducible numbers, what fixtures does it use?\n2. `apps/validate/` — read all of `src/*.ts` (baseline.ts, compare.ts, fixtures.ts, run-react-docgen-typescript.ts, run-react-docgen.ts, types.ts, and any others). I need to understand exactly:\n   - How it invokes react-docgen-typescript and react-docgen for comparison\n   - What comparison logic already exists (prop-by-prop diff?) and what its output looks like today (is it a summary count, a full diff, JSON?)\n   - What the `compare:all` moon/pnpm task actually runs and what fixtures it covers\n   - Whether it already computes anything like an \"agreement rate\" percentage, and whether mismatches are categorized/bucketed by type of divergence or just listed\n   - Any known limitations already documented in code comments (e.g. \"RDT requires real TS source, can't compare DTS fixtures\")\n3. `fixtures/` directory — list what libraries/components are available (shadcn, mui, chakra, mantine, radix, react-aria, panda, rdt-compat or similar) and roughly how many components/fixtures total, and which are `.tsx` (real source, comparable to both RDT and react-docgen) vs `.d.ts` (declaration-only, only comparable to this tool's own output, not RDT/react-docgen since those need real source)\n4. `docs/rdt-coverage.md` if it exists — what does it currently claim about RDT compatibility/coverage, is it up to date or stale\n5. Any prior adversarial-analysis or gap-fix docs (docs/archive/adversarial-analysis-2026-06-28.md, docs/archive/2026-06-28-structural-gap-fixes.md, docs/archive/2026-07-07-adversarial-analysis-fixes.md) — list every KNOWN unresolved gap/limitation mentioned there that would still need to go in a \"shortcomings\" section today (cross-reference against current code if you can quickly grep for whether each was actually fixed already, e.g. search for \"Union\" handling in resolve_base_as_chain, or discriminant detection using all union members not just members[0])\n6. Whether `criterion` or any other Rust benchmark crate is set up as a dev-dependency, and whether `cargo bench` currently produces a report file/HTML anywhere\n7. package.json scripts in apps/validate and the root — what commands exist today for running comparisons or benchmarks\n\nReport back with concrete file:line references and a clear structured summary. I want to know exactly what I can reuse vs. what I need to build from scratch for a \"cold + incremental perf\" and \"prop-agreement-rate + divergence taxonomy\" benchmark report comparing oxc-react-docgen against react-docgen-typescript and react-docgen.","subagent_type":"Explore","run_in_background":false},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1461,"cache_read_input_tokens":213429,"output_tokens":1461,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1461,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1461,"cache_read_input_tokens":213429,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1461},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdPGtzjb9pCQ4sJpG9H9G","type":"assistant","uuid":"ac9ae3ce-22e9-4ea2-a589-b3fb861371a7","timestamp":"2026-07-25T18:48:19.668Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"ac9ae3ce-22e9-4ea2-a589-b3fb861371a7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"3129edff-280e-4bc2-ac9a-02718d779751","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0169JGzWKNNfcc2MbEoEHqcP","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a complete picture. Here is the full inventory.\n\n# Inventory: benchmarking + RDT/react-docgen comparison infrastructure\n\n## 1. Rust benchmarks — `crates/core/benches/extraction.rs` (18 lines total)\n\n- **Harness:** `divan` (0.1, workspace dep, `Cargo.toml:72`), **not criterion**. `crates/core/Cargo.toml` declares:\n  ```\n  [dev-dependencies]\n  divan.workspace = true\n  ...\n  [[bench]]\n  name = \"extraction\"\n  harness = false\n  ```\n- **What it measures:** a single parametrized bench, `extract_file_bench`, over 4 hardcoded fixture args (`extraction.rs:5`): `shadcn/button.tsx`, `shadcn/input.tsx`, `radix/button.d.ts`, `mui/Button.d.ts`. For each, it resolves the fixture's **parent directory** (not the single file) and calls `oxc_react_docgen_core::pipeline::extract()` with `src_dirs` pointed at that whole directory (`extraction.rs:7-17`) — so it's really benchmarking \"extract this entire fixture library dir,\" named misleadingly after one file in it.\n- **Reproducibility:** divan reports mean/median/stdev over N iterations to stdout only; there is **no saved report/HTML/JSON output** anywhere (checked `target/`, no `criterion/` or `divan/` report dirs exist). `[profile.bench]` inherits `release` with `debug = true` (`Cargo.toml:82-84`) — good for consistent optimized numbers, but nothing pins CPU affinity, disables turbo/frequency scaling, or archives historical runs for trend comparison.\n- **Invocation:** `just bench` → `cargo bench --workspace --exclude oxc-react-docgen-napi` (`justfile:32-34`); moon mirrors this at `.moon/tasks.yml:46-48`. No `cargo bench` output file is captured by either.\n- **Cold vs incremental:** nothing here distinguishes cold-cache vs warm/incremental extraction (e.g. DTS parse cache in `cache.rs` — see STATUS.md gap below); every divan iteration presumably reuses whatever process-level caches exist, so it's implicitly measuring warm/incremental performance only, with no cold-start variant.\n\n**Verdict:** minimal micro-bench scaffold exists and works, but it's narrow (4 fixtures, whole-directory not single-file granularity), produces no artifact/report, and has no cold-vs-incremental split — largely build-from-scratch territory for a \"perf report.\"\n\n## 2. `apps/validate/` — full read of `src/*.ts`\n\nFiles: `fixtures.ts`, `baseline.ts`, `run-react-docgen.ts`, `run-react-docgen-typescript.ts`, `run-ours.ts`, `compare.ts`, `types.ts`. No others.\n\n### fixtures.ts (`apps/validate/src/fixtures.ts`)\n`discoverFixtures()` walks `fixtures/` (one level), returns `{name, path, ext, isTsx, isDts}`. Explicitly **skips the `panda` directory entirely** (line 19: `\"skip styled-system subdir complexity for now\"`) and skips `index.ts` barrel files (line 22). This means panda isn't in any of the three tool runners' comparisons at all despite being a real fixture dir.\n\n### How each tool is invoked\n- **react-docgen** (`run-react-docgen.ts:30`): `discoverFixtures().filter(f => f.isTsx)` — **only `.tsx` files**, calls `parse(source, {filename})` from the `react-docgen` npm package directly, normalizes into `NormalizedOutput`.\n- **react-docgen-typescript** (`run-react-docgen-typescript.ts:22`): `discoverFixtures().filter(f => !f.isDts)` — comment explicitly states \"RDT needs real TS files\" — excludes `.d.ts`. Uses `withCustomConfig(tsconfigPath, {...})` from the root `tsconfig.json`, falls back to a simpler config if that throws (lines 9-19). Keys results per-component as `${fixture.name}/${comp.displayName}`.\n- **oxc-react-docgen** (`run-ours.ts`): shells out to the **built CLI binary** (`target/release/oxc-react-docgen`, falling back to `target/debug`, lines 9-11) via `execSync`, one invocation **per whole library directory** (`--src ${libPath} --format canonical`), not per file — so timing here is library-granularity, inconsistent with the per-file granularity of the other two runners. Has its own `propTypeToString()` reimplementation of type-to-string logic (lines 21-109) duplicating logic that also exists in the Rust `PropType::raw_string()`.\n\n### Comparison logic (`compare.ts`, 160 lines)\n- Loads three JSON baseline files by tool name into `Map<fixture, ToolResult>` (lines 4-13).\n- Output today is a **printed console log**, not JSON/structured — for each fixture × component: timing line, prop-count line, prop-name-set diffs (`oursOnly`, `rdtOnly`, `rdgOnly`), a special carve-out that treats `rdt-only` props as \"covered by inheritance\" if they appear in `notableInheritedNames` (our own tool's metadata) vs `trueMisses` (lines 84-104), a small **type-diff** section limited to props common to both sides where the raw type string differs (lines 107-124), and a heuristic **win/tie/miss** classification per component (lines 126-147).\n- End-of-run **summary** (lines 153-159): total prop counts, `Coverage: oursTotal/rdtTotal (%)`, and `Identical matches / Both empty / Misses` counts. **This is the only \"agreement rate\"-like number that exists today** — it's a raw prop-count ratio, not a true per-prop match rate, and it's global across all fixtures, not broken out per-library or per-divergence-type.\n- **No categorization/taxonomy of mismatch types** beyond the single ad hoc \"HTML-attribute-inheritance-covered vs real miss\" bucket — type diffs, missing props, extra props are otherwise just flat lists, not bucketed by *kind* of divergence (e.g. union-ordering, opaque-vs-resolved, literal-vs-widened, JSDoc-only).\n- **react-docgen (rdg) results are loaded but barely used** in the console output — `ComparisonResult.rdgOnly`/`rdgOnlyComponents` are declared in `types.ts` but **`compare.ts` never actually populates or prints them** (only `r_rdg?.error` and its timing are surfaced at lines 40, 46). The whole rdg comparison axis is effectively vestigial today.\n- `ComparisonResult` type (`types.ts:30-42`) is more complete than what `compare.ts` actually computes/prints (e.g. `rdtOnlyComponents`/`rdgOnlyComponents` aren't computed anywhere in `compare.ts`).\n\n### `compare:all` task\n`apps/validate/package.json:13`: `\"compare:all\": \"tsx src/baseline.ts && tsx src/compare.ts\"`. `baseline.ts` runs all three tool scripts via `spawnSync('tsx', [script], {maxBuffer: 100MB, ...})` (note the comment at lines 11-15 about a real prior bug where Node's default 1MB `maxBuffer` silently truncated stdout mid-JSON) and writes each to `apps/validate/baselines/*.json`. moon's `compare` task (`apps/validate/moon.yml`) depends on `cli:build` first and disables caching. Covers **all fixtures found by `discoverFixtures()` except `panda`**, split per-tool by the `.tsx`/`.d.ts` filters above.\n\n### Documented limitations found in code comments\n- `fixtures.ts:19` — panda skipped (\"styled-system subdir complexity for now\").\n- `run-react-docgen-typescript.ts:22` and `baseline.ts` — RDT can't run on `.d.ts`-only fixtures (needs real TS source).\n- `run-react-docgen.ts:30` — react-docgen only runs on `.tsx` (not plain `.ts`, not `.d.ts`).\n- `baseline.ts:11-15` — maxBuffer gotcha already fixed once (documented so it isn't re-introduced).\n\n## 3. `fixtures/` inventory\n\n21 library directories (panda excluded from validate comparisons but present on disk). Per-directory counts (files by extension, excluding barrels the validate script skips):\n\n| dir | .tsx | .d.ts | plain .ts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| antd | 2 | 0 | 0 |\n| ariakit | 4 | 0 | 1 |\n| ark-ui | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| base-ui | 2 | 0 | 0 |\n| blueprint | 2 | 0 | 1 |\n| chakra | 0 | 2 | 1 |\n| day-picker | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| fluentui | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| headlessui | 2 | 0 | 1 |\n| mantine | 0 | 2 | 1 |\n| mui | 0 | 2 | 0 |\n| panda | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| radix | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| rdt-compat | 9 | 0 | 0 |\n| react-aria | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| react-final-form | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| react-resizable-panels | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| shadcn | 3 | 0 | 0 |\n| storybook-emotion | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| tanstack-table | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| zendesk-garden | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| **Totals** | **33** | **8** | **14** |\n\n- **`.tsx` (33 files)**: real source, comparable to react-docgen, react-docgen-typescript, and this tool.\n- **`.d.ts` (8 files, in: mui, chakra, mantine, radix, react-aria)**: declaration-only — **not comparable to RDT or react-docgen** (both need real TS/TSX source); only this tool's own output is available for these.\n- **plain `.ts` (14 files)**: mostly non-JSX type/helper files (e.g. `props.ts`, `types.ts`) that RDT can parse (not filtered by `isDts`) but react-docgen cannot (filtered to `isTsx` only) — a **partial-comparability tier** worth calling out separately in any new report (RDT + ours comparable; react-docgen not).\n- `rdt-compat/` (9 `.tsx` files) is a purpose-built synthetic fixture set (types.tsx, memo.tsx, jsdoc.tsx, controlled.tsx, discriminated-union.tsx, pick-source.tsx, etc.) specifically engineered to exercise individual `PropType` kinds/patterns per `docs/rdt-coverage.md`, not a real library — good for correctness taxonomy, less meaningful for perf/\"real world\" framing.\n\n## 4. `docs/rdt-coverage.md` (431 lines, last substantive fixes dated 2026-07-07)\n\nCurrent, actively maintained \"spec coverage matrix,\" **not stale** — it cross-references its own fixes with fixture names and is consistent with what I found in the archive docs and current source (verified two specific historical gaps are indeed now fixed in code — see §5). Structure:\n- `PropType` kind coverage table (line 7-41) — flags `void`/`never`/`any` as intentionally uncovered (not realistic prop types).\n- `ComponentEntry` field coverage (45-62), `ParsedProp` field coverage (65-88), component-pattern coverage (91-130) — very granular ✅/❌/⚠️ tables with fixture cross-refs.\n- `HtmlAttributeMode` (curated/full/none) explanation (148+).\n- **\"Known gaps summary\" table (160-213)**: dozens of \"✅ done\" (already fixed, with root-cause narrative below each), 2 \"N/A\" (React 19 ref-as-prop, compound components — investigated and found not to be real gaps), and exactly **3 currently open ❌ not-fixed gaps**:\n  1. `styled.X.attrs<T>()` component detection (zendesk-garden) — \"shared blind spot with real RDT, not a competitive gap.\"\n  2. `ComponentProps<typeof StyledButton>` for `@emotion/styled`'s two-arg `styled(tag, options)<T>(fn)` overload (storybook-emotion) — needs a new library-specific pattern; \"real RDT needs a type checker for this too.\"\n  3. Same-namespace sibling reference resolution for some `@types/react` internals (`EventHandler`, `TrustedHTML`) — narrower residual, degrades gracefully.\n- It says explicitly: \"The 8 real-world library fixtures added since [the original snapshot set] ... aren't snapshot-tested — validate them via `compare:all`\" (line 3) — i.e. it self-identifies `apps/validate` as the authoritative validation path for the newer fixtures, reinforcing that any new benchmark report should build on/extend that harness rather than replace it.\n\n## 5. Prior adversarial-analysis / gap-fix docs\n\n- **`docs/archive/adversarial-analysis-2026-06-28.md`**: the original analysis. Cross-checked two of its findings against current code:\n  - **I1** (\"`find_discriminant_prop` only scans the first union member for candidates\") — **confirmed fixed**: current `resolver/chain.rs:260-300` builds `candidate_names` from the **intersection of all members'** prop names (lines 267-274), not `members[0]`.\n  - **I4** (\"`resolve_base_as_chain` silently returns empty for Union base type\") — **confirmed fixed**: `resolver/alias.rs:194` has an explicit `CollectedType::Union(members) =>` arm.\n  - C1 (inline union in forwardRef silently dropping the component) and I2/I3/M4 (other union-related resolver gaps) are also listed as \"✅ done\" in `rdt-coverage.md`'s summary table with fixture cross-refs (discriminated-union.tsx, day-picker), consistent with the code check above.\n- **`docs/archive/2026-06-28-structural-gap-fixes.md`**: ends with a **\"Deferred gaps (require Corsa/TypeScript 7.1 type checker)\"** table (lines 582-589) — generic parameter substitution, conditional types (already emitted as `opaque` — correct), mapped types (already `opaque` — correct), multi-level `typeof` depth, multi-file generic propagation. These remain open by design (see `docs/type-checker-integration.md`) and are still accurate today per `STATUS.md`'s \"Known gaps that won't get fixed without a type checker\" section.\n- **`docs/archive/2026-07-07-adversarial-analysis-fixes.md`**: an 8-team implementation plan (Tasks A–H: parser safety, JSDoc/perf, defaultProps, resolver/cva perf, CLI validation, watch-mode, output format, path normalization). Its **\"Explicitly deferred (not in this pass)\"** section (lines 15-22) lists 3 items still open per `STATUS.md`, which independently repeats them nearly verbatim:\n  1. Scoped-key string allocation caching in `resolver/chain.rs`/`named.rs`/`template.rs` (a `\"{file}:{name}\"` string built on every lookup) — \"real but unbenchmarked.\"\n  2. `cache.rs` DTS cache has no dirty-flag and no size cap — full rewrite every run, unbounded growth.\n  3. Migration guide/README/NAPI-publish/help-text — non-code items, irrelevant here.\n\n**Net \"shortcomings\" list still valid today** (for a new report's limitations section): the 3 open items from `rdt-coverage.md` §4 above, the 5 deferred type-checker-only gaps, and the 2 real-but-unaddressed perf items (scoped-key allocation, cache.rs no dirty-flag/no cap) — all independently corroborated by the current `docs/STATUS.md` (dated 2026-07-22, self-described as the up-to-date source of truth when other docs conflict).\n\n## 6. Criterion / other bench crates\n\n- **No `criterion` dependency anywhere** (`grep` across `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` for \"criterion\" — none). Only `divan = \"0.1\"` is a workspace dev-dependency (`Cargo.toml:72`).\n- `cargo bench` today **produces no report file** — divan prints directly to stdout; no `target/criterion/`, no HTML, no JSON artifact anywhere in `target/`.\n\n## 7. package.json / task scripts\n\n**`apps/validate/package.json`** scripts (lines 8-13):\n```\nrun:rdg   → tsx src/run-react-docgen.ts\nrun:rdt   → tsx src/run-react-docgen-typescript.ts\nrun:ours  → tsx src/run-ours.ts\ncompare   → tsx src/compare.ts\nbaseline  → tsx src/baseline.ts\ncompare:all → tsx src/baseline.ts && tsx src/compare.ts\n```\n**Root `package.json`** scripts (lines 12-15): only `build`/`test`/`lint`, all delegating to `moon run :build` etc. — **no root-level `bench` or `compare` script**; benchmarking is only reachable via `just bench` / `moon run core:bench` (cargo side) or `moon run validate:compare` / `pnpm --filter @oxc-react-docgen/validate compare:all` (TS comparison side). These two systems are entirely separate today — no unified \"benchmark report\" command exists that combines Rust perf numbers with cross-tool agreement numbers.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line: reuse vs. build-from-scratch\n\n**Reusable as-is:**\n- `apps/validate/src/fixtures.ts` discovery logic (minus the panda skip, which you may want to lift).\n- The three tool-runner scripts (`run-react-docgen.ts`, `run-react-docgen-typescript.ts`, `run-ours.ts`) and their `.tsx`/`.d.ts` filtering rules — these correctly encode which fixtures are comparable to which tool.\n- `baseline.ts`'s orchestration + the `maxBuffer` fix already applied.\n- The 21-library, 55-file fixture corpus and `docs/rdt-coverage.md`'s already-verified taxonomy of PropType-kind/pattern coverage (useful as a starting divergence taxonomy rather than inventing one from scratch).\n- `crates/core/benches/extraction.rs` as a starting point for Rust-side timing (divan, no criterion migration needed) — but it needs broadening (more fixtures, real per-file not per-dir granularity, cold vs. incremental cache variants) and a report-artifact step (none exists today).\n\n**Needs building from scratch:**\n- Any actual **agreement-rate percentage** with a rigorous per-prop match definition (today's \"Coverage %\" in `compare.ts` is a raw count ratio, not per-prop precision/recall).\n- **Divergence taxonomy/bucketing** — today it's flat lists (`oursOnly`/`rdtOnly`/`rdgOnly`/`typeDiffs`) plus one ad hoc HTML-inheritance carve-out; no classification by divergence *kind* (opaque-vs-resolved, literal-widening, union-ordering, JSDoc-only, etc.) exists anywhere in code.\n- Real activation of the **react-docgen comparison axis** — `rdg` data is loaded but essentially unused in `compare.ts`'s output/summary today.\n- A **cold vs. incremental perf split** for the Rust benches (nothing distinguishes cache-cold from cache-warm runs currently).\n- Any **saved/reproducible report artifact** (HTML/JSON/markdown) for either the Rust bench numbers or the comparison numbers — both currently only print to stdout with no persistence or diffable history.\n- A unified script/command that ties Rust perf + prop-agreement into one \"benchmark report,\" since today these are two disconnected systems (`cargo bench` vs. `pnpm compare:all`)."}]}]},"uuid":"f0141971-c236-4365-a7ce-916f81096e2e","timestamp":"2026-07-25T18:51:09.489Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen, I need a thorough inventory of existing benchmarking and RDT/react-docgen comparison infrastructure before I build a new \"proper benchmarks\" report. Investigate and report back (this is research only — do not write or edit any files):\n\n1. `crates/core/benches/extraction.rs` (or wherever Rust benchmarks live) — what does it measure, what harness (criterion?), does it produce reproducible numbers, what fixtures does it use?\n2. `apps/validate/` — read all of `src/*.ts` (baseline.ts, compare.ts, fixtures.ts, run-react-docgen-typescript.ts, run-react-docgen.ts, types.ts, and any others). I need to understand exactly:\n   - How it invokes react-docgen-typescript and react-docgen for comparison\n   - What comparison logic already exists (prop-by-prop diff?) and what its output looks like today (is it a summary count, a full diff, JSON?)\n   - What the `compare:all` moon/pnpm task actually runs and what fixtures it covers\n   - Whether it already computes anything like an \"agreement rate\" percentage, and whether mismatches are categorized/bucketed by type of divergence or just listed\n   - Any known limitations already documented in code comments (e.g. \"RDT requires real TS source, can't compare DTS fixtures\")\n3. `fixtures/` directory — list what libraries/components are available (shadcn, mui, chakra, mantine, radix, react-aria, panda, rdt-compat or similar) and roughly how many components/fixtures total, and which are `.tsx` (real source, comparable to both RDT and react-docgen) vs `.d.ts` (declaration-only, only comparable to this tool's own output, not RDT/react-docgen since those need real source)\n4. `docs/rdt-coverage.md` if it exists — what does it currently claim about RDT compatibility/coverage, is it up to date or stale\n5. Any prior adversarial-analysis or gap-fix docs (docs/archive/adversarial-analysis-2026-06-28.md, docs/archive/2026-06-28-structural-gap-fixes.md, docs/archive/2026-07-07-adversarial-analysis-fixes.md) — list every KNOWN unresolved gap/limitation mentioned there that would still need to go in a \"shortcomings\" section today (cross-reference against current code if you can quickly grep for whether each was actually fixed already, e.g. search for \"Union\" handling in resolve_base_as_chain, or discriminant detection using all union members not just members[0])\n6. Whether `criterion` or any other Rust benchmark crate is set up as a dev-dependency, and whether `cargo bench` currently produces a report file/HTML anywhere\n7. package.json scripts in apps/validate and the root — what commands exist today for running comparisons or benchmarks\n\nReport back with concrete file:line references and a clear structured summary. I want to know exactly what I can reuse vs. what I need to build from scratch for a \"cold + incremental perf\" and \"prop-agreement-rate + divergence taxonomy\" benchmark report comparing oxc-react-docgen against react-docgen-typescript and react-docgen.","agentId":"a08bfa0a9a03243c3","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a complete picture. Here is the full inventory.\n\n# Inventory: benchmarking + RDT/react-docgen comparison infrastructure\n\n## 1. Rust benchmarks — `crates/core/benches/extraction.rs` (18 lines total)\n\n- **Harness:** `divan` (0.1, workspace dep, `Cargo.toml:72`), **not criterion**. `crates/core/Cargo.toml` declares:\n  ```\n  [dev-dependencies]\n  divan.workspace = true\n  ...\n  [[bench]]\n  name = \"extraction\"\n  harness = false\n  ```\n- **What it measures:** a single parametrized bench, `extract_file_bench`, over 4 hardcoded fixture args (`extraction.rs:5`): `shadcn/button.tsx`, `shadcn/input.tsx`, `radix/button.d.ts`, `mui/Button.d.ts`. For each, it resolves the fixture's **parent directory** (not the single file) and calls `oxc_react_docgen_core::pipeline::extract()` with `src_dirs` pointed at that whole directory (`extraction.rs:7-17`) — so it's really benchmarking \"extract this entire fixture library dir,\" named misleadingly after one file in it.\n- **Reproducibility:** divan reports mean/median/stdev over N iterations to stdout only; there is **no saved report/HTML/JSON output** anywhere (checked `target/`, no `criterion/` or `divan/` report dirs exist). `[profile.bench]` inherits `release` with `debug = true` (`Cargo.toml:82-84`) — good for consistent optimized numbers, but nothing pins CPU affinity, disables turbo/frequency scaling, or archives historical runs for trend comparison.\n- **Invocation:** `just bench` → `cargo bench --workspace --exclude oxc-react-docgen-napi` (`justfile:32-34`); moon mirrors this at `.moon/tasks.yml:46-48`. No `cargo bench` output file is captured by either.\n- **Cold vs incremental:** nothing here distinguishes cold-cache vs warm/incremental extraction (e.g. DTS parse cache in `cache.rs` — see STATUS.md gap below); every divan iteration presumably reuses whatever process-level caches exist, so it's implicitly measuring warm/incremental performance only, with no cold-start variant.\n\n**Verdict:** minimal micro-bench scaffold exists and works, but it's narrow (4 fixtures, whole-directory not single-file granularity), produces no artifact/report, and has no cold-vs-incremental split — largely build-from-scratch territory for a \"perf report.\"\n\n## 2. `apps/validate/` — full read of `src/*.ts`\n\nFiles: `fixtures.ts`, `baseline.ts`, `run-react-docgen.ts`, `run-react-docgen-typescript.ts`, `run-ours.ts`, `compare.ts`, `types.ts`. No others.\n\n### fixtures.ts (`apps/validate/src/fixtures.ts`)\n`discoverFixtures()` walks `fixtures/` (one level), returns `{name, path, ext, isTsx, isDts}`. Explicitly **skips the `panda` directory entirely** (line 19: `\"skip styled-system subdir complexity for now\"`) and skips `index.ts` barrel files (line 22). This means panda isn't in any of the three tool runners' comparisons at all despite being a real fixture dir.\n\n### How each tool is invoked\n- **react-docgen** (`run-react-docgen.ts:30`): `discoverFixtures().filter(f => f.isTsx)` — **only `.tsx` files**, calls `parse(source, {filename})` from the `react-docgen` npm package directly, normalizes into `NormalizedOutput`.\n- **react-docgen-typescript** (`run-react-docgen-typescript.ts:22`): `discoverFixtures().filter(f => !f.isDts)` — comment explicitly states \"RDT needs real TS files\" — excludes `.d.ts`. Uses `withCustomConfig(tsconfigPath, {...})` from the root `tsconfig.json`, falls back to a simpler config if that throws (lines 9-19). Keys results per-component as `${fixture.name}/${comp.displayName}`.\n- **oxc-react-docgen** (`run-ours.ts`): shells out to the **built CLI binary** (`target/release/oxc-react-docgen`, falling back to `target/debug`, lines 9-11) via `execSync`, one invocation **per whole library directory** (`--src ${libPath} --format canonical`), not per file — so timing here is library-granularity, inconsistent with the per-file granularity of the other two runners. Has its own `propTypeToString()` reimplementation of type-to-string logic (lines 21-109) duplicating logic that also exists in the Rust `PropType::raw_string()`.\n\n### Comparison logic (`compare.ts`, 160 lines)\n- Loads three JSON baseline files by tool name into `Map<fixture, ToolResult>` (lines 4-13).\n- Output today is a **printed console log**, not JSON/structured — for each fixture × component: timing line, prop-count line, prop-name-set diffs (`oursOnly`, `rdtOnly`, `rdgOnly`), a special carve-out that treats `rdt-only` props as \"covered by inheritance\" if they appear in `notableInheritedNames` (our own tool's metadata) vs `trueMisses` (lines 84-104), a small **type-diff** section limited to props common to both sides where the raw type string differs (lines 107-124), and a heuristic **win/tie/miss** classification per component (lines 126-147).\n- End-of-run **summary** (lines 153-159): total prop counts, `Coverage: oursTotal/rdtTotal (%)`, and `Identical matches / Both empty / Misses` counts. **This is the only \"agreement rate\"-like number that exists today** — it's a raw prop-count ratio, not a true per-prop match rate, and it's global across all fixtures, not broken out per-library or per-divergence-type.\n- **No categorization/taxonomy of mismatch types** beyond the single ad hoc \"HTML-attribute-inheritance-covered vs real miss\" bucket — type diffs, missing props, extra props are otherwise just flat lists, not bucketed by *kind* of divergence (e.g. union-ordering, opaque-vs-resolved, literal-vs-widened, JSDoc-only).\n- **react-docgen (rdg) results are loaded but barely used** in the console output — `ComparisonResult.rdgOnly`/`rdgOnlyComponents` are declared in `types.ts` but **`compare.ts` never actually populates or prints them** (only `r_rdg?.error` and its timing are surfaced at lines 40, 46). The whole rdg comparison axis is effectively vestigial today.\n- `ComparisonResult` type (`types.ts:30-42`) is more complete than what `compare.ts` actually computes/prints (e.g. `rdtOnlyComponents`/`rdgOnlyComponents` aren't computed anywhere in `compare.ts`).\n\n### `compare:all` task\n`apps/validate/package.json:13`: `\"compare:all\": \"tsx src/baseline.ts && tsx src/compare.ts\"`. `baseline.ts` runs all three tool scripts via `spawnSync('tsx', [script], {maxBuffer: 100MB, ...})` (note the comment at lines 11-15 about a real prior bug where Node's default 1MB `maxBuffer` silently truncated stdout mid-JSON) and writes each to `apps/validate/baselines/*.json`. moon's `compare` task (`apps/validate/moon.yml`) depends on `cli:build` first and disables caching. Covers **all fixtures found by `discoverFixtures()` except `panda`**, split per-tool by the `.tsx`/`.d.ts` filters above.\n\n### Documented limitations found in code comments\n- `fixtures.ts:19` — panda skipped (\"styled-system subdir complexity for now\").\n- `run-react-docgen-typescript.ts:22` and `baseline.ts` — RDT can't run on `.d.ts`-only fixtures (needs real TS source).\n- `run-react-docgen.ts:30` — react-docgen only runs on `.tsx` (not plain `.ts`, not `.d.ts`).\n- `baseline.ts:11-15` — maxBuffer gotcha already fixed once (documented so it isn't re-introduced).\n\n## 3. `fixtures/` inventory\n\n21 library directories (panda excluded from validate comparisons but present on disk). Per-directory counts (files by extension, excluding barrels the validate script skips):\n\n| dir | .tsx | .d.ts | plain .ts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| antd | 2 | 0 | 0 |\n| ariakit | 4 | 0 | 1 |\n| ark-ui | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| base-ui | 2 | 0 | 0 |\n| blueprint | 2 | 0 | 1 |\n| chakra | 0 | 2 | 1 |\n| day-picker | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| fluentui | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| headlessui | 2 | 0 | 1 |\n| mantine | 0 | 2 | 1 |\n| mui | 0 | 2 | 0 |\n| panda | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| radix | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| rdt-compat | 9 | 0 | 0 |\n| react-aria | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| react-final-form | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| react-resizable-panels | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| shadcn | 3 | 0 | 0 |\n| storybook-emotion | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| tanstack-table | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n| zendesk-garden | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| **Totals** | **33** | **8** | **14** |\n\n- **`.tsx` (33 files)**: real source, comparable to react-docgen, react-docgen-typescript, and this tool.\n- **`.d.ts` (8 files, in: mui, chakra, mantine, radix, react-aria)**: declaration-only — **not comparable to RDT or react-docgen** (both need real TS/TSX source); only this tool's own output is available for these.\n- **plain `.ts` (14 files)**: mostly non-JSX type/helper files (e.g. `props.ts`, `types.ts`) that RDT can parse (not filtered by `isDts`) but react-docgen cannot (filtered to `isTsx` only) — a **partial-comparability tier** worth calling out separately in any new report (RDT + ours comparable; react-docgen not).\n- `rdt-compat/` (9 `.tsx` files) is a purpose-built synthetic fixture set (types.tsx, memo.tsx, jsdoc.tsx, controlled.tsx, discriminated-union.tsx, pick-source.tsx, etc.) specifically engineered to exercise individual `PropType` kinds/patterns per `docs/rdt-coverage.md`, not a real library — good for correctness taxonomy, less meaningful for perf/\"real world\" framing.\n\n## 4. `docs/rdt-coverage.md` (431 lines, last substantive fixes dated 2026-07-07)\n\nCurrent, actively maintained \"spec coverage matrix,\" **not stale** — it cross-references its own fixes with fixture names and is consistent with what I found in the archive docs and current source (verified two specific historical gaps are indeed now fixed in code — see §5). Structure:\n- `PropType` kind coverage table (line 7-41) — flags `void`/`never`/`any` as intentionally uncovered (not realistic prop types).\n- `ComponentEntry` field coverage (45-62), `ParsedProp` field coverage (65-88), component-pattern coverage (91-130) — very granular ✅/❌/⚠️ tables with fixture cross-refs.\n- `HtmlAttributeMode` (curated/full/none) explanation (148+).\n- **\"Known gaps summary\" table (160-213)**: dozens of \"✅ done\" (already fixed, with root-cause narrative below each), 2 \"N/A\" (React 19 ref-as-prop, compound components — investigated and found not to be real gaps), and exactly **3 currently open ❌ not-fixed gaps**:\n  1. `styled.X.attrs<T>()` component detection (zendesk-garden) — \"shared blind spot with real RDT, not a competitive gap.\"\n  2. `ComponentProps<typeof StyledButton>` for `@emotion/styled`'s two-arg `styled(tag, options)<T>(fn)` overload (storybook-emotion) — needs a new library-specific pattern; \"real RDT needs a type checker for this too.\"\n  3. Same-namespace sibling reference resolution for some `@types/react` internals (`EventHandler`, `TrustedHTML`) — narrower residual, degrades gracefully.\n- It says explicitly: \"The 8 real-world library fixtures added since [the original snapshot set] ... aren't snapshot-tested — validate them via `compare:all`\" (line 3) — i.e. it self-identifies `apps/validate` as the authoritative validation path for the newer fixtures, reinforcing that any new benchmark report should build on/extend that harness rather than replace it.\n\n## 5. Prior adversarial-analysis / gap-fix docs\n\n- **`docs/archive/adversarial-analysis-2026-06-28.md`**: the original analysis. Cross-checked two of its findings against current code:\n  - **I1** (\"`find_discriminant_prop` only scans the first union member for candidates\") — **confirmed fixed**: current `resolver/chain.rs:260-300` builds `candidate_names` from the **intersection of all members'** prop names (lines 267-274), not `members[0]`.\n  - **I4** (\"`resolve_base_as_chain` silently returns empty for Union base type\") — **confirmed fixed**: `resolver/alias.rs:194` has an explicit `CollectedType::Union(members) =>` arm.\n  - C1 (inline union in forwardRef silently dropping the component) and I2/I3/M4 (other union-related resolver gaps) are also listed as \"✅ done\" in `rdt-coverage.md`'s summary table with fixture cross-refs (discriminated-union.tsx, day-picker), consistent with the code check above.\n- **`docs/archive/2026-06-28-structural-gap-fixes.md`**: ends with a **\"Deferred gaps (require Corsa/TypeScript 7.1 type checker)\"** table (lines 582-589) — generic parameter substitution, conditional types (already emitted as `opaque` — correct), mapped types (already `opaque` — correct), multi-level `typeof` depth, multi-file generic propagation. These remain open by design (see `docs/type-checker-integration.md`) and are still accurate today per `STATUS.md`'s \"Known gaps that won't get fixed without a type checker\" section.\n- **`docs/archive/2026-07-07-adversarial-analysis-fixes.md`**: an 8-team implementation plan (Tasks A–H: parser safety, JSDoc/perf, defaultProps, resolver/cva perf, CLI validation, watch-mode, output format, path normalization). Its **\"Explicitly deferred (not in this pass)\"** section (lines 15-22) lists 3 items still open per `STATUS.md`, which independently repeats them nearly verbatim:\n  1. Scoped-key string allocation caching in `resolver/chain.rs`/`named.rs`/`template.rs` (a `\"{file}:{name}\"` string built on every lookup) — \"real but unbenchmarked.\"\n  2. `cache.rs` DTS cache has no dirty-flag and no size cap — full rewrite every run, unbounded growth.\n  3. Migration guide/README/NAPI-publish/help-text — non-code items, irrelevant here.\n\n**Net \"shortcomings\" list still valid today** (for a new report's limitations section): the 3 open items from `rdt-coverage.md` §4 above, the 5 deferred type-checker-only gaps, and the 2 real-but-unaddressed perf items (scoped-key allocation, cache.rs no dirty-flag/no cap) — all independently corroborated by the current `docs/STATUS.md` (dated 2026-07-22, self-described as the up-to-date source of truth when other docs conflict).\n\n## 6. Criterion / other bench crates\n\n- **No `criterion` dependency anywhere** (`grep` across `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` for \"criterion\" — none). Only `divan = \"0.1\"` is a workspace dev-dependency (`Cargo.toml:72`).\n- `cargo bench` today **produces no report file** — divan prints directly to stdout; no `target/criterion/`, no HTML, no JSON artifact anywhere in `target/`.\n\n## 7. package.json / task scripts\n\n**`apps/validate/package.json`** scripts (lines 8-13):\n```\nrun:rdg   → tsx src/run-react-docgen.ts\nrun:rdt   → tsx src/run-react-docgen-typescript.ts\nrun:ours  → tsx src/run-ours.ts\ncompare   → tsx src/compare.ts\nbaseline  → tsx src/baseline.ts\ncompare:all → tsx src/baseline.ts && tsx src/compare.ts\n```\n**Root `package.json`** scripts (lines 12-15): only `build`/`test`/`lint`, all delegating to `moon run :build` etc. — **no root-level `bench` or `compare` script**; benchmarking is only reachable via `just bench` / `moon run core:bench` (cargo side) or `moon run validate:compare` / `pnpm --filter @oxc-react-docgen/validate compare:all` (TS comparison side). These two systems are entirely separate today — no unified \"benchmark report\" command exists that combines Rust perf numbers with cross-tool agreement numbers.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line: reuse vs. build-from-scratch\n\n**Reusable as-is:**\n- `apps/validate/src/fixtures.ts` discovery logic (minus the panda skip, which you may want to lift).\n- The three tool-runner scripts (`run-react-docgen.ts`, `run-react-docgen-typescript.ts`, `run-ours.ts`) and their `.tsx`/`.d.ts` filtering rules — these correctly encode which fixtures are comparable to which tool.\n- `baseline.ts`'s orchestration + the `maxBuffer` fix already applied.\n- The 21-library, 55-file fixture corpus and `docs/rdt-coverage.md`'s already-verified taxonomy of PropType-kind/pattern coverage (useful as a starting divergence taxonomy rather than inventing one from scratch).\n- `crates/core/benches/extraction.rs` as a starting point for Rust-side timing (divan, no criterion migration needed) — but it needs broadening (more fixtures, real per-file not per-dir granularity, cold vs. incremental cache variants) and a report-artifact step (none exists today).\n\n**Needs building from scratch:**\n- Any actual **agreement-rate percentage** with a rigorous per-prop match definition (today's \"Coverage %\" in `compare.ts` is a raw count ratio, not per-prop precision/recall).\n- **Divergence taxonomy/bucketing** — today it's flat lists (`oursOnly`/`rdtOnly`/`rdgOnly`/`typeDiffs`) plus one ad hoc HTML-inheritance carve-out; no classification by divergence *kind* (opaque-vs-resolved, literal-widening, union-ordering, JSDoc-only, etc.) exists anywhere in code.\n- Real activation of the **react-docgen comparison axis** — `rdg` data is loaded but essentially unused in `compare.ts`'s output/summary today.\n- A **cold vs. incremental perf split** for the Rust benches (nothing distinguishes cache-cold from cache-warm runs currently).\n- Any **saved/reproducible report artifact** (HTML/JSON/markdown) for either the Rust bench numbers or the comparison numbers — both currently only print to stdout with no persistence or diffable history.\n- A unified script/command that ties Rust perf + prop-agreement into one \"benchmark report,\" since today these are two disconnected systems (`cargo bench` vs. `pnpm compare:all`)."}],"resolvedModel":"claude-sonnet-5","totalDurationMs":163050,"totalTokens":64742,"totalToolUseCount":32,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1914,"cache_read_input_tokens":55814,"output_tokens":7012,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1914},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7012,"cache_read_input_tokens":55814,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1914,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_to