{"parentUuid":"a2898e87-bdcb-4f0f-99ed-d12f9f08426a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdPMzS2aKTdvJgbX9ANmN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2251,"cache_read_input_tokens":504961,"output_tokens":926,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2251,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":926,"cache_read_input_tokens":504961,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2251,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2251},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdPMzMK4KE1aKVvrrCF3z","type":"assistant","uuid":"854e5453-94c8-4a32-8088-ac16b9d34c8e","timestamp":"2026-07-25T19:54:57.488Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"854e5453-94c8-4a32-8088-ac16b9d34c8e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdPMzS2aKTdvJgbX9ANmN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No — it's the opposite for the low-agreement cases. Quick clarification with the real breakdown:\n\n- **Against RDT** (16.8% agreement): the 10 low-scoring components are ones where we find *fewer* props than RDT (e.g. `fluentui/Button`: we find 8, RDT finds 303) — RDT has a real type checker and can resolve generic/conditional-type patterns we can't yet. That's a real gap, not us over-identifying.\n- **Against react-docgen** (22.7% agreement, \"extra in ours: 106\"): here we do find *more* than the comparator — but that's not us being noisy or hallucinating. It's react-docgen returning **zero props for 9 of 20 components** (its own well-known weak TS support), so nearly everything we correctly extract shows up as \"extra\" relative to its near-empty output. That's react-docgen's incompleteness, not ours.\n\nSo the \"low agreement rate\" as a single number conflates two very different situations: sometimes it means *we're missing things RDT catches* (the real, actionable gap — 10 components needing a type checker), sometimes it means *the comparator is missing things we catch* (react-docgen's weakness, not ours). The aggregate percentage can't tell those apart on its own — that's exactly why the report breaks it into the outlier table and the \"zero props\" stat instead of leaning on the single headline number."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2251,"cache_read_input_tokens":504961,"output_tokens":926,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2251,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":926,"cache_read_input_tokens":504961,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2251,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2251},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdPMzMK4KE1aKVvrrCF3z","type":"assistant","uuid":"3dc243a9-f131-4acf-bec8-63a822c63d28","timestamp":"2026-07-25T19:55:02.938Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"3dc243a9-f131-4acf-bec8-63a822c63d28","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdPMzS2aKTdvJgbX9ANmN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U1Uu6xtYm7hvqESzBssZ4R","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen\ncat -n docs/benchmarks.md | sed -n '145,185p'"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2251,"cache_read_input_tokens":504961,"output_tokens":926,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2251,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":926,"cache_read_input_tokens":504961,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2251,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2251},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdPMzMK4KE1aKVvrrCF3z","type":"assistant","uuid":"2e84f963-2e29-4940-940f-9a9b52492974","timestamp":"2026-07-25T19:55:03.568Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"2e84f963-2e29-4940-940f-9a9b52492974","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"808a5445-bf63-43b5-80bb-2c806292ef39","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U1Uu6xtYm7hvqESzBssZ4R","type":"tool_result","content":" 145 \n 146 `optional-undefined-representation` (21 of 61) is `react-docgen`'s own quirk, not a gap here — it frequently appends `| undefined` to optional props' printed type where this tool (and RDT, with `shouldRemoveUndefinedFromOptional`) doesn't.\n 147 \n 148 ---\n 149 \n 150 ## What this tool can provide that they can't\n 151 \n 152 Direct answers to \"what information do we have that RDT and react-docgen don't,\" each backed by a number above or a concrete check run against this corpus, not a claim:\n 153 \n 154 1. **Structured, typed degradation diagnostics.** Running this tool across all 21 fixture libraries produces **82 diagnostics** with typed codes (`UNRESOLVABLE_IMPORT`: 30, `OPAQUE_TYPE`: 29, `INDEXED_ACCESS_OPAQUE`: 19, `DISCRIMINATED_UNION`: 2, `TEMPLATE_LITERAL_OPAQUE`: 2), file/line context, and human-readable help text (e.g. _\"Enable typescript-go to resolve indexed access types.\"_). Neither RDT nor react-docgen tell you _why_ a prop came out wrong or missing — they silently omit it or print a generic parse error. This tool's non-negotiable #6 (\"always emit a Diagnostic when degrading, never fail silently,\" `CLAUDE.md`) makes every gap in the tables above something a consumer's tooling can actually detect and act on, not something they discover by manually diffing output.\n 155 2. **`.d.ts`-only fixtures.** 8 of the 55 fixture files in this corpus (`mui`, `chakra`, `mantine`, `radix`, `react-aria`) are declaration-only — real published library type definitions with no accompanying `.tsx` source. **Neither RDT nor react-docgen can run on these at all** (both need a real component implementation to parse); this tool extracts full prop tables from them today, which is the entire reason it can validate against `node_modules`-vendored third-party types instead of only first-party source.\n 156 3. **Incremental extraction with a real API** (see the perf section above) — `WatchSession::update_file` for editor/HMR integration. RDT and react-docgen have nothing playing this role; the comparison isn't \"we're faster at the same operation,\" it's \"this operation doesn't exist for them.\"\n 157 4. **Cross-package monorepo import resolution** (`ImportResolutionMap`, barrel/re-export chain following, `extra_paths` workspace aliasing) — RDT resolves within a single `ts.Program`'s file set; getting it to follow a monorepo's package boundaries requires configuring that whole program correctly. This tool's import resolution is a first-class, independently-tested layer (`import_map.rs`, `resolver/import.rs`).\n 158 5. **Machine-consumable structured types**, not stringified type names. This tool's `PropType` is a real tagged union (`union`, `literalUnion`, `array`, `intersection`, `eventHandler`, ...) serialized as JSON — a consumer can programmatically ask \"is this a union of string literals\" without parsing a type string. RDT and react-docgen both hand back a printed type string (or in react-docgen's case, a partial tagged shape with a `.raw` escape hatch) as the primary representation.\n 159 \n 160 ---\n 161 \n 162 ## Shortcomings and remediation plan\n 163 \n 164 Honest accounting of what's actually wrong today, ordered by how directly actionable each one is — not by how bad it sounds.\n 165 \n 166 ### Fixed during this report\n 167 \n 168 Two real, independently-verified bugs — one in the resolver, one in the pipeline itself — surfaced while root-causing the outliers above. Both are fixed, tested, and the numbers throughout this report already reflect the fix.\n 169 \n 170 - **Generic `SVGAttributes<T>`/`HTMLProps<T>` extends weren't element-mapped.** `fixtures/rdt-compat/svg-icon.tsx`'s `IconProps extends React.SVGAttributes<SVGSVGElement>` resolved to only 2 own props even with `--html-attributes full`, because `resolver/named.rs`'s step-6 silent-no-op list treated `SVGAttributes`/`HTMLProps` as unconditionally opaque — unlike concrete `<Element>HTMLAttributes` types (`ButtonHTMLAttributes`, etc.), which already map to a real element and expand structurally. `react_types::html_element_from_type_arg` now derives the element from the type argument for these three generic forms specifically. `Icon` went from 2 props to 487 (RDT: 488); `Box` from 2 to 381 (RDT: 382).\n 171 - **Components silently collided across files sharing a basename.** `fluentui/Button` looked like an unexplained resolver gap — until a single-directory extraction of `fixtures/fluentui` alone found it fine, but it vanished the moment the whole `fixtures/` corpus was extracted in one call (the only realistic way this tool is ever actually invoked). Root cause: the pipeline's duplicate-display-name disambiguation used `\"{name} ({file_stem})\"`, and file stem isn't unique across directories — 5 of this repo's own fixture libraries (`chakra`, `fluentui`, `mantine`, `mui`, `panda`) each ship a component literally named `Button` in a file literally named `Button.tsx`/`Button.d.ts`/`button.tsx`. All 5 landed on the identical fallback key, silently overwriting one another with **zero diagnostic** — a real violation of this project's own \"never fail silently\" rule (`CLAUDE.md` non-negotiable #6), and the reason `fluentui/Button` was missing from every table in the first version of this report rather than merely inaccurate. Fixed by disambiguating with the full file path (always unique) instead of the bare stem. `extract --src fixtures` (all 21 libraries, one call) now finds 50 components instead of 45.\n 172 \n 173 ### Already tracked, deferred pending a real type checker (Corsa/`typescript-go`)\n 174 \n 175 Confirmed still accurate against current code during this report — see `docs/type-checker-integration.md` and `docs/STATUS.md`:\n 176 \n 177 - **Generic type-parameter substitution through multi-level type-alias chains.** Root-caused here for the `ariakit`/`ark-ui` outliers above: Ariakit's own `Props<T, O> = O & Omit<ComponentPropsWithoutRef<T>, keyof O>` pattern requires substituting a generic param (`T = \"button\"`) through a nested `Omit`+`ComponentPropsWithoutRef` chain — exactly the \"generic parameter substitution\" item already listed as Corsa-deferred. This report adds concrete evidence (4+ real-world outlier components in one corpus) that this isn't a theoretical gap.\n 178 - **`@emotion/styled`'s two-arg `styled(tag, options)<T>(fn)` overload** (`storybook-emotion/Button` — already `docs/rdt-coverage.md`'s open gap #2). RDT needs a type checker for this too — not a competitive gap.\n 179 - **`styled.X.attrs<T>()` component detection** (`zendesk-garden` — already gap #1). Shared blind spot with RDT.\n 180 - **Same-namespace sibling reference resolution** for select `@types/react` internals (`EventHandler`, `TrustedHTML` — already gap #3; visible in the 29 `UNRESOLVABLE_IMPORT` diagnostics above).\n 181 \n 182 ### Already tracked, real but not yet benchmarked (perf, not correctness)\n 183 \n 184 From `docs/STATUS.md`, still accurate:\n 185","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3cc3529b-55c7-4118-964b-f908888e9a8e","timestamp":"2026-07-25T19:55:03.699Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":" 145 \n 146 `optional-undefined-representation` (21 of 61) is `react-docgen`'s own quirk, not a gap here — it frequently appends `| undefined` to optional props' printed type where this tool (and RDT, with `shouldRemoveUndefinedFromOptional`) doesn't.\n 147 \n 148 ---\n 149 \n 150 ## What this tool can provide that they can't\n 151 \n 152 Direct answers to \"what information do we have that RDT and react-docgen don't,\" each backed by a number above or a concrete check run against this corpus, not a claim:\n 153 \n 154 1. **Structured, typed degradation diagnostics.** Running this tool across all 21 fixture libraries produces **82 diagnostics** with typed codes (`UNRESOLVABLE_IMPORT`: 30, `OPAQUE_TYPE`: 29, `INDEXED_ACCESS_OPAQUE`: 19, `DISCRIMINATED_UNION`: 2, `TEMPLATE_LITERAL_OPAQUE`: 2), file/line context, and human-readable help text (e.g. _\"Enable typescript-go to resolve indexed access types.\"_). Neither RDT nor react-docgen tell you _why_ a prop came out wrong or missing — they silently omit it or print a generic parse error. This tool's non-negotiable #6 (\"always emit a Diagnostic when degrading, never fail silently,\" `CLAUDE.md`) makes every gap in the tables above something a consumer's tooling can actually detect and act on, not something they discover by manually diffing output.\n 155 2. **`.d.ts`-only fixtures.** 8 of the 55 fixture files in this corpus (`mui`, `chakra`, `mantine`, `radix`, `react-aria`) are declaration-only — real published library type definitions with no accompanying `.tsx` source. **Neither RDT nor react-docgen can run on these at all** (both need a real component implementation to parse); this tool extracts full prop tables from them today, which is the entire reason it can validate against `node_modules`-vendored third-party types instead of only first-party source.\n 156 3. **Incremental extraction with a real API** (see the perf section above) — `WatchSession::update_file` for editor/HMR integration. RDT and react-docgen have nothing playing this role; the comparison isn't \"we're faster at the same operation,\" it's \"this operation doesn't exist for them.\"\n 157 4. **Cross-package monorepo import resolution** (`ImportResolutionMap`, barrel/re-export chain following, `extra_paths` workspace aliasing) — RDT resolves within a single `ts.Program`'s file set; getting it to follow a monorepo's package boundaries requires configuring that whole program correctly. This tool's import resolution is a first-class, independently-tested layer (`import_map.rs`, `resolver/import.rs`).\n 158 5. **Machine-consumable structured types**, not stringified type names. This tool's `PropType` is a real tagged union (`union`, `literalUnion`, `array`, `intersection`, `eventHandler`, ...) serialized as JSON — a consumer can programmatically ask \"is this a union of string literals\" without parsing a type string. RDT and react-docgen both hand back a printed type string (or in react-docgen's case, a partial tagged shape with a `.raw` escape hatch) as the primary representation.\n 159 \n 160 ---\n 161 \n 162 ## Shortcomings and remediation plan\n 163 \n 164 Honest accounting of what's actually wrong today, ordered by how directly actionable each one is — not by how bad it sounds.\n 165 \n 166 ### Fixed during this report\n 167 \n 168 Two real, independently-verified bugs — one in the resolver, one in the pipeline itself — surfaced while root-causing the outliers above. Both are fixed, tested, and the numbers throughout this report already reflect the fix.\n 169 \n 170 - **Generic `SVGAttributes<T>`/`HTMLProps<T>` extends weren't element-mapped.** `fixtures/rdt-compat/svg-icon.tsx`'s `IconProps extends React.SVGAttributes<SVGSVGElement>` resolved to only 2 own props even with `--html-attributes full`, because `resolver/named.rs`'s step-6 silent-no-op list treated `SVGAttributes`/`HTMLProps` as unconditionally opaque — unlike concrete `<Element>HTMLAttributes` types (`ButtonHTMLAttributes`, etc.), which already map to a real element and expand structurally. `react_types::html_element_from_type_arg` now derives the element from the type argument for these three generic forms specifically. `Icon` went from 2 props to 487 (RDT: 488); `Box` from 2 to 381 (RDT: 382).\n 171 - **Components silently collided across files sharing a basename.** `fluentui/Button` looked like an unexplained resolver gap — until a single-directory extraction of `fixtures/fluentui` alone found it fine, but it vanished the moment the whole `fixtures/` corpus was extracted in one call (the only realistic way this tool is ever actually invoked). Root cause: the pipeline's duplicate-display-name disambiguation used `\"{name} ({file_stem})\"`, and file stem isn't unique across directories — 5 of this repo's own fixture libraries (`chakra`, `fluentui`, `mantine`, `mui`, `panda`) each ship a component literally named `Button` in a file literally named `Button.tsx`/`Button.d.ts`/`button.tsx`. All 5 landed on the identical fallback key, silently overwriting one another with **zero diagnostic** — a real violation of this project's own \"never fail silently\" rule (`CLAUDE.md` non-negotiable #6), and the reason `fluentui/Button` was missing from every table in the first version of this report rather than merely inaccurate. Fixed by disambiguating with the full file path (always unique) instead of the bare stem. `extract --src fixtures` (all 21 libraries, one call) now finds 50 components instead of 45.\n 172 \n 173 ### Already tracked, deferred pending a real type checker (Corsa/`typescript-go`)\n 174 \n 175 Confirmed still accurate against current code during this report — see `docs/type-checker-integration.md` and `docs/STATUS.md`:\n 176 \n 177 - **Generic type-parameter substitution through multi-level type-alias chains.** Root-caused here for the `ariakit`/`ark-ui` outliers above: Ariakit's own `Props<T, O> = O & Omit<ComponentPropsWithoutRef<T>, keyof O>` pattern requires substituting a generic param (`T = \"button\"`) through a nested `Omit`+`ComponentPropsWithoutRef` chain — exactly the \"generic parameter substitution\" item already listed as Corsa-deferred. This report adds concrete evidence (4+ real-world outlier components in one corpus) that this isn't a theoretical gap.\n 178 - **`@emotion/styled`'s two-arg `styled(tag, options)<T>(fn)` overload** (`storybook-emotion/Button` — already `docs/rdt-coverage.md`'s open gap #2). RDT needs a type checker for this too — not a competitive gap.\n 179 - **`styled.X.attrs<T>()` component detection** (`zendesk-garden` — already gap #1). Shared blind spot with RDT.\n 180 - **Same-namespace sibling reference resolution** for select `@types/react` internals (`EventHandler`, `TrustedHTML` — already gap #3; visible in the 29 `UNRESOLVABLE_IMPORT` diagnostics above).\n 181 \n 182 ### Already tracked, real but not yet benchmarked (perf, not correctness)\n 183 \n 184 From `docs/STATUS.md`, still accurate:\n 185","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2e84f963-2e29-4940-940f-9a9b52492974","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
