{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-07-25T19:59:45.200Z","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac09e7834ea5d2515</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-oxc-react-codegen/6e77fa42-b39d-4d85-9370-fe0b0fa9b104/tasks/ac09e7834ea5d2515.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Comment quality sweep: cli + binding crates\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Now I have full coverage. Here's the audit.\n\n# Comment Quality Audit — `crates/cli/src` and `crates/binding/src`\n\n## Overall calibration\n\nThis codebase is already close to the target house style. The large majority of non-trivial comments explain a hidden constraint, a bug that was fixed, or a reason a design choice looks the way it does, and they're terse. `config.rs` and `output.rs` in particular are close to exemplary and can be used as the reference standard for the rest of the codebase. Issues found are minor and few — mostly a couple of WHAT-restating comments and one vague \"process\" comment with no actual content.\n\nI did **not** flag the `///` doc comments on clap `Args`/`Subcommand`/`ValueEnum` fields in `main.rs` (lines 27–119) — these compile into `--help` text shown to end users, so their job is literally to describe what the flag does; that's a different contract than an internal implementation comment explaining WHY, and they're all fine for that purpose (concise, no filler, no hedging).\n\n---\n\n## `crates/binding/src/lib.rs`\n\n**Line 20–21** (in `next_session_id`):\n```rust\n// Combine pid (upper 16 bits) with counter (lower 16 bits) into u32.\n// This avoids collisions across concurrent Vite dev server instances.\n```\n- Category: 1 (first line restates the bit-shifting the next line of code already makes obvious) mixed with a good WHY (second line).\n- Suggestion: keep only the WHY line — `// Avoids session-ID collisions across concurrent Vite dev server instances.` Delete the mechanical restatement.\n\n**Line 39**:\n```rust\n// Fields from architectural review:\n```\n- Category: 4 (word salad / vague). Doesn't say what the review found, why these fields exist, or what invariant they protect — just gestures at an external, unrecorded event. Not calibrated with e.g. the `TryFrom` doc comment at line 55 which *does* name the actual finding.\n- Suggestion: delete, or replace with the actual reasoning (e.g., what gap the review found that these fields close).\n\n**Line 55–57** (GOOD example):\n```rust\n/// Names the bad `reactVersion` value — a typo (or a caller bypassing the\n/// `'react18' | 'react19'` TS type, e.g. via `as any`) must not silently\n/// fall back to react19 (crates/core/CLAUDE.md non-negotiable #6).\n```\nExemplary: names a hidden constraint, the failure mode it guards against, and points to the authoritative rule. This is the house style working as intended.\n\n**Line 45, 118–119, 131–132, 142–144, 170–172, 193** — all good, terse, mostly \"what this is for / when to call it\" (useful integration context, not restating obvious code).\n\n---\n\n## `crates/cli/src/main.rs`\n\nNo internal implementation comments beyond the `--help`-generating doc comments discussed above (all fine). No issues.\n\n---\n\n## `crates/cli/src/config.rs`\n\nThis file is the strongest in the audit — treat it as the calibration reference.\n\n**GOOD examples**:\n- Lines 5–12 (doc on `DocgenConfigSchema`): explains *why* it's a subset of `PipelineOptions`, why `deny_unknown_fields`, and links the reasoning to a rule stated elsewhere.\n- Lines 103–111 (doc on `try_load_config`): explicitly states the failure-mode rationale (\"a config a user explicitly wrote getting quietly ignored is a plausible-looking but wrong result forever\") — genuinely good writing, no filler.\n- Lines 115–116: names the actual security risk avoided (command injection via crafted filenames).\n- Lines 130–134: explains a non-obvious two-part reason for `current_dir`, both concrete.\n- Lines 179–180 (doc on `BuildOptionsArgs`): explains why the struct exists (clippy threshold), not just what it holds.\n- Test comments at lines 261–262, 271, 290–295, 320–325, 333–336, 362–365: all explain *why* the test exists (an adversarial-review finding, a bug that was fixed) rather than restating assertions. This is exactly the target style for regression tests.\n\n**Minor flag — Line 143**:\n```rust\n// Write the script to stdin, then close stdin to signal EOF.\n```\n- Category: 1, borderline. The two lines below (`write_all` + `drop(stdin)`) are fairly self-explanatory; the only non-obvious nugget is \"closing signals EOF to node,\" which is real but could be folded into fewer words.\n- Suggestion: shorten to `// drop(stdin) signals EOF so node's read finishes.` or delete — mild, not a priority fix.\n\n**Line 92**: `// Stop at workspace root signals` — borderline WHAT, but short enough and adjacent to non-obvious logic (a `for` loop checking marker files) that it earns its keep. Keep as-is.\n\n---\n\n## `crates/cli/src/output.rs`\n\nNo issues found. Every doc comment explains a WHY (why stderr not stdout, why grouping exists, why the formatter is a separate pure function for testability). This file is a second strong calibration example, e.g.:\n```rust\n/// Human-readable extraction summary. Always written to stderr — stdout is reserved for the\n/// JSON payload (canonical/RDT/storybook), in every mode, so `oxc-react-docgen extract | jq .`\n/// never sees this interleaved with the data.\n```\nand\n```rust\n/// Collapses near-duplicate diagnostics (same code + same quoted subject) into groups, so a\n/// single root cause that produced hundreds of diagnostics reports as one line with a count\n/// instead of flooding the terminal.\n```\n\n---\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/check.rs`\n\nNo issues. The doc comment on `cmd_check` (lines 6–8) cross-references `cmd_extract`'s doc comment instead of duplicating it — good instinct to avoid redundant restatement (category 5 avoided deliberately).\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/completions.rs`\n\nTrivial file, no comments, nothing to flag.\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/extract.rs`\n\n**GOOD examples**:\n- Lines 6–9 (doc on `cmd_extract`): explains why the function returns an exit code instead of calling `process::exit` directly.\n- Line 44: `// --json ignores --out/--format — see ExtractArgs::json's doc comment.` — good, non-redundant cross-reference.\n- Lines 65–66: `// Must run regardless of --json — this is the one thing CI actually depends on the exit code for.` — good WHY.\n- Lines 75–79 (doc on `rdt_type_json`): explains the actual RDT consumer convention and why it matters (Storybook addon pattern-matching), not just what the function returns.\n- Test comment lines 180–182: explains the real bug this test guards against — exemplary regression-test comment.\n\n**Minor flags**:\n\n**Line 97**: `// RDT-compatible shape: flatten to Record&lt;string, ComponentDoc&gt;`\n- Category: 1 (mostly restates what the following map-building code already visibly does; function is even named `serialize_rdt`).\n- Suggestion: rewrite as a WHY, e.g. why RDT expects a flat `Record` instead of an array (matches its known consumer shape) — or delete if that's already implied by the `rdt_type_json` doc comment above.\n\n**Line 130**: `// Storybook __docgenInfo shape`\n- Category: 1, same pattern as above — restates the obvious purpose of the following block without adding info not in the function name.\n- Suggestion: delete, or fold into a doc comment on `serialize_storybook` if there's a non-obvious constraint to state (e.g. why `methods: []` is always empty — that's the one field here that actually needs a WHY and doesn't have one).\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/inspect.rs`\n\nNo comments in the function body at all — nothing to flag (also nothing exemplary to point to).\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/watch.rs`\n\n**Line 53**: `// Keyboard input thread (q=quit, r=re-extract)`\n- Category: 1, mild — restates what the thread spawned right below obviously does (matches keys to quit/re-extract). Somewhat useful as a structural label in a long function.\n- Suggestion: keep as a section label but shorten to `// q=quit, r=re-extract` (drop \"Keyboard input thread\" since `std::thread::spawn` on the next line already says that).\n\n**Line 78**: `// File watcher using watchexec 8.x (synchronous constructor, async main)`\n- Category: mostly fine — the \"sync constructor, async main\" part is a real, non-obvious API-shape note about the `watchexec` crate. The version pin (`8.x`) is currently accurate (`Cargo.toml` pins `watchexec = \"8.2\"`), so not stale today, but it's the kind of comment that goes stale silently on a major-version bump with no compiler-enforced reminder.\n- Suggestion: keep, but consider dropping the version number (or moving the version-specific caveat to reference the actual API shape that requires it, so it stays true across minor bumps and only needs revisiting on a major version change).\n\nNo other issues in this file — the rest is plain, no filler.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>51267</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>133895</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
