one line with a count\n/// instead of flooding the terminal.\n```\n\n---\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/check.rs`\n\nNo issues. The doc comment on `cmd_check` (lines 6–8) cross-references `cmd_extract`'s doc comment instead of duplicating it — good instinct to avoid redundant restatement (category 5 avoided deliberately).\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/completions.rs`\n\nTrivial file, no comments, nothing to flag.\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/extract.rs`\n\n**GOOD examples**:\n- Lines 6–9 (doc on `cmd_extract`): explains why the function returns an exit code instead of calling `process::exit` directly.\n- Line 44: `// --json ignores --out/--format — see ExtractArgs::json's doc comment.` — good, non-redundant cross-reference.\n- Lines 65–66: `// Must run regardless of --json — this is the one thing CI actually depends on the exit code for.` — good WHY.\n- Lines 75–79 (doc on `rdt_type_json`): explains the actual RDT consumer convention and why it matters (Storybook addon pattern-matching), not just what the function returns.\n- Test comment lines 180–182: explains the real bug this test guards against — exemplary regression-test comment.\n\n**Minor flags**:\n\n**Line 97**: `// RDT-compatible shape: flatten to Record&lt;string, ComponentDoc&gt;`\n- Category: 1 (mostly restates what the following map-building code already visibly does; function is even named `serialize_rdt`).\n- Suggestion: rewrite as a WHY, e.g. why RDT expects a flat `Record` instead of an array (matches its known consumer shape) — or delete if that's already implied by the `rdt_type_json` doc comment above.\n\n**Line 130**: `// Storybook __docgenInfo shape`\n- Category: 1, same pattern as above — restates the obvious purpose of the following block without adding info not in the function name.\n- Suggestion: delete, or fold into a doc comment on `serialize_storybook` if there's a non-obvious constraint to state (e.g. why `methods: []` is always empty — that's the one field here that actually needs a WHY and doesn't have one).\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/inspect.rs`\n\nNo comments in the function body at all — nothing to flag (also nothing exemplary to point to).\n\n## `crates/cli/src/commands/watch.rs`\n\n**Line 53**: `// Keyboard input thread (q=quit, r=re-extract)`\n- Category: 1, mild — restates what the thread spawned right below obviously does (matches keys to quit/re-extract). Somewhat useful as a structural label in a long function.\n- Suggestion: keep as a section label but shorten to `// q=quit, r=re-extract` (drop \"Keyboard input thread\" since `std::thread::spawn` on the next line already says that).\n\n**Line 78**: `// File watcher using watchexec 8.x (synchronous constructor, async main)`\n- Category: mostly fine — the \"sync constructor, async main\" part is a real, non-obvious API-shape note about the `watchexec` crate. The version pin (`8.x`) is currently accurate (`Cargo.toml` pins `watchexec = \"8.2\"`), so not stale today, but it's the kind of comment that goes stale silently on a major-version bump with no compiler-enforced reminder.\n- Suggestion: keep, but consider dropping the version number (or moving the version-specific caveat to reference the actual API shape that requires it, so it stays true across minor bumps and only needs revisiting on a major version change).\n\nNo other issues in this file — the rest is plain, no filler.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>51267</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>133895</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"872a33e1-9e4f-4045-8fd4-646407e0337d","timestamp":"2026-07-25T19:59:45.231Z","permissionMode":"auto","origin":{"kind":"task-notification"},"promptSource":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
