{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-07-25T19:59:48.033Z","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac5419e3d60b2c3cd</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-oxc-react-codegen/6e77fa42-b39d-4d85-9370-fe0b0fa9b104/tasks/ac5419e3d60b2c3cd.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Comment quality sweep: TS packages\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Comment Audit — oxc-react-docgen\n\nScope covered: `packages/vite-plugin/src/index.ts`, `packages/napi/index.d.ts` (binding.d.ts and index.js are auto-generated, out of scope), `packages/cli/bin/cli.js`, and all files under `apps/validate/src/` plus `bench-cold.sh`.\n\nOverall: this codebase's comments are unusually good already — most are genuine WHY explanations, several read like real incident/bug notes. Issues found are mostly small: a couple of restatements, one duplicated rationale, one dead-code doc block, and one comment whose claim is slightly broader than what the code actually does.\n\n---\n\n### packages/vite-plugin/src/index.ts\n\n- **Line 36** — `// Holds the in-flight init promise so hotUpdate can await it before first incremental call.`\n  Good WHY comment, but nearly duplicated at line 129 (see below). Category 5 (redundant with distant comment). Suggestion: keep this one (declaration site), drop the one at 129.\n\n- **Lines 48–51** — \n  ```\n  // configureServer (dev-only) is where cold extraction normally runs and\n  // populates `currentOutput` for the virtual module. Vite never calls\n  // configureServer during `vite build`, so without this, a production\n  // build's virtual module always resolved to the empty placeholder below.\n  ```\n  and **Lines 76–78** —\n  ```\n  // `vite build` never calls configureServer — that hook only fires for\n  // the dev server — so the build path needs its own cold extraction here.\n  // Skipped for `serve`, where configureServer already handles it.\n  ```\n  Both make the identical point (\"vite build never calls configureServer\") within ~30 lines, at two different call sites. Category 5. Suggestion: keep the fuller one at 48–51 (it's the first place a reader hits the concept), shrink 76–78 to just `// Build-only counterpart to coldExtract() in configureServer; see that comment.`\n\n- **Line 129** — `// Wait for cold extraction to finish before the first incremental call.`\n  Redundant with line 36's `initPromise` comment — same fact stated at declaration and at use site. Minor. Suggestion: delete, or replace with something only true at this call site (e.g., why hotUpdate specifically needs it vs. other hooks).\n\n- **Line 110–111** — `// Return a teardown function — Vite calls this on dev-server close. // buildEnd is not called in dev mode, so this is the only reliable cleanup hook.`\n  First clause (\"Return a teardown function\") just restates the `return () =&gt; {...}` below it; second clause is the real WHY. Minor. Suggestion: trim to `// buildEnd isn't called in dev mode — this is the only reliable cleanup hook.`\n\n- **GOOD examples**: lines 89–95 (oxlint-disable rationale — genuinely non-obvious), line 100 (`environments.client.hot is Vite 6+ API...`), lines 118–120 (this-cast rationale), and especially **lines 152–153** (`Return undefined (NOT []) — returning [] suppresses React Fast Refresh...`) — exactly the \"bug/gotcha + why the fix looks this way\" style you want.\n\n---\n\n### packages/napi/index.d.ts\n\n- **Line 46** — `/** PropType is a tagged union via the 'kind' discriminant */`\n  Restates the obvious shape of the type immediately below it (any reader sees the `kind` discriminant in the union). Category 1, minor. Suggestion: delete.\n\n- **GOOD examples**: header block (lines 1–7) is an excellent WHY comment — explains the file's relationship to `binding.d.ts` and the Rust source, and why NAPI-RS's codegen can't produce these types itself. `extraPathsJson`/`knownTypeOverridesJson` inline JSDoc (lines 46–49) usefully decode an opaque JSON-string field — keep as-is.\n\n`binding.d.ts` and `index.js` are excluded — both explicitly marked `/* auto-generated by NAPI-RS */`, not hand-written.\n\n---\n\n### packages/cli/bin/cli.js\n\nNo issues found. Every comment (header block lines 2–6, dev-candidates rationale lines 31–32, `require.resolve` note line 43) is a terse, specific WHY. This is a good calibration file for house style.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/analyze.ts\n\nNo issues found. Comments consistently explain non-obvious design choices (the `oursForRdg` fallback baseline, the divergence-bucket taxonomy, the \"unreachable\" invariant note at line 193, the \"honest denominator\" JSDoc). All WHY-focused, appropriately terse, cross-reference docs where useful. This file reads like a good second-pass already.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/run-ours.ts\n\n- **Line 13** — `// Find library directories under fixtures/` above `function discoverLibraries()`.\n  Pure restatement of the function name. Category 1. Suggestion: delete.\n\n- **Line 20** — `// Map our PropType to a human-readable string for comparison` above `function propTypeToString(pt: any)`.\n  Same — restates the function name/signature. Category 1. Suggestion: delete.\n\n- **GOOD examples**: line 8 (release-vs-debug build preference), lines 135–141 (HTML_ATTRIBUTES_MODE rationale, references ARCHITECTURE.md), lines 147–154 (single-invocation-vs-per-library, with actual measured numbers — excellent), lines 177–178 (why filePath parsing changed given single combined invocation). This file, despite being new, is already strong; the two restatement comments are the only weak spots.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/run-react-docgen.ts\n\nNo issues found. Lines 6–13 (`tsType` vs `type` field explanation) and lines 54–60 (the multi-component keying bug that was fixed, and why) are exemplary \"bug that was fixed and why the fix looks this way\" comments — some of the best in the audited set.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/run-react-docgen-typescript.ts\n\n- **Line 8** — `// Create parser — falls back gracefully if tsconfig not found`\n  The catch block actually catches *any* error from `withCustomConfig` with the extended options (not specifically a missing tsconfig), so \"if tsconfig not found\" is more specific than what the code guarantees — borderline stale/imprecise (category 3/4). Suggestion: rewrite to name the actual failure mode if known (e.g., \"some react-docgen-typescript option combos throw on certain tsconfigs — retry with a minimal option set\"), or soften to \"falls back if the extended option set throws.\"\n\n- **GOOD examples**: lines 21–26 (`enum`-shaped type explanation) and line 36 (`// RDT needs real TS files`) are both terse, specific WHY comments.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/bench-cold.sh\n\nNo issues found. Header comment (lines 2–6) is a solid WHY: explains why hyperfine over a hand-rolled loop and what each invocation represents.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/baseline.ts\n\nNo issues found. Lines 10–14 (maxBuffer rationale with concrete numbers) and lines 34–38 (why a separate html-attributes=none baseline exists) are both good WHY comments, appropriately positioned at their own call site.\n\n(Note: the \"why oxc-react-docgen-none baseline exists\" rationale appears with different wording in three places — here, in `analyze.ts` lines 14–17, and in `run-ours.ts` lines 135–141. Each is justified in its own file/context rather than truly adjacent-redundant, so not flagging as a violation, but worth knowing this same design point is now explained three times across the codebase if you ever want to consolidate into one doc reference.)\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/compare.ts\n\n- **Line 44** — `// Timing` — trivial section label immediately above self-evident code (`times.push(\\`rdg: ${...}ms\\`)`). Category 1. Suggestion: delete.\n\n- **Line 51** — `// Components per tool` — same, trivial label. Category 1. Suggestion: delete.\n\n- **Line 68** — `// Props ours has that rdt doesn't` above `const oursOnly = oursProps.filter((p) =&gt; !rdtProps.includes(p))`. Pure restatement — the variable name `oursOnly` already says this. Category 1. Suggestion: delete.\n\n- **Lines 81–82 vs 85–86** —\n  ```\n  // For rdt-only props: distinguish HTML attrs covered by our notableInherited from true misses.\n  // We trust our notableInherited list as the ground truth for what we surface from HTML elements.\n  ...\n  // A prop is \"covered by our inheritance\" if it appears in our notableInherited list.\n  // Props not in notableInherited and not in our own props are true misses.\n  ```\n  These two adjacent comment blocks say the same thing twice, back to back. Category 5. Suggestion: delete the second block (85–86); the first already establishes the rule.\n\n- **Line 126** — `// A win is: no real misses, no unexpected extra props, no type diffs, and we found something.`\n  Borderline — restates the following `if` condition somewhat, but \"win\" isn't self-explanatory otherwise (it's a scoring/reporting concept, not obvious from code). Keep, but could shorten.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/fixtures.ts\n\nNo significant issues. `dir === 'panda'` and `file === 'index.ts'` skip comments (lines 19, 22) are terse and WHY-appropriate (\"skip styled-system subdir complexity for now\" / \"skip barrel files for now\") — fine as-is, though \"for now\" is mild hedging worth removing if there's no concrete follow-up plan.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/types.ts\n\n- **Line 5** — `type: string // raw type string` — restates the field name almost verbatim. Category 1, minor. Suggestion: delete or replace with something concrete (e.g., \"verbatim source text, not normalized — compared as-is against other tools' output\").\n\n- **Lines 32–39** — the entire `ComparisonResult` interface's JSDoc:\n  ```\n  /** Props in rdt but not in rdg */\n  rdtOnly: string[]\n  /** Props in rdg but not in rdt */\n  rdgOnly: string[]\n  /** Props in both but with different types */\n  typeDiffs: Array&lt;{ prop: string; rdt: string; rdg: string }&gt;\n  /** Components detected by rdt but not rdg */\n  rdtOnlyComponents: string[]\n  /** Components detected by rdg but not rdt */\n  rdgOnlyComponents: string[]\n  ```\n  All four comments are pure restatements of the field names (category 1). Worse: `ComparisonResult` itself appears to be dead code — grepping the whole repo, `types.ts` is the only place this identifier appears; nothing constructs or consumes it (`compare.ts` computes the same shape ad hoc with local variables instead of this type). This is a stale type, and its comments compound the problem by documenting something unused. Suggestion: either delete the unused interface entirely, or if kept for future use, drop the restating JSDoc.\n\n- **GOOD example**: line 1 (`Normalized prop representation — common subset of rdg + rdt output`) explains the type's *purpose* (why this shape exists) rather than its structure — keep as the model for the rest of this file.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>57539</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>16</tool_uses><duration_ms>122814</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
