er block (lines 1–7) is an excellent WHY comment — explains the file's relationship to `binding.d.ts` and the Rust source, and why NAPI-RS's codegen can't produce these types itself. `extraPathsJson`/`knownTypeOverridesJson` inline JSDoc (lines 46–49) usefully decode an opaque JSON-string field — keep as-is.\n\n`binding.d.ts` and `index.js` are excluded — both explicitly marked `/* auto-generated by NAPI-RS */`, not hand-written.\n\n---\n\n### packages/cli/bin/cli.js\n\nNo issues found. Every comment (header block lines 2–6, dev-candidates rationale lines 31–32, `require.resolve` note line 43) is a terse, specific WHY. This is a good calibration file for house style.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/analyze.ts\n\nNo issues found. Comments consistently explain non-obvious design choices (the `oursForRdg` fallback baseline, the divergence-bucket taxonomy, the \"unreachable\" invariant note at line 193, the \"honest denominator\" JSDoc). All WHY-focused, appropriately terse, cross-reference docs where useful. This file reads like a good second-pass already.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/run-ours.ts\n\n- **Line 13** — `// Find library directories under fixtures/` above `function discoverLibraries()`.\n  Pure restatement of the function name. Category 1. Suggestion: delete.\n\n- **Line 20** — `// Map our PropType to a human-readable string for comparison` above `function propTypeToString(pt: any)`.\n  Same — restates the function name/signature. Category 1. Suggestion: delete.\n\n- **GOOD examples**: line 8 (release-vs-debug build preference), lines 135–141 (HTML_ATTRIBUTES_MODE rationale, references ARCHITECTURE.md), lines 147–154 (single-invocation-vs-per-library, with actual measured numbers — excellent), lines 177–178 (why filePath parsing changed given single combined invocation). This file, despite being new, is already strong; the two restatement comments are the only weak spots.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/run-react-docgen.ts\n\nNo issues found. Lines 6–13 (`tsType` vs `type` field explanation) and lines 54–60 (the multi-component keying bug that was fixed, and why) are exemplary \"bug that was fixed and why the fix looks this way\" comments — some of the best in the audited set.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/run-react-docgen-typescript.ts\n\n- **Line 8** — `// Create parser — falls back gracefully if tsconfig not found`\n  The catch block actually catches *any* error from `withCustomConfig` with the extended options (not specifically a missing tsconfig), so \"if tsconfig not found\" is more specific than what the code guarantees — borderline stale/imprecise (category 3/4). Suggestion: rewrite to name the actual failure mode if known (e.g., \"some react-docgen-typescript option combos throw on certain tsconfigs — retry with a minimal option set\"), or soften to \"falls back if the extended option set throws.\"\n\n- **GOOD examples**: lines 21–26 (`enum`-shaped type explanation) and line 36 (`// RDT needs real TS files`) are both terse, specific WHY comments.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/bench-cold.sh\n\nNo issues found. Header comment (lines 2–6) is a solid WHY: explains why hyperfine over a hand-rolled loop and what each invocation represents.\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/baseline.ts\n\nNo issues found. Lines 10–14 (maxBuffer rationale with concrete numbers) and lines 34–38 (why a separate html-attributes=none baseline exists) are both good WHY comments, appropriately positioned at their own call site.\n\n(Note: the \"why oxc-react-docgen-none baseline exists\" rationale appears with different wording in three places — here, in `analyze.ts` lines 14–17, and in `run-ours.ts` lines 135–141. Each is justified in its own file/context rather than truly adjacent-redundant, so not flagging as a violation, but worth knowing this same design point is now explained three times across the codebase if you ever want to consolidate into one doc reference.)\n\n---\n\n### apps/validate/src/compare.ts\n\n- **Line 44** — `// Timing