2` (test): `// silently produce an empty-but-valid-looking result (crates/core/CLAUDE.md #6).`\n\n(Same stale pattern also appears outside the audited scope in `crates/cli/src/config.rs:32,109` and `crates/binding/src/lib.rs:57`, plus ADR/benchmark docs — so this is a repo-wide drift, not a one-off.)\n\n- **Category:** 3 (stale/contradicts current code — the referenced numbered item doesn't exist in the current doc)\n- **Suggestion:** Either restore a numbered non-negotiables list in `CLAUDE.md`, or reword these comments to state the rule inline (\"never fail silently on a missing/misconfigured src dir\") instead of pointing at a citation that no longer resolves.\n\n## extractor/component.rs — minor restates\n\n- `line 74`: `// Strip React. prefix for matching` — directly above `type_name.strip_prefix(\"React.\")`. **Restates.** Delete.\n- `line 208`: `// Extract props type from first parameter annotation` — above code doing exactly that. **Restates.** Delete.\n- `lines 240-241`: `// Look for ForwardRefExoticComponent&lt;P &amp; RefAttributes&lt;E&gt;&gt; / // or ForwardRefExoticComponent&lt;P&gt;` — restates the match immediately below. Delete (the function's own doc comment at line 229 already states this).\n- `line 256`: `// Find the member that is NOT RefAttributes/RefAttributes&lt;E&gt;` — restates the closure it sits above almost verbatim. Delete.\n- `line 201`: `// Must be PascalCase — not anonymous utility functions` — borderline; adds a sliver of WHY (ruling out anonymous helpers) but mostly restates the `is_pascal_case` check right below. Shorten to just the WHY clause or delete.\n\n**Good calibration examples in this file:** the doc comment on `unwrap_as_expression` (lines 11-15, cites the Fluent UI source comment as evidence), and especially `try_rename_identifier_wrapped_component`'s doc comment (lines 289-309) — a textbook WHY comment: explains the real-world pattern it handles, why the naive approach doesn't work, and why cloning-not-mutating was the fix.\n\n## extractor/visit.rs — minor restates\n\n- `line 259`: `// Walk children for nested declarations` directly above `walk::walk_variable_declaration(self, node);` — pure restatement. Delete.\n- `lines 297, 299`: `// Scan for \\`Button.displayName = \"Button\"\\` assignments` / `// Scan for \\`Button.defaultProps = {...}\\` assignments` — restate the self-explanatory function names (`try_scan_display_name`, `try_scan_default_props`) called directly beneath them. Delete.\n- `lines 58, 152` (`// Don't walk children — we've handled/extracted everything`): **keep** — these explain a real design decision (deliberately *not* calling the default `walk::walk_*`), which is genuinely non-obvious for a visitor pattern.\n- The \"Pattern 4/5\" labels (lines 249, 264) are a deliberate catalog convention across the file (referenced from other files too, e.g. `component.rs`) — keep, this is good organizational signposting, not filler.\n\n**Good example:** lines 120-122 (claiming the interface's own JSDoc before descending into props) is an excellent, specific WHY comment about an ordering bug that would otherwise recur.\n\n## extractor/jsdoc.rs — restates in the low-level string parsing\n\nLines 66, 74, 95, 104, 108, 111 (`// Strip \\`/**\\` prefix and \\`*/\\` suffix`, `// Strip leading \\`* \\` or \\`*\\``, `// Parse tag: \\`@tagname rest\\``, `// Skip {type} if present`, `// First word is the prop name`, etc.) all restate trivial string-slicing operations one line below them.\n- **Category:** 1 (restates code)\n- **Suggestion:** delete all of these — the code itself (string literals like `\"* \"` and `.trim_start_matches(\"/**\")`) is self-documenting.\n\n**Excellent calibration examples in the same file:** the doc comment on `find_jsdoc_with_tags` (lines 19-31) is one of the best in the codebase — explains a real bug class (a tag bleeding across sibling props), why the fix works (shared consumed-set), and why binary search is used (avoiding a quadratic rescan). Also `find_jsdoc`'s doc comment (10-14).\n\n## extractor/interface.rs — minor restates\n\n- `line 95`: `// Check callee is a known variant function` — restates the match below. Minor, could delete.\n- `line 121`: `// Find the \"variants\" property` — restates the `find_map` immediately below. Minor, could delete.\n- `line 106`: `// cva(baseClasses, { variants }) takes config at index 1; all other callees take it at index 0` — **keep**, this is genuinely non-obvious and explains the `usize::from(callee_name == \"cva\")` line below it.\n\n**Good examples:** the doc comments on `as_const_literal` (10-14), `try_collect_const_array` (59-64), `try_scan_default_props` (218-222) are all solid WHY comments.\n\n## resolver/ — near-zero issues (the strongest files in the audit)\n\n`resolver/mod.rs`, `resolver/chain.rs`, `resolver/alias.rs`, `resolver/import.rs`, `resolver/named.rs`, `resolver/extends.rs`, `resolver/react.rs`, `resolver/substitute.rs`, `resolver/primitives.rs`, `resolver/collected.rs`, `resolver/func.rs`, `resolver/template.rs` are consistently excellent — nearly every comment explains a real regression, a hidden constraint (e.g. why `@types/react` needs special resolution, why cycle detection must include type args, why `Omit&lt;T, keyof U&gt;`'s key set needs a separate visited-set branch), or a design tradeoff. I did not find a single comment in this directory that restates code or reads as filler. Representative best-in-class examples worth using as the calibration standard:\n\n- `resolver/mod.rs` — `unresolved_location_note`, `is_ts_utility_type`, `ResolutionContext::enum_bare_index` doc comments; `ResolvedChain::merge_parent`'s discriminant-propagation comment (lines 387-393).\n- `resolver/import.rs` — `follow_reexports`'s doc comment (67-73) and the \"shared descendants\" comment (88-92); `resolve_import_specifier`'s explanation of why \"react\" needs special-cased `@types` fallback (214-221).\n- `resolver/alias.rs` — the `Omit`/`keyof U` branch-state comment (40-56); the exhaustive-match justification at the end of `resolve_base_as_chain` (205-210) explaining *why* it's a named list instead of a wildcard.\n- `resolver/extends.rs` — `real_html_attrs_chain`'s doc comment (93-102).\n\nThe one minor nit: `resolver/react.rs` uses short section-label comments above match arms (`// Ref types.`, `// FC / FunctionComponent — return as Named.`, etc.) that mostly restate the arm below. These function as scan-aids in a long match and are consistent with the file's own style — borderline, not worth changing, but if trimming is wanted these are the first candidates (category 1, low severity).\n\n## pipeline/mod.rs, pipeline/discover.rs, pipeline/watch.rs — no issues beyond the CLAUDE.md citation\n\nComments here are dense but substantive — e.g. the doc comment on `extract_with_global` (194-204) justifying why `capture_source_data` exists and what watch mode needs from it; the Phase 3.5/3.6 comments explaining why `@types/react` and `lib.d.ts` merges are handled specially; the dedup-key collision comment (366-377) documenting a real fixed bug (5 real fixture libraries colliding on `Button.tsx`). All good WHY style. Test-only comments documenting regressions (e.g. `test_generic_svg_attributes_extends_resolves_to_its_element_in_full_mode`) are also excellent and match the file's overall standard.\n\n## import_map.rs — no issues\n\nClean top to bottom. `ImportResolutionMap`'s doc comment on why the map is nested rather than flat-keyed (42-43) and the note about storing specifiers vs. resolved paths (44-45) are good, minimal WHY comments. Nothing to flag.\n\n## known.rs, react_types.rs, cache.rs — sampled, consistent with the rest\n\n- `cache.rs`: `CACHE_SCHEMA_VERSION`'s doc comment (16-23) is an excellent example — explains a genuinely dangerous MessagePack positional-encoding footgun and why a schema bump matters.\n- `known.rs`: `push_known_opaque_diagnostic`'s doc comment (24-30) and `resolve_known`'s doc comment (63-74) are good; one minor restate at `known.rs` line 99 (`// We can use the base union and note the extension is opaque` directly above code doing exactly that) — could be deleted.\n- `react_types.rs`: `parse_react_version`'s doc comment (229-235) is a good WHY comment about failing loudly on unknown react-version strings, though it uses the vaguer, non-numbered phrase \"this crate's non-negotiables\" rather than the stale \"#6\" — not itself broken, but worth fixing in the same pass as the CLAUDE.md issue above for consistency.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThis is a low-noise audit. If I had to prioritize two actions:\n1. Fix the \"non-negotiable #6\" dangling reference (either restore the numbered list in `crates/core/CLAUDE.md` or de-reference the citations).\n2. Sweep `extractor/jsdoc.rs`, `extractor/component.rs`, and `extractor/visit.rs` for the ~10 restates-the-line-below comments listed above — all safe, low-risk deletions.\n\nEverything else — and it's the large majority of the ~1,900 comment lines across these files — is already at or above the bar the skill describes.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>164920</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>32</tool_uses><duration_ms>279203</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"parentUuid":"94c1c4b9-7c37-4d08-b4b9-9ffbbeee3f23","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdPNXyEfMgU9sDcARBjP7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NfyWoddDomupoPs5tpp8ne","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/apps/validate/src/compare.ts","old_string":"  // Components per tool\n  const rdgComps = Object.keys(r_rdg?.output ?? {})","new_string":"  const rdgComps = Object.keys(r_rdg?.output ?? {})"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":899,"cache_read_input_tokens":545977,"output_tokens":213,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":899,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":213,"cache_read_input_tokens":545977,"cache_creation_input_tokens":899,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":899},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdPNXu4t33PU42Qdsm67s","type":"assistant","uuid":"3060e122-a187-47f4-869d-f2ef5d73be2a","timestamp":"2026-07-25T20:02:00.495Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"3060e122-a187-47f4-869d-f2ef5d73be2a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0cb60a71-2d64-41e9-8fe3-12f3a2549892","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NfyWoddDomupoPs5tpp8ne","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/apps/validate/src/compare.ts has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"a91971e6-6356-4625-914e-a667ee6e3fd1","timestamp":"2026-07-25T20:02:00.605Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/apps/validate/src/compare.ts","oldString":"  // Components per tool\n  const rdgComps = Object.keys(r_rdg?.output ?? {})","newString":"  const rdgComps = Object.keys(r_rdg?.output ?? {})","originalFile":"import { readFileSync, existsSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport type { ToolResult } from './types.ts'\n\nfunction loadBaseline(name: string): Map<string, ToolResult> | null {\n  const p = `./baselines/${name}.json`\n  if (!existsSync(p)) return null\n  const results: ToolResult[] = JSON.parse(readFileSync(p, 'utf8'))\n  return new Map(results.map((r) => [r.fixture, r]))\n}\n\nconst rdg = loadBaseline('react-docgen')\nconst rdt = loadBaseline('react-docgen-typescript')\nconst ours = loadBaseline('oxc-react-docgen')\n\nif (!rdg && !rdt && !ours) {\n  console.error('No baselines found. Run at least one tool first.')\n  process.exit(1)\n}\n\nconst allFixtures = new Set([\n  ...(rdg ? [...rdg.keys()] : []),\n  ...(rdt ? [...rdt.keys()] : []),\n  ...(ours ? [...ours.keys()] : []),\n])\n\nlet totalOwn = 0,\n  rdtTotal = 0,\n  oursTotal = 0,\n  wins = 0,\n  ties = 0,\n  misses = 0\n\nfor (const fixture of [...allFixtures].toSorted()) {\n  const r_rdg = rdg?.get(fixture)\n  const r_rdt = rdt?.get(fixture)\n  const r_ours = ours?.get(fixture)\n\n  console.log(`\\n## ${fixture}`)\n\n  if (r_rdg?.error) console.log(`  ❌ react-docgen: ${r_rdg.error.slice(0, 100)}`)\n  if (r_rdt?.error) console.log(`  ❌ react-docgen-typescript: ${r_rdt.error.slice(0, 100)}`)\n  if (r_ours?.error) console.log(`  ❌ oxc-react-docgen: ${r_ours.error.slice(0, 100)}`)\n\n  const times: string[] = []\n  if (r_rdg) times.push(`rdg: ${r_rdg.durationMs.toFixed(1)}ms`)\n  if (r_rdt) times.push(`rdt: ${r_rdt.durationMs.toFixed(1)}ms`)\n  if (r_ours) times.push(`ours: ${r_ours.durationMs.toFixed(1)}ms`)\n  if (times.length > 0) console.log(`  ⏱  ${times.join('  |  ')}`)\n\n  // Components per tool\n  const rdgComps = Object.keys(r_rdg?.output ?? {})\n  const rdtComps = Object.keys(r_rdt?.output ?? {})\n  const oursComps = Object.keys(r_ours?.output ?? {})\n  const allComps = new Set([...rdgComps, ...rdtComps, ...oursComps])\n\n  for (const comp of [...allComps].toSorted()) {\n    const rdgProps = Object.keys(r_rdg?.output?.[comp]?.props ?? {})\n    const rdtProps = Object.keys(r_rdt?.output?.[comp]?.props ?? {})\n    const oursProps = Object.keys(r_ours?.output?.[comp]?.props ?? {})\n\n    console.log(`\\n  ### ${comp}`)\n    console.log(`    props: rdg=${rdgProps.length}  rdt=${rdtProps.length}  ours=${oursProps.length}`)\n\n    rdtTotal += rdtProps.length\n    oursTotal += oursProps.length\n\n    // Props ours has that rdt doesn't\n    const oursOnly = oursProps.filter((p) => !rdtProps.includes(p))\n    const rdtOnly = rdtProps.filter((p) => !oursProps.includes(p))\n    const rdgOnly = rdgProps.filter((p) => !oursProps.includes(p))\n    const common = oursProps.filter((p) => rdtProps.includes(p))\n    const ourInheritedElements = r_ours?.inheritedElements ?? []\n\n    if (oursOnly.length > 0) console.log(`    ours-only (${oursOnly.length}): ${oursOnly.slice(0, 8).join(', ')}`)\n    if (rdgOnly.length > 0)\n      console.log(\n        `    rdg-only  (${rdgOnly.length}): ${rdgOnly.slice(0, 5).join(', ')}${rdgOnly.length > 5 ? '...' : ''}`,\n      )\n\n    // For rdt-only props: distinguish HTML attrs covered by our notableInherited from true misses.\n    // We trust our notableInherited list as the ground truth for what we surface from HTML elements.\n    const ourNotableNames = new Set(r_ours?.notableInheritedNames)\n    if (rdtOnly.length > 0) {\n      // A prop is \"covered by our inheritance\" if it appears in our notableInherited list.\n      // Props not in notableInherited and not in our own props are true misses.\n      const coveredByInheritance = rdtOnly.filter((p) => ourNotableNames.has(p))\n      const trueMisses = rdtOnly.filter((p) => !ourNotableNames.has(p) && !oursProps.includes(p))\n      if (trueMisses.length > 0) {\n        console.log(`    ❗ rdt-only REAL MISSES (${trueMisses.length}): ${trueMisses.slice(0, 8).join(', ')}`)\n        misses += trueMisses.length\n      }\n      if (coveredByInheritance.length > 0) {\n        console.log(`    ℹ️  rdt expands ${coveredByInheritance.length} attrs we surface via notableInherited`)\n      }\n      const unexpandedHtmlAttrs = rdtOnly.filter(\n        (p) => !ourNotableNames.has(p) && !oursProps.includes(p) && /^(on[A-Z]|aria-|data-)/.test(p),\n      )\n      if (unexpandedHtmlAttrs.length > 0 && ourInheritedElements.length > 0) {\n        console.log(\n          `    ℹ️  ${unexpandedHtmlAttrs.length} additional HTML event/ARIA attrs rdt expands (not in our notableInherited)`,\n        )\n      }\n    }\n\n    // Type comparison (ours vs rdt)\n    const typeDiffs = common\n      .filter((p) => {\n        const o = r_ours?.output?.[comp]?.props?.[p]?.type\n        const t = r_rdt?.output?.[comp]?.props?.[p]?.type\n        return o && t && o !== t\n      })\n      .map((p) => ({\n        prop: p,\n        ours: r_ours?.output?.[comp]?.props?.[p]?.type ?? '?',\n        rdt: r_rdt?.output?.[comp]?.props?.[p]?.type ?? '?',\n      }))\n\n    if (typeDiffs.length > 0) {\n      console.log(`    type diffs (${typeDiffs.length}):`)\n      for (const d of typeDiffs.slice(0, 5)) {\n        console.log(`      ${d.prop}: ours=\"${d.ours}\"  rdt=\"${d.rdt}\"`)\n      }\n    }\n\n    // A win is: no real misses, no unexpected extra props, no type diffs, and we found something.\n    const rdtOnlyRealCount = rdtOnly.filter((p) => !ourNotableNames.has(p) && !oursProps.includes(p)).length\n\n    if (oursOnly.length === 0 && rdtOnlyRealCount === 0 && typeDiffs.length === 0 && oursProps.length > 0) {\n      console.log(`    ✅ own props match rdt`)\n      wins++\n    } else if (oursProps.length === 0 && rdtProps.length === 0) {\n      ties++\n    } else if (oursProps.length === 0 && rdtProps.length > 0 && ourInheritedElements.length > 0) {\n      const notableCount = ourNotableNames.size\n      const rdtCovered = rdtOnly.filter((p) => ourNotableNames.has(p)).length\n      if (rdtCovered === rdtProps.length) {\n        console.log(`    ✅ 0 own props, all ${rdtProps.length} rdt props covered by notableInherited`)\n        wins++\n      } else {\n        console.log(\n          `    ⚠️  0 own props; we surface ${notableCount} notableInherited, rdt has ${rdtProps.length} total (inherits ${ourInheritedElements.join(', ')})`,\n        )\n      }\n    } else if (oursProps.length === 0 && rdtProps.length > 0 && ourInheritedElements.length === 0) {\n      console.log(`    ❌ ours found nothing, rdt found ${rdtProps.length} props`)\n    }\n\n    totalOwn += oursProps.length\n  }\n}\n\nconsole.log(`\\n${'='.repeat(60)}`)\nconsole.log('SUMMARY')\nconsole.log('='.repeat(60))\nif (rdt) console.log(`react-docgen-typescript: ${rdtTotal} total props across all components`)\nif (ours) console.log(`oxc-react-docgen:        ${oursTotal} total props across all components`)\nconsole.log(`Coverage: ${oursTotal}/${rdtTotal} (${Math.round((oursTotal / Math.max(rdtTotal, 1)) * 100)}%)`)\nconsole.log(`Identical matches: ${wins}  |  Both empty: ${ties}  |  Misses: ${misses}`)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":47,"oldLines":7,"newStart":47,"newLines":6,"lines":["   if (r_ours) times.push(`ours: ${r_ours.durationMs.toFixed(1)}ms`)","   if (times.length > 0) console.log(`  ⏱  ${times.join('  |  ')}`)"," ","-  // Components per tool","   const rdgComps = Object.keys(r_rdg?.output ?? {})","   const rdtComps = Object.keys(r_rdt?.output ?? {})","   const oursComps = Object.keys(r_ours?.output ?? {})"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3060e122-a187-47f4-869d-f2ef5d73be2a","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"a91971e6-6356-4625-914e-a667ee6e3fd1","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Edit","toolUseID":"toolu_01NfyWoddDomupoPs5tpp8ne","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Edit\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.1.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":65},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6b54cdf8-c16c-4815-9eb4-373a25600bf2","timestamp":"2026-07-25T20:02:00.604Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"6b54cdf8-c16c-4815-9eb4-373a25600bf2","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"queued_command","prompt":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a2ff91682380df7b3</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01AfVtgsUgWRbCh2t35ETQbU</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-oxc-react-codegen/6e77fa42-b39d-4d85-9370-fe0b0fa9b104/tasks/a2ff91682380df7b3.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Comment quality sweep: crates/core\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Summary\n\nI read through `extractor/`, `resolver/`, `pipeline/`, `import_map.rs`, `known.rs`, `react_types.rs`, and `cache.rs`. **Overall quality is very high** — this codebase is a strong example of the house style already being followed. The large majority of comments (especially module-level `//!` docs, function-level `///` docs, and comments justifying non-obvious branches) are exactly the WHY-focused, terse, no-filler style the skill describes. I found zero AI-slop phrases (\"It's important to note\", \"Basically\", etc. — grepped, zero hits) and zero word-salad. The issues that exist are narrow: a handful of trivial \"restates the code\" comments in the extractor's lower-level parsing helpers, and one real systemic staleness bug.\n\n## Real finding: stale cross-reference to a non-existent CLAUDE.md section\n\n`crates/core/CLAUDE.md` currently has **no numbered \"non-negotiables\" list** (I read it — it's organized as prose sections: Module layout, Types discipline, Resolver, Snapshot tests). But comments/tests across the codebase repeatedly cite **\"non-negotiable #6\"** as if such a numbered list still exists there:\n\n- `pipeline/mod.rs:214`: `// result — see crates/core/CLAUDE.md non-negotiable #6.`\n- `pipeline/mod.rs:592` (test): `// silently produce an empty-but-valid-looking result (crates/core/CLAUDE.md #6).`\n\n(Same stale pattern also appears outside the audited scope in `crates/cli/src/config.rs:32,109` and `crates/binding/src/lib.rs:57`, plus ADR/benchmark docs — so this is a repo-wide drift, not a one-off.)\n\n- **Category:** 3 (stale/contradicts current code — the referenced numbered item doesn't exist in the current doc)\n- **Suggestion:** Either restore a numbered non-negotiables list in `CLAUDE.md`, or reword these comments to state the rule inline (\"never fail silently on a missing/misconfigured src dir\") instead of pointing at a citation that no longer resolves.\n\n## extractor/component.rs — minor restates\n\n- `line 74`: `// Strip React. prefix for matching` — directly above `type_name.strip_prefix(\"React.\")`. **Restates.** Delete.\n- `line 208`: `// Extract props type from first parameter annotation` — above code doing exactly that. **Restates.** Delete.\n- `lines 240-241`: `// Look for ForwardRefExoticComponent&lt;P &amp; RefAttributes&lt;E&gt;&gt; / // or ForwardRefExoticComponent&lt;P&gt;` — restates the match immediately below. Delete (the function's own doc comment at line 229 already states this).\n- `line 256`: `// Find the member that is NOT RefAttributes/RefAttributes&lt;E&gt;` — restates the closure it sits above almost verbatim. Delete.\n- `line 201`: `// Must be PascalCase — not anonymous utility functions` — borderline; adds a sliver of WHY (ruling out anonymous helpers) but mostly restates the `is_pascal_case` check right below. Shorten to just the WHY clause or delete.\n\n**Good calibration examples in this file:** the doc comment on `unwrap_as_expression` (lines 11-15, cites the Fluent UI source comment as evidence), and especially `try_rename_identifier_wrapped_component`'s doc comment (lines 289-309) — a textbook WHY comment: explains the real-world pattern it handles, why the naive approach doesn't work, and why cloning-not-mutating was the fix.\n\n## extractor/visit.rs — minor restates\n\n- `line 259`: `// Walk children for nested declarations` directly above `walk::walk_variable_declaration(self, node);` — pure restatement. Delete.\n- `lines 297, 299`: `// Scan for \\`Button.displayName = \"Button\"\\` assignments` / `// Scan for \\`Button.defaultProps = {...}\\` assignments` — restate the self-explanatory function names (`try_scan_display_name`, `try_scan_default_props`) called directly beneath them. Delete.\n- `lines 58, 152` (`// Don't walk children — we've handled/extracted everything`): **keep** — these explain a real design decision (deliberately *not* calling the default `walk::walk_*`), which is genuinely non-obvious for a visitor pattern.\n- The \"Pattern 4/5\" labels (lines 249, 264) are a deliberate catalog convention across the file (referenced from other files too, e.g. `component.rs`) — keep, this is good organizational signposting, not filler.\n\n**Good example:** lines 120-122 (claiming the interface's own JSDoc before descending into props) is an excellent, specific WHY comment about an ordering bug that would otherwise recur.\n\n## extractor/jsdoc.rs — restates in the low-level string parsing\n\nLines 66, 74, 95, 104, 108, 111 (`// Strip \\`/**\\` prefix and \\`*/\\` suffix`, `// Strip leading \\`* \\` or \\`*\\``, `// Parse tag: \\`@tagname rest\\``, `// Skip {type} if present`, `// First word is the prop name`, etc.) all restate trivial string-slicing operations one line below them.\n- **Category:** 1 (restates code)\n- **Suggestion:** delete all of these — the code itself (string literals like `\"* \"` and `.trim_start_matches(\"/**\")`) is self-documenting.\n\n**Excellent calibration examples in the same file:** the doc comment on `find_jsdoc_with_tags` (lines 19-31) is one of the best in the codebase — explains a real bug class (a tag bleeding across sibling props), why the fix works (shared consumed-set), and why binary search is used (avoiding a quadratic rescan). Also `find_jsdoc`'s doc comment (10-14).\n\n## extractor/interface.rs — minor restates\n\n- `line 95`: `// Check callee is a known variant function` — restates the match below. Minor, could delete.\n- `line 121`: `// Find the \"variants\" property` — restates the `find_map` immediately below. Minor, could delete.\n- `line 106`: `// cva(baseClasses, { variants }) takes config at index 1; all other callees take it at index 0` — **keep**, this is genuinely non-obvious and explains the `usize::from(callee_name == \"cva\")` line below it.\n\n**Good examples:** the doc comments on `as_const_literal` (10-14), `try_collect_const_array` (59-64), `try_scan_default_props` (218-222) are all solid WHY comments.\n\n## resolver/ — near-zero issues (the strongest files in the audit)\n\n`resolver/mod.rs`, `resolver/chain.rs`, `resolver/alias.rs`, `resolver/import.rs`, `resolver/named.rs`, `resolver/extends.rs`, `resolver/react.rs`, `resolver/substitute.rs`, `resolver/primitives.rs`, `resolver/collected.rs`, `resolver/func.rs`, `resolver/template.rs` are consistently excellent — nearly every comment explains a real regression, a hidden constraint (e.g. why `@types/react` needs special resolution, why cycle detection must include type args, why `Omit&lt;T, keyof U&gt;`'s key set needs a separate visited-set branch), or a design tradeoff. I did not find a single comment in this directory that restates code or reads as filler. Representative best-in-class examples worth using as the calibration standard:\n\n- `resolver/mod.rs` — `unresolved_location_note`, `is_ts_utility_type`, `ResolutionContext::enum_bare_index` doc comments; `ResolvedChain::merge_parent`'s discriminant-propagation comment (lines 387-393).\n- `resolver/import.rs` — `follow_reexports`'s doc comment (67-73) and the \"shared descendants\" comment (88-92); `resolve_import_specifier`'s explanation of why \"react\" needs special-cased `@types` fallback (214-221).\n- `resolver/alias.rs` — the `Omit`/`keyof U` branch-state comment (40-56); the exhaustive-match justification at the end of `resolve_base_as_chain` (205-210) explaining *why* it's a named list instead of a wildcard.\n- `resolver/extends.rs` — `real_html_attrs_chain`'s doc comment (93-102).\n\nThe one minor nit: `resolver/react.rs` uses short section-label comments above match arms (`// Ref types.`, `// FC / FunctionComponent — return as Named.`, etc.) that mostly restate the arm below. These function as scan-aids in a long match and are consistent with the file's own style — borderline, not worth changing, but if trimming is wanted these are the first candidates (category 1, low severity).\n\n## pipeline/mod.rs, pipeline/discover.rs, pipeline/watch.rs — no issues beyond the CLAUDE.md citation\n\nComments here are dense but substantive — e.g. the doc comment on `extract_with_global` (194-204) justifying why `capture_source_data` exists and what watch mode needs from it; the Phase 3.5/3.6 comments explaining why `@types/react` and `lib.d.ts` merges are handled specially; the dedup-key collision comment (366-377) documenting a real fixed bug (5 real fixture libraries colliding on `Button.tsx`). All good WHY style. Test-only comments documenting regressions (e.g. `test_generic_svg_attributes_extends_resolves_to_its_element_in_full_mode`) are also excellent and match the file's overall standard.\n\n## import_map.rs — no issues\n\nClean top to bottom. `ImportResolutionMap`'s doc comment on why the map is nested rather than flat-keyed (42-43) and the note about storing specifiers vs. resolved paths (44-45) are good, minimal WHY comments. Nothing to flag.\n\n## known.rs, react_types.rs, cache.rs — sampled, consistent with the rest\n\n- `cache.rs`: `CACHE_SCHEMA_VERSION`'s doc comment (16-23) is an excellent example — explains a genuinely dangerous MessagePack positional-encoding footgun and why a schema bump matters.\n- `known.rs`: `push_known_opaque_diagnostic`'s doc comment (24-30) and `resolve_known`'s doc comment (63-74) are good; one minor restate at `known.rs` line 99 (`// We can use the base union and note the extension is opaque` directly above code doing exactly that) — could be deleted.\n- `react_types.rs`: `parse_react_version`'s doc comment (229-235) is a good WHY comment about failing loudly on unknown react-version strings, though it uses the vaguer, non-numbered phrase \"this crate's non-negotiables\" rather than the stale \"#6\" — not itself broken, but worth fixing in the same pass as the CLAUDE.md issue above for consistency.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThis is a low-noise audit. If I had to prioritize two actions:\n1. Fix the \"non-negotiable #6\" dangling reference (either restore the numbered list in `crates/core/CLAUDE.md` or de-reference the citations).\n2. Sweep `extractor/jsdoc.rs`, `extractor/component.rs`, and `extractor/visit.rs` for the ~10 restates-the-line-below comments listed above — all safe, low-risk deletions.\n\nEverything else — and it's the large majority of the ~1,900 comment lines across these files — is already at or above the bar the skill describes.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>164920</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>32</tool_uses><duration_ms>279203</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","commandMode":"task-notification","timestamp":"2026-07-25T20:01:59.101Z"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"63a4fa47-500b-4651-9f05-2eabe072e176","timestamp":"2026-07-25T20:01:59.101Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.219","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
