s class expressions vs class declarations, components exported via `export { X as Y }` or `export * from`, components with no props param, components using `React.FC<Props>` vs plain function typing, generic components (`function Comp<T>(props: Props<T>)`), components declared in .ts (not .tsx) files, multiple components per file, components with the same name in different files.\n2. **Prop-type declaration parsing edge cases**: interfaces vs type aliases vs inline object literals, intersections/unions of many members, recursive/self-referential types, generic type parameters with defaults or constraints, mapped types, conditional types, template literal types, `as const`, satisfies expressions, re-exported types from other files, namespaced types (`Foo.Bar`), ambient/declare types.\n3. **JSDoc parsing edge cases**: malformed or partial JSDoc, `@default` tag variants, multi-line descriptions, JSDoc on re-exported/aliased types, missing JSDoc, JSDoc with unusual tag ordering, block comments that look like JSDoc but aren't (`/** */` on unrelated code).\n4. **Default value extraction edge cases**: destructured defaults (`{ foo = 'bar' }`), defaultProps static property (legacy class components), complex default expressions (function calls, spread, ternaries), defaults referencing outer-scope variables/constants.\n5. **Panic/crash risks**: any indexing, slicing, `.unwrap()`, `.expect()`, arithmetic that isn't behind a bounds/overflow check, when walking malformed or adversarial ASTs (e.g. deeply nested types causing stack overflow via recursion, extremely large files).\n6. **Silent failure risks**: places where a parse/extraction failure would just skip data with no `Diagnostic` emitted, violating the \"always emit Diagnostic when degrading\" rule.\n\nFor each finding, note: exact file:line, whether existing tests in crates/core/tests/ or inline #[cfg(test)] modules already cover it (search first), and severity (crash risk / silent data loss / known-and-documented limitation / cosmetic).\n\nReport as a structured list grouped by category (1-6 above). Be concrete — cite code, don't speculate abstractly. Aim for thoroughness over brevity; this feeds a comprehensive gap-tracking document. Under 2500 words.","teammate_id":"audit-extractor@session-0ae3953f","agent_id":"audit-extractor@session-0ae3953f","agent_type":"Explore","model":"claude-sonnet-5","name":"audit-extractor","color":"blue","tmux_session_name":"in-process","tmux_window_name":"in-process","tmux_pane_id":"in-process","team_name":"session-0ae3953f","is_splitpane":false,"plan_mode_required":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d71027f8-04d1-4a62-9ab0-378e5c0af3d6","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"84e268df-81e6-4ea5-8bff-17cbaa6efb23","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Agent","toolUseID":"toolu_01PcLsgfGVDHSCmVn9nNh1Ce","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Agent\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.1.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":58},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9c0bb77a-8404-4ae0-a6a6-53eadfd81f57","timestamp":"2026-08-04T16:55:42.842Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"9c0bb77a-8404-4ae0-a6a6-53eadfd81f57","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi4NkszKi8hQyNymux6h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XXKmiZc64LtDFFPDRWzwnj","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit resolver subsystem edge cases","subagent_type":"Explore","name":"audit-resolver","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"We're doing a comprehensive edge-case/failure-mode audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript). This is research/documentation only — do NOT modify any code.\n\nYour scope: the resolver subsystem in crates/core/src/resolver/ (mod.rs, chain.rs, alias.rs, collected.rs, extends.rs, func.rs, html.rs, import.rs, named.rs, primitives.rs, react.rs, substitute.rs, template.rs). This layer takes `SourceData` + `GlobalSourceData` (all files merged) and resolves a component's props type into a final `ComponentEntry` — this is where generic substitution, intersection/union merging, cross-file type-alias following, and \"give up gracefully\" logic (composes, opaque types) lives. `ResolveState` in mod.rs accumulates diagnostics and visited-type tracking per resolution call and is NOT shared across threads (resolve_component runs in parallel via rayon, per crates/core/CLAUDE.md).\n\nProject non-negotiables (CLAUDE.md): no `unwrap()` outside tests, `FxHashMap`/`BTreeMap` used correctly, always emit a `Diagnostic` when degrading rather than failing silently. Note: `ResolvedChain::empty_with_compose()` is the established pattern for \"give up but record what we know\" — check whether all give-up paths use it consistently or whether some still return `ResolvedChain::default()` silently (this was a real bug found and partially fixed recently — check if other similar dead ends remain).\n\nFor each source file in scope, identify:\n1. **Cycle / infinite recursion risks**: self-referential types (`type Tree = { children: Tree[] }`), mutually recursive types (A references B references A), circular imports across files, generic types instantiated with themselves. Does `ResolveState`'s visited-type tracking actually prevent infinite loops in all these cases, or only some? Look for recursion depth limits or lack thereof — could a pathological/adversarial type definition cause a stack overflow or hang?\n2. **Generic substitution edge cases**: generics with multiple type parameters, generics with constraints (`T extends Foo`), generics with defaults, partial application, generic type aliases used without type arguments, higher-kinded-ish patterns (generic type referencing another generic), `Props<'button', Options>`-style utility patterns (relevant: earlier work found Ariakit's pattern isn't matched at all — check current state).\n3. **Intersection/union merging edge cases**: conflicting prop types across union members, optional vs required conflicts, deeply nested intersections, unions of 20+ literal members, discriminated unions where the discriminant itself is computed/generic.\n4. **Cross-file/cross-package resolution edge cases**: types imported from node_modules .d.ts files, types re-exported through multiple hops (A imports from B imports from C), circular file imports, missing/unresolvable imports (typo'd import, deleted file), ambient global types with no import at all.\n5. **\"Give up gracefully\" consistency**: enumerate every place the resolver returns an empty/default chain or otherwise gives up (grep for `ResolvedChain::default()`, early returns, etc.) and check whether each one records a Diagnostic and/or populates `composes` — flag any that silently vanish.\n6. **Panic/crash risks**: unwrap/expect/indexing/arithmetic in hot paths, especially under adversarial or malformed type graphs.\n\nFor each finding: exact file:line, whether existing tests already cover it (check crates/core/tests/ and inline test modules first), severity (crash/hang risk, silent data loss, known documented limitation per docs/rdt-coverage.md or docs/type-checker-integration.md, or cosmetic).\n\nReport as a structured list grouped by category (1-6). Be concrete, cite code. Under 2800 words."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1416,"cache_read_input_tokens":106187,"output_tokens":8585,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1416,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8585,"cache_read_input_tokens":106187,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1416,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1416},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdi4NjDmn5BruE5v9CKUM","type":"assistant","uuid":"801384df-8621-479b-86e1-5a2121e6cff0","timestamp":"2026-08-04T16:55:58.738Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"801384df-8621-479b-86e1-5a2121e6cff0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"31ac2396-b3e2-4a0c-aa3a-65aa0375a150","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XXKmiZc64LtDFFPDRWzwnj","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Spawned successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the ID below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagent_id: audit-resolver@session-0ae3953f\nname: audit-resolver\nThe agent is now running and will receive instructions via mailbox."}]}]},"uuid":"ab8f656f-0a77-4ea1-ba18-4fac5dc7f2af","timestamp":"2026-08-04T16:56:00.835Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"teammate_spawned","prompt":"We're doing a comprehensive edge-case/failure-mode audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript). This is research/documentation only — do NOT modify any code.\n\nYour scope: the resolver subsystem in crates/core/src/resolver/ (mod.rs, chain.rs, alias.rs, collected.rs, extends.rs, func.rs, html.rs, import.rs, named.rs, primitives.rs, react.rs, substitute.rs, template.rs). This layer takes `SourceData` + `GlobalSourceData` (all files merged) and resolves a component's props type into a final `ComponentEntry` — this is where generic substitution, intersection/union merging, cross-file type-alias following, and \"give up gracefully\" logic (composes, opaque types) lives. `ResolveState` in mod.rs accumulates diagnostics and visited-type tracking per resolution call and is NOT shared across threads (resolve_component runs in parallel via rayon, per crates/core/CLAUDE.md).\n\nProject non-negotiables (CLAUDE.md): no `unwrap()` outside tests, `FxHashMap`/`BTreeMap` used correctly, always emit a `Diagnostic` when degrading rather than failing silently. Note: `ResolvedChain::empty_with_compose()` is the established pattern for \"give up but record what we know\" — check whether all give-up paths use it consistently or whether some still return `ResolvedChain::default()` silently (this was a real bug found and partially fixed recently — check if other similar dead ends remain).\n\nFor each source file in scope, identify:\n1. **Cycle / infinite recursion risks**: self-referential types (`type Tree = { children: Tree[] }`), mutually recursive types (A references B references A), circular imports across files, generic types instantiated with themselves. Does `ResolveState`'s visited-type tracking actually prevent infinite loops in all these cases, or only some? Look for recursion depth limits or lack thereof — could a pathological/adversarial type definition cause a stack overflow or hang?\n2. **Generic substitution edge cases**: generics with multiple type parameters, generics with constraints (`T extends Foo`), generics with defaults, partial application, generic type aliases used without type arguments, higher-kinded-ish patterns (generic type referencing another generic), `Props<'button', Options>`-style utility patterns (relevant: earlier work found Ariakit's pattern isn't matched at all — check current state).\n3. **Intersection/union merging edge cases**: conflicting prop types across union members, optional vs required conflicts, deeply nested intersections, unions of 20+ literal members, discriminated unions where the discriminant itself is computed/generic.\n4. **Cross-file/cross-package resolution edge cases**: types imported from node_modules .d.ts files, types re-exported through multiple hops (A imports from B imports from C), circular file imports, missing/unresolvable imports (typo'd import, deleted file), ambient global types with no import at all.\n5. **\"Give up gracefully\" consistency**: enumerate every place the resolver returns an empty/default chain or otherwise gives up (grep for `ResolvedChain::default()`, early returns, etc.) and check whether each one records a Diagnostic and/or populates `composes` — flag any that silently vanish.\n6. **Panic/crash risks**: unwrap/expect/indexing/arithmetic in hot paths, especially under adversarial or malformed type graphs.\n\nFor each finding: exact file:line, whether existing tests already cover it (check crates/core/tests/ and inline test modules first), severity (crash/hang risk, silent data loss, known documented limitation per docs/rdt-coverage.md or docs/type-checker-integration.md, or cosmetic).\n\nReport as a structured list grouped by category (1-6). Be concrete, cite code. Under 2800 words.","teammate_id":"audit-resolver@session-0ae3953f","agent_id":"audit-resolver@session-0ae3953f","agent_type":"Explore","model":"claude-sonnet-5","name":"audit-resolver","color":"green","tmux_session_name":"in-process","tmux_window_name":"in-process","tmux_pane_id":"in-process","team_name":"session-0ae3953f","is_splitpane":false,"plan_mode_required":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"801384df-8621-479b-86e1-5a2121e6cff0","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"ab8f656f-0a77-4ea1-ba18-4fac5dc7f2af","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Agent","toolUseID":"toolu_01XXKmiZc64LtDFFPDRWzwnj","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Agent\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.1.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":47},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8be9a2c9-713a-4319-bc74-998cd4b89a7d","timestamp":"2026-08-04T16:56:00.834Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"8be9a2c9-713a-4319-bc74-998cd4b89a7d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi4NkszKi8hQyNymux6h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Axp28TJ8xkAWkdqy3pv9VZ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit pipeline, cache, import_map edge cases","subagent_type":"Explore","name":"audit-pipeline","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"We're doing a comprehensive edge-case/failure-mode audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript). This is research/documentation only — do NOT modify any code.\n\nYour scope: crates/core/src/pipeline/mod.rs, crates/core/src/pipeline/discover.rs, crates/core/src/pipeline/watch.rs, crates/core/src/cache.rs, crates/core/src/import_map.rs. This is file discovery → parallel parse (rayon) → merge into GlobalSourceData → parallel resolve → dedup/collision handling → ExtractionOutput, plus the DTS parse cache (mtime+size invalidation, msgpack, atomic temp-file swap, 5000-entry eviction cap per docs/STATUS.md) and watch mode (file-system event handling).\n\nProject non-negotiables (CLAUDE.md): no `unwrap()` outside tests, always emit Diagnostic on degrade, FxHashMap internal / BTreeMap for JSON output.\n\nIdentify edge cases and failure modes in:\n1. **File discovery**: symlink loops, permission-denied directories/files, non-UTF8 filenames or paths, extremely deep directory nesting, empty src_dirs, src_dirs that don't exist, glob/ignore pattern edge cases (node_modules exclusion, .gitignore respect or not), case-insensitive filesystem collisions (Foo.tsx vs foo.tsx on macOS), files that change between discovery and read (TOCTOU).\n2. **Parallel parse/merge (rayon)**: what happens if one file panics mid-parse — does it poison the whole batch or degrade gracefully per-file? Race conditions in merging into GlobalSourceData. Determinism: does parallel execution produce the same output every run (important for caching/snapshot tests), or could iteration order cause nondeterminism in diagnostics ordering, component dedup winner selection, etc.?\n3. **Component name collisions / dedup**: same component name in different files (recently fixed per git history — verify current dedup key logic in mod.rs's extract() function is actually collision-proof; check the exact key construction), same file processed twice, case-sensitivity issues.\n4. **Cache subsystem** (cache.rs): what happens on a corrupted cache file (partial write from a crash, wrong msgpack version, disk full during atomic swap), concurrent processes sharing the same cache dir, cache dir on read-only filesystem, mtime granularity issues (some filesystems have 1s mtime resolution — could a fast edit+rebuild within the same second get a stale cache hit?), eviction correctness at exactly 5000 entries, negative/zero-size files.\n5. **Cross-package .d.ts resolution / import_map.rs**: package.json parsing edge cases (missing, malformed, no \"types\"/\"typings\" field, exports map complexity), monorepo workspace symlinks (pnpm/yarn), circular package dependencies, missing node_modules entirely.\n6. **Watch mode** (watch.rs): rapid successive file changes (debounce correctness), file deleted while being processed, editor atomic-save patterns (write to temp file + rename — does the watcher see the right final file?), watch mode + cache interaction correctness.\n7. **Panic/crash risks**: unwrap/expect/indexing/arithmetic, especially any that could be triggered by filesystem state outside the tool's control (untrusted/adversarial input isn't the threat model here, but real-world messy filesystems are).\n\nFor each finding: exact file:line, whether existing tests already cover it (check crates/core/tests/ first), severity. Report grouped by category 1-7, concrete with code citations. Under 2800 words."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1416,"cache_read_input_tokens":106187,"output_tokens":8585,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1416,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8585,"cache_read_input_tokens":106187,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1416,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1416},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdi4NjDmn5BruE5v9CKUM","type":"assistant","uuid":"84036792-a417-411c-a644-ed4a635f0166","timestamp":"2026-08-04T16:56:16.094Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
