{"parentUuid":"cb4ba13c-e3ad-49db-ac61-27bd24bdf866","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi67zHT3B9USxWmmiZd5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Phase 0 finished — the result is truncated in the notification, so I'll read the full output file before reporting back."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4212,"cache_read_input_tokens":173556,"output_tokens":196,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4212,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":196,"cache_read_input_tokens":173556,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4212,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4212},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdi67xdV48JTXwu9oLRnP","type":"assistant","uuid":"310cc2b6-098e-4bf2-935a-c661ce17852b","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:18:03.960Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"310cc2b6-098e-4bf2-935a-c661ce17852b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi67zHT3B9USxWmmiZd5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QJk73CUKQ1bA4cYDDQMguY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-oxc-react-codegen/0ae3953f-9438-46c4-a76a-88fce9eed529/tasks/wruyd6jkk.output"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4212,"cache_read_input_tokens":173556,"output_tokens":196,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4212,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":196,"cache_read_input_tokens":173556,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4212,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4212},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdi67xdV48JTXwu9oLRnP","type":"assistant","uuid":"f3e970de-7ac6-42f2-b887-36ff6f83cf45","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:18:05.000Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"f3e970de-7ac6-42f2-b887-36ff6f83cf45","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"33c33088-8732-4685-a296-f012e6bf5131","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QJk73CUKQ1bA4cYDDQMguY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"summary\": \"Phase 0: find architectural root causes behind the edge-case audit findings and propose fix-pattern clusters + enforcement mechanisms\",\n3\t  \"agentCount\": 5,\n4\t  \"logs\": [],\n5\t  \"result\": {\n6\t    \"clusterResults\": [\n7\t      {\n8\t        \"clusterName\": \"Give-up-silently: graceful-degradation paths that skip the Diagnostic pairing\",\n9\t        \"rootCause\": \"Verified against actual code (resolver/chain.rs, resolver/func.rs, resolver/collected.rs, extractor/alias.rs, extractor/mod.rs, extractor/visit.rs, pipeline/discover.rs, pipeline/mod.rs). This is not one root cause but three, stratified by whether a shared mechanism already exists:\\n\\n(1) RESOLVER (chain.rs, func.rs): a convention already exists — `ResolvedChain::empty_with_compose(name)` bundles the \\\"give up, record what we couldn't resolve\\\" case, and `collected.rs::push_opaque_diagnostic` is a shared helper that pairs a `PropType::Opaque` with an Info diagnostic. But neither is the *only* way to construct these values: `ResolvedChain` derives `Default` and is a plain struct with no private fields, so `ResolvedChain::default()` (bare, no composes/diagnostic) is just as callable as the named constructor — chain.rs:40 (cycle path) reaches for the wrong one and nothing stops it. Likewise `PropType::Opaque { raw, reason }` is a plain struct-literal-constructible variant; I found ~10 construction sites across 5 files (collected.rs, func.rs, primitives.rs, named.rs, template.rs) and only 4 of them (collected.rs:79,103,107,150) route through `push_opaque_diagnostic` — func.rs:61 (MultiParamFunction), primitives.rs:181 (IndexedAccess), template.rs:39 (TemplateLiteral) build `PropType::Opaque` directly with no diagnostic call, invisible to any reviewer scanning the \\\"happy path.\\\" The helper functions are advisory, not enforced — classic drift from a convention that isn't structurally mandatory. This is the \\\"no lint/test enforces its use\\\" case, not \\\"no mechanism exists.\\\"\\n\\n(2) EXTRACTOR (alias.rs classify_type_alias, mod.rs extract_type_name_from_type, visit.rs component detectors): fundamentally more foundational — these are `Option<T>`-returning functions chained with `?` against AST shapes (`tp.params.first()?`, `_ => None`) or nested `if let Some(...) = a.or_else(b).or_else(c)` detector chains with no `else` branch. There is no signal channel distinguishing \\\"this AST shape doesn't apply here\\\" from \\\"this looked like a match but was malformed/unsupported\\\" — both collapse to the same `None`. `SourceData.diagnostics` does exist and extractor code CAN push into it (confirmed at extractor/mod.rs:108,134, both unrelated to this cluster — nesting-depth guard and a syntax error), but classify_type_alias and friends have never been wired to it despite having `&mut self` in scope. This is a design gap, not a helper-drift gap: no helper could be \\\"used inconsistently\\\" here because none was ever built for this call shape.\\n\\n(3) PIPELINE (discover.rs, mod.rs): three unrelated bugs masquerading as one pattern. discover.rs:17's `walker.flatten()` drops `ignore::Walk`'s `Err` items — structural, because `discover_files` returns a bare `Vec<Utf8PathBuf>` with no diagnostic-return channel at all (same class of gap as (2)). mod.rs:220's empty-`src_dirs` miss is a plain boolean short-circuit bug (`!src_dirs.is_empty() && ...`) — a logic error, not a missing abstraction. mod.rs:386's `components.insert(key, entry)` collision discards `insert`'s `Option<V>` return value that already tells you a collision happened — the data needed to diagnose it is right there and simply unused.\",\n10\t        \"mechanismProposal\": \"Three different fixes for three different root causes — do not apply one mechanism to all findings:\\n\\n**(1) Resolver — make the bare/wrong constructor uncallable, not just discouraged.**\\n- In `resolver/mod.rs`, stop deriving `Default` on `ResolvedChain` as a public-within-module escape hatch: keep a private `fn empty() -> Self` used *only* inside `empty_with_compose`, and add one more named constructor, `ResolvedChain::give_up(type_name: String, diag: Option<Diagnostic>, state: &mut ResolveState) -> Self`, which pushes the diagnostic AND the composes entry in one call, replacing every current two-statement `state.diagnostics.push(...); ResolvedChain::empty_with_compose(...)` pair as well as the bare-`default()` cycle path at chain.rs:40 (which becomes `ResolvedChain::give_up(type_name.to_owned(), None, state)` — reviewers can now see explicitly that no diagnostic is attached there, or add one). Because `Default`/struct-literal construction of `ResolvedChain` outside `resolver/mod.rs`'s own impl block no longer compiles, every future give-up site in chain.rs/alias.rs/extends.rs is forced through the named constructors.\\n- Do the equivalent for `PropType::Opaque`: change it from `Opaque { raw: String, reason: OpaqueReason }` to a single-field tuple variant `Opaque(OpaqueDetail)` where `OpaqueDetail`'s fields are private to a new `resolver/opaque.rs`, and the only public entry point is `OpaqueDetail::give_up(state: &mut ResolveState, what: &str, ct_or_raw, file, reason: OpaqueReason) -> PropType` — folding `push_opaque_diagnostic` into the constructor itself instead of leaving it as a sibling function callers can skip. Update the ~10 existing call sites (collected.rs ×6, func.rs, primitives.rs, named.rs, template.rs) to go through it — this mechanically fixes func.rs:54-61's missing diagnostic and prevents any new Opaque-degradation site from omitting one, since there is no other way to build the variant. This does touch `PropType`'s manual `to_json_value`/`from_json_value` (ADR 0002) — update both match arms, no new ADR needed for that part since the wire shape is unchanged, only the Rust-side constructor surface.\\n- Add one clippy.toml `disallowed-methods`/`disallowed-types` entry (or a `rg 'ResolvedChain\\\\s*\\\\{' -g '!resolver/mod.rs'` / `rg 'PropType::Opaque\\\\s*\\\\{' -g '!resolver/opaque.rs'` check wired into the existing CI/check flow referenced by the `/check` skill) as a backstop in case someone reintroduces a bare literal via `..Default::default()` spread syntax, which the type system alone won't catch.\\n\\n**(2) Extractor — add the missing signal channel, then make it a review-checklist item (no compiler enforcement is practical here given `?`-chained `Option` returns over AST shape matching).**\\n- Add one helper next to the two existing `self.data.diagnostics.push(...)` call sites in `extractor/mod.rs`: `fn record_skip(&mut self, code: DiagnosticCode, message: impl Into<String>, span: Span)` (Info/Warning severity, reuses the existing `Diagnostic` shape). Add a new `DiagnosticCode` variant, e.g. `SkippedCandidate`, so these are filterable/countable separately from parse errors.\\n- Require (via the `crates/core/CLAUDE.md` checklist, not the compiler): every `match ref_name.as_str() { \\\"Omit\\\" => { ...?...  } ... _ => passthrough_or_none }` arm in `classify_type_alias` that bails on a *matched-but-malformed* shape (wrong arg count, unrecognized inner type) calls `self.record_skip(...)` before the `?`/`return None`; every component-detector `.or_else()` chain in `visit.rs` (both the PascalCase binding chain at 236-247 and `visit_function` at 262-293) ends with an explicit `else { self.record_skip(...) }` rather than silent fallthrough. This is a checklist addition, not a lint, because distinguishing \\\"not this shape, silently fine\\\" from \\\"this shape but unsupported, worth a diagnostic\\\" requires domain judgment a lint can't make.\\n- New detector coverage (anonymous default export, class components, `Object.assign` compound components, class-expression components, `satisfies`) is a separate, larger feature-gap task — record_skip only makes the *existing* miss visible; it doesn't add the missing detectors.\\n\\n**(3) Pipeline — three independent one-line-scale fixes, no shared abstraction:**\\n- `discover_files` gains a `&mut Vec<Diagnostic>` (or returns `(Vec<Utf8PathBuf>, Vec<Diagnostic>)`) and the `walker.flatten()` becomes an explicit match that pushes an IoError diagnostic on `Err`, same pattern already used at mod.rs:269-272 for read failures — reuse `Diagnostic::io_read_error` or a sibling constructor for consistency. Non-UTF8 filenames get the same treatment at the `Utf8PathBuf::from_path_buf` fallback.\\n- mod.rs:220: fix the boolean condition (drop the `!options.src_dirs.is_empty() &&` guard, or explicitly handle the empty case with its own diagnostic) and add a regression test with `src_dirs: []` asserting at least one diagnostic is emitted — this is a logic-bug fix, not a new mechanism.\\n- mod.rs:386: check `components.insert(key, entry)`'s returned `Option<ComponentEntry>` and push a diagnostic when `Some(_)` comes back (collision) — the data is already there, just discarded; one `if let Some(prev) = ...` fixes both the same-name-same-file and overlapping-src_dirs findings at once.\\n\\n**ADR:** worth recording as an ADR (see adrNeeded) specifically for decision (1) — it changes `PropType`'s and `ResolvedChain`'s construction surface project-wide and establishes \\\"graceful-degradation values are built only through a diagnostic-pairing smart constructor, never a bare struct literal\\\" as a durable rule future contributors need to know about before adding a new Opaque-producing or give-up code path. Decisions (2) and (3) are local fixes/checklist items, not architectural.\",\n11\t        \"findingIds\": [\n12\t          \"extractor-anonymous-default-export\",\n13\t          \"extractor-class-components\",\n14\t          \"extractor-object-assign-compound\",\n15\t          \"extractor-class-expression-components\",\n16\t          \"extractor-satisfies-expressions\",\n17\t          \"extractor-classify-type-alias-omit-pick-etc\",\n18\t          \"extractor-extract-props-arg-terminal-none\",\n19\t          \"extractor-failed-detector-silence\",\n20\t          \"extractor-computed-property-keys\",\n21\t          \"resolver-chain-cycle-detected-default\",\n22\t          \"resolver-func-multiparam-no-diagnostic\",\n23\t          \"resolver-literalunion-as-props-base\",\n24\t          \"pipeline-permission-denied-files\",\n25\t          \"pipeline-non-utf8-filenames\",\n26\t          \"pipeline-empty-src-dirs\",\n27\t          \"pipeline-same-name-same-file-collision\",\n28\t          \"pipeline-overlapping-src-dirs\"\n29\t        ],\n30\t        \"misassignedNotes\": \"Two findings originally bundled into this cluster don't fit the \\\"give-up path with no Diagnostic\\\" shape and belong elsewhere:\\n- `resolver/substitute.rs:33-43` (generic aliases with more type params than supplied args — `build_substitution`'s `.zip()` silently stops) is a silent *truncation*, not a give-up-and-return-nothing path — no ResolvedChain/PropType is being short-circuited, a substitution map is just incomplete. It's closer to the Priority 0 \\\"silent correctness bug\\\" tier (wrong substitution, not missing output) than this cluster; excluded from findingIds above.\\n- `cli/commands/watch.rs:107-112` (`--out` write failure via `let _ = std::fs::write(...)`) is CLI-layer, not extraction-pipeline degradation, and CLAUDE.md's non-negotiable #6 is scoped to the extraction pipeline (crates/core) — crates/cli's own CLAUDE.md separately mandates all user-facing output go through `output.rs`/tracing/miette, which this violates on its own terms. It's a real bug but its fix is \\\"route through miette error reporting like every other CLI failure,\\\" a CLI-layer mechanism unrelated to the resolver/extractor/pipeline diagnostic-pairing story above. Excluded from findingIds.\\n- Types/serialization findings (NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity, zero-member LiteralUnion) are genuinely silent but are serialization-boundary edge cases with no realistic reachable input (per the doc's own \\\"likely unreachable via normal TS literal syntax\\\" caveat) rather than a repeated give-up-path pattern — they don't share the root cause (bare struct literal vs. named constructor) diagnosed above, so I left them out of findingIds; they're better tracked as standalone P2 tickets than folded into this mechanism.\\n\\nAll findings I did verify against source match the doc's line citations closely (chain.rs:40 confirmed bare `ResolvedChain::default()`; func.rs:61 confirmed no diagnostic push; alias.rs `classify_type_alias` confirmed `?`-chained `None` collapse; discover.rs:17 confirmed `.flatten()`; mod.rs:220 confirmed the `&&`-guarded condition; mod.rs:386 confirmed `components.insert` return value discarded).\",\n31\t        \"adrNeeded\": true\n32\t      },\n33\t      {\n34\t        \"clusterName\": \"No panic-boundary convention: unwind containment is accidental, not designed\",\n35\t        \"rootCause\": \"grep confirms zero uses of catch_unwind anywhere in the repo. The three async NAPI entry points (extract_all, extract_file_incremental, initialize_session) are only 'safe' from a panic today because they happen to run inside tokio::task::spawn_blocking, whose JoinHandle converts a panic into an Err the caller already unwraps via .map_err(...)? — that's incidental protection from tokio's plumbing, not a deliberate design decision anyone wrote down. Because it was never written down anywhere (not in crates/core/CLAUDE.md, not in crates/cli/CLAUDE.md, no shared helper to import), every subsequent concurrent/cross-boundary addition had to independently reinvent whether panics are contained, and got inconsistent answers: (1) pipeline/mod.rs:260,357 wrap rayon .map() closures with no catch_unwind at all, relying on the unenforced invariant 'the extractor/resolver never panic' (true today per the audit's own non-findings, but not a boundary — it's a hope); (2) plugin.rs's DocgenPlugin trait is explicitly documented as third-party-extensible ('Plugins can hook into extraction events') yet PluginRegistry::run_on_file_extracted/run_on_component_resolved just call `plugin.on_x(...)` directly in a for loop with zero isolation between plugins or from the pipeline that invokes them from pipeline/mod.rs:293,385; (3) crates/binding/src/lib.rs's create_session/close_session are synchronous fns that skip spawn_blocking entirely (unlike the other three), so they don't even get the incidental protection those get; (4) pipeline/watch.rs:93's `initialized.lock().expect(...)` uses std::sync::Mutex, whose poison-on-panic default becomes a permanent trap specifically because the entire cold extraction (rayon batches + plugin hooks, i.e. exactly the unprotected code from (1) and (2)) runs while that lock is held inside initialize(). These four are one causal chain, not four coincidences: the same missing boundary shows up once as 'kills the whole batch,' once as 'kills the whole pipeline from one plugin,' once as 'silently unsafe compared to its siblings,' and once as 'permanently wedges a session' because nothing upstream ever catches the panic that would otherwise poison it.\",\n36\t        \"mechanismProposal\": \"Add one shared helper — `crates/core/src/panic_guard.rs`, `pub(crate) fn contain_panic<T>(file: Option<&str>, label: &str, f: impl FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe) -> Result<T, Diagnostic>` — wrapping std::panic::catch_unwind and converting any payload into a Diagnostic with a new `DiagnosticCode::InternalPanic` variant (types/diagnostic.rs, following the same #[non_exhaustive] pattern already used for the unused `Unknown` variant). This becomes the single sanctioned catch point for the whole crate. Force every non-provably-panic-free call site through it: (a) pipeline/mod.rs:260 and :357 — wrap the body of each rayon `.map()` closure, turning a panicking file/component into one Diagnostic instead of aborting the batch; (b) plugin.rs's `run_on_file_extracted`/`run_on_component_resolved` — wrap each individual `plugin.on_x(...)` call inside the existing `for plugin in &self.plugins` loop (not the whole loop) so one bad plugin degrades only itself, tagging the Diagnostic with `plugin.name()`; (c) crates/binding/src/lib.rs's `create_session`/`close_session` — wrap their bodies with `contain_panic`, converting to `napi::Error`, bringing them to explicit parity with the other three NAPI functions, which should themselves start calling `contain_panic` inside their `spawn_blocking` closures so their safety stops being an accident of tokio's JoinError and becomes the same documented mechanism as everywhere else. Once (a)-(b) exist, `watch.rs`'s `initialized: Mutex<bool>` structurally can't poison anymore (nothing panics while it's held), but as cheap defense-in-depth also replace `.expect(\\\"init lock poisoned\\\")` with `.unwrap_or_else(|poisoned| poisoned.into_inner())` (std) so a future gap doesn't wedge the session even if (a)-(c) are ever bypassed. Document the rule in crates/core/CLAUDE.md as a new short 'Panic containment' section next to the existing 'Resolver' section: 'Anything reachable from a rayon `.map()` or a `DocgenPlugin` impl must go through `panic_guard::contain_panic` — see pipeline/mod.rs and plugin.rs for the call sites.' That's the natural place because every other cross-cutting rule in this codebase (FxHashMap/BTreeMap discipline, Diagnostic-on-degrade) already lives there and subsystem authors demonstrably read it.\",\n37\t        \"findingIds\": [\n38\t          \"P1-3\",\n39\t          \"P1-4\",\n40\t          \"P1-5\",\n41\t          \"P1-9\"\n42\t        ],\n43\t        \"adrNeeded\": true,\n44\t        \"adrRationale\": \"This is a cross-cutting safety-boundary decision spanning crates/core and crates/binding, sets a pattern every future concurrent/plugin/FFI entry point will copy, and is expensive to reverse quietly (picking the wrong granularity — per-batch vs per-file/per-plugin — or the wrong lock type now means re-touching every call site later). None of the existing ADRs (0001 OXC choice, 0002 manual serde, 0003 msgpack cache encoding, 0004 defer type-checker) cover panic/unwind policy. Propose 0005-panic-containment-boundary.md: what's the one sanctioned catch point (`panic_guard::contain_panic`), why per-file/per-plugin granularity was chosen over per-batch, and that it also fixes the NAPI create_session/close_session inconsistency.\",\n45\t        \"misassignedNotes\": \"The other five findings originally bundled into this cluster do NOT share the panic-boundary root cause above and should be split into three separate groups: (1) P1-1 (resolver/template.rs:94-104 Cartesian product) and P1-2 (cli/commands/lsp.rs:36 Content-Length alloc) are 'unbounded allocation from input-derived size' — nothing here panics, they just compute a size and allocate it uncapped. Root cause: extractor/mod.rs already solved exactly this shape of problem once (MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH + max_bracket_nesting_depth, with a detailed doc comment explaining why), but nobody generalized or even cross-referenced that precedent when template.rs's Cartesian loop or lsp.rs's header parsing were written later — it's a discoverability gap, not a missing type. Fix: add `const MAX_TEMPLATE_LITERAL_EXPANSIONS: usize = 4096` (or similar) checked inside `try_expand_template_literal`'s accumulation loop, returning None early to fall through to the Opaque+diagnostic path `resolve_template_literal` already has; add `const MAX_LSP_MESSAGE_BYTES` gating the `vec![0u8; len]` allocation in lsp.rs, rejecting/resyncing on an oversized header. Since the two live in different crates with different data shapes, a shared function isn't practical — the mechanism is a documented precedent (point future authors at extractor/mod.rs's doc comment) plus a code-review checklist line: 'does this size come from parsed/protocol input, and is it capped before use?' (2) P1-6 (lsp.rs:56,78 advertised-but-unimplemented hover) and P1-7 (lsp.rs:32-34 malformed-header desync) — and arguably P1-2 too — trace to a different root cause: lsp.rs is a brand-new, zero-test, hand-rolled LSP-framing scaffold with no capabilities/handlers consistency check. Fix: don't advertise `hoverProvider: true` until a handler exists (or implement a trivial one); make the `content_length: None` branch actually resync (consume to the next blank line or close the connection) instead of `continue`ing past an unconsumed body; longer-term, adopt `lsp-server`/`lsp-types` crates so framing, capability-advertisement, and bounds are handled by a maintained library instead of reinvented here. (3) P1-8 (extractor/mod.rs:38-40,105-120 guard vs :311-471 recursion) is a standalone proxy-metric mismatch: `max_bracket_nesting_depth` bounds raw-text bracket depth as a stand-in for AST recursion depth, but `ts_type_to_collected` recurses with zero depth-tracking of its own — unlike the resolver's `resolve_*` functions (e.g. template.rs), which already thread an explicit `depth: u8` parameter and bail past a limit. Fix: bring the extractor's TSType-conversion functions into that same already-established resolver convention (thread a depth counter through `ts_type_to_collected` and friends, bail with a Diagnostic past e.g. depth 500) instead of relying solely on the pre-parse text heuristic.\"\n46\t      },\n47\t      {\n48\t        \"clusterName\": \"Duplicated invariants with no single callable source of truth (precedence rules, truncation formatting, and struct-to-schema mirroring all hand-copied instead of shared)\",\n49\t        \"rootCause\": \"In all three findings, an invariant that must hold identically across two or more locations is expressed as inline, ad hoc code plus an explanatory comment at each site, rather than as one named function/derive/helper that every site is forced to route through. named.rs comments 'Check source-defined types BEFORE known library patterns... intentional to avoid this exact collision' but that ordering is re-typed by hand as chain.rs's own sequence of numbered steps (step 2 known-pattern check runs before step 4/5 type-alias/interface lookup — the exact inverse of named.rs's step 2-4-before-5 order). Nothing (type system, lint, or test) ties the two orderings together, so chain.rs was free to drift the moment it was written independently of named.rs, and nothing since has caught it. toon.rs's Union/Intersection branches are the same failure in miniature: LiteralUnion's `.truncate(6)` + `if len>6 push('...(+{n})')` pattern was not factored into a helper, so when Union/Intersection needed the same truncate-with-indicator behavior, the indicator half was dropped — in the *same function*, proving this isn't about code aging apart over time so much as the codebase having no mechanism that makes 'format truncated list' a single call site. schema.rs is the same disease at the crate boundary: it hand-writes a JSON Schema as a `serde_json::json!()` literal instead of deriving it from `ComponentEntry`/`ExtractionStats`/`Diagnostic`, so every new field (`.methods`, `.tags`, 5 of 9 stats fields, `.line`/`.column`/`.help`) requires someone to remember to edit a second, structurally unrelated file — and the only test (`test_cmd_schema_valid_json`) checks that the hand-written JSON is syntactically valid, never that it matches real serialized output, so drift is invisible to CI.\",\n50\t        \"mechanismProposal\": \"Three separate but same-shaped fixes, one per artifact kind — not one generic 'anti-drift' abstraction, the concepts differ: (1) resolver precedence — extract the shared 'try source before falling back to known-pattern shortcuts' logic out of both named.rs and chain.rs into one function in resolver/mod.rs (or a new resolver/precedence.rs), e.g. `fn resolve_source_defined_or_known<T>(name, consuming_file, ctx, state, depth, on_type_alias: impl FnOnce(...) -> T, on_interface: impl FnOnce(...) -> T, on_known: impl FnOnce(KnownPatternResult) -> T) -> Option<T>` that internally does resolve_to_canonical -> lookup_type_alias -> lookup_interface -> (only then) resolve_known, in that fixed order; named.rs and chain.rs both call it and supply only their differing terminal handling (Named vs ResolvedChain). This makes it structurally impossible for one call site to reorder the checks. Pair with a regression fixture: a project defining its own `interface SxProps` extended via `extends`, asserting the hardcoded MUI shortcut is NOT substituted. (2) toon.rs truncation — add one helper, e.g. `fn truncate_with_indicator(parts: &[String], limit: usize, sep: &str) -> String { let mut v: Vec<_> = parts.iter().take(limit).cloned().collect(); if parts.len() > limit { v.push(format!(\\\"...(+{})\\\", parts.len() - limit)); } v.join(sep) }`, and route LiteralUnion, Union, and Intersection through it instead of each doing its own `.truncate`/`.take`. Cheapest fix of the three since all call sites are already colocated in `format_type_compact`. (3) schema.rs — derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on `ComponentEntry`, `ParsedProp`, `ExtractionStats`, `Diagnostic`, `InheritedLayer`, etc. (plain structs, not the recursive enums ADR-0002 exempted from derive machinery) and call `schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput)` from `cmd_schema()`; write one manual `impl JsonSchema for PropType`/`CollectedType`/`OpaqueReason` by hand, following the exact precedent ADR-0002 set for `Serialize` on those same recursive enums, so the recursion-limit problem that motivated ADR-0002 isn't reintroduced. If adding schemars is undesirable, the floor-level fix is a CI test that serializes a real fixture (reuse an existing snapshot) and validates it against schema.rs's hand-written output with a JSON-schema-validator crate, so any new struct field fails the build until schema.rs catches up.\",\n51\t        \"findingIds\": [\n52\t          \"P0-1\",\n53\t          \"P0-4\",\n54\t          \"P0-5\"\n55\t        ],\n56\t        \"adrNeeded\": true,\n57\t        \"adrRationale\": \"Only the schema.rs fix is architecture-level: choosing to derive schemars::JsonSchema (and hand-writing a JsonSchema impl for PropType/CollectedType/OpaqueReason to mirror the existing manual Serialize) is a decision in the same space as ADR-0002 (manual-serialize-for-prop-type) and should be recorded alongside it — it commits the project to a second hand-maintained trait impl per recursive enum whenever a variant is added, the same 'compiler won't remind you' tradeoff ADR-0002 already flagged for Serialize/Deserialize. The resolver-precedence extraction (named.rs/chain.rs) and the toon.rs truncation helper are ordinary internal refactors — easily reversible, no cross-cutting tradeoff — and don't need an ADR of their own.\",\n58\t        \"misassignedNotes\": \"All three findings genuinely belong, but split into two sub-patterns worth naming explicitly: P0-1 and P0-4 are 'the same procedural rule reimplemented at multiple call sites without a shared function' (P0-4 is the purer case — both branches are in the *same* function, so it's a copy-paste omission, not independent evolution over time); P0-5 is 'two independent representations of the same data shape (Rust struct vs hand-written JSON Schema) with no derivation or test tying them together' — closer to the cluster's other stated frame ('two independent implementations of the same concept drifting out of sync') than to precedence/ordering specifically. Keep all three together since the root cause (no enforced single source of truth) is genuinely shared, but the fixes are three unrelated code changes, not one shared helper.\"\n59\t      },\n60\t      {\n61\t        \"clusterName\": \"CLI exit-code/behavior contract inconsistency across commands (P3-1)\",\n62\t        \"rootCause\": \"The 0/1/2 exit-code contract exists only as a doc-comment convention repeated on each command function, not as a type or shared function. `ExtractionOutput` (crates/core/src/types/output.rs) has no `has_errors()`/`exit_code()` method, so \\\"does this output contain an Error-severity diagnostic\\\" is inline boilerplate reimplemented per call site: extract.rs:68-71 does `.any(|d| matches!(d.severity, ... Error))`, check.rs:21-30 does the same via two separate `.filter()` calls (duplicated, not shared - already drifted syntactically). Because this check lives in the CLI crate rather than on the core type - plausibly because crates/core's CLAUDE.md rule \\\"no terminal/display code in crates/core\\\" got over-applied to mean \\\"no exit-code logic belongs in core either\\\" - nothing forces a new command handler to remember it. watch.rs's cmd_watch returns (), not Result&lt;i32&gt; (main.rs hardcodes exit 0 for that arm), and inspect.rs's cmd_inspect never touches output.diagnostics at all - it only errors via component-not-found lookup. Both were plausibly written by copying the general command-handler shape (build_options -&gt; pipeline::extract -&gt; print) without copying the exit-code block from extract.rs/check.rs, because there is no compiler-enforced signal (return type, trait, lint) that a command producing an ExtractionOutput must decide an exit code from it.\",\n63\t        \"mechanismProposal\": \"Add `impl ExtractionOutput { pub fn max_severity(&self) -> Option<DiagnosticSeverity>; pub fn exit_code(&self, strict: bool) -> i32 }` in crates/core/src/types/output.rs - pure data-in/data-out over an already-owned struct, same category as the existing html_element() helper right above ParsedProp, so it doesn't violate \\\"no terminal/display code in core\\\". Then require every CLI command that calls pipeline::extract to route through it: change cmd_watch's signature to return Result<i32> and have main.rs's watch arm call output.exit_code(false) after each pass (or explicitly gate propagation behind a --ci/non-interactive flag if 0-always is intentional for interactive use, documenting the tradeoff instead of leaving it silent); change cmd_inspect to also call output.exit_code(false) after a successful component lookup so an Error diagnostic elsewhere in the tree surfaces even when the inspected component itself is fine. Once exit_code() is the single place severity maps to an exit code, extract.rs and check.rs collapse their inline .any()/.filter() blocks into one call each, removing the drift, and any future command that forgets to call it is now visibly incomplete against a documented, testable contract rather than silently non-compliant.\",\n64\t        \"findingIds\": [\n65\t          \"P3-1\"\n66\t        ],\n67\t        \"adrNeeded\": false,\n68\t        \"adrRationale\": \"This is a refactor extracting an already-existing implicit contract into one shared method, not a new architectural boundary or a hard-to-reverse decision. None of ADR 0001-0004 cover CLI exit-code semantics, and none is needed - a doc comment on ExtractionOutput::exit_code documenting the 0/1/2 mapping is sufficient, matching how the contract is already (informally) documented today.\",\n69\t        \"misassignedNotes\": \"P3-2 (DiagnosticCode::Unknown never constructed) and the two cache/state findings (P0-2, P0-3) are unrelated to the exit-code group and don't share a mechanism story with it or with each other. Cache staleness (P0-2, cache.rs:181-191): key_for builds CacheKey{path, size, mtime_ns} straight from std::fs::metadata. This is a genuine filesystem-timestamp-resolution limitation, not a logic bug or missing abstraction - there is exactly one call site, the code already does the best available thing (nanosecond mtime plus size), and the residual risk is inherent to mtime-based cache invalidation on coarse-resolution filesystems, not something a shared helper would fix. A content hash (e.g. blake3 of the file bytes) would close the gap but changes the cache's performance/complexity tradeoff rather than being \\\"the missing shared check\\\" - worth a scoped follow-up, not a cross-cutting mechanism. Contradictory ParsedProp state (P0-3, types/output.rs:79-99): ParsedProp has all-public fields and is constructed via bare struct literals at 10 call sites across 6 files (known.rs, resolver/chain.rs, resolver/alias.rs, resolver/mod.rs, toon.rs, types/output.rs itself) with no constructor/builder and no validation, so required:true + default_value:Some(_) is representable and never checked. This is a missing type invariant, not duplicated logic that drifted - there was never a shared constructor to unify. Root cause: the rust-types skill's checklist (referenced by crates/core/CLAUDE.md) covers naming/serde/derives for new public types but has no item requiring invariant-bearing structs to be built only through a validating constructor. Fix: add ParsedProp::new(...) (or a builder) in types/output.rs that normalizes the required+default_value relationship per RDT convention (a supplied default implies optional), make the struct's fields pub(crate) to force all 10 call sites through the constructor, and add a line to the rust-types skill checklist: \\\"if a struct has two fields whose combination can be contradictory, provide a validating constructor and consider whether public fields should be private.\\\" This is a single-type fix, independent of the cache issue and independent of the exit-code cluster. DiagnosticCode::Unknown (P3-2, types/diagnostic.rs:61) is genuinely dead code, not a bug: every one of the ~19 Diagnostic-construction call sites across the resolver/extractor/pipeline picks a specific named variant (UnresolvableImport, OpaqueType, MaxDepthExceeded, etc.) because the author always knows the specific cause at the call site; Unknown exists only as headroom on a #[non_exhaustive] enum for external consumers who might synthesize a Diagnostic without a matching code. No mechanism needed - this is correctly categorized in the doc as \\\"though the enum is non_exhaustive so may be intentional headroom.\\\"\"\n70\t      }\n71\t    ],\n72\t    \"synthesis\": \"# Phase 0 Root-Cause Report\\n\\nFour analysis agents, four cluster assignments — but the actual root-cause count is higher because three of the four agents found their assigned cluster wasn't one mechanism, it was several. After splitting, **11 confirmed mechanism-level root causes** and **9 genuinely standalone findings** remain. The overall takeaway: this codebase has good conventions in places that got established once (a named constructor, a depth counter, a truncation pattern) but nothing makes those conventions load-bearing — they're advisory, so parallel/later code paths silently diverge from them. The fix shape repeats: turn an advisory helper into the only callable constructor, or add the one missing signal channel a class of code was never given.\\n\\n## 1. Resolver give-up constructor discipline\\n**Root cause:** `ResolvedChain::default()` and `PropType::Opaque{..}` are plain struct literals, so the named \\\"give up, record a diagnostic\\\" constructors (`empty_with_compose`, `push_opaque_diagnostic`) are optional, not enforced — 6 of ~10 Opaque construction sites and the cycle-detected path in `chain.rs:40` bypass them.\\n**Mechanism:** In `resolver/mod.rs`, make `ResolvedChain`'s bare/`Default` construction private and add `ResolvedChain::give_up(type_name, diag, state)`. Convert `PropType::Opaque{raw, reason}` to `PropType::Opaque(OpaqueDetail)` with private fields in a new `resolver/opaque.rs`, exposing only `OpaqueDetail::give_up(state, what, reason)`. Update the ~10 call sites (collected.rs, func.rs, primitives.rs, named.rs, template.rs) and the two `PropType` serde match arms (ADR 0002 territory, no new ADR needed). Add an `rg` backstop check to `/check`.\\n**Resolves:** resolver-chain-cycle-detected-default, resolver-func-multiparam-no-diagnostic, resolver-literalunion-as-props-base.\\n**ADR:** No — internal refactor of an existing pattern's enforcement, not a new boundary.\\n\\n## 2. Extractor missing give-up signal channel\\n**Root cause:** `classify_type_alias`, `extract_type_name_from_type`, and the component-detector `.or_else()` chains are `Option`-returning `?`-chains over AST shapes with no way to distinguish \\\"wrong shape, fine\\\" from \\\"matched but malformed, worth flagging\\\" — `SourceData.diagnostics` exists but was never wired to these functions.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `fn record_skip(&mut self, code: DiagnosticCode, message: impl Into<String>, span: Span)` in `extractor/mod.rs`, plus a new `DiagnosticCode::SkippedCandidate` variant in `types/diagnostic.rs`. Wire it into every malformed-but-matched arm in `alias.rs::classify_type_alias` and the end of each detector chain in `visit.rs` (lines 236-247, 262-293). This is a checklist requirement (crates/core/CLAUDE.md), not a lint — domain judgment is needed per call site.\\n**Resolves:** extractor-classify-type-alias-omit-pick-etc, extractor-extract-props-arg-terminal-none, extractor-failed-detector-silence, extractor-computed-property-keys. (New-detector coverage — anonymous default export, class components, `Object.assign`, class-expressions, `satisfies` — is a separate feature-gap task; record_skip only makes the existing miss visible.)\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps\\n**Root cause:** Three independent bugs, not one mechanism: `discover_files` has no diagnostic-return channel at all (`walker.flatten()` drops `Err`s); `mod.rs:220`'s empty-`src_dirs` guard is a plain `&&`-short-circuit logic error; `mod.rs:386`'s `components.insert` discards the `Option<V>` that already reveals a collision.\\n**Mechanism:** Give `discover_files` a `&mut Vec<Diagnostic>` param and push on `Err`/non-UTF8-path fallback (reuse the `Diagnostic` constructor pattern at `mod.rs:269-272`). Fix the `&&` guard at `mod.rs:220` and add a `src_dirs: []` regression test. Check `components.insert`'s return at `mod.rs:386` and push a collision diagnostic on `Some(_)`.\\n**Resolves:** pipeline-permission-denied-files, pipeline-non-utf8-filenames, pipeline-empty-src-dirs, pipeline-same-name-same-file-collision, pipeline-overlapping-src-dirs.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 4. No panic-containment boundary\\n**Root cause:** Zero `catch_unwind` in the repo. The three async NAPI entry points are only panic-safe by accident of running inside `tokio::spawn_blocking`; `create_session`/`close_session` skip that entirely, `plugin.rs` hooks run with no isolation, and rayon `.map()` in `pipeline/mod.rs:260,357` has no per-item boundary — so `watch.rs`'s `std::sync::Mutex` can permanently poison.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `crates/core/src/panic_guard.rs`: `pub(crate) fn contain_panic<T>(label: &str, f: impl FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe) -> Result<T, Diagnostic>`, plus `DiagnosticCode::InternalPanic`. Wrap each rayon closure body in `pipeline/mod.rs:260,357`; wrap each `plugin.on_x(...)` call individually inside `plugin.rs`'s loops (not the whole loop); wrap `create_session`/`close_session` bodies in `crates/binding/src/lib.rs`, and have the other three NAPI entry points call it inside their `spawn_blocking` closures too. Defense-in-depth: replace `watch.rs:93`'s `.expect(\\\"init lock poisoned\\\")` with `.unwrap_or_else(|p| p.into_inner())`. Document in crates/core/CLAUDE.md.\\n**Resolves:** P1-3, P1-4, P1-5, P1-9.\\n**ADR: Yes — 0005-panic-containment-boundary.md.**\\n*Context:* No existing ADR covers panic/unwind policy; safety today is an accident of tokio's `JoinError`, inconsistent across NAPI entry points, plugin hooks, and rayon batches, and getting the granularity wrong (per-batch vs per-file/per-plugin) is expensive to fix retroactively.\\n*Decision:* Panics reachable from a rayon `.map()`, a `DocgenPlugin` impl, or a NAPI entry point are contained at per-file/per-plugin/per-call granularity through the single `panic_guard::contain_panic` helper, converting the payload into a `Diagnostic` (or `napi::Error`) instead of aborting a batch, killing the pipeline, or poisoning a session lock.\\n\\n## 5. Unbounded allocation from input-derived size\\n**Root cause:** `extractor/mod.rs` already solved this shape (`MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH`, with a doc comment explaining why) but nobody generalized or cross-referenced it when `template.rs`'s Cartesian-product loop or `lsp.rs`'s header parsing were written later.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `const MAX_TEMPLATE_LITERAL_EXPANSIONS: usize = 4096` checked inside `try_expand_template_literal`'s accumulation loop (`resolver/template.rs`), falling through to the existing Opaque+diagnostic path. Add `const MAX_LSP_MESSAGE_BYTES` gating `vec![0u8; len]` in `cli/commands/lsp.rs:36`. No shared function across crates — add a code-review checklist line instead: \\\"does this size come from parsed/protocol input, and is it capped?\\\"\\n**Resolves:** P1-1, P1-2.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 6. LSP scaffold immaturity\\n**Root cause:** `lsp.rs` is a new, zero-test, hand-rolled framing layer with no capability/handler consistency check.\\n**Mechanism:** Don't advertise `hoverProvider: true` until a handler exists; make the `content_length: None` branch resync (consume to next blank line) instead of `continue`; longer-term, adopt `lsp-server`/`lsp-types`.\\n**Resolves:** P1-6, P1-7.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 7. Extractor depth-tracking proxy mismatch\\n**Root cause:** `max_bracket_nesting_depth` bounds raw-text bracket depth as a stand-in for AST recursion, but `ts_type_to_collected` recurses with no depth counter of its own — unlike the resolver's `resolve_*` functions, which already thread an explicit `depth: u8`.\\n**Mechanism:** Thread a depth counter through `ts_type_to_collected` and sibling functions in `extractor/mod.rs`, bailing with a Diagnostic past e.g. depth 500, matching the resolver's existing convention.\\n**Resolves:** P1-8.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 8. Resolver precedence: two hand-copied orderings\\n**Root cause:** `named.rs` documents \\\"check source-defined types before known-pattern shortcuts\\\" as intentional, but `chain.rs`'s `extends`-clause path re-implements the sequence independently and gets the order backwards. Nothing ties the two together.\\n**Mechanism:** Extract the shared precedence logic into `resolve_source_defined_or_known` in `resolver/mod.rs` (or new `resolver/precedence.rs`): fixed order `resolve_to_canonical → lookup_type_alias → lookup_interface → resolve_known`, taking closures for the differing terminal handling. Both `named.rs` and `chain.rs` call it. Add a regression fixture: a project-defined `interface SxProps` extended via `extends`, asserting no MUI-shortcut substitution.\\n**Resolves:** P0-1.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 9. TOON truncation indicator dropped in one branch\\n**Root cause:** `LiteralUnion`'s truncate-with-`\\\"...(+N)\\\"` pattern wasn't factored into a helper, so `Union`/`Intersection` (same function, `toon.rs`) reimplemented truncation without the indicator half.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `fn truncate_with_indicator(parts: &[String], limit: usize, sep: &str) -> String` in `toon.rs`; route `LiteralUnion`, `Union`, `Intersection` through it.\\n**Resolves:** P0-4.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 10. Schema hand-maintained separately from real structs\\n**Root cause:** `cli/commands/schema.rs` hand-writes JSON Schema as a `json!()` literal instead of deriving it; the only test checks syntactic validity, never drift against real serialized output.\\n**Mechanism:** Derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on `ComponentEntry`, `ParsedProp`, `ExtractionStats`, `Diagnostic`, `InheritedLayer`; call `schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput)` from `cmd_schema()`; hand-write `JsonSchema` impls for `PropType`/`CollectedType`/`OpaqueReason` following ADR-0002's precedent for `Serialize`. If schemars is undesirable, floor-level fix: a CI test serializing an existing fixture and validating it against `schema.rs`'s output.\\n**Resolves:** P0-5.\\n**ADR: Yes** — record alongside ADR-0002 as a follow-up decision (a second hand-maintained trait impl per recursive enum whenever a variant is added). *Decision:* derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on plain output structs and hand-write matching impls for the three recursive enums already exempted from derive by ADR-0002, replacing the hand-written `json!()` schema literal.\\n\\n## 11. CLI exit-code contract has no shared type\\n**Root cause:** The 0/1/2 exit-code mapping is a doc-comment convention re-typed per command (`extract.rs:68-71`, `check.rs:21-30`, syntactically already diverged); `watch.rs` returns `()` and `inspect.rs` never checks diagnostics at all, because nothing compiler-enforces that a command producing `ExtractionOutput` decides an exit code from it.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `ExtractionOutput::max_severity()` / `exit_code(&self, strict: bool) -> i32` in `core/types/output.rs`. Route `extract.rs`, `check.rs` through it (collapsing their inline checks); change `cmd_watch` to return `Result<i32>` and call it per pass; call it in `cmd_inspect` after a successful lookup.\\n**Resolves:** P3-1.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## Standalone findings (no shared mechanism — treat as independent tasks)\\n- `substitute.rs:33-43` — generic alias `.zip()` silently truncates trailing unsubstituted type params (silent-correctness bug, not a give-up path).\\n- `watch.rs:107-112` — `--out` write failure discarded via `let _ =`; route through the CLI's existing miette/output.rs error path. Same file: also fix the non-atomic `fs::write` (write-to-temp + rename).\\n- `cache.rs:181-191` (P0-2) — mtime/size cache key is a coarse-filesystem-resolution limitation, not a logic bug; scoped follow-up (content hash) if warranted, no shared mechanism.\\n- `types/output.rs:79-99` (P0-3) — `ParsedProp` allows contradictory `required:true` + `default_value:Some`; add `ParsedProp::new(...)` validating constructor, make fields `pub(crate)`, add a rust-types skill checklist line for invariant-bearing structs.\\n- `types/diagnostic.rs:61` (P3-2) — `DiagnosticCode::Unknown` unused but intentional headroom on a `#[non_exhaustive]` enum; no action.\\n- `types/output.rs:314,423` — NaN/Infinity `NumberLiteral` round-trips to `0.0`; low-priority, likely unreachable.\\n- Zero-member `LiteralUnion` serializes as an empty enum; low-priority.\\n\\n## Phase 1 task breakdown\\n1. Resolver constructor discipline (touches `resolver/mod.rs`, `chain.rs`, `func.rs`, `collected.rs`, `primitives.rs`, `named.rs`, `template.rs`, new `resolver/opaque.rs`, `types/output.rs`) — **sequential with #5 and #8** (shared `template.rs`/`chain.rs`/`named.rs`).\\n2. Extractor diagnostic channel (`extractor/mod.rs`, `alias.rs`, `visit.rs`, `types/diagnostic.rs`) — **sequential with #4 and #7** (shared `extractor/mod.rs`, `types/diagnostic.rs`).\\n3. Pipeline discovery/merge fixes (`pipeline/discover.rs`, `pipeline/mod.rs`) — **sequential with #4** (shared `pipeline/mod.rs`).\\n4. Panic-containment boundary + ADR 0005 (new `panic_guard.rs`, `pipeline/mod.rs`, `plugin.rs`, `crates/binding/src/lib.rs`, `pipeline/watch.rs`, `types/diagnostic.rs`) — do first if possible, other tasks touching same files should rebase on it.\\n5. Allocation caps (`resolver/template.rs`, `cli/commands/lsp.rs`) — parallel-safe except vs #1/#6.\\n6. LSP scaffold hardening (`cli/commands/lsp.rs`) — merge with #5 (same file).\\n7. Extractor depth-tracking (`extractor/mod.rs`) — merge with #2.\\n8. Resolver precedence extraction (`resolver/mod.rs` or new file, `named.rs`, `chain.rs`) — merge with #1.\\n9. TOON truncation helper (`toon.rs`) — fully parallel, disjoint.\\n10. Schema derivation (`cli/commands/schema.rs`, `core/types/*.rs` derives) — parallel-safe, additive derives only.\\n11. CLI exit-code contract (`core/types/output.rs`, `extract.rs`, `check.rs`, `watch.rs`, `inspect.rs`, `main.rs`) — parallel-safe.\\n12. Standalone fixes (`substitute.rs`, `watch.rs`, `cache.rs`, `types/output.rs` ParsedProp ctor) — each independently parallel, but `watch.rs` and `types/output.rs` tasks should merge with #4/#1 respectively if scheduled concurrently.\"\n73\t  },\n74\t  \"workflowProgress\": [\n75\t    {\n76\t      \"type\": \"workflow_phase\",\n77\t      \"index\": 1,\n78\t      \"title\": \"Cluster Analysis\"\n79\t    },\n80\t    {\n81\t      \"type\": \"workflow_phase\",\n82\t      \"index\": 2,\n83\t      \"title\": \"Synthesize\"\n84\t    },\n85\t    {\n86\t      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n87\t      \"index\": 1,\n88\t      \"label\": \"silent-degrade\",\n89\t      \"phaseIndex\": 1,\n90\t      \"phaseTitle\": \"Cluster Analysis\",\n91\t      \"agentId\": \"a30ff11631cc17ab8\",\n92\t      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n93\t      \"state\": \"done\",\n94\t      \"startedAt\": 1785863565606,\n95\t      \"queuedAt\": 1785863562301,\n96\t      \"attempt\": 1,\n97\t      \"lastToolName\": \"StructuredOutput\",\n98\t      \"lastToolSummary\": \"Give-up-silently: graceful-degradation paths that skip the …\",\n99\t      \"promptPreview\": \"Read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md first for full context — it's a comprehensive audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript) covering crash risks, silent data loss, and correctness bugs across the whole pipeline. Also read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-…\",\n100\t      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863765917,\n101\t      \"tokens\": 61393,\n102\t      \"toolCalls\": 18,\n103\t      \"durationMs\": 200311,\n104\t      \"resultPreview\": \"{\\\"clusterName\\\":\\\"Give-up-silently: graceful-degradation paths that skip the Diagnostic pairing\\\",\\\"rootCause\\\":\\\"Verified against actual code (resolver/chain.rs, resolver/func.rs, resolver/collected.rs, extractor/alias.rs, extractor/mod.rs, extractor/visit.rs, pipeline/discover.rs, pipeline/mod.rs). This is not one root cause but three, stratified by whether a shared mechanism already exists:\\\\n\\\\n(1) RE…\"\n105\t    },\n106\t    {\n107\t      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n108\t      \"index\": 2,\n109\t      \"label\": \"panic-containment\",\n110\t      \"phaseIndex\": 1,\n111\t      \"phaseTitle\": \"Cluster Analysis\",\n112\t      \"agentId\": \"ad8efce0df55a3924\",\n113\t      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n114\t      \"state\": \"done\",\n115\t      \"startedAt\": 1785863564614,\n116\t      \"queuedAt\": 1785863562301,\n117\t      \"attempt\": 1,\n118\t      \"lastToolName\": \"StructuredOutput\",\n119\t      \"lastToolSummary\": \"No panic-boundary convention: unwind containment is acciden…\",\n120\t      \"promptPreview\": \"Read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md first for full context — it's a comprehensive audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript) covering crash risks, silent data loss, and correctness bugs across the whole pipeline. Also read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-…\",\n121\t      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863788655,\n122\t      \"tokens\": 65635,\n123\t      \"toolCalls\": 13,\n124\t      \"durationMs\": 224041,\n125\t      \"resultPreview\": \"{\\\"clusterName\\\":\\\"No panic-boundary convention: unwind containment is accidental, not designed\\\",\\\"rootCause\\\":\\\"grep confirms zero uses of catch_unwind anywhere in the repo. The three async NAPI entry points (extract_all, extract_file_incremental, initialize_session) are only 'safe' from a panic today because they happen to run inside tokio::task::spawn_blocking, whose JoinHandle converts a panic into …\"\n126\t    },\n127\t    {\n128\t      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n129\t      \"index\": 3,\n130\t      \"label\": \"duplicated-logic-drift\",\n131\t      \"phaseIndex\": 1,\n132\t      \"phaseTitle\": \"Cluster Analysis\",\n133\t      \"agentId\": \"a8c2dba27b5dbe6dc\",\n134\t      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n135\t      \"state\": \"done\",\n136\t      \"startedAt\": 1785863564865,\n137\t      \"queuedAt\": 1785863562301,\n138\t      \"attempt\": 1,\n139\t      \"lastToolName\": \"StructuredOutput\",\n140\t      \"lastToolSummary\": \"Duplicated invariants with no single callable source of tru…\",\n141\t      \"promptPreview\": \"Read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md first for full context — it's a comprehensive audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript) covering crash risks, silent data loss, and correctness bugs across the whole pipeline. Also read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-…\",\n142\t      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863694859,\n143\t      \"tokens\": 50069,\n144\t      \"toolCalls\": 11,\n145\t      \"durationMs\": 129994,\n146\t      \"resultPreview\": \"{\\\"clusterName\\\":\\\"Duplicated invariants with no single callable source of truth (precedence rules, truncation formatting, and struct-to-schema mirroring all hand-copied instead of shared)\\\",\\\"rootCause\\\":\\\"In all three findings, an invariant that must hold identically across two or more locations is expressed as inline, ad hoc code plus an explanatory comment at each site, rather than as one named funct…\"\n147\t    },\n148\t    {\n149\t      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n150\t      \"index\": 4,\n151\t      \"label\": \"contract-and-cache-correctness\",\n152\t      \"phaseIndex\": 1,\n153\t      \"phaseTitle\": \"Cluster Analysis\",\n154\t      \"agentId\": \"a658bd73151e35be4\",\n155\t      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n156\t      \"state\": \"done\",\n157\t      \"startedAt\": 1785863564872,\n158\t      \"queuedAt\": 1785863562301,\n159\t      \"attempt\": 1,\n160\t      \"lastToolName\": \"StructuredOutput\",\n161\t      \"lastToolSummary\": \"CLI exit-code/behavior contract inconsistency across comman…\",\n162\t      \"promptPreview\": \"Read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md first for full context — it's a comprehensive audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript) covering crash risks, silent data loss, and correctness bugs across the whole pipeline. Also read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-…\",\n163\t      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863678294,\n164\t      \"tokens\": 44692,\n165\t      \"toolCalls\": 14,\n166\t      \"durationMs\": 113422,\n167\t      \"resultPreview\": \"{\\\"clusterName\\\":\\\"CLI exit-code/behavior contract inconsistency across commands (P3-1)\\\",\\\"rootCause\\\":\\\"The 0/1/2 exit-code contract exists only as a doc-comment convention repeated on each command function, not as a type or shared function. `ExtractionOutput` (crates/core/src/types/output.rs) has no `has_errors()`/`exit_code()` method, so \\\\\\\"does this output contain an Error-severity diagnostic\\\\\\\" is in…\"\n168\t    },\n169\t    {\n170\t      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n171\t      \"index\": 5,\n172\t      \"label\": \"synthesis\",\n173\t      \"phaseIndex\": 2,\n174\t      \"phaseTitle\": \"Synthesize\",\n175\t      \"agentId\": \"a7c04cf36f291ca4f\",\n176\t      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n177\t      \"state\": \"done\",\n178\t      \"startedAt\": 1785863795360,\n179\t      \"queuedAt\": 1785863791186,\n180\t      \"attempt\": 1,\n181\t      \"lastToolName\": \"Read\",\n182\t      \"lastToolSummary\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/adr/README.md\",\n183\t      \"promptPreview\": \"You are synthesizing a Phase 0 architectural root-cause analysis for oxc-react-docgen into a final report. Below are the outputs from 4 independent analysis agents, each examining a different candidate root-cause cluster behind the findings in /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md.\\n\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\\"clusterName\\\": \\\"Give-up-silently: graceful-degradation paths that skip the Diagnostic …\",\n184\t      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863879958,\n185\t      \"tokens\": 41885,\n186\t      \"toolCalls\": 2,\n187\t      \"durationMs\": 84598,\n188\t      \"resultPreview\": \"# Phase 0 Root-Cause Report\\n\\nFour analysis agents, four cluster assignments — but the actual root-cause count is higher because three of the four agents found their assigned cluster wasn't one mechanism, it was several. After splitting, **11 confirmed mechanism-level root causes** and **9 genuinely standalone findings** remain. The overall takeaway: this codebase has good conventions in places tha…\"\n189\t    }\n190\t  ],\n191\t  \"totalTokens\": 263674,\n192\t  \"totalToolCalls\": 58\n193\t}"}]},"uuid":"4d2160f0-0f23-4766-8717-8e9addb7b73a","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:18:05.218Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-oxc-react-codegen/0ae3953f-9438-46c4-a76a-88fce9eed529/tasks/wruyd6jkk.output","content":"{\n  \"summary\": \"Phase 0: find architectural root causes behind the edge-case audit findings and propose fix-pattern clusters + enforcement mechanisms\",\n  \"agentCount\": 5,\n  \"logs\": [],\n  \"result\": {\n    \"clusterResults\": [\n      {\n        \"clusterName\": \"Give-up-silently: graceful-degradation paths that skip the Diagnostic pairing\",\n        \"rootCause\": \"Verified against actual code (resolver/chain.rs, resolver/func.rs, resolver/collected.rs, extractor/alias.rs, extractor/mod.rs, extractor/visit.rs, pipeline/discover.rs, pipeline/mod.rs). This is not one root cause but three, stratified by whether a shared mechanism already exists:\\n\\n(1) RESOLVER (chain.rs, func.rs): a convention already exists — `ResolvedChain::empty_with_compose(name)` bundles the \\\"give up, record what we couldn't resolve\\\" case, and `collected.rs::push_opaque_diagnostic` is a shared helper that pairs a `PropType::Opaque` with an Info diagnostic. But neither is the *only* way to construct these values: `ResolvedChain` derives `Default` and is a plain struct with no private fields, so `ResolvedChain::default()` (bare, no composes/diagnostic) is just as callable as the named constructor — chain.rs:40 (cycle path) reaches for the wrong one and nothing stops it. Likewise `PropType::Opaque { raw, reason }` is a plain struct-literal-constructible variant; I found ~10 construction sites across 5 files (collected.rs, func.rs, primitives.rs, named.rs, template.rs) and only 4 of them (collected.rs:79,103,107,150) route through `push_opaque_diagnostic` — func.rs:61 (MultiParamFunction), primitives.rs:181 (IndexedAccess), template.rs:39 (TemplateLiteral) build `PropType::Opaque` directly with no diagnostic call, invisible to any reviewer scanning the \\\"happy path.\\\" The helper functions are advisory, not enforced — classic drift from a convention that isn't structurally mandatory. This is the \\\"no lint/test enforces its use\\\" case, not \\\"no mechanism exists.\\\"\\n\\n(2) EXTRACTOR (alias.rs classify_type_alias, mod.rs extract_type_name_from_type, visit.rs component detectors): fundamentally more foundational — these are `Option<T>`-returning functions chained with `?` against AST shapes (`tp.params.first()?`, `_ => None`) or nested `if let Some(...) = a.or_else(b).or_else(c)` detector chains with no `else` branch. There is no signal channel distinguishing \\\"this AST shape doesn't apply here\\\" from \\\"this looked like a match but was malformed/unsupported\\\" — both collapse to the same `None`. `SourceData.diagnostics` does exist and extractor code CAN push into it (confirmed at extractor/mod.rs:108,134, both unrelated to this cluster — nesting-depth guard and a syntax error), but classify_type_alias and friends have never been wired to it despite having `&mut self` in scope. This is a design gap, not a helper-drift gap: no helper could be \\\"used inconsistently\\\" here because none was ever built for this call shape.\\n\\n(3) PIPELINE (discover.rs, mod.rs): three unrelated bugs masquerading as one pattern. discover.rs:17's `walker.flatten()` drops `ignore::Walk`'s `Err` items — structural, because `discover_files` returns a bare `Vec<Utf8PathBuf>` with no diagnostic-return channel at all (same class of gap as (2)). mod.rs:220's empty-`src_dirs` miss is a plain boolean short-circuit bug (`!src_dirs.is_empty() && ...`) — a logic error, not a missing abstraction. mod.rs:386's `components.insert(key, entry)` collision discards `insert`'s `Option<V>` return value that already tells you a collision happened — the data needed to diagnose it is right there and simply unused.\",\n        \"mechanismProposal\": \"Three different fixes for three different root causes — do not apply one mechanism to all findings:\\n\\n**(1) Resolver — make the bare/wrong constructor uncallable, not just discouraged.**\\n- In `resolver/mod.rs`, stop deriving `Default` on `ResolvedChain` as a public-within-module escape hatch: keep a private `fn empty() -> Self` used *only* inside `empty_with_compose`, and add one more named constructor, `ResolvedChain::give_up(type_name: String, diag: Option<Diagnostic>, state: &mut ResolveState) -> Self`, which pushes the diagnostic AND the composes entry in one call, replacing every current two-statement `state.diagnostics.push(...); ResolvedChain::empty_with_compose(...)` pair as well as the bare-`default()` cycle path at chain.rs:40 (which becomes `ResolvedChain::give_up(type_name.to_owned(), None, state)` — reviewers can now see explicitly that no diagnostic is attached there, or add one). Because `Default`/struct-literal construction of `ResolvedChain` outside `resolver/mod.rs`'s own impl block no longer compiles, every future give-up site in chain.rs/alias.rs/extends.rs is forced through the named constructors.\\n- Do the equivalent for `PropType::Opaque`: change it from `Opaque { raw: String, reason: OpaqueReason }` to a single-field tuple variant `Opaque(OpaqueDetail)` where `OpaqueDetail`'s fields are private to a new `resolver/opaque.rs`, and the only public entry point is `OpaqueDetail::give_up(state: &mut ResolveState, what: &str, ct_or_raw, file, reason: OpaqueReason) -> PropType` — folding `push_opaque_diagnostic` into the constructor itself instead of leaving it as a sibling function callers can skip. Update the ~10 existing call sites (collected.rs ×6, func.rs, primitives.rs, named.rs, template.rs) to go through it — this mechanically fixes func.rs:54-61's missing diagnostic and prevents any new Opaque-degradation site from omitting one, since there is no other way to build the variant. This does touch `PropType`'s manual `to_json_value`/`from_json_value` (ADR 0002) — update both match arms, no new ADR needed for that part since the wire shape is unchanged, only the Rust-side constructor surface.\\n- Add one clippy.toml `disallowed-methods`/`disallowed-types` entry (or a `rg 'ResolvedChain\\\\s*\\\\{' -g '!resolver/mod.rs'` / `rg 'PropType::Opaque\\\\s*\\\\{' -g '!resolver/opaque.rs'` check wired into the existing CI/check flow referenced by the `/check` skill) as a backstop in case someone reintroduces a bare literal via `..Default::default()` spread syntax, which the type system alone won't catch.\\n\\n**(2) Extractor — add the missing signal channel, then make it a review-checklist item (no compiler enforcement is practical here given `?`-chained `Option` returns over AST shape matching).**\\n- Add one helper next to the two existing `self.data.diagnostics.push(...)` call sites in `extractor/mod.rs`: `fn record_skip(&mut self, code: DiagnosticCode, message: impl Into<String>, span: Span)` (Info/Warning severity, reuses the existing `Diagnostic` shape). Add a new `DiagnosticCode` variant, e.g. `SkippedCandidate`, so these are filterable/countable separately from parse errors.\\n- Require (via the `crates/core/CLAUDE.md` checklist, not the compiler): every `match ref_name.as_str() { \\\"Omit\\\" => { ...?...  } ... _ => passthrough_or_none }` arm in `classify_type_alias` that bails on a *matched-but-malformed* shape (wrong arg count, unrecognized inner type) calls `self.record_skip(...)` before the `?`/`return None`; every component-detector `.or_else()` chain in `visit.rs` (both the PascalCase binding chain at 236-247 and `visit_function` at 262-293) ends with an explicit `else { self.record_skip(...) }` rather than silent fallthrough. This is a checklist addition, not a lint, because distinguishing \\\"not this shape, silently fine\\\" from \\\"this shape but unsupported, worth a diagnostic\\\" requires domain judgment a lint can't make.\\n- New detector coverage (anonymous default export, class components, `Object.assign` compound components, class-expression components, `satisfies`) is a separate, larger feature-gap task — record_skip only makes the *existing* miss visible; it doesn't add the missing detectors.\\n\\n**(3) Pipeline — three independent one-line-scale fixes, no shared abstraction:**\\n- `discover_files` gains a `&mut Vec<Diagnostic>` (or returns `(Vec<Utf8PathBuf>, Vec<Diagnostic>)`) and the `walker.flatten()` becomes an explicit match that pushes an IoError diagnostic on `Err`, same pattern already used at mod.rs:269-272 for read failures — reuse `Diagnostic::io_read_error` or a sibling constructor for consistency. Non-UTF8 filenames get the same treatment at the `Utf8PathBuf::from_path_buf` fallback.\\n- mod.rs:220: fix the boolean condition (drop the `!options.src_dirs.is_empty() &&` guard, or explicitly handle the empty case with its own diagnostic) and add a regression test with `src_dirs: []` asserting at least one diagnostic is emitted — this is a logic-bug fix, not a new mechanism.\\n- mod.rs:386: check `components.insert(key, entry)`'s returned `Option<ComponentEntry>` and push a diagnostic when `Some(_)` comes back (collision) — the data is already there, just discarded; one `if let Some(prev) = ...` fixes both the same-name-same-file and overlapping-src_dirs findings at once.\\n\\n**ADR:** worth recording as an ADR (see adrNeeded) specifically for decision (1) — it changes `PropType`'s and `ResolvedChain`'s construction surface project-wide and establishes \\\"graceful-degradation values are built only through a diagnostic-pairing smart constructor, never a bare struct literal\\\" as a durable rule future contributors need to know about before adding a new Opaque-producing or give-up code path. Decisions (2) and (3) are local fixes/checklist items, not architectural.\",\n        \"findingIds\": [\n          \"extractor-anonymous-default-export\",\n          \"extractor-class-components\",\n          \"extractor-object-assign-compound\",\n          \"extractor-class-expression-components\",\n          \"extractor-satisfies-expressions\",\n          \"extractor-classify-type-alias-omit-pick-etc\",\n          \"extractor-extract-props-arg-terminal-none\",\n          \"extractor-failed-detector-silence\",\n          \"extractor-computed-property-keys\",\n          \"resolver-chain-cycle-detected-default\",\n          \"resolver-func-multiparam-no-diagnostic\",\n          \"resolver-literalunion-as-props-base\",\n          \"pipeline-permission-denied-files\",\n          \"pipeline-non-utf8-filenames\",\n          \"pipeline-empty-src-dirs\",\n          \"pipeline-same-name-same-file-collision\",\n          \"pipeline-overlapping-src-dirs\"\n        ],\n        \"misassignedNotes\": \"Two findings originally bundled into this cluster don't fit the \\\"give-up path with no Diagnostic\\\" shape and belong elsewhere:\\n- `resolver/substitute.rs:33-43` (generic aliases with more type params than supplied args — `build_substitution`'s `.zip()` silently stops) is a silent *truncation*, not a give-up-and-return-nothing path — no ResolvedChain/PropType is being short-circuited, a substitution map is just incomplete. It's closer to the Priority 0 \\\"silent correctness bug\\\" tier (wrong substitution, not missing output) than this cluster; excluded from findingIds above.\\n- `cli/commands/watch.rs:107-112` (`--out` write failure via `let _ = std::fs::write(...)`) is CLI-layer, not extraction-pipeline degradation, and CLAUDE.md's non-negotiable #6 is scoped to the extraction pipeline (crates/core) — crates/cli's own CLAUDE.md separately mandates all user-facing output go through `output.rs`/tracing/miette, which this violates on its own terms. It's a real bug but its fix is \\\"route through miette error reporting like every other CLI failure,\\\" a CLI-layer mechanism unrelated to the resolver/extractor/pipeline diagnostic-pairing story above. Excluded from findingIds.\\n- Types/serialization findings (NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity, zero-member LiteralUnion) are genuinely silent but are serialization-boundary edge cases with no realistic reachable input (per the doc's own \\\"likely unreachable via normal TS literal syntax\\\" caveat) rather than a repeated give-up-path pattern — they don't share the root cause (bare struct literal vs. named constructor) diagnosed above, so I left them out of findingIds; they're better tracked as standalone P2 tickets than folded into this mechanism.\\n\\nAll findings I did verify against source match the doc's line citations closely (chain.rs:40 confirmed bare `ResolvedChain::default()`; func.rs:61 confirmed no diagnostic push; alias.rs `classify_type_alias` confirmed `?`-chained `None` collapse; discover.rs:17 confirmed `.flatten()`; mod.rs:220 confirmed the `&&`-guarded condition; mod.rs:386 confirmed `components.insert` return value discarded).\",\n        \"adrNeeded\": true\n      },\n      {\n        \"clusterName\": \"No panic-boundary convention: unwind containment is accidental, not designed\",\n        \"rootCause\": \"grep confirms zero uses of catch_unwind anywhere in the repo. The three async NAPI entry points (extract_all, extract_file_incremental, initialize_session) are only 'safe' from a panic today because they happen to run inside tokio::task::spawn_blocking, whose JoinHandle converts a panic into an Err the caller already unwraps via .map_err(...)? — that's incidental protection from tokio's plumbing, not a deliberate design decision anyone wrote down. Because it was never written down anywhere (not in crates/core/CLAUDE.md, not in crates/cli/CLAUDE.md, no shared helper to import), every subsequent concurrent/cross-boundary addition had to independently reinvent whether panics are contained, and got inconsistent answers: (1) pipeline/mod.rs:260,357 wrap rayon .map() closures with no catch_unwind at all, relying on the unenforced invariant 'the extractor/resolver never panic' (true today per the audit's own non-findings, but not a boundary — it's a hope); (2) plugin.rs's DocgenPlugin trait is explicitly documented as third-party-extensible ('Plugins can hook into extraction events') yet PluginRegistry::run_on_file_extracted/run_on_component_resolved just call `plugin.on_x(...)` directly in a for loop with zero isolation between plugins or from the pipeline that invokes them from pipeline/mod.rs:293,385; (3) crates/binding/src/lib.rs's create_session/close_session are synchronous fns that skip spawn_blocking entirely (unlike the other three), so they don't even get the incidental protection those get; (4) pipeline/watch.rs:93's `initialized.lock().expect(...)` uses std::sync::Mutex, whose poison-on-panic default becomes a permanent trap specifically because the entire cold extraction (rayon batches + plugin hooks, i.e. exactly the unprotected code from (1) and (2)) runs while that lock is held inside initialize(). These four are one causal chain, not four coincidences: the same missing boundary shows up once as 'kills the whole batch,' once as 'kills the whole pipeline from one plugin,' once as 'silently unsafe compared to its siblings,' and once as 'permanently wedges a session' because nothing upstream ever catches the panic that would otherwise poison it.\",\n        \"mechanismProposal\": \"Add one shared helper — `crates/core/src/panic_guard.rs`, `pub(crate) fn contain_panic<T>(file: Option<&str>, label: &str, f: impl FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe) -> Result<T, Diagnostic>` — wrapping std::panic::catch_unwind and converting any payload into a Diagnostic with a new `DiagnosticCode::InternalPanic` variant (types/diagnostic.rs, following the same #[non_exhaustive] pattern already used for the unused `Unknown` variant). This becomes the single sanctioned catch point for the whole crate. Force every non-provably-panic-free call site through it: (a) pipeline/mod.rs:260 and :357 — wrap the body of each rayon `.map()` closure, turning a panicking file/component into one Diagnostic instead of aborting the batch; (b) plugin.rs's `run_on_file_extracted`/`run_on_component_resolved` — wrap each individual `plugin.on_x(...)` call inside the existing `for plugin in &self.plugins` loop (not the whole loop) so one bad plugin degrades only itself, tagging the Diagnostic with `plugin.name()`; (c) crates/binding/src/lib.rs's `create_session`/`close_session` — wrap their bodies with `contain_panic`, converting to `napi::Error`, bringing them to explicit parity with the other three NAPI functions, which should themselves start calling `contain_panic` inside their `spawn_blocking` closures so their safety stops being an accident of tokio's JoinError and becomes the same documented mechanism as everywhere else. Once (a)-(b) exist, `watch.rs`'s `initialized: Mutex<bool>` structurally can't poison anymore (nothing panics while it's held), but as cheap defense-in-depth also replace `.expect(\\\"init lock poisoned\\\")` with `.unwrap_or_else(|poisoned| poisoned.into_inner())` (std) so a future gap doesn't wedge the session even if (a)-(c) are ever bypassed. Document the rule in crates/core/CLAUDE.md as a new short 'Panic containment' section next to the existing 'Resolver' section: 'Anything reachable from a rayon `.map()` or a `DocgenPlugin` impl must go through `panic_guard::contain_panic` — see pipeline/mod.rs and plugin.rs for the call sites.' That's the natural place because every other cross-cutting rule in this codebase (FxHashMap/BTreeMap discipline, Diagnostic-on-degrade) already lives there and subsystem authors demonstrably read it.\",\n        \"findingIds\": [\n          \"P1-3\",\n          \"P1-4\",\n          \"P1-5\",\n          \"P1-9\"\n        ],\n        \"adrNeeded\": true,\n        \"adrRationale\": \"This is a cross-cutting safety-boundary decision spanning crates/core and crates/binding, sets a pattern every future concurrent/plugin/FFI entry point will copy, and is expensive to reverse quietly (picking the wrong granularity — per-batch vs per-file/per-plugin — or the wrong lock type now means re-touching every call site later). None of the existing ADRs (0001 OXC choice, 0002 manual serde, 0003 msgpack cache encoding, 0004 defer type-checker) cover panic/unwind policy. Propose 0005-panic-containment-boundary.md: what's the one sanctioned catch point (`panic_guard::contain_panic`), why per-file/per-plugin granularity was chosen over per-batch, and that it also fixes the NAPI create_session/close_session inconsistency.\",\n        \"misassignedNotes\": \"The other five findings originally bundled into this cluster do NOT share the panic-boundary root cause above and should be split into three separate groups: (1) P1-1 (resolver/template.rs:94-104 Cartesian product) and P1-2 (cli/commands/lsp.rs:36 Content-Length alloc) are 'unbounded allocation from input-derived size' — nothing here panics, they just compute a size and allocate it uncapped. Root cause: extractor/mod.rs already solved exactly this shape of problem once (MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH + max_bracket_nesting_depth, with a detailed doc comment explaining why), but nobody generalized or even cross-referenced that precedent when template.rs's Cartesian loop or lsp.rs's header parsing were written later — it's a discoverability gap, not a missing type. Fix: add `const MAX_TEMPLATE_LITERAL_EXPANSIONS: usize = 4096` (or similar) checked inside `try_expand_template_literal`'s accumulation loop, returning None early to fall through to the Opaque+diagnostic path `resolve_template_literal` already has; add `const MAX_LSP_MESSAGE_BYTES` gating the `vec![0u8; len]` allocation in lsp.rs, rejecting/resyncing on an oversized header. Since the two live in different crates with different data shapes, a shared function isn't practical — the mechanism is a documented precedent (point future authors at extractor/mod.rs's doc comment) plus a code-review checklist line: 'does this size come from parsed/protocol input, and is it capped before use?' (2) P1-6 (lsp.rs:56,78 advertised-but-unimplemented hover) and P1-7 (lsp.rs:32-34 malformed-header desync) — and arguably P1-2 too — trace to a different root cause: lsp.rs is a brand-new, zero-test, hand-rolled LSP-framing scaffold with no capabilities/handlers consistency check. Fix: don't advertise `hoverProvider: true` until a handler exists (or implement a trivial one); make the `content_length: None` branch actually resync (consume to the next blank line or close the connection) instead of `continue`ing past an unconsumed body; longer-term, adopt `lsp-server`/`lsp-types` crates so framing, capability-advertisement, and bounds are handled by a maintained library instead of reinvented here. (3) P1-8 (extractor/mod.rs:38-40,105-120 guard vs :311-471 recursion) is a standalone proxy-metric mismatch: `max_bracket_nesting_depth` bounds raw-text bracket depth as a stand-in for AST recursion depth, but `ts_type_to_collected` recurses with zero depth-tracking of its own — unlike the resolver's `resolve_*` functions (e.g. template.rs), which already thread an explicit `depth: u8` parameter and bail past a limit. Fix: bring the extractor's TSType-conversion functions into that same already-established resolver convention (thread a depth counter through `ts_type_to_collected` and friends, bail with a Diagnostic past e.g. depth 500) instead of relying solely on the pre-parse text heuristic.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"clusterName\": \"Duplicated invariants with no single callable source of truth (precedence rules, truncation formatting, and struct-to-schema mirroring all hand-copied instead of shared)\",\n        \"rootCause\": \"In all three findings, an invariant that must hold identically across two or more locations is expressed as inline, ad hoc code plus an explanatory comment at each site, rather than as one named function/derive/helper that every site is forced to route through. named.rs comments 'Check source-defined types BEFORE known library patterns... intentional to avoid this exact collision' but that ordering is re-typed by hand as chain.rs's own sequence of numbered steps (step 2 known-pattern check runs before step 4/5 type-alias/interface lookup — the exact inverse of named.rs's step 2-4-before-5 order). Nothing (type system, lint, or test) ties the two orderings together, so chain.rs was free to drift the moment it was written independently of named.rs, and nothing since has caught it. toon.rs's Union/Intersection branches are the same failure in miniature: LiteralUnion's `.truncate(6)` + `if len>6 push('...(+{n})')` pattern was not factored into a helper, so when Union/Intersection needed the same truncate-with-indicator behavior, the indicator half was dropped — in the *same function*, proving this isn't about code aging apart over time so much as the codebase having no mechanism that makes 'format truncated list' a single call site. schema.rs is the same disease at the crate boundary: it hand-writes a JSON Schema as a `serde_json::json!()` literal instead of deriving it from `ComponentEntry`/`ExtractionStats`/`Diagnostic`, so every new field (`.methods`, `.tags`, 5 of 9 stats fields, `.line`/`.column`/`.help`) requires someone to remember to edit a second, structurally unrelated file — and the only test (`test_cmd_schema_valid_json`) checks that the hand-written JSON is syntactically valid, never that it matches real serialized output, so drift is invisible to CI.\",\n        \"mechanismProposal\": \"Three separate but same-shaped fixes, one per artifact kind — not one generic 'anti-drift' abstraction, the concepts differ: (1) resolver precedence — extract the shared 'try source before falling back to known-pattern shortcuts' logic out of both named.rs and chain.rs into one function in resolver/mod.rs (or a new resolver/precedence.rs), e.g. `fn resolve_source_defined_or_known<T>(name, consuming_file, ctx, state, depth, on_type_alias: impl FnOnce(...) -> T, on_interface: impl FnOnce(...) -> T, on_known: impl FnOnce(KnownPatternResult) -> T) -> Option<T>` that internally does resolve_to_canonical -> lookup_type_alias -> lookup_interface -> (only then) resolve_known, in that fixed order; named.rs and chain.rs both call it and supply only their differing terminal handling (Named vs ResolvedChain). This makes it structurally impossible for one call site to reorder the checks. Pair with a regression fixture: a project defining its own `interface SxProps` extended via `extends`, asserting the hardcoded MUI shortcut is NOT substituted. (2) toon.rs truncation — add one helper, e.g. `fn truncate_with_indicator(parts: &[String], limit: usize, sep: &str) -> String { let mut v: Vec<_> = parts.iter().take(limit).cloned().collect(); if parts.len() > limit { v.push(format!(\\\"...(+{})\\\", parts.len() - limit)); } v.join(sep) }`, and route LiteralUnion, Union, and Intersection through it instead of each doing its own `.truncate`/`.take`. Cheapest fix of the three since all call sites are already colocated in `format_type_compact`. (3) schema.rs — derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on `ComponentEntry`, `ParsedProp`, `ExtractionStats`, `Diagnostic`, `InheritedLayer`, etc. (plain structs, not the recursive enums ADR-0002 exempted from derive machinery) and call `schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput)` from `cmd_schema()`; write one manual `impl JsonSchema for PropType`/`CollectedType`/`OpaqueReason` by hand, following the exact precedent ADR-0002 set for `Serialize` on those same recursive enums, so the recursion-limit problem that motivated ADR-0002 isn't reintroduced. If adding schemars is undesirable, the floor-level fix is a CI test that serializes a real fixture (reuse an existing snapshot) and validates it against schema.rs's hand-written output with a JSON-schema-validator crate, so any new struct field fails the build until schema.rs catches up.\",\n        \"findingIds\": [\n          \"P0-1\",\n          \"P0-4\",\n          \"P0-5\"\n        ],\n        \"adrNeeded\": true,\n        \"adrRationale\": \"Only the schema.rs fix is architecture-level: choosing to derive schemars::JsonSchema (and hand-writing a JsonSchema impl for PropType/CollectedType/OpaqueReason to mirror the existing manual Serialize) is a decision in the same space as ADR-0002 (manual-serialize-for-prop-type) and should be recorded alongside it — it commits the project to a second hand-maintained trait impl per recursive enum whenever a variant is added, the same 'compiler won't remind you' tradeoff ADR-0002 already flagged for Serialize/Deserialize. The resolver-precedence extraction (named.rs/chain.rs) and the toon.rs truncation helper are ordinary internal refactors — easily reversible, no cross-cutting tradeoff — and don't need an ADR of their own.\",\n        \"misassignedNotes\": \"All three findings genuinely belong, but split into two sub-patterns worth naming explicitly: P0-1 and P0-4 are 'the same procedural rule reimplemented at multiple call sites without a shared function' (P0-4 is the purer case — both branches are in the *same* function, so it's a copy-paste omission, not independent evolution over time); P0-5 is 'two independent representations of the same data shape (Rust struct vs hand-written JSON Schema) with no derivation or test tying them together' — closer to the cluster's other stated frame ('two independent implementations of the same concept drifting out of sync') than to precedence/ordering specifically. Keep all three together since the root cause (no enforced single source of truth) is genuinely shared, but the fixes are three unrelated code changes, not one shared helper.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"clusterName\": \"CLI exit-code/behavior contract inconsistency across commands (P3-1)\",\n        \"rootCause\": \"The 0/1/2 exit-code contract exists only as a doc-comment convention repeated on each command function, not as a type or shared function. `ExtractionOutput` (crates/core/src/types/output.rs) has no `has_errors()`/`exit_code()` method, so \\\"does this output contain an Error-severity diagnostic\\\" is inline boilerplate reimplemented per call site: extract.rs:68-71 does `.any(|d| matches!(d.severity, ... Error))`, check.rs:21-30 does the same via two separate `.filter()` calls (duplicated, not shared - already drifted syntactically). Because this check lives in the CLI crate rather than on the core type - plausibly because crates/core's CLAUDE.md rule \\\"no terminal/display code in crates/core\\\" got over-applied to mean \\\"no exit-code logic belongs in core either\\\" - nothing forces a new command handler to remember it. watch.rs's cmd_watch returns (), not Result&lt;i32&gt; (main.rs hardcodes exit 0 for that arm), and inspect.rs's cmd_inspect never touches output.diagnostics at all - it only errors via component-not-found lookup. Both were plausibly written by copying the general command-handler shape (build_options -&gt; pipeline::extract -&gt; print) without copying the exit-code block from extract.rs/check.rs, because there is no compiler-enforced signal (return type, trait, lint) that a command producing an ExtractionOutput must decide an exit code from it.\",\n        \"mechanismProposal\": \"Add `impl ExtractionOutput { pub fn max_severity(&self) -> Option<DiagnosticSeverity>; pub fn exit_code(&self, strict: bool) -> i32 }` in crates/core/src/types/output.rs - pure data-in/data-out over an already-owned struct, same category as the existing html_element() helper right above ParsedProp, so it doesn't violate \\\"no terminal/display code in core\\\". Then require every CLI command that calls pipeline::extract to route through it: change cmd_watch's signature to return Result<i32> and have main.rs's watch arm call output.exit_code(false) after each pass (or explicitly gate propagation behind a --ci/non-interactive flag if 0-always is intentional for interactive use, documenting the tradeoff instead of leaving it silent); change cmd_inspect to also call output.exit_code(false) after a successful component lookup so an Error diagnostic elsewhere in the tree surfaces even when the inspected component itself is fine. Once exit_code() is the single place severity maps to an exit code, extract.rs and check.rs collapse their inline .any()/.filter() blocks into one call each, removing the drift, and any future command that forgets to call it is now visibly incomplete against a documented, testable contract rather than silently non-compliant.\",\n        \"findingIds\": [\n          \"P3-1\"\n        ],\n        \"adrNeeded\": false,\n        \"adrRationale\": \"This is a refactor extracting an already-existing implicit contract into one shared method, not a new architectural boundary or a hard-to-reverse decision. None of ADR 0001-0004 cover CLI exit-code semantics, and none is needed - a doc comment on ExtractionOutput::exit_code documenting the 0/1/2 mapping is sufficient, matching how the contract is already (informally) documented today.\",\n        \"misassignedNotes\": \"P3-2 (DiagnosticCode::Unknown never constructed) and the two cache/state findings (P0-2, P0-3) are unrelated to the exit-code group and don't share a mechanism story with it or with each other. Cache staleness (P0-2, cache.rs:181-191): key_for builds CacheKey{path, size, mtime_ns} straight from std::fs::metadata. This is a genuine filesystem-timestamp-resolution limitation, not a logic bug or missing abstraction - there is exactly one call site, the code already does the best available thing (nanosecond mtime plus size), and the residual risk is inherent to mtime-based cache invalidation on coarse-resolution filesystems, not something a shared helper would fix. A content hash (e.g. blake3 of the file bytes) would close the gap but changes the cache's performance/complexity tradeoff rather than being \\\"the missing shared check\\\" - worth a scoped follow-up, not a cross-cutting mechanism. Contradictory ParsedProp state (P0-3, types/output.rs:79-99): ParsedProp has all-public fields and is constructed via bare struct literals at 10 call sites across 6 files (known.rs, resolver/chain.rs, resolver/alias.rs, resolver/mod.rs, toon.rs, types/output.rs itself) with no constructor/builder and no validation, so required:true + default_value:Some(_) is representable and never checked. This is a missing type invariant, not duplicated logic that drifted - there was never a shared constructor to unify. Root cause: the rust-types skill's checklist (referenced by crates/core/CLAUDE.md) covers naming/serde/derives for new public types but has no item requiring invariant-bearing structs to be built only through a validating constructor. Fix: add ParsedProp::new(...) (or a builder) in types/output.rs that normalizes the required+default_value relationship per RDT convention (a supplied default implies optional), make the struct's fields pub(crate) to force all 10 call sites through the constructor, and add a line to the rust-types skill checklist: \\\"if a struct has two fields whose combination can be contradictory, provide a validating constructor and consider whether public fields should be private.\\\" This is a single-type fix, independent of the cache issue and independent of the exit-code cluster. DiagnosticCode::Unknown (P3-2, types/diagnostic.rs:61) is genuinely dead code, not a bug: every one of the ~19 Diagnostic-construction call sites across the resolver/extractor/pipeline picks a specific named variant (UnresolvableImport, OpaqueType, MaxDepthExceeded, etc.) because the author always knows the specific cause at the call site; Unknown exists only as headroom on a #[non_exhaustive] enum for external consumers who might synthesize a Diagnostic without a matching code. No mechanism needed - this is correctly categorized in the doc as \\\"though the enum is non_exhaustive so may be intentional headroom.\\\"\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"synthesis\": \"# Phase 0 Root-Cause Report\\n\\nFour analysis agents, four cluster assignments — but the actual root-cause count is higher because three of the four agents found their assigned cluster wasn't one mechanism, it was several. After splitting, **11 confirmed mechanism-level root causes** and **9 genuinely standalone findings** remain. The overall takeaway: this codebase has good conventions in places that got established once (a named constructor, a depth counter, a truncation pattern) but nothing makes those conventions load-bearing — they're advisory, so parallel/later code paths silently diverge from them. The fix shape repeats: turn an advisory helper into the only callable constructor, or add the one missing signal channel a class of code was never given.\\n\\n## 1. Resolver give-up constructor discipline\\n**Root cause:** `ResolvedChain::default()` and `PropType::Opaque{..}` are plain struct literals, so the named \\\"give up, record a diagnostic\\\" constructors (`empty_with_compose`, `push_opaque_diagnostic`) are optional, not enforced — 6 of ~10 Opaque construction sites and the cycle-detected path in `chain.rs:40` bypass them.\\n**Mechanism:** In `resolver/mod.rs`, make `ResolvedChain`'s bare/`Default` construction private and add `ResolvedChain::give_up(type_name, diag, state)`. Convert `PropType::Opaque{raw, reason}` to `PropType::Opaque(OpaqueDetail)` with private fields in a new `resolver/opaque.rs`, exposing only `OpaqueDetail::give_up(state, what, reason)`. Update the ~10 call sites (collected.rs, func.rs, primitives.rs, named.rs, template.rs) and the two `PropType` serde match arms (ADR 0002 territory, no new ADR needed). Add an `rg` backstop check to `/check`.\\n**Resolves:** resolver-chain-cycle-detected-default, resolver-func-multiparam-no-diagnostic, resolver-literalunion-as-props-base.\\n**ADR:** No — internal refactor of an existing pattern's enforcement, not a new boundary.\\n\\n## 2. Extractor missing give-up signal channel\\n**Root cause:** `classify_type_alias`, `extract_type_name_from_type`, and the component-detector `.or_else()` chains are `Option`-returning `?`-chains over AST shapes with no way to distinguish \\\"wrong shape, fine\\\" from \\\"matched but malformed, worth flagging\\\" — `SourceData.diagnostics` exists but was never wired to these functions.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `fn record_skip(&mut self, code: DiagnosticCode, message: impl Into<String>, span: Span)` in `extractor/mod.rs`, plus a new `DiagnosticCode::SkippedCandidate` variant in `types/diagnostic.rs`. Wire it into every malformed-but-matched arm in `alias.rs::classify_type_alias` and the end of each detector chain in `visit.rs` (lines 236-247, 262-293). This is a checklist requirement (crates/core/CLAUDE.md), not a lint — domain judgment is needed per call site.\\n**Resolves:** extractor-classify-type-alias-omit-pick-etc, extractor-extract-props-arg-terminal-none, extractor-failed-detector-silence, extractor-computed-property-keys. (New-detector coverage — anonymous default export, class components, `Object.assign`, class-expressions, `satisfies` — is a separate feature-gap task; record_skip only makes the existing miss visible.)\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps\\n**Root cause:** Three independent bugs, not one mechanism: `discover_files` has no diagnostic-return channel at all (`walker.flatten()` drops `Err`s); `mod.rs:220`'s empty-`src_dirs` guard is a plain `&&`-short-circuit logic error; `mod.rs:386`'s `components.insert` discards the `Option<V>` that already reveals a collision.\\n**Mechanism:** Give `discover_files` a `&mut Vec<Diagnostic>` param and push on `Err`/non-UTF8-path fallback (reuse the `Diagnostic` constructor pattern at `mod.rs:269-272`). Fix the `&&` guard at `mod.rs:220` and add a `src_dirs: []` regression test. Check `components.insert`'s return at `mod.rs:386` and push a collision diagnostic on `Some(_)`.\\n**Resolves:** pipeline-permission-denied-files, pipeline-non-utf8-filenames, pipeline-empty-src-dirs, pipeline-same-name-same-file-collision, pipeline-overlapping-src-dirs.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 4. No panic-containment boundary\\n**Root cause:** Zero `catch_unwind` in the repo. The three async NAPI entry points are only panic-safe by accident of running inside `tokio::spawn_blocking`; `create_session`/`close_session` skip that entirely, `plugin.rs` hooks run with no isolation, and rayon `.map()` in `pipeline/mod.rs:260,357` has no per-item boundary — so `watch.rs`'s `std::sync::Mutex` can permanently poison.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `crates/core/src/panic_guard.rs`: `pub(crate) fn contain_panic<T>(label: &str, f: impl FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe) -> Result<T, Diagnostic>`, plus `DiagnosticCode::InternalPanic`. Wrap each rayon closure body in `pipeline/mod.rs:260,357`; wrap each `plugin.on_x(...)` call individually inside `plugin.rs`'s loops (not the whole loop); wrap `create_session`/`close_session` bodies in `crates/binding/src/lib.rs`, and have the other three NAPI entry points call it inside their `spawn_blocking` closures too. Defense-in-depth: replace `watch.rs:93`'s `.expect(\\\"init lock poisoned\\\")` with `.unwrap_or_else(|p| p.into_inner())`. Document in crates/core/CLAUDE.md.\\n**Resolves:** P1-3, P1-4, P1-5, P1-9.\\n**ADR: Yes — 0005-panic-containment-boundary.md.**\\n*Context:* No existing ADR covers panic/unwind policy; safety today is an accident of tokio's `JoinError`, inconsistent across NAPI entry points, plugin hooks, and rayon batches, and getting the granularity wrong (per-batch vs per-file/per-plugin) is expensive to fix retroactively.\\n*Decision:* Panics reachable from a rayon `.map()`, a `DocgenPlugin` impl, or a NAPI entry point are contained at per-file/per-plugin/per-call granularity through the single `panic_guard::contain_panic` helper, converting the payload into a `Diagnostic` (or `napi::Error`) instead of aborting a batch, killing the pipeline, or poisoning a session lock.\\n\\n## 5. Unbounded allocation from input-derived size\\n**Root cause:** `extractor/mod.rs` already solved this shape (`MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH`, with a doc comment explaining why) but nobody generalized or cross-referenced it when `template.rs`'s Cartesian-product loop or `lsp.rs`'s header parsing were written later.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `const MAX_TEMPLATE_LITERAL_EXPANSIONS: usize = 4096` checked inside `try_expand_template_literal`'s accumulation loop (`resolver/template.rs`), falling through to the existing Opaque+diagnostic path. Add `const MAX_LSP_MESSAGE_BYTES` gating `vec![0u8; len]` in `cli/commands/lsp.rs:36`. No shared function across crates — add a code-review checklist line instead: \\\"does this size come from parsed/protocol input, and is it capped?\\\"\\n**Resolves:** P1-1, P1-2.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 6. LSP scaffold immaturity\\n**Root cause:** `lsp.rs` is a new, zero-test, hand-rolled framing layer with no capability/handler consistency check.\\n**Mechanism:** Don't advertise `hoverProvider: true` until a handler exists; make the `content_length: None` branch resync (consume to next blank line) instead of `continue`; longer-term, adopt `lsp-server`/`lsp-types`.\\n**Resolves:** P1-6, P1-7.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 7. Extractor depth-tracking proxy mismatch\\n**Root cause:** `max_bracket_nesting_depth` bounds raw-text bracket depth as a stand-in for AST recursion, but `ts_type_to_collected` recurses with no depth counter of its own — unlike the resolver's `resolve_*` functions, which already thread an explicit `depth: u8`.\\n**Mechanism:** Thread a depth counter through `ts_type_to_collected` and sibling functions in `extractor/mod.rs`, bailing with a Diagnostic past e.g. depth 500, matching the resolver's existing convention.\\n**Resolves:** P1-8.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 8. Resolver precedence: two hand-copied orderings\\n**Root cause:** `named.rs` documents \\\"check source-defined types before known-pattern shortcuts\\\" as intentional, but `chain.rs`'s `extends`-clause path re-implements the sequence independently and gets the order backwards. Nothing ties the two together.\\n**Mechanism:** Extract the shared precedence logic into `resolve_source_defined_or_known` in `resolver/mod.rs` (or new `resolver/precedence.rs`): fixed order `resolve_to_canonical → lookup_type_alias → lookup_interface → resolve_known`, taking closures for the differing terminal handling. Both `named.rs` and `chain.rs` call it. Add a regression fixture: a project-defined `interface SxProps` extended via `extends`, asserting no MUI-shortcut substitution.\\n**Resolves:** P0-1.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 9. TOON truncation indicator dropped in one branch\\n**Root cause:** `LiteralUnion`'s truncate-with-`\\\"...(+N)\\\"` pattern wasn't factored into a helper, so `Union`/`Intersection` (same function, `toon.rs`) reimplemented truncation without the indicator half.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `fn truncate_with_indicator(parts: &[String], limit: usize, sep: &str) -> String` in `toon.rs`; route `LiteralUnion`, `Union`, `Intersection` through it.\\n**Resolves:** P0-4.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## 10. Schema hand-maintained separately from real structs\\n**Root cause:** `cli/commands/schema.rs` hand-writes JSON Schema as a `json!()` literal instead of deriving it; the only test checks syntactic validity, never drift against real serialized output.\\n**Mechanism:** Derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on `ComponentEntry`, `ParsedProp`, `ExtractionStats`, `Diagnostic`, `InheritedLayer`; call `schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput)` from `cmd_schema()`; hand-write `JsonSchema` impls for `PropType`/`CollectedType`/`OpaqueReason` following ADR-0002's precedent for `Serialize`. If schemars is undesirable, floor-level fix: a CI test serializing an existing fixture and validating it against `schema.rs`'s output.\\n**Resolves:** P0-5.\\n**ADR: Yes** — record alongside ADR-0002 as a follow-up decision (a second hand-maintained trait impl per recursive enum whenever a variant is added). *Decision:* derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on plain output structs and hand-write matching impls for the three recursive enums already exempted from derive by ADR-0002, replacing the hand-written `json!()` schema literal.\\n\\n## 11. CLI exit-code contract has no shared type\\n**Root cause:** The 0/1/2 exit-code mapping is a doc-comment convention re-typed per command (`extract.rs:68-71`, `check.rs:21-30`, syntactically already diverged); `watch.rs` returns `()` and `inspect.rs` never checks diagnostics at all, because nothing compiler-enforces that a command producing `ExtractionOutput` decides an exit code from it.\\n**Mechanism:** Add `ExtractionOutput::max_severity()` / `exit_code(&self, strict: bool) -> i32` in `core/types/output.rs`. Route `extract.rs`, `check.rs` through it (collapsing their inline checks); change `cmd_watch` to return `Result<i32>` and call it per pass; call it in `cmd_inspect` after a successful lookup.\\n**Resolves:** P3-1.\\n**ADR:** No.\\n\\n## Standalone findings (no shared mechanism — treat as independent tasks)\\n- `substitute.rs:33-43` — generic alias `.zip()` silently truncates trailing unsubstituted type params (silent-correctness bug, not a give-up path).\\n- `watch.rs:107-112` — `--out` write failure discarded via `let _ =`; route through the CLI's existing miette/output.rs error path. Same file: also fix the non-atomic `fs::write` (write-to-temp + rename).\\n- `cache.rs:181-191` (P0-2) — mtime/size cache key is a coarse-filesystem-resolution limitation, not a logic bug; scoped follow-up (content hash) if warranted, no shared mechanism.\\n- `types/output.rs:79-99` (P0-3) — `ParsedProp` allows contradictory `required:true` + `default_value:Some`; add `ParsedProp::new(...)` validating constructor, make fields `pub(crate)`, add a rust-types skill checklist line for invariant-bearing structs.\\n- `types/diagnostic.rs:61` (P3-2) — `DiagnosticCode::Unknown` unused but intentional headroom on a `#[non_exhaustive]` enum; no action.\\n- `types/output.rs:314,423` — NaN/Infinity `NumberLiteral` round-trips to `0.0`; low-priority, likely unreachable.\\n- Zero-member `LiteralUnion` serializes as an empty enum; low-priority.\\n\\n## Phase 1 task breakdown\\n1. Resolver constructor discipline (touches `resolver/mod.rs`, `chain.rs`, `func.rs`, `collected.rs`, `primitives.rs`, `named.rs`, `template.rs`, new `resolver/opaque.rs`, `types/output.rs`) — **sequential with #5 and #8** (shared `template.rs`/`chain.rs`/`named.rs`).\\n2. Extractor diagnostic channel (`extractor/mod.rs`, `alias.rs`, `visit.rs`, `types/diagnostic.rs`) — **sequential with #4 and #7** (shared `extractor/mod.rs`, `types/diagnostic.rs`).\\n3. Pipeline discovery/merge fixes (`pipeline/discover.rs`, `pipeline/mod.rs`) — **sequential with #4** (shared `pipeline/mod.rs`).\\n4. Panic-containment boundary + ADR 0005 (new `panic_guard.rs`, `pipeline/mod.rs`, `plugin.rs`, `crates/binding/src/lib.rs`, `pipeline/watch.rs`, `types/diagnostic.rs`) — do first if possible, other tasks touching same files should rebase on it.\\n5. Allocation caps (`resolver/template.rs`, `cli/commands/lsp.rs`) — parallel-safe except vs #1/#6.\\n6. LSP scaffold hardening (`cli/commands/lsp.rs`) — merge with #5 (same file).\\n7. Extractor depth-tracking (`extractor/mod.rs`) — merge with #2.\\n8. Resolver precedence extraction (`resolver/mod.rs` or new file, `named.rs`, `chain.rs`) — merge with #1.\\n9. TOON truncation helper (`toon.rs`) — fully parallel, disjoint.\\n10. Schema derivation (`cli/commands/schema.rs`, `core/types/*.rs` derives) — parallel-safe, additive derives only.\\n11. CLI exit-code contract (`core/types/output.rs`, `extract.rs`, `check.rs`, `watch.rs`, `inspect.rs`, `main.rs`) — parallel-safe.\\n12. Standalone fixes (`substitute.rs`, `watch.rs`, `cache.rs`, `types/output.rs` ParsedProp ctor) — each independently parallel, but `watch.rs` and `types/output.rs` tasks should merge with #4/#1 respectively if scheduled concurrently.\"\n  },\n  \"workflowProgress\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"workflow_phase\",\n      \"index\": 1,\n      \"title\": \"Cluster Analysis\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"workflow_phase\",\n      \"index\": 2,\n      \"title\": \"Synthesize\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n      \"index\": 1,\n      \"label\": \"silent-degrade\",\n      \"phaseIndex\": 1,\n      \"phaseTitle\": \"Cluster Analysis\",\n      \"agentId\": \"a30ff11631cc17ab8\",\n      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n      \"state\": \"done\",\n      \"startedAt\": 1785863565606,\n      \"queuedAt\": 1785863562301,\n      \"attempt\": 1,\n      \"lastToolName\": \"StructuredOutput\",\n      \"lastToolSummary\": \"Give-up-silently: graceful-degradation paths that skip the …\",\n      \"promptPreview\": \"Read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md first for full context — it's a comprehensive audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript) covering crash risks, silent data loss, and correctness bugs across the whole pipeline. Also read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-…\",\n      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863765917,\n      \"tokens\": 61393,\n      \"toolCalls\": 18,\n      \"durationMs\": 200311,\n      \"resultPreview\": \"{\\\"clusterName\\\":\\\"Give-up-silently: graceful-degradation paths that skip the Diagnostic pairing\\\",\\\"rootCause\\\":\\\"Verified against actual code (resolver/chain.rs, resolver/func.rs, resolver/collected.rs, extractor/alias.rs, extractor/mod.rs, extractor/visit.rs, pipeline/discover.rs, pipeline/mod.rs). This is not one root cause but three, stratified by whether a shared mechanism already exists:\\\\n\\\\n(1) RE…\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n      \"index\": 2,\n      \"label\": \"panic-containment\",\n      \"phaseIndex\": 1,\n      \"phaseTitle\": \"Cluster Analysis\",\n      \"agentId\": \"ad8efce0df55a3924\",\n      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n      \"state\": \"done\",\n      \"startedAt\": 1785863564614,\n      \"queuedAt\": 1785863562301,\n      \"attempt\": 1,\n      \"lastToolName\": \"StructuredOutput\",\n      \"lastToolSummary\": \"No panic-boundary convention: unwind containment is acciden…\",\n      \"promptPreview\": \"Read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md first for full context — it's a comprehensive audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript) covering crash risks, silent data loss, and correctness bugs across the whole pipeline. Also read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-…\",\n      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863788655,\n      \"tokens\": 65635,\n      \"toolCalls\": 13,\n      \"durationMs\": 224041,\n      \"resultPreview\": \"{\\\"clusterName\\\":\\\"No panic-boundary convention: unwind containment is accidental, not designed\\\",\\\"rootCause\\\":\\\"grep confirms zero uses of catch_unwind anywhere in the repo. The three async NAPI entry points (extract_all, extract_file_incremental, initialize_session) are only 'safe' from a panic today because they happen to run inside tokio::task::spawn_blocking, whose JoinHandle converts a panic into …\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n      \"index\": 3,\n      \"label\": \"duplicated-logic-drift\",\n      \"phaseIndex\": 1,\n      \"phaseTitle\": \"Cluster Analysis\",\n      \"agentId\": \"a8c2dba27b5dbe6dc\",\n      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n      \"state\": \"done\",\n      \"startedAt\": 1785863564865,\n      \"queuedAt\": 1785863562301,\n      \"attempt\": 1,\n      \"lastToolName\": \"StructuredOutput\",\n      \"lastToolSummary\": \"Duplicated invariants with no single callable source of tru…\",\n      \"promptPreview\": \"Read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md first for full context — it's a comprehensive audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript) covering crash risks, silent data loss, and correctness bugs across the whole pipeline. Also read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-…\",\n      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863694859,\n      \"tokens\": 50069,\n      \"toolCalls\": 11,\n      \"durationMs\": 129994,\n      \"resultPreview\": \"{\\\"clusterName\\\":\\\"Duplicated invariants with no single callable source of truth (precedence rules, truncation formatting, and struct-to-schema mirroring all hand-copied instead of shared)\\\",\\\"rootCause\\\":\\\"In all three findings, an invariant that must hold identically across two or more locations is expressed as inline, ad hoc code plus an explanatory comment at each site, rather than as one named funct…\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n      \"index\": 4,\n      \"label\": \"contract-and-cache-correctness\",\n      \"phaseIndex\": 1,\n      \"phaseTitle\": \"Cluster Analysis\",\n      \"agentId\": \"a658bd73151e35be4\",\n      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n      \"state\": \"done\",\n      \"startedAt\": 1785863564872,\n      \"queuedAt\": 1785863562301,\n      \"attempt\": 1,\n      \"lastToolName\": \"StructuredOutput\",\n      \"lastToolSummary\": \"CLI exit-code/behavior contract inconsistency across comman…\",\n      \"promptPreview\": \"Read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md first for full context — it's a comprehensive audit of oxc-react-docgen (a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript) covering crash risks, silent data loss, and correctness bugs across the whole pipeline. Also read /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-…\",\n      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863678294,\n      \"tokens\": 44692,\n      \"toolCalls\": 14,\n      \"durationMs\": 113422,\n      \"resultPreview\": \"{\\\"clusterName\\\":\\\"CLI exit-code/behavior contract inconsistency across commands (P3-1)\\\",\\\"rootCause\\\":\\\"The 0/1/2 exit-code contract exists only as a doc-comment convention repeated on each command function, not as a type or shared function. `ExtractionOutput` (crates/core/src/types/output.rs) has no `has_errors()`/`exit_code()` method, so \\\\\\\"does this output contain an Error-severity diagnostic\\\\\\\" is in…\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"workflow_agent\",\n      \"index\": 5,\n      \"label\": \"synthesis\",\n      \"phaseIndex\": 2,\n      \"phaseTitle\": \"Synthesize\",\n      \"agentId\": \"a7c04cf36f291ca4f\",\n      \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5\",\n      \"state\": \"done\",\n      \"startedAt\": 1785863795360,\n      \"queuedAt\": 1785863791186,\n      \"attempt\": 1,\n      \"lastToolName\": \"Read\",\n      \"lastToolSummary\": \"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/adr/README.md\",\n      \"promptPreview\": \"You are synthesizing a Phase 0 architectural root-cause analysis for oxc-react-docgen into a final report. Below are the outputs from 4 independent analysis agents, each examining a different candidate root-cause cluster behind the findings in /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md.\\n\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\\"clusterName\\\": \\\"Give-up-silently: graceful-degradation paths that skip the Diagnostic …\",\n      \"lastProgressAt\": 1785863879958,\n      \"tokens\": 41885,\n      \"toolCalls\": 2,\n      \"durationMs\": 84598,\n      \"resultPreview\": \"# Phase 0 Root-Cause Report\\n\\nFour analysis agents, four cluster assignments — but the actual root-cause count is higher because three of the four agents found their assigned cluster wasn't one mechanism, it was several. After splitting, **11 confirmed mechanism-level root causes** and **9 genuinely standalone findings** remain. The overall takeaway: this codebase has good conventions in places tha…\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"totalTokens\": 263674,\n  \"totalToolCalls\": 58\n}","numLines":193,"startLine":1,"totalLines":193}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f3e970de-7ac6-42f2-b887-36ff6f83cf45","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
