{"parentUuid":"8a44bceb-39b9-40e0-8621-d8c7d63216e8","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi7RTKVCza2vKQ6ZXsAS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":406,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"output_tokens":13647,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":406,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":13647,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"cache_creation_input_tokens":406,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":406},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdi7RQttDu8DzDg8ABS5U","attributionSkill":"superpowers:writing-plans","attributionPlugin":"superpowers","type":"assistant","uuid":"da82349a-2a98-4013-874f-335cdca5c5cc","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:35:32.940Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"da82349a-2a98-4013-874f-335cdca5c5cc","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi7RTKVCza2vKQ6ZXsAS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CsesuQdSVrpwYUcz99A5Hv","name":"Workflow","input":{"script":"export const meta = {\n  name: 'edge-case-plan-drafting',\n  description: 'Phase 1: draft TDD implementation tasks (real code, real file paths) for each root-cause cluster from root-cause-analysis.md',\n  phases: [\n    { title: 'Draft Tasks', detail: 'one agent per cluster group, reads real code, writes bite-sized TDD steps' },\n  ],\n}\n\nconst CONTEXT = `Project: oxc-react-docgen — a Rust tool that parses TSX/TS with OXC and extracts React component prop metadata, a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript.\n\nRead these first, in full, before writing anything:\n1. /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/root-cause-analysis.md — the root-cause findings and mechanism proposals (your assignment is one section of this).\n2. /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/CLAUDE.md and /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/crates/core/CLAUDE.md and /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/crates/cli/CLAUDE.md — non-negotiables: no unwrap()/expect() outside #[cfg(test)], FxHashMap for internal maps / BTreeMap for JSON-output maps, CompactString for type/prop names in hot paths, no AST refs escape parse_file(), always emit a Diagnostic when degrading, no terminal/display code in crates/core, no println! in CLI command handlers (output.rs/tracing only).\n3. Then READ THE ACTUAL CURRENT SOURCE at every file/line cited in your assigned section below — do not trust the root-cause doc's summary of what the code looks like, verify it yourself and use the REAL current code in your task drafts. If the doc's line numbers are slightly off (files may have shifted since the audit), find the actual current location.\n\nYour job: write this as a set of bite-sized, TDD implementation tasks in EXACTLY this format (this project uses the writing-plans skill convention):\n\n### Task N: [Component Name]\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: \\`exact/path/to/file.rs\\`\n- Modify: \\`exact/path/to/existing.rs:123-145\\`\n- Test: inline #[cfg(test)] module in the same file, OR \\`crates/core/tests/whatever.rs\\` if that's this project's convention for this kind of test (check how similar existing tests are structured first)\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**\n(real, complete Rust test code — not a description of a test)\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run test to verify it fails**\nRun: \\`cargo test -p oxc-react-docgen-core <test_name> -- --nocapture\\` (or the cli-crate equivalent)\nExpected: FAIL with [specific reason]\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Write minimal implementation**\n(real, complete Rust code — not a description)\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Run test to verify it passes**\nRun: [command]\nExpected: PASS\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**\n\\`\\`\\`bash\ngit add [exact files]\ngit commit -m \"[type]: [message]\"\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nRULES (violating these is a plan failure, not a style nit):\n- No placeholders. Never write \"TBD\", \"add appropriate error handling\", \"write tests for the above\", \"similar to Task N\" — every step needs the actual code.\n- Every step changing code shows the code in a fenced block.\n- Reference the REAL current function signatures, struct fields, and match arms you read from the actual files — if you're not 100% sure of an exact signature, say so explicitly in a one-line note rather than guessing silently.\n- Order tasks so earlier tasks in your group don't depend on later ones where avoidable; note explicit dependencies between your own tasks if unavoidable.\n- Follow this project's existing test conventions (inline #[cfg(test)] modules are the norm in crates/core per what you'll see reading the files — match that unless the file already uses a separate test file).\n- Every new public type/function should follow this project's rust-style conventions you'll see in the surrounding code (let-else, full match with no wildcards, newtypes over bare bools, FxHashMap/CompactString discipline).\n\nReturn ONLY the markdown task blocks for your group (### Task N: ... through the Step 5 commit block, one after another) — no preamble, no summary, just the tasks ready to paste into a larger plan document. Number your tasks starting from 1 within your own group (they'll be renumbered when merged).`\n\nconst GROUPS = [\n  {\n    name: 'panic-containment',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assigned cluster: #4 — No panic-containment boundary (root-cause-analysis.md section \"4. No panic-containment boundary\")\n\nRoot cause: zero catch_unwind anywhere in the repo. The three async NAPI entry points are only panic-safe by accident of tokio::spawn_blocking's JoinError. pipeline/mod.rs's rayon .map() closures (around line 260 and 357), plugin.rs's hook-invocation loops (around pipeline/mod.rs:293,385 calling into plugin.rs), and crates/binding/src/lib.rs's create_session/close_session (around lines 132-138, 193-195) all lack any panic boundary. pipeline/watch.rs:93's std::sync::Mutex .expect(\"init lock poisoned\") can wedge a session permanently as a direct consequence.\n\nMechanism: add crates/core/src/panic_guard.rs with pub(crate) fn contain_panic<T>(label: &str, f: impl FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe) -> Result<T, Diagnostic>, plus a new DiagnosticCode::InternalPanic variant in types/diagnostic.rs. Wrap the rayon closure bodies in pipeline/mod.rs. Wrap each individual plugin.on_x(...) call in plugin.rs's loops (not the whole loop) so one bad plugin only degrades itself — tag the resulting Diagnostic with the plugin's name (check DocgenPlugin trait for a name()/similar method, or note if one needs adding). Wrap create_session/close_session bodies in crates/binding/src/lib.rs, and have the other three NAPI entry points (extract_all, extract_file_incremental, initialize_session) call contain_panic inside their existing spawn_blocking closures too. Replace watch.rs:93's .expect(...) with .unwrap_or_else(|p| p.into_inner()).\n\nThis cluster also needs ADR 0005 written for real: read docs/adr/0000-template.md and docs/adr/0002-manual-serialize-for-prop-type.md for the exact format/tone this project uses (no hard line-wrapping, active voice, concrete numbers, cut filler — banned words: leverage/utilize/robust/seamless/cutting-edge/essentially/basically/in order to/it's worth noting). The draft Context/Decision/Consequences already exist in root-cause-analysis.md's \"Proposed: 0005\" section — turn that into the real file content (docs/adr/0005-panic-containment-boundary.md, Status: Accepted, today's date) as part of your task list (one task: \"write and commit the ADR file\", with the exact file content as the code block, after the implementation tasks land).\n\nWrite your task group now. Expect roughly 5-7 tasks: panic_guard module + its own unit test, DiagnosticCode::InternalPanic variant, rayon-batch wrapping (parse phase), rayon-batch wrapping (resolve phase), plugin-hook wrapping, NAPI wrapping (all 5 entry points), watch.rs mutex fix, ADR file.`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'resolver-constructors-and-precedence',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assigned clusters: #1 (resolver give-up constructor discipline) and #8 (resolver precedence: two hand-copied orderings) from root-cause-analysis.md — merge these into one task group since they touch the same files (resolver/mod.rs, chain.rs, named.rs).\n\nCluster #1 root cause: ResolvedChain::default() and PropType::Opaque { raw, reason } are plain struct literals bypassing the named \"give up, record a diagnostic\" constructors (ResolvedChain::empty_with_compose, collected.rs::push_opaque_diagnostic). Bare-default sites found: chain.rs's cycle-detected path (around line 40), func.rs's multi-param function type degrade (around lines 54-61), primitives.rs's indexed-access fallback (around line 181), template.rs's template-literal opaque fallback (around line 39), plus check named.rs for any similar site.\n\nCluster #1 mechanism: in resolver/mod.rs, make ResolvedChain's bare/Default construction private (keep a private empty() used only inside empty_with_compose); add ResolvedChain::give_up(type_name: String, diag: Option<Diagnostic>, state: &mut ResolveState) -> Self as the sanctioned \"give up\" constructor, replacing the chain.rs:40 bare default. Convert PropType::Opaque { raw, reason } to a single-field tuple variant PropType::Opaque(OpaqueDetail) with OpaqueDetail's fields private in a new resolver/opaque.rs, exposing only OpaqueDetail::give_up(state, what, reason) -> PropType (folding push_opaque_diagnostic's behavior into the constructor). Update every construction site across collected.rs, func.rs, primitives.rs, named.rs, template.rs, AND both match arms of PropType's manual to_json_value/from_json_value (find these — likely in types/output.rs, per ADR-0002 the wire format doesn't change, only the Rust constructor surface and the match arm's field access pattern).\n\nCluster #8 root cause: named.rs correctly checks source-defined types before known-pattern shortcuts (with a comment explaining why); chain.rs's extends-clause resolution path (reached from ExtendsRef::SameFile and ExtendsRef::Imported, around lines 90-106) re-implements the same sequence independently and checks known-pattern shortcuts FIRST — the opposite, incorrect order. This is the single most concrete confirmed bug in the whole audit (P0-1).\n\nCluster #8 mechanism: extract the shared \"try source-defined before falling back to known shortcuts\" logic into one function in resolver/mod.rs (or a new resolver/precedence.rs) — read named.rs's actual current logic (function likely named something like resolve_named, check for the exact steps/order) and chain.rs's extends-handling function to determine the right shared signature (something like fn resolve_source_defined_or_known<T>(name, consuming_file, ctx, state, depth, on_type_alias: impl FnOnce(...) -> T, on_interface: impl FnOnce(...) -> T, on_known: impl FnOnce(...) -> T) -> Option<T>, adjust based on what you actually find) with the fixed order: try canonical resolution → type alias lookup → interface lookup → only then known-pattern shortcuts. Both named.rs and chain.rs call it. Add a regression fixture: a project defining its own \\`interface SxProps { ... }\\` extended via \\`extends\\`, asserting the hardcoded MUI/React-Aria shortcut is NOT substituted — this is the most important test in this whole plan since it's a confirmed real bug, make sure the test would have failed before your fix and passes after.\n\nWrite your task group now. Expect roughly 6-9 tasks covering: the ResolvedChain constructor change, the PropType::Opaque restructure (may need 2-3 tasks given the number of call sites), the shared precedence-extraction function, wiring named.rs through it, wiring chain.rs through it (this is the bug fix — should have its own dedicated failing-test-first task using the SxProps regression fixture described above).`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'extractor-diagnostics-and-depth',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assigned clusters: #2 (extractor missing give-up signal channel) and #7 (extractor depth-tracking proxy mismatch) from root-cause-analysis.md — merge since both touch extractor/mod.rs.\n\nCluster #2 root cause: classify_type_alias (in extractor/alias.rs), extract_type_name_from_type (in extractor/mod.rs), and the component-detector .or_else() chains in extractor/visit.rs (around lines 236-247 for the PascalCase-binding chain, 262-293 for visit_function) are Option-returning ?-chains over AST shapes with no way to signal \"matched but malformed, worth flagging\" vs \"wrong shape, fine.\" SourceData.diagnostics already exists and is used elsewhere in extractor/mod.rs (the nesting-depth guard, parse errors — find these exact call sites to match the existing Diagnostic-construction style).\n\nCluster #2 mechanism: add fn record_skip(&mut self, code: DiagnosticCode, message: impl Into<String>, span: Span) in extractor/mod.rs (on whatever struct/visitor holds SourceData/diagnostics — read the file to find the right impl block), plus a new DiagnosticCode::SkippedCandidate variant in types/diagnostic.rs (check the existing enum for style — likely #[non_exhaustive], check severity conventions used for similar \"we chose not to support this\" cases, probably Info or Warning). Wire it into: classify_type_alias's Omit/Pick/Partial/Required/Readonly arms in alias.rs where they currently \\`return None\\` via \\`?\\` on missing/malformed type arguments (read the actual current arms to find exact insertion points); and the end of both detector chains in visit.rs (add an else-branch/final .or_else() that calls record_skip before giving up, instead of silent fallthrough).\n\nCluster #7 root cause: extractor/mod.rs's nesting guard (look for MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH or max_bracket_nesting_depth, around lines 38-40 and 105-120) counts raw-text bracket depth as a proxy for AST recursion depth, but ts_type_to_collected (around lines 311-471) recurses with no depth counter of its own — unlike the resolver's resolve_* functions which already thread an explicit depth: u8 parameter (check resolver/template.rs or similar for the exact pattern to mirror).\n\nCluster #7 mechanism: thread a depth counter through ts_type_to_collected and its sibling recursive functions in extractor/mod.rs, bailing with a diagnostic (use record_skip from cluster #2, or a dedicated diagnostic if that fits better — your call, but be consistent) past a reasonable depth limit (proposal was 500, adjust if the resolver's actual MAX_DEPTH constant suggests a different reasonable number — check resolver/mod.rs for its MAX_DEPTH value for consistency). Write a test using deeply chained conditional types (A extends B ? C extends D ? ... : ... : ...) to prove this actually triggers where the bracket-counting heuristic would miss it — construct a source fixture with enough chained conditionals to exceed your chosen limit but with a bracket count that would NOT trip the existing max_bracket_nesting_depth guard, to prove this is closing a real gap not just adding redundant coverage.\n\nWrite your task group now. Expect roughly 6-8 tasks: record_skip helper + DiagnosticCode::SkippedCandidate variant, wiring into classify_type_alias's several arms (may be 1-2 tasks depending on how many arms), wiring into visit.rs's two detector chains (1-2 tasks), the depth-counter threading through ts_type_to_collected with its adversarial conditional-type test.`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'pipeline-discovery-merge',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assigned cluster: #3 — Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps, from root-cause-analysis.md.\n\nThree independent bugs in pipeline/discover.rs and pipeline/mod.rs — read both files in full.\n\nBug A: discover_files (pipeline/discover.rs, currently returns a bare Vec<Utf8PathBuf> per the file's current signature — verify this) uses walker.flatten() (around line 17) which silently drops every ignore::Walk Err (permission denied, broken symlink, I/O error mid-walk). Also around lines 24 and 41, non-UTF8 filenames get silently dropped at the path_str/Utf8PathBuf::from_path_buf conversion. Fix: give discover_files a way to report these — either change its signature to return (Vec<Utf8PathBuf>, Vec<Diagnostic>) or take a &mut Vec<Diagnostic> param (pick whichever fits the actual call site in pipeline/mod.rs better after reading it), and push a Diagnostic (reuse whatever constructor pattern pipeline/mod.rs already uses around lines 269-272 for read failures — read that to match the style, likely something like Diagnostic::io_read_error or similar) on both the Err case and the non-UTF8-path fallback case.\n\nBug B: pipeline/mod.rs around line 220 has a guard shaped like \\`if !options.src_dirs.is_empty() && missing_src_dirs.len() == options.src_dirs.len()\\` — read the actual current code, the \\`&&\\`-short-circuit means an explicitly empty src_dirs list bypasses the \"all src dirs missing\" diagnostic entirely, producing a silent zero-file, zero-diagnostic run. Fix the condition so an empty src_dirs also produces a diagnostic (or an explicit, deliberate no-op with its own clear diagnostic explaining why, if empty-on-purpose should be silent-but-explained rather than error — read the surrounding code/comments to judge which behavior was actually intended, then make it explicit either way).\n\nBug C: pipeline/mod.rs around line 386, \\`components.insert(key, entry)\\` (read the actual line — find the real dedup/insert call) discards the Option<ComponentEntry> return value that FxHashMap/BTreeMap's insert() already gives you, which would reveal a same-key collision. Fix: capture the return value, and on Some(_) (a collision), push a Diagnostic noting the display name and both file paths involved. This one fix should resolve both the same-name-same-file collision finding AND the overlapping-src_dirs-causes-duplicate-parse finding, since they both manifest as this same insert-time collision.\n\nWrite your task group now. Expect roughly 5-6 tasks: discover_files diagnostic-reporting change + test for a permission-denied subtree (may need a temp dir with restricted permissions, or find how this project's existing discover.rs tests set up fixtures) + test for a non-UTF8 filename, the empty-src_dirs fix + regression test with src_dirs: [] asserting a diagnostic is emitted, the components.insert collision-diagnostic fix + a test using overlapping src_dirs (e.g. [\"./src\", \"./src/components\"] equivalent) proving only one collision diagnostic-bearing component survives instead of a silent second copy.`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'allocation-caps-and-lsp',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assigned clusters: #5 (unbounded allocation from input-derived size) and #6 (LSP scaffold immaturity) from root-cause-analysis.md — merge since both touch cli/commands/lsp.rs.\n\nCluster #5, part A: resolver/template.rs's try_expand_template_literal (around lines 94-104) computes an unbounded Cartesian product across template-literal parts with no size cap — read the actual current loop structure. Fix: add a const MAX_TEMPLATE_LITERAL_EXPANSIONS (propose 4096, adjust if you find a more fitting number given the actual accumulator logic) checked inside the accumulation loop; on exceeding it, fall through to whatever this function's existing Opaque+diagnostic degrade path is (read resolve_template_literal or whatever the caller is to find the existing pattern — this project's convention per the CLAUDE.md non-negotiable is to always degrade with a diagnostic, so use the same OpaqueDetail::give_up pattern if the resolver-constructors-and-precedence task group's PropType::Opaque(OpaqueDetail) change has landed, otherwise note the dependency explicitly and use the current PropType::Opaque{raw,reason} shape as a fallback). Test: a template literal type with enough parts × union-member sizes to exceed your cap, asserting it degrades gracefully instead of hanging/OOMing (you obviously can't literally let it hang in a test — construct a case that would take an impractically long time or huge memory without the cap, and assert the capped behavior kicks in before that).\n\nCluster #5, part B: cli/commands/lsp.rs around line 36 does something like vec![0u8; len] where len comes from a parsed but unbounded Content-Length header. Fix: add a const MAX_LSP_MESSAGE_BYTES (propose a few MB, e.g. 16 * 1024 * 1024) and reject/error before allocating if the parsed length exceeds it — read the actual current header-parsing code to find the right insertion point and figure out what \"reject\" should mean in context (return an error to the caller, log and skip the message, etc. — match whatever error-handling convention the rest of lsp.rs uses).\n\nCluster #6: cli/commands/lsp.rs advertises hoverProvider: true in its initialize response (around line 56) with no hover handler anywhere — unhandled methods fall into a silent _ => {} catch-all (around line 78) that sends no response, hanging a client's textDocument/hover request indefinitely since it has an id. Also around lines 32-34, content_length: None after the header loop just continues without consuming a body, which can desync the stream on a malformed/headerless frame. Fix hoverProvider: false (or implement a minimal real hover handler if that's not too large a scope — your judgment; the simpler and more honest fix given this is explicitly a scaffold is to stop advertising a capability with no handler, note this as the recommended choice but flag if you think a minimal hover implementation is actually in scope). Fix the content_length: None branch to actually resync (consume/discard bytes up to the next blank line, or close the connection with an error) instead of silently continuing past an unconsumed body.\n\nWrite your task group now. Expect roughly 5-6 tasks: template-literal expansion cap + adversarial test, LSP Content-Length cap + test with an oversized header, hoverProvider capability fix + test asserting no hover handler is advertised (or that one now exists and responds, if you chose to implement one), malformed-header resync fix + test with a headerless/malformed frame proving the stream doesn't desync.`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'toon-schema-cli-contract',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assigned clusters: #9 (TOON truncation indicator), #10 (schema hand-maintained separately) and #11 (CLI exit-code contract) from root-cause-analysis.md — these are three unrelated but each small/self-contained fixes across toon.rs, cli/commands/schema.rs, and core/types/output.rs + several cli/commands/*.rs files. Group them together since none is large enough to warrant its own agent.\n\nCluster #9: toon.rs's format_type_compact function has a LiteralUnion branch that correctly truncates to some limit with a \"...(+N)\" indicator, but the Union/Intersection branches (same function) truncate (likely via .take(4) or similar — read the actual current code around lines 110-132) with NO indicator. Fix: add fn truncate_with_indicator(parts: &[String], limit: usize, sep: &str) -> String in toon.rs (build it from the LiteralUnion branch's existing correct logic) and route all three (LiteralUnion, Union, Intersection) through it. Test: a Union/Intersection with more members than the limit, asserting the rendered TOON string now contains the \"...(+N)\" marker where it previously silently omitted it.\n\nCluster #10: cli/commands/schema.rs's cmd_schema (or equivalent — read the actual function) hand-writes a JSON Schema as a serde_json::json!() literal instead of deriving it, and it's already drifted from the real ComponentEntry/ExtractionStats/Diagnostic struct fields (confirmed missing: methods, tags on ComponentEntry; several ExtractionStats fields; line/column/help on Diagnostic — verify current drift by reading both the real structs in core/types/output.rs + diagnostic.rs and the current schema.rs). Two implementation options, pick ONE and justify: (a) add schemars as a new dependency, derive JsonSchema on the plain structs (ComponentEntry, ParsedProp, ExtractionStats, Diagnostic, InheritedLayer — check Cargo.toml first to see if schemars or a similar crate is already available anywhere in the workspace), hand-write JsonSchema impls for the three recursive enums (PropType, CollectedType, OpaqueReason) mirroring their existing hand-written Serialize impl style per ADR-0002, and have cmd_schema call schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput); or (b) if adding a new dependency seems like too large a change for this task, the floor-level fix: add a test that serializes a real fixture/snapshot ExtractionOutput and validates every top-level field name it produces appears somewhere in schema.rs's hand-written schema (a drift-detection test, not full validation) — this is the fallback option, prefer (a) if the codebase's Cargo.toml discipline (workspace deps, minimal footprint) doesn't obviously rule it out. Note: this cluster also proposes ADR 0006 (see root-cause-analysis.md's \"Proposed: 0006\" draft) — if you choose option (a), include a final task writing docs/adr/0006-derive-json-schema.md using the same format/tone as docs/adr/0002-manual-serialize-for-prop-type.md (read that file for the exact style) with the draft content from root-cause-analysis.md as your starting point, Status: Accepted, today's date.\n\nCluster #11: the 0/1/2 exit-code mapping (\"does this ExtractionOutput contain an Error-severity diagnostic\") is reimplemented with different code shapes in cli/commands/extract.rs (around lines 68-71) and cli/commands/check.rs (around lines 21-30) — read both to see the actual current duplication. cli/commands/watch.rs's cmd_watch returns () not Result<i32>, and main.rs hardcodes exit 0 for the watch arm (read main.rs's command dispatch to find this). cli/commands/inspect.rs's cmd_inspect never checks output.diagnostics for Error severity at all. Fix: add impl ExtractionOutput { pub fn max_severity(&self) -> Option<DiagnosticSeverity>; pub fn exit_code(&self, strict: bool) -> i32 } in core/types/output.rs (read the file for where similar small helper methods already live, e.g. near html_element() if that exists, to match placement/style conventions) — the strict param and exact severity-to-code mapping should mirror whatever extract.rs/check.rs's current logic actually does (read both to derive the real mapping, don't invent one). Route extract.rs and check.rs through it (collapsing their inline checks — this should be a refactor with no behavior change, provable by the existing tests for those two commands still passing unmodified). Change cmd_watch's signature to return Result<i32> and call exit_code(false) after each extraction pass, updating main.rs's dispatch accordingly. Add the same exit_code(false) call to cmd_inspect after a successful component lookup.\n\nWrite your task group now. Expect roughly 8-10 tasks total across the three sub-clusters: TOON helper + test (1-2 tasks), schema fix (2-4 tasks depending on which option you choose, possibly +1 for the ADR), exit_code() method + test (1 task), wiring extract.rs/check.rs through it (1 task, should be a refactor verified by existing tests), fixing watch.rs's exit code (1-2 tasks), fixing inspect.rs (1 task).`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'standalone-fixes',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assigned cluster: the 7 standalone findings from root-cause-analysis.md's \"Standalone findings\" table — each independent, no shared mechanism, but small enough to batch into one task group.\n\n1. resolver/substitute.rs around lines 33-43, build_substitution's .zip() silently stops when a generic alias has more declared type parameters than the call site supplies, leaving trailing type params unsubstituted with no diagnostic. Read the actual current function. Fix: after the zip, if declared params outnumber supplied args, push a diagnostic (Warning severity, following whatever pattern this file/its neighbors use) noting which type parameters were left unsubstituted. Write a test with a generic alias like \\`type Foo<T, U> = { a: T; b: U }\\` called as \\`Foo<string>\\` (missing the second arg), asserting a diagnostic is now emitted (previously silent).\n\n2. cli/commands/watch.rs around lines 107-112, a watch --out write does \\`let _ = std::fs::write(...)\\` — the Result is fully discarded, so a write failure (disk full, permission revoked) is completely silent, and the write itself is non-atomic (plain truncate+write, no temp+rename). Read the actual current code. Fix: (a) route the Result through this crate's normal error-reporting convention (miette/output.rs/tracing — check crates/cli/CLAUDE.md and how extract.rs's --out write failure is already handled, at whatever its wrap_err(...) call looks like, and mirror that instead of silently swallowing); (b) make the write atomic via a temp-file-then-rename pattern in the same directory as the target (std::fs::write to a sibling temp path, then std::fs::rename over the real target — this avoids the truncate-then-fail-midway corruption risk). Write a test proving a write failure (e.g. writing to a path whose parent doesn't exist, or an unwritable location) now surfaces visibly rather than silently succeeding-looking.\n\n3. types/output.rs around lines 79-99, ParsedProp has independent public required: bool and default_value: Option<DefaultValue> fields with nothing preventing the contradictory required: true + default_value: Some(_) state. Read the actual current struct and its ~10 construction call sites across known.rs, resolver/chain.rs, resolver/alias.rs, resolver/mod.rs, toon.rs, types/output.rs itself (find and confirm these are the real call sites). Fix: add a ParsedProp::new(...) constructor (or builder, your call based on how many fields the struct actually has) that normalizes the required/default_value relationship (RDT convention: a supplied default value implies the prop is effectively optional even if required was passed true — read RDT's actual behavior/existing tests in this codebase if referenced, otherwise just enforce required=false whenever default_value is Some), and make the struct's fields pub(crate) so all real call sites are forced through the constructor (update all ~10 call sites accordingly — this is the bulk of the work in this task). Write a test constructing via ParsedProp::new with required:true and a Some default, asserting the resulting required is actually false.\n\n4. types/diagnostic.rs around line 61, DiagnosticCode::Unknown is defined but never constructed anywhere — confirmed genuinely dead code but intentional headroom on a #[non_exhaustive] enum. NO CODE CHANGE for this one — just add a doc comment on the Unknown variant explicitly stating it's reserved for external consumers synthesizing a Diagnostic outside this crate, so a future contributor doesn't \"clean it up\" by mistake or wonder why it's unused. This should be your smallest task — just the doc comment, no test needed (verify via cargo doc or just visual read that the comment was added).\n\n5. types/output.rs around lines 314 and 423, NumberLiteral(f64) values that are NaN/Infinity serialize to JSON null and round-trip back to a silent 0.0 via unwrap_or(0.0). Read the actual current to_json_value/from_json_value logic for this variant. This is low-priority/likely-unreachable per the doc, but still worth a defensive fix: change the round-trip to distinguish \"field was genuinely null/absent\" from \"field was a non-finite number\" if practical, OR at minimum add a code comment explaining this is a known, accepted lossy edge case if a real fix isn't worth the complexity — use your judgment on which given what you actually find, and say which you chose and why.\n\n6. cli/commands/extract.rs's rdt_type_json / is_literal_union(), a zero-member LiteralUnion is treated as a valid enum, producing {\"name\":\"enum\",\"value\":[]} in RDT output. Read the actual current is_literal_union() logic (likely in types/output.rs where PropType is defined, check both files). Low-priority cosmetic gap. Fix: either exclude zero-member and one-member LiteralUnions from the enum-shape special case (falling back to plain raw_string() output), or leave as-is with a comment explaining why an empty enum select is acceptable output — your judgment, but justify the choice you make in the task's step.\n\n7. cache.rs around lines 181-191 — mtime+size cache staleness on same-tick edits. Per root-cause-analysis.md this is explicitly a \"scoped follow-up, not a mechanism fix\" and NOT recommended for immediate action (the doc says a content hash would close the gap but changes the cache's performance/complexity tradeoff). DO NOT implement a fix for this — instead write a single task that just adds a doc comment on key_for (or CacheKey, whichever is more appropriate) explicitly documenting this known limitation (coarse-mtime-resolution filesystems can serve a stale hit on a same-tick, same-size edit) so it doesn't get \"discovered\" again as a surprise bug later. No test needed for a doc-comment-only task.\n\nWrite your task group now, one task per numbered item above (7 tasks total, task 4 and 7 being doc-comment-only with no test step — note explicitly in those two tasks that no test is required and why, don't force a fake test).`,\n  },\n]\n\nphase('Draft Tasks')\nconst drafts = await parallel(GROUPS.map(g => () => agent(g.prompt, { label: g.name, phase: 'Draft Tasks' })))\n\nreturn { drafts: GROUPS.map((g, i) => ({ name: g.name, content: drafts[i] })) }\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":406,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"output_tokens":13647,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":406,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":13647,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"cache_creation_input_tokens":406,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":406},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdi7RQttDu8DzDg8ABS5U","attributionSkill":"superpowers:writing-plans","attributionPlugin":"superpowers","type":"assistant","uuid":"1ac3c187-9069-4052-8a0d-092c2d6ab1dd","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:37:38.473Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
