{"parentUuid":"802f6d64-e6f3-4102-b238-f576e96a27c2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi7d9L6Apg7fbnn815AJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good call — before locking in an implementation plan built on Phase 0's conclusions, worth stress-testing whether those root-cause claims and mechanism proposals actually hold up. The plan-drafting workflow I just launched is already running in the background (it'll produce raw task drafts either way, and I still need to review/assemble those manually), so I'll run adversarial verification against `root-cause-analysis.md` concurrently rather than block on it — if the adversarial pass finds a cluster's root cause or mechanism is wrong, I'll correct the analysis doc and adjust the drafted tasks before assembling the final plan, rather than presenting something built on a flawed premise."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14451,"cache_read_input_tokens":216869,"output_tokens":7049,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14451,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7049,"cache_read_input_tokens":216869,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14451},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdi7d6VSBFU9eL9ipH9F4","attributionSkill":"superpowers:writing-plans","attributionPlugin":"superpowers","type":"assistant","uuid":"ab2e1409-6d8d-41af-8a2b-62d7ab49cce8","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:38:04.482Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"ab2e1409-6d8d-41af-8a2b-62d7ab49cce8","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi7d9L6Apg7fbnn815AJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017tF9TWF3bAdT8Z9Jyxy1w9","name":"Workflow","input":{"script":"export const meta = {\n  name: 'edge-case-adversarial-verify',\n  description: 'Adversarially stress-test root-cause-analysis.md: is each root cause actually correct, and would each mechanism proposal actually work',\n  phases: [\n    { title: 'Skeptic Review', detail: 'one skeptic per cluster group, defaults to refuting' },\n    { title: 'Verdict Synthesis', detail: 'merge into a pass/fail list with required corrections' },\n  ],\n}\n\nconst VERDICT_SCHEMA = {\n  type: 'object',\n  required: ['groupName', 'perClusterVerdicts', 'overallVerdict'],\n  properties: {\n    groupName: { type: 'string' },\n    perClusterVerdicts: {\n      type: 'array',\n      items: {\n        type: 'object',\n        required: ['clusterOrFindingName', 'rootCauseVerdict', 'mechanismVerdict', 'issues'],\n        properties: {\n          clusterOrFindingName: { type: 'string' },\n          rootCauseVerdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['confirmed', 'partially-wrong', 'wrong'] },\n          mechanismVerdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['sound', 'needs-adjustment', 'unsound'] },\n          issues: { type: 'string', description: 'concrete problems found, with file:line evidence — empty string if none. Be specific: what would break, what claim is false, what was missed.' },\n          correction: { type: 'string', description: 'if rootCauseVerdict or mechanismVerdict is not fully confirmed/sound, the specific correction to make — empty string if nothing to correct' },\n        },\n      },\n    },\n    overallVerdict: { type: 'string', description: 'one paragraph: is this group of clusters safe to build a plan on, or does root-cause-analysis.md need revision first' },\n  },\n}\n\nconst CONTEXT = `Project: oxc-react-docgen — Rust tool parsing TSX/TS with OXC, extracting React component prop metadata as a drop-in replacement for react-docgen-typescript.\n\nYou are a SKEPTIC. Your default assumption is that any claim in /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/root-cause-analysis.md is wrong until you personally verify it against the actual current source code. This document was written by other agents in a prior pass — your job is to find where they were wrong, not to confirm they were right. Being agreeable here wastes everyone's time; a real implementation plan is about to be built on top of these conclusions.\n\nRead /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/root-cause-analysis.md in full first, then /Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/edge-cases.md for the original findings those root causes claim to explain. Then read the ACTUAL CURRENT CODE at every file/line cited in your assigned section — don't trust summaries, verify directly.\n\nFor each cluster/finding in your assigned section, check:\n1. **Root cause accuracy**: does the actual code match what the doc claims? Is the \"why this happened\" explanation actually supported by what you see, or is it a plausible-sounding story that doesn't hold up? (e.g. does chain.rs really check known-pattern shortcuts before interfaces, in that exact order? Does ResolvedChain really derive Default and allow bare construction outside the named constructor?)\n2. **Mechanism soundness**: would the proposed fix actually work? Specifically: would it compile given the real current types/signatures? Would it break any existing test (search for tests that might depend on the CURRENT, allegedly-buggy behavior — e.g. does any existing test actually rely on chain.rs's current wrong precedence order, meaning \"fixing\" it would be a breaking behavior change that needs more care)? Is the proposed fix actually scoped correctly, or does it miss call sites / other places the same pattern appears that the original analysis didn't find? Would it violate any of this project's non-negotiables (no unwrap outside tests, FxHashMap/BTreeMap discipline, no AST refs escaping parse_file, always emit Diagnostic on degrade)?\n3. **Severity/scope accuracy**: is the finding's claimed impact accurate, or overstated/understated? Is anything claimed to be \"confirmed\" actually just inferred without verification?\n\nReturn your findings via the required schema — one entry per cluster/finding you were assigned. Be concrete: cite file:line for every issue you raise, don't gesture vaguely at \"this might be a problem.\" If you genuinely find nothing wrong after checking hard, say so plainly in rootCauseVerdict:\"confirmed\"/mechanismVerdict:\"sound\" — don't manufacture a fake issue to seem thorough, but don't rubber-stamp either.`\n\nconst GROUPS = [\n  {\n    name: 'panic-containment',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assignment: root-cause-analysis.md section \"4. No panic-containment boundary\" and \"Proposed: 0005\" ADR draft.\n\nSpecifically verify: is it really true that zero catch_unwind exists anywhere in the repo (grep it yourself)? Do the three NAPI entry points (extract_all, extract_file_incremental, initialize_session in crates/binding/src/lib.rs) really run inside tokio::task::spawn_blocking, and does spawn_blocking's JoinError really convert a panic into a catchable Err rather than something else? Do create_session/close_session really skip spawn_blocking? Does pipeline/watch.rs really have a std::sync::Mutex whose poisoning would be a direct consequence of an unprotected panic during initialize()? Is the proposed panic_guard::contain_panic signature actually usable given Rust's UnwindSafe bounds on the real closures involved (rayon closures capturing &mut state, plugin trait objects, etc. — UnwindSafe can be a real practical obstacle, check if AssertUnwindSafe would be needed and whether that's actually safe here or just a rug-pull for the safety guarantee)? Would wrapping plugin.on_x(...) calls individually actually compile given DocgenPlugin's real trait signature (check plugin.rs)?`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'resolver-constructors-and-precedence',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assignment: root-cause-analysis.md sections \"1. Resolver give-up constructor discipline\" and \"8. Resolver precedence: two hand-copied orderings\".\n\nThis is the highest-stakes cluster in the whole plan — P0-1 (the precedence bug) is the single most concrete confirmed bug in the entire audit. Verify hard: read the ACTUAL current chain.rs extends-clause resolution code (around lines 90-106) and named.rs's resolution code (around lines 41-72) side by side. Does chain.rs really check known-pattern shortcuts BEFORE interface/type-alias lookup? Is that really the opposite order from named.rs, or did the original audit misread something (e.g. maybe chain.rs's \"known check\" only applies to a subset of cases named.rs doesn't handle, meaning it's not actually a precedence bug at all)? If it IS a real bug, would fixing it change output for any EXISTING passing test or snapshot fixture (search crates/core/tests/ and the snapshot fixtures for any component that currently relies on chain.rs's current behavior — e.g. does any fixture extend a locally-defined SxProps-named or similar interface today, where \"fixing\" this would change its resolved output)? For the ResolvedChain::default()/PropType::Opaque{} constructor-privacy proposal: would making these private actually be a breaking change for any other file in the resolver that constructs them directly today (grep ALL construction sites, not just the ones the original analysis named, to check if the \"~10 call sites\" count is accurate or missed any)?`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'extractor-diagnostics-and-depth',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assignment: root-cause-analysis.md sections \"2. Extractor missing give-up signal channel\" and \"7. Extractor depth-tracking proxy mismatch\".\n\nVerify: does classify_type_alias in extractor/alias.rs really \\`return None\\` via \\`?\\` on malformed type arguments with zero diagnostic, for the Omit/Pick/Partial/Required/Readonly arms specifically? Read every arm, not just a sample — is the claim \"the whole type alias vanishes\" accurate for all of them or only some? Does SourceData actually have a diagnostics field reachable with &mut self from within classify_type_alias's actual call context (check the real call chain — is classify_type_alias even a method with &mut self access, or a free function that would need a larger signature change than the doc implies)? For the depth-tracking proxy mismatch: does max_bracket_nesting_depth really fail to catch a realistic deeply-chained-conditional-type case — can you actually construct a concrete example type expression where bracket count stays low but conditional nesting goes deep, and would it really pass through today's guard undetected? Or is this theoretical/unlikely to matter in practice given what real TSX code looks like?`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'pipeline-discovery-merge',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assignment: root-cause-analysis.md section \"3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps\".\n\nVerify: does discover_files in pipeline/discover.rs really return a bare Vec<Utf8PathBuf> with walker.flatten() dropping Err values, at the line numbers claimed? Read pipeline/mod.rs's actual empty-src_dirs guard around line 220 — is the boolean logic really wrong, or does something upstream (CLI layer, config.rs) already prevent an empty src_dirs from ever reaching this code, making the \"bug\" unreachable in practice? Check the actual components.insert call around line 386 — does FxHashMap/whatever map type is used here actually have an insert() that returns Option<V> (some map types don't), and does the surrounding code already do anything with a discarded return that the analysis missed? Would the proposed \"one fix resolves both the same-name-same-file collision AND the overlapping-src_dirs collision\" claim actually hold, or are these genuinely two different code paths needing separate fixes?`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'allocation-caps-and-lsp',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assignment: root-cause-analysis.md sections \"5. Unbounded allocation from input-derived size\" and \"6. LSP scaffold immaturity\".\n\nVerify: does resolver/template.rs's try_expand_template_literal really have zero cap on Cartesian product size — check the actual loop, is there perhaps an implicit bound already (e.g. from the extractor's own nesting-depth guard limiting how many union members could even reach this function) that the analysis missed, which would make this less severe than claimed? Does cli/commands/lsp.rs really do vec![0u8; len] with a fully unbounded len from Content-Length — check the actual parsing code, is there any existing sanity check upstream? Is lsp.rs really advertising hoverProvider: true with genuinely zero hover handler anywhere — grep for \"hover\" across the whole crate to be sure nothing partial exists that the analysis missed?`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'toon-schema-cli-contract',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assignment: root-cause-analysis.md sections \"9. TOON truncation indicator dropped in one branch\", \"10. Schema hand-maintained separately from the real structs\", and \"11. CLI exit-code contract has no shared type\".\n\nVerify: read toon.rs's actual format_type_compact function — does the LiteralUnion branch really have a correct \"...(+N)\" indicator while Union/Intersection genuinely lack it, in the SAME function? For schema.rs: verify the claimed drift is real by diffing the actual current schema.rs content against the actual current ComponentEntry/ExtractionStats/Diagnostic struct definitions — is EVERY specific field named as missing (.methods, .tags, 5 of 9 ExtractionStats fields, .line/.column/.help) really absent from the schema today, or did some get added since the audit? Check if schemars or an equivalent crate is already a workspace dependency anywhere (would change the mechanism proposal's cost). For the exit-code contract: verify extract.rs and check.rs really have syntactically-different-but-semantically-same exit code logic (quote both), and confirm cmd_watch really returns () and cmd_inspect really never checks diagnostics — or did the analysis miss a code path.`,\n  },\n  {\n    name: 'standalone-fixes',\n    prompt: `${CONTEXT}\n\n## Your assignment: root-cause-analysis.md's \"Standalone findings\" table (7 entries — substitute.rs, watch.rs --out, ParsedProp, DiagnosticCode::Unknown, cache.rs mtime staleness, NumberLiteral NaN, zero-member LiteralUnion).\n\nFor each of the 7, verify the claim against actual code. Pay particular attention to: is ParsedProp really constructed via bare struct literal at ~10 call sites across 6 files with no existing constructor — find and list the REAL call sites yourself, don't trust the count. Is DiagnosticCode::Unknown really never constructed anywhere (grep it) — and is the enum really #[non_exhaustive]? Does watch.rs's --out write really discard the Result via \\`let _ =\\` verbatim? For cache.rs's mtime staleness claim — is this really unfixable without a content hash, or is there a cheaper mitigation (e.g. checking file content length in addition to what's already checked, or a coarser-but-safer invalidation strategy) the analysis dismissed too quickly by calling it \"not a mechanism fix, scoped follow-up\"?`,\n  },\n]\n\nphase('Skeptic Review')\nconst verdicts = await parallel(GROUPS.map(g => () => agent(g.prompt, { label: g.name, phase: 'Skeptic Review', schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA })))\n\nphase('Verdict Synthesis')\nconst synthesis = await agent(\n  `You're synthesizing adversarial-verification results for root-cause-analysis.md in the oxc-react-docgen project. Below are 7 skeptic reports, each independently checking a group of clusters against the actual current source code.\n\n${JSON.stringify(verdicts.filter(Boolean), null, 2)}\n\nProduce a final verdict report as markdown:\n1. A one-paragraph overall verdict: is root-cause-analysis.md safe to build an implementation plan on as-is, or does it need corrections first?\n2. A table: cluster/finding name | root cause verdict | mechanism verdict | required correction (or \"none\").\n3. A \"must-fix-before-planning\" section listing anything a skeptic marked \"wrong\" or \"unsound\" with the specific correction, in enough detail that root-cause-analysis.md's text can be directly edited.\n4. A \"worth noting but not blocking\" section for \"partially-wrong\"/\"needs-adjustment\" items that don't invalidate the overall plan but should be caveated.\n\nBe decisive and concrete. Under 1500 words.`,\n  { label: 'verdict-synthesis', phase: 'Verdict Synthesis' }\n)\n\nreturn { verdicts, synthesis }\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14451,"cache_read_input_tokens":216869,"output_tokens":7049,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14451,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7049,"cache_read_input_tokens":216869,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14451},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdi7d6VSBFU9eL9ipH9F4","attributionSkill":"superpowers:writing-plans","attributionPlugin":"superpowers","type":"assistant","uuid":"87bfd02d-03bf-4594-b05a-f9ceae01b385","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:39:10.289Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
