{"parentUuid":"56a4248f-070a-4899-b5d0-70c8888693fd","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi8MyXBwPijum4eozhuy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27104,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"output_tokens":344,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27104,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27104,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27104},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"messages_changed","cache_missed_input_tokens":180806}}},"requestId":"req_011Cdi8MuSckiKnSzfnhVLbB","type":"assistant","uuid":"91eecced-6724-4881-b9d7-85f966ce1d59","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:47:28.894Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"91eecced-6724-4881-b9d7-85f966ce1d59","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi8MyXBwPijum4eozhuy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adversarial verification is done — already found a real, concrete defect (the panic-guard mechanism for plugin hooks won't compile as proposed due to `&mut` capturing breaking `UnwindSafe`). Let me read the full output before the plan-drafting workflow lands, so I can correct `root-cause-analysis.md` first."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27104,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"output_tokens":344,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27104,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27104,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27104},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"messages_changed","cache_missed_input_tokens":180806}}},"requestId":"req_011Cdi8MuSckiKnSzfnhVLbB","type":"assistant","uuid":"6db8c096-e7f6-42ea-bd04-bedead4a959f","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:47:30.676Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"6db8c096-e7f6-42ea-bd04-bedead4a959f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cdi8MyXBwPijum4eozhuy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013DUXtUzXdJDCzJpfib41eZ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-oxc-react-codegen/0ae3953f-9438-46c4-a76a-88fce9eed529/tasks/w33aybccq.output"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27104,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"output_tokens":344,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27104,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":216463,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27104,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27104},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"messages_changed","cache_missed_input_tokens":180806}}},"requestId":"req_011Cdi8MuSckiKnSzfnhVLbB","type":"assistant","uuid":"ea40a096-bda1-482d-9126-d6d621464740","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:47:31.247Z","effort":"medium","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"ea40a096-bda1-482d-9126-d6d621464740","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"8cfd87e5-e489-465b-a51f-4926a8373775","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013DUXtUzXdJDCzJpfib41eZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"summary\": \"Adversarially stress-test root-cause-analysis.md: is each root cause actually correct, and would each mechanism proposal actually work\",\n3\t  \"agentCount\": 8,\n4\t  \"logs\": [],\n5\t  \"result\": {\n6\t    \"verdicts\": [\n7\t      {\n8\t        \"groupName\": \"4. No panic-containment boundary / ADR 0005\",\n9\t        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n10\t          {\n11\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"4. No panic-containment boundary (root cause)\",\n12\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n13\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n14\t            \"issues\": \"Root-cause claims verified accurate: (1) `grep -rn catch_unwind` across the repo (excluding target/) returns zero hits in any .rs file — only doc mentions. (2) crates/binding/src/lib.rs:118-127 (extract_all), 144-166 (extract_file_incremental), 171-189 (initialize_session) all wrap their body in `tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {...}).await.map_err(...)?`, and a panic inside the closure produces `JoinError` on `.await` rather than propagating the unwind — accurate. (3) create_session (lib.rs:131-138) and close_session (lib.rs:192-195) are plain sync `#[napi] pub fn` with no spawn_blocking — accurate, matches doc's cited lines almost exactly. (4) pipeline/mod.rs:260 (parse rayon `.map()`) and :357 (resolve rayon `.map()`) have no per-item catch_unwind — accurate. (5) plugin.rs's `run_on_file_extracted`/`run_on_component_resolved` loops (lines 47-56) call `plugin.on_file_extracted(file_path, data)` / `plugin.on_component_resolved(entry)` directly with no isolation between plugins — accurate; these are called sequentially from pipeline/mod.rs:293 and :385 (not inside the rayon closures — both plugin hook call sites are in the sequential post-collect loops, so the doc's mechanism of wrapping 'each individual call' is targeting the right, already-sequential granularity). (6) watch.rs:93 `self.initialized.lock().expect(\\\"init lock poisoned\\\")` holds the guard across the entire body of `initialize()`, which calls `extract_with_global` (the same rayon+plugin pipeline) — so a panic during that call while the guard is held does poison the mutex permanently, exactly as claimed. All root-cause claims check out.\\n\\nHowever the Mechanism paragraph (line 45) has a real, verified gap: it proposes wrapping `plugin.on_x(...)` calls individually via `contain_panic<T>(label, f: impl FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe)`, but `plugin.rs:49` (`plugin.on_file_extracted(file_path, data)` where `data: &mut SourceData`) and `plugin.rs:55` (`plugin.on_component_resolved(entry)` where `entry: &mut ComponentEntry`) each capture a `&mut` reference. I verified empirically (rustc E0277) that `&mut T` unconditionally does NOT implement `UnwindSafe` in std, regardless of T — this is a hard compile error, not a style nit. The doc never mentions this and gives no indication the fix needs `AssertUnwindSafe`. Using `AssertUnwindSafe` here isn't just plumbing either: it papers over the real hazard std's UnwindSafe bound exists to flag — if a plugin panics mid-mutation of `data`/`entry`, the caller (pipeline/mod.rs:293/385) unconditionally continues using that possibly half-mutated value afterward (`diagnostics.append(&mut data.diagnostics)`, `global.merge(&path, data)` at mod.rs:292/297; `components.insert(key, entry)` at mod.rs:386) with no rollback or corruption check. The mechanism needs to explicitly address this — either specify AssertUnwindSafe with a documented rationale for why partial mutation is acceptable here, or change the design so a caught panic discards the mutated value rather than trusting it. As written, 'wrap each individual plugin.on_x(...) call' is not directly buildable against the stated `contain_panic` signature.\"\n15\t          },\n16\t          {\n17\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"Proposed ADR-0005\",\n18\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n19\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n20\t            \"issues\": \"Context/Decision/Consequences text is consistent with the verified root cause and doesn't overstate anything — 'accident of spawn_blocking's JoinError' is accurate per my read of lib.rs. One thing worth noting: the ADR (and root-cause doc) present create_session/close_session's lack of spawn_blocking as purely an oversight relative to their siblings, without checking whether napi-rs itself has any opt-in panic-safety knob that would change the fix's shape. I checked: napi-derive-backend 5.1.1 (matches pinned napi=\\\"3\\\"/napi-derive=\\\"3\\\") has an opt-in `#[napi(catch_unwind)]` attribute (napi-derive-backend/src/ast.rs:35, codegen/fn.rs:142-145) that wraps the generated function body in `std::panic::catch_unwind` — but it is opt-in (bool, unset by default) and none of the four `#[napi]` fns in lib.rs use it. So napi-rs does NOT auto-protect create_session/close_session (confirms edge-cases.md's P1-9 'needs verification' item in the direction the doc assumed), but this also surfaces a second, simpler sanctioned tool already available at the FFI boundary specifically (`#[napi(catch_unwind)]`) that the ADR doesn't mention as an alternative/complement to the custom `panic_guard::contain_panic` helper — worth a line in the ADR's reasoning since it changes the 'one sanctioned helper' framing at that boundary.\\n\\nSeparately: the ADR's Decision line says panics from 'a rayon `.map()`, a DocgenPlugin hook, or a NAPI entry point' are contained through 'one sanctioned helper' — but per the finding above, the plugin-hook case can't cleanly use that same helper without the AssertUnwindSafe caveat, so the ADR's claim of one uniform mechanism for all three call-site classes is slightly optimistic; the plugin case needs an explicit, documented exception/wrapper convention, not just a drop-in call.\"\n21\t          }\n22\t        ],\n23\t        \"overallVerdict\": \"The empirical root-cause claims for cluster 4 hold up well under direct verification — zero catch_unwind in the repo, all three async NAPI entry points genuinely rely on spawn_blocking's JoinError as accidental protection, create_session/close_session genuinely skip it, the rayon closures at pipeline/mod.rs:260 and :357 genuinely have no per-item boundary, and watch.rs:93's mutex genuinely stays locked across the exact pipeline call that would panic. This section is safe to build a plan on as far as diagnosis goes. But the proposed mechanism has one concrete, verified defect that must be corrected before implementation: wrapping the plugin hook calls (plugin.rs:49,55) in `contain_panic<T: FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe>` will not compile as literally described, because both calls capture a `&mut` reference and `&mut T` is unconditionally !UnwindSafe in std (verified via rustc reproduction). The plan needs an explicit decision — AssertUnwindSafe plus a stated rationale for tolerating partially-mutated SourceData/ComponentEntry after a caught panic, or a design that discards/validates the mutated value on panic rather than trusting it — before this task is handed to an implementer. The rayon closures at mod.rs:260/357 are comparatively low-risk (no &mut captures observed; ResolutionContext at resolver/mod.rs:118-143 doesn't carry the PluginRegistry/trait-object fields that would create their own RefUnwindSafe problems), though DashMap/oxc_resolver::Resolver's exact RefUnwindSafe status wasn't exhaustively verified. The ADR draft is reasonable but should note napi-rs's own opt-in `#[napi(catch_unwind)]` (confirmed present in napi-derive-backend 5.1.1, opt-in only) as an available complementary mechanism at the FFI boundary specifically, and should soften its 'one sanctioned helper covers all three call-site classes' framing given the plugin-hook UnwindSafe wrinkle. No existing test locks in the current panic-crashes-everything behavior (no should_panic tests found touching pipeline/plugin/binding code), so fixing this is not a breaking-test-change concern.\"\n24\t      },\n25\t      {\n26\t        \"groupName\": \"Resolver give-up constructor discipline (cluster 1) + Resolver precedence ordering (cluster 8)\",\n27\t        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n28\t          {\n29\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"8. Resolver precedence: two hand-copied orderings (P0-1)\",\n30\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n31\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n32\t            \"issues\": \"Verified directly: chain.rs's resolve_props_chain checks known-pattern shortcuts (Step 2, chain.rs:92-141, calling resolve_known) BEFORE canonical/type-alias/interface lookup (Steps 3-5, chain.rs:142-158). named.rs's resolve_named checks canonical resolution -> type-alias (named.rs:41-58) -> interface (named.rs:60-65) BEFORE the known-pattern fallback (Step 5, named.rs:67-84), with an explicit comment at named.rs:41-43 stating this order is intentional. The doc's core claim and cited line ranges (chain.rs:90-106, named.rs:41-72) are accurate. I searched every fixture under crates/core/tests and the top-level fixtures/ tree, and grepped every known.rs pattern name (SxProps, SlotProps, VariantProps, StylesApiProps, ThemingProps, RecipeVariantProps, RecipeVariants) against 'extends' clauses -- zero hits anywhere. So fixing the precedence bug will NOT change any current snapshot/test output; regression risk is effectively nil (worth stating positively rather than leaving it an open question). Where the doc's mechanism needs adjustment: it frames the situation as 'two hand-copied orderings' of one shared 4-step sequence, but the two functions actually diverge in at least two further, unmentioned ways beyond the P0-1 step: (1) named.rs checks react-builtin FIRST (named.rs:31-34, before canonical resolution) while chain.rs checks it AFTER the known-pattern block (chain.rs Step 2.5, ~line 142); (2) chain.rs checks is_ts_utility_type very early (Step 1, chain.rs:86-89, before known-pattern) while named.rs's analogous silent-no-op check happens last (Step 6, named.rs:88-113, after known-pattern AND after interface/alias). The proposed `resolve_source_defined_or_known(...)` shared function (fixed order resolve_to_canonical -> lookup_type_alias -> lookup_interface -> resolve_known) is scoped correctly to fix P0-1 specifically and is compileable as a plan (both callers already have the necessary state/ctx/depth in scope, and closures for the differing terminal type (Named vs ResolvedChain) are a standard generic-return pattern), so 'Resolves: P0-1' is not overstated for that narrow claim. But calling it 'the same sequence' independently drifting is an incomplete diagnosis -- there are two more silent ordering divergences the doc doesn't catch, so a reviewer implementing only the P0-1 fix should not assume chain.rs and named.rs are now unified; they will still diverge on react-builtin and ts-utility-type ordering.\"\n33\t          },\n34\t          {\n35\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"1. Resolver give-up constructor discipline\",\n36\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"partially-wrong\",\n37\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"unsound\",\n38\t            \"issues\": \"The premise that opaque/give-up construction is inconsistent is real, but only for exactly 3 sites, not '6 of ~10': func.rs:61 (MultiParamFunction) has zero diagnostic push anywhere nearby -- confirmed silent give-up. chain.rs:40 (cycle-detected `return ResolvedChain::default();`) has zero diagnostic push -- confirmed silent give-up. alias.rs:116 (`CollectedTypeAlias::LiteralUnion => ResolvedChain::default()`) has zero diagnostic, unlike the parallel diagnosed path at alias.rs:234-253 -- confirmed silent give-up. These three exactly match edge-cases.md's own three listed findings, so the *scope* of the real bug is 3 sites, not '6 of ~10 Opaque construction sites... invisible to a reviewer scanning the happy path' as root-cause-analysis.md states at line 13. The two other sites the doc explicitly names as bypassing -- primitives.rs:181 and template.rs:39 -- both already push a full, correctly-shaped Diagnostic immediately before constructing the Opaque (primitives.rs:171-179, template.rs:29-37); they just don't call collected.rs's private `push_opaque_diagnostic` helper (which is `fn`, not `pub`/`pub(crate)`, so it can't even be called from primitives.rs/template.rs today without a visibility change the doc never mentions). That's a duplication/style issue, not a give-up-with-no-diagnostic bug, and conflating it with func.rs:61 overstates severity. Mechanism is unsound as literally described, for two independent reasons: (1) Reverse-dependency violation: known.rs owns ~7 of the real PropType::Opaque construction sites (known.rs:102,108,126,133,193,221,228) and is explicitly documented (known.rs:74) and required by CLAUDE.md:30 ('No reverse dependencies') to never depend on `resolver::ResolveState`. The proposed `OpaqueDetail::give_up(state: &mut ResolveState, ...)` as the *only* public constructor cannot be called from known.rs at all -- the plan doesn't address this, and it's not a minor gap: it's ~7 of the ~18 real crate-wide Opaque construction sites (the '~10' count itself undercounts; resolver/ alone has ~9, plus known.rs's ~7, plus types/output.rs's hand-written Deserialize impl at lines 502-533 which constructs `PropType::Opaque{raw,reason}` twice (529, 531) from wire JSON with no ResolveState in scope at all -- deserialization isn't a give-up event and the plan never accounts for it). Existing tests (mod.rs:2117-2137 `test_known_opaque_result_emits_diagnostic`, mod.rs:2139-2160 `test_known_opaque_result_emits_diagnostic_at_chain_level`) confirm this two-step design is intentional and already covered: known.rs constructs the bare Opaque value, the *caller* (chain.rs:122-124, named.rs:74-76) diagnoses it via `push_known_opaque_diagnostic` using context (type_name_bare, consuming_file) that known.rs doesn't have. Collapsing construction+diagnosis into one constructor is incompatible with this tested split. (2) ResolvedChain privacy is close to a non-issue as stated, and actively harmful if implemented literally: the struct (`struct ResolvedChain` at mod.rs:346, no `pub`) and every field are already private to the resolver module tree -- unreachable from pipeline/ or anywhere outside resolver/ today. Within resolver/, Rust's privacy model gives chain.rs, alias.rs, named.rs, and extends.rs (all descendant modules of `resolver`) *equal* access to resolver::mod.rs's private items -- there is no Rust mechanism to let alias.rs use `ResolvedChain::default()` for legitimate accumulator initialization while blocking chain.rs's give-up call, since both are the same privacy tier. And the bare/Default construction is overwhelmingly NOT give-up usage: of the 11 `ResolvedChain::default()`/bare-literal sites I found (chain.rs:40,89,108,120,191; extends.rs:65; alias.rs:106,168,178,291,380), only chain.rs:40 is a true silent give-up. The rest are legitimate accumulator seeds later filled by `chain.merge_parent(...)` in a loop (alias.rs:106,168,178,291,380; chain.rs:191), a deliberately-silent no-op for a non-error case (chain.rs:89's TS-utility-type short-circuit, and the intentional `HtmlAttributes`-as-inheritance-layer case at extends.rs:65), or a success-path struct-update literal (chain.rs:108,120). Forcing all of these through a single diagnostic-emitting `give_up()` as 'the only public entry point' would either spuriously diagnose non-error paths or require a second, undocumented constructor -- the doc doesn't distinguish these categories at all, so 'make bare/Default construction private, add `give_up` as the only public entry point' does not describe an implementable, compiling change without significant redesign beyond what's written. Correction needed: scope the mechanism down to the 3 confirmed silent sites (func.rs:61, chain.rs:40, alias.rs:116) using a small helper that's callable where ResolveState is already in scope (all three qualify), leave known.rs's construction+caller-diagnoses split as-is (it's correct and tested), fix types/output.rs's Deserialize impl to use whatever crate-visible raw constructor is needed for wire reconstruction (not give_up), and drop the ResolvedChain-privacy half of the proposal or redesign it to keep a separate non-diagnosing `ResolvedChain::new_accumulator()`/similar alongside `give_up()`, explicitly acknowledging both are needed.\"\n39\t          }\n40\t        ],\n41\t        \"overallVerdict\": \"P0-1 (cluster 8) is real and correctly cited down to line numbers; the fix is soundly scoped for that narrow claim, carries zero regression risk against current fixtures/snapshots (verified by exhaustive grep, no fixture extends a known-pattern-named interface), though the doc understates that chain.rs and named.rs diverge in two further ordering respects (react-builtin position, ts-utility-type position) it doesn't flag or fix. Cluster 1 is the weaker of the two: the underlying premise (some give-up paths silently drop diagnostics) is real but the true blast radius is 3 call sites, not '6 of ~10' -- most of the sites the doc cites as bypassing the pattern already diagnose correctly, just via duplicated inline code rather than a shared helper. Worse, the proposed mechanism as written does not compile/does not work: it ignores that known.rs's ~7 Opaque-construction sites are architecturally barred from touching resolver::ResolveState (CLAUDE.md's no-reverse-deps rule), ignores types/output.rs's Deserialize impl which also constructs Opaque with no ResolveState available, and proposes making ResolvedChain's bare construction 'private' when Rust's privacy model can't actually distinguish resolver's give-up call sites from its (far more numerous) legitimate accumulator-init call sites at the same module tier. Before a plan is built on cluster 1, its mechanism section needs to be rewritten: narrow the fix to the 3 confirmed silent sites, and either drop or substantially redesign the ResolvedChain/PropType::Opaque constructor-privacy proposal so it accounts for known.rs, the Deserialize impl, and the accumulator-vs-give-up split. Cluster 8 can proceed largely as written, with a note added about the two additional ordering divergences so the fix isn't assumed to fully unify the two functions.\\\"\"\n42\t      },\n43\t      {\n44\t        \"groupName\": \"Extractor give-up signal channel (cluster 2) and depth-tracking proxy mismatch (cluster 7)\",\n45\t        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n46\t          {\n47\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"2. Extractor missing give-up signal channel\",\n48\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n49\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n50\t            \"issues\": \"Verified every arm of classify_type_alias (crates/core/src/extractor/alias.rs:12-133): Omit (line 19, `tr.type_arguments.as_ref()?`), Pick (line 32, same), Partial (line 45-46, `tp.params.first()?` then `extract_type_name_from_type(...)?`), Required (line 54-55), Readonly (line 63-64) all silently `return None` via `?` on missing/malformed type arguments — confirmed for all five, not just a sample. classify_type_alias is `pub(super) fn classify_type_alias<'a>(&mut self, ...)` (alias.rs:12) — it does have `&mut self`, and SourceDataCollector holds `data: SourceData` (mod.rs:174) whose `.diagnostics` is already pushed into elsewhere (mod.rs:108, 134), so the doc's claim that the diagnostics channel is reachable with &mut self from this exact call context is accurate — no larger signature change is needed, contrary to what a skeptical read might expect. Also confirmed: grep across all of extractor/*.rs shows diagnostics.push only occurs at mod.rs:108 and mod.rs:134 (nesting guard + parse errors) — nowhere else — which supports the 'design gap, not helper drift' framing exactly as claimed. extract_type_name_from_type's `_ => None` fallback (mod.rs:650) is verified exactly as described — any TSType variant not covered by the TSTypeReference/Parenthesized/Union/Intersection/TypeLiteral arms silently produces no ComponentMapping. Minor imprecision only: the doc's 'component-detector .or_else() chains' framing, when applied to the second cited line range (visit.rs:262-293, visit_function), doesn't quite fit — that block is a single nested if-let detector (Pattern 4, function components), not an .or_else() chain across multiple named detectors like visit_variable_declaration's block at visit.rs:236-247 actually is. The 'end of each detector chain' phrasing slightly overgeneralizes what's actually two different failure shapes (multi-detector or_else vs. single nested if-let), but the underlying silent-failure claim holds for both. New DiagnosticCode::SkippedCandidate variant is safe to add — DiagnosticCode is #[non_exhaustive] with derived (not hand-written) Serialize (types/diagnostic.rs:54-56), so no serde match-arm updates are needed, unlike the PropType/CollectedType recursive-enum caveat elsewhere in the document.\"\n51\t          },\n52\t          {\n53\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"7. Extractor depth-tracking proxy mismatch\",\n54\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n55\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n56\t            \"issues\": \"Verified ts_type_to_collected (extractor/mod.rs:311-471) has no depth parameter and recurses freely through TSConditionalType (line 416-421: check/extends_type/true_type/false_type all recurse via self.ts_type_to_collected with no counter), while every resolver resolve_* function threads an explicit `depth: u8` and bails via `if depth > MAX_DEPTH` (resolver/collected.rs:23-26, MAX_DEPTH=20 at resolver/mod.rs:46) — the asymmetry is real and exactly as described. Constructed the concrete counter-example the doc gestures at: `A extends B ? C extends D ? E extends F ? ... : X : X : X` requires literally zero brackets (no `(`, `{`, or `[`) at any nesting depth — max_bracket_nesting_depth (mod.rs:54-94) only counts those three bracket types, so a long chained-conditional file would report max_depth=0 regardless of how many levels deep the conditional nesting goes, sailing straight through the MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH=2000 guard undetected. This is actually a stronger case than the doc's 'proportionally fewer brackets per level' phrasing suggests (which undersells it — it's not just fewer, it's potentially none). Notably self-undermining: the guard's own doc comment (mod.rs:36-39) explicitly claims to guard against 'nested parenthesized types, object type literals, or conditional types' causing parser stack overflow, yet the implementation only scans brackets and has no way to see conditional-type nesting at all — a direct contradiction between the guard's stated purpose and its actual mechanism, strengthening the finding. Confirmed the only test for this guard (test_excessive_nesting_guard, mod.rs:1464-1478) uses pure paren nesting only, exactly as claimed — no conditional-type-shaped test exists. Mechanism check: call sites of ts_type_to_collected/ts_tuple_element_to_collected/ts_signature_to_object_field outside mod.rs are limited to alias.rs and component.rs:236 — all of them are recursion *entry points* (self.ts_type_to_collected(ty) called once, depth starts at 0), not participants in the internal recursion, so threading depth: u8 through 'ts_type_to_collected and sibling functions in extractor/mod.rs' as the doc scopes it is correctly bounded and does not require touching alias.rs/component.rs call-site signatures, contrary to what I initially suspected. The doc's illustrative 'depth 500' threshold is arbitrary relative to the resolver's MAX_DEPTH=20, but that's not an inconsistency — extraction-time structural recursion depth and resolution-time depth serve different purposes and don't need to share a constant.\"\n57\t          }\n58\t        ],\n59\t        \"overallVerdict\": \"Both clusters in this section hold up well under direct code verification — no fabricated claims, no stale line numbers, and the two most checkable technical assertions (that all five classify_type_alias arms silently vanish via `?`, and that a chained-conditional-type example defeats the bracket-counting guard) are not just plausible but concretely demonstrable in the current code, in the depth-tracking case even more damning than the doc states (zero brackets required, not just fewer). The only defect worth a light touch-up before planning is cluster 2's slightly loose description of visit.rs:262-293 as part of an '.or_else() chain' when it's actually a distinct single-detector failure shape — cosmetic, doesn't change the fix or its scope. Safe to build an implementation plan on both clusters as written.\"\n60\t      },\n61\t      {\n62\t        \"groupName\": \"3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps\",\n63\t        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n64\t          {\n65\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps\",\n66\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n67\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n68\t            \"issues\": \"Line-level claims check out exactly: discover.rs:6 `pub(super) fn discover_files(...) -> Vec<Utf8PathBuf>` with no diagnostic channel, `walker.flatten()` at discover.rs:17 drops ignore::Walk Err values, and the non-UTF8 fallback silently drops files at discover.rs:41 (`if let Ok(utf8) = Utf8PathBuf::from_path_buf(...)`). pipeline/mod.rs:220's guard `if !options.src_dirs.is_empty() && missing_src_dirs.len() == options.src_dirs.len()` and pipeline/mod.rs:386's `components.insert(key, entry);` are exactly as described, both discarding relevant information. Reachability confirmed, not upstream-blocked: crates/cli/src/config.rs:35-36 does `if let Some(dirs) = self.src_dirs { opts.src_dirs = dirs.into_iter().map(Into::into).collect(); }` with no non-empty check, so an explicit `srcDirs: []` in docgen.config.ts reaches extract_with_global with a genuinely empty Vec (PipelineOptions::default() only supplies `./src` as a fallback, which this override replaces). No existing test exercises this path — test_extract_empty_src (mod.rs:574) uses a real-but-empty tmp *directory*, not an empty src_dirs list, so 'fixing' the guard doesn't break it.\\n\\nTwo real problems with the mechanism as written:\\n\\n1. The claim that fixing components.insert's discarded Option<V> return 'fixes both the same-name-same-file collision AND the overlapping-src_dirs collision at once' does not hold for the common 2-way overlap case. Tracing the actual key logic (mod.rs:363-381): the disambiguation key is `format!(\\\"{} ({})\\\", base_name, entry.file_path)` for every occurrence after the first, regardless of count — so with exactly two duplicate ComponentMappings from the same file (the realistic overlapping-src_dirs case, e.g. [\\\"./src\\\", \\\"./src/components\\\"], produces exactly a 2x duplicate via `component_mappings.extend()` in types/global.rs:97 with no dedup anywhere, and discover.rs:54 sorts but never dedups src_files), the first duplicate gets the plain key ('Button') and the second gets the disambiguated key ('Button (path)') — two *different* keys, so `components.insert` never sees a collision and its discarded Option<V> would be None both times. The insert-return check only catches a true key collision, which requires 3+ duplicate mappings sharing the same name+file (e.g. 3-way overlapping dirs, or a literal duplicate declaration in one file colliding with a pre-existing 2nd-occurrence key) — a narrower and rarer case than 'overlapping src_dirs' as generally described. The mechanism section should call out that overlapping-src_dirs additionally needs its own targeted fix (e.g. `files.dedup()` after `files.sort()` in discover.rs, or deduping component_mappings/src_files by canonical path) rather than assuming the insert-return check alone resolves it.\\n\\n2. The 220 guard is not really a 'plain boolean logic error' fixable by a one-line operator swap, and the described fix ('fix the && guard') is underspecified: simply dropping `!options.src_dirs.is_empty() &&` makes the empty-src_dirs case fall into the Error branch, but that branch's message does `options.src_dirs.iter().map(...).join(\\\", \\\")` (empty string) and `options.src_dirs.first()` (None) — producing a diagnostic reading 'None of the configured source directories exist: ' with no file attached, which is confusing/wrong wording for 'no src dirs were configured at all' vs 'the configured dirs don't exist on disk'. The real fix needs a distinct branch/message for the empty-vec case, not just a boolean flip.\\n\\nMinor scope gap: mod.rs:542 and mod.rs:562 (`test_discover_files`, `test_exclude_stories`) call `discover_files(&[dir], &[])` directly and would need updating for the new `&mut Vec<Diagnostic>` parameter — not fatal, but the mechanism section's task list doesn't mention these call sites.\",\n69\t            \"correction\": \"In the Mechanism paragraph, replace 'this one change fixes both the same-name-same-file collision AND the overlapping-src_dirs collision at once' with something like: 'the insert-return check catches genuine 3+-way same-key collisions; overlapping-src_dirs additionally needs its own fix (dedup src_files after sort in discover.rs, or dedup component_mappings by (name, file_path) before the keying loop) since a simple 2-directory overlap produces two differently-keyed entries that never collide at the BTreeMap level.' Also specify that the 220 fix needs a distinct diagnostic message/branch for 'src_dirs is empty' vs 'all configured src_dirs are missing', not just removing the `!is_empty()` conjunct. Add mod.rs:542/562 to the list of call sites needing updates when discover_files gains a diagnostics parameter.\"\n70\t          }\n71\t        ],\n72\t        \"overallVerdict\": \"The root-cause claims for cluster 3 are accurate at the line level and genuinely reachable (verified via config.rs, not blocked upstream) — this part of the document is trustworthy. However the proposed mechanism has a real gap worth fixing before planning: the 'insert() discard fixes both collisions at once' claim is false for the realistic 2-directory overlapping-src_dirs case (traced through the actual keying logic, it produces two differently-keyed entries, not a same-key overwrite), so the plan needs a second, explicit fix for that case (e.g. dedup in discover.rs) rather than relying solely on the insert-return check. The empty-src_dirs guard fix is also underspecified regarding what diagnostic message the fixed branch should produce. Revise root-cause-analysis.md's mechanism section for cluster 3 before building an implementation plan on top of it.\"\n73\t      },\n74\t      {\n75\t        \"groupName\": \"root-cause-analysis.md sections 5 & 6 (Unbounded allocation / LSP scaffold immaturity)\",\n76\t        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n77\t          {\n78\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"5. Unbounded allocation from input-derived size\",\n79\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n80\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n81\t            \"issues\": \"Verified against actual code. resolver/template.rs:94-104 has the exact Cartesian-product loop the doc describes (`result = next` accumulating per_part alternatives with zero size cap), and it does fall through to the Opaque+diagnostic path via the caller at template.rs:29-39 when try_expand_template_literal returns None (though a cap-triggered None looks identical to an ordinary unexpandable-template None to the diagnostic message text — minor, not incorrect). Checked for an implicit bound the analysis might have missed: resolver/mod.rs:46 defines MAX_DEPTH=20 and it is threaded through chain.rs/named.rs/collected.rs/primitives.rs (all confirmed at cited lines), and template.rs's own resolve_named_to_string_literals/try_expand_template_literal also thread depth+1 — but that only bounds *recursion depth* (how many nested type aliases get chased), not the *width* of any single union or the number of template-literal parts. A 6-part template literal where each part resolves to a 50-member LiteralUnion produces 50^6 (~15.6B) strings while depth stays at 1-2, nowhere near MAX_DEPTH=20. No union-member truncation exists anywhere in extractor or resolver (grepped, found nothing). So the claimed absence of an implicit bound is correct — the depth guard genuinely does not save this case. cli/commands/lsp.rs:36 confirmed: `vec![0u8; len]` where len comes straight from `header.trim().parse().ok()` at line 28, no upstream range/sanity check of any kind before the allocation. Only failure mode caught is non-numeric/negative-for-usize text (parse() returns None -> handled via content_length None branch), not large-but-valid values. Both root causes and the const-cap mechanism are sound and would compile (Opaque fallback path and diagnostic machinery already exist and are reused, not invented).\"\n82\t          },\n83\t          {\n84\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"6. LSP scaffold immaturity\",\n85\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n86\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n87\t            \"issues\": \"hoverProvider claim verified exactly: lsp.rs:56 advertises `\\\"hoverProvider\\\": true`, and a repo-wide grep for 'hover' turns up zero handler code anywhere in crates/ — only the doc comment (lsp.rs:3), the CLI help string (main.rs:60), and an unrelated `onHoverStart`/`onHoverEnd` HTML-attribute name in resolver/html.rs:8-9 (a synthetic-event name string, not LSP hover logic). match method at lsp.rs:47-79 has arms only for initialize/shutdown/exit, with `_ => {}` as the catch-all (line 78) — confirms a `textDocument/hover` request with an id gets no response, matching P1-6 exactly. Zero-test claim also confirmed: no #[test] or mod tests in lsp.rs, and `find crates/cli/tests -iname '*lsp*'` returns nothing. The desync/resync claim (P1-7) is real but the proposed fix's framing is imprecise: the doc says the mechanism is to 'make the content_length: None branch actually resync (consume to the next blank line) instead of silently continuing past an unconsumed body' — but tracing the actual control flow (lsp.rs:19-34), the inner header-reading loop already reads and breaks on the blank line (line 24-26) *before* the `let Some(len) = content_length else { continue }` check at line 32. So by the time that branch is reached, everything up to and including the blank line has already been consumed; 'consume to the next blank line' as stated would be a no-op, not a fix. The actual unrecoverable problem is that with no Content-Length there is no way to know how many bytes the (headerless) message body occupies, so it truly cannot be skip-consumed at all — the next read_line call will misinterpret body bytes as headers of a new message, which is the real desync mechanism. A correct fix needs a different strategy than 'consume to the next blank line' (e.g., treating any non-blank line that doesn't match a known header format, once inside a message with no declared length, as unrecoverable and closing/resetting the connection with a diagnostic, since LSP framing gives no other resync signal). This is a mechanism-soundness gap worth flagging before implementation, not a rejection of the overall diagnosis, which is otherwise accurate.\"\n88\t          }\n89\t        ],\n90\t        \"overallVerdict\": \"Both clusters in this section hold up under direct code verification — no fabricated claims, no overstated severity, and the code at every cited line matches the doc's description almost exactly (template.rs:94-104 Cartesian loop, lsp.rs:36 vec![0u8; len], lsp.rs:56 hoverProvider:true with a confirmed-empty grep for any hover handler, zero LSP tests). Cluster 5's mechanism (const caps falling through to the existing Opaque+diagnostic path) is sound and low-risk. Cluster 6's hoverProvider/no-handler and zero-test findings are solid and its 'don't advertise hoverProvider until implemented' fix is trivially correct, but the proposed resync mechanism for the content_length:None branch is imprecise/likely ineffective as literally described (the blank line is already consumed by the time that branch executes; the real problem — an unknown, unconsumable body length — needs a different fix than 'consume to the next blank line'). This section is safe to build a plan on provided the resync fix gets rethought during implementation rather than implemented as literally written.</overallVerdict>\\n\"\n91\t      },\n92\t      {\n93\t        \"groupName\": \"TOON truncation, schema drift, exit-code contract\",\n94\t        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n95\t          {\n96\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"9. TOON truncation indicator dropped in one branch\",\n97\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n98\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n99\t            \"issues\": \"Verified against crates/core/src/toon.rs:95-144. LiteralUnion (line 109-116) truncates to 6 members and correctly appends `format!(\\\"...(+{})\\\", members.len() - 6)` when truncated. Union (125-128) and Intersection (129-132), in the same format_type_compact function, truncate to 4 members via `.take(4)` with no indicator appended at all when members.len() > 4 — exactly as claimed. No existing test exercises Union/Intersection with >4 members (only test_format_type_compact_complex_types at line ~237 and the LiteralUnion truncation test at ~245), so adding the indicator would not break any test. One minor unaddressed nuance: Union/Intersection currently only format the first 4 members (cheap), while a straightforward `truncate_with_indicator(&[String], limit, sep)` helper as proposed would require formatting all members first then truncating (slightly more eager), which is a harmless implementation detail the doc doesn't call out but doesn't invalidate the mechanism.\",\n100\t            \"correction\": \"\"\n101\t          },\n102\t          {\n103\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"10. Schema hand-maintained separately from the real structs\",\n104\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n105\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n106\t            \"issues\": \"Field-drift claims verified exactly: schema.rs (crates/cli/src/commands/schema.rs:1-83) is a hand-written serde_json::json!() literal with only one test (test_cmd_schema_valid_json, line 79-82) checking .is_ok(), never diffing against real serialized output. ComponentEntry (core/types/output.rs:47-68) really has `.methods` (line 67) and `.tags` (line 65) absent from schema.rs. ExtractionStats (output.rs:609-619) has exactly 9 fields; schema.rs's stats block (schema.rs:58-67) documents only 4 (componentsExtracted, filesParsed, dtsCacheHits, durationMs) — the other 5 (componentsSkipped, dtsFilesParsed, tier1Count, tier3Count, opaqueCount) are missing, matching the '5 of 9' claim exactly. Diagnostic (core/types/diagnostic.rs:8-16) really has line/column/help absent from schema.rs's diagnostics block (schema.rs:45-57). schemars is confirmed absent from the workspace (no hits in Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock for any crate), so the 'new dependency' framing is accurate.\\n\\nHowever the mechanism's claim that OpaqueReason is one of 'the three recursive enums ADR-0002 already exempted from derive machinery for Serialize' is FALSE against the actual current code. OpaqueReason at core/types/output.rs:565-567 has `#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]` — an ordinary derive, not a hand-written impl — and it is not recursive (no field of type OpaqueReason nests into itself). Only PropType (output.rs:280, hand-written Serialize) and CollectedType (types/collected.rs:421, hand-written Serialize, genuinely recursive via Vec<CollectedType>/Box<CollectedType>) actually match that description. Interestingly ADR-0002 itself (docs/adr/0002-manual-serialize-for-prop-type.md line 11) asserts OpaqueReason also has a hand-written impl and doesn't derive — that claim in the ADR is stale/wrong relative to current code, and root-cause-analysis.md propagated the ADR's claim without checking the actual source, which is exactly the kind of unverified inference this audit is meant to catch.\",\n107\t            \"correction\": \"The mechanism should say: derive schemars::JsonSchema normally on OpaqueReason (it already derives Serialize/Deserialize with no recursion problem) alongside the other plain structs; reserve the hand-written JsonSchema treatment for PropType and CollectedType only, the two enums that are actually recursive and actually hand-write Serialize today. Also worth flagging separately (not part of this cluster's fix) that ADR-0002's own text is inaccurate about OpaqueReason and should be corrected or annotated.\"\n108\t          },\n109\t          {\n110\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"11. CLI exit-code contract has no shared type\",\n111\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n112\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n113\t            \"issues\": \"Verified extract.rs:68-73 (`output.diagnostics.iter().any(|d| matches!(d.severity, ...Error))` -> Ok(if has_errors {2} else {0})) and check.rs:21-46 (separate `.filter().collect()` for errors and warnings, then `if !errors.is_empty() { return Ok(2) }`, `if args.strict && !warnings.is_empty() { return Ok(1) }`, else Ok(0)) — syntactically different, semantically overlapping but not identical: check.rs additionally supports a --strict warnings-escalate-to-1 path that extract.rs has no equivalent flag for (ExtractArgs at main.rs:68-108 has no `strict` field). Confirmed main.rs:181-198 exactly: Command::Watch discards cmd_watch's return and hardcodes exit code 0 (line 182-185), Command::Inspect does the same (187-190). Confirmed cmd_watch (crates/cli/src/commands/watch.rs:6) returns Result<()>, and cmd_inspect (crates/cli/src/commands/inspect.rs:5) returns Result<()> and never reads output.diagnostics anywhere in the file (grep for 'diagnostic' in inspect.rs returns zero hits) — it only errors on a missing-component lookup, exactly as claimed.\\n\\nGap in the mechanism: the proposed `max_severity(&self) -> Option<DiagnosticSeverity>` requires an ordering over DiagnosticSeverity (Error worse than Warning worse than Info) to compute a 'max', but DiagnosticSeverity (core/types/diagnostic.rs:44-51) currently derives only `Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize` — no Ord/PartialOrd, and no manual Ord impl exists anywhere in the file. The doc's one-line method signature doesn't mention that either DiagnosticSeverity needs an Ord/rank added (with attention to derived-Ord's declaration-order semantics being the *inverse* of severity-worst-first) or that max_severity needs a hand-written comparison, e.g. matching to an integer rank. This is a small but real gap someone implementing the mechanism as literally stated would trip on.\",\n114\t            \"correction\": \"Add either `#[derive(PartialOrd, Ord, Eq)]` to DiagnosticSeverity with variants reordered so Error < Warning < Info reads correctly for whichever direction 'max' is meant to go (or add a private rank() -> u8 helper and implement max_severity via that instead of relying on derived Ord), and state that explicitly as part of this cluster's mechanism rather than leaving it implicit in the method signature.\"\n115\t          }\n116\t        ],\n117\t        \"overallVerdict\": \"All three clusters' factual root-cause claims check out precisely against current source (toon.rs line-level truncation behavior, schema.rs field-by-field drift counts, and the extract/check/watch/inspect exit-code asymmetry are all exactly as described, down to line numbers and specific missing field names). Cluster 9 is safe to implement as-is. Clusters 10 and 11 are safe to build a plan on but need small corrections before implementation: cluster 10's mechanism incorrectly extends ADR-0002's hand-written-Serialize/recursion-limit rationale to OpaqueReason, which is not actually recursive and already uses a normal derive in current code (the analysis trusted ADR-0002's prose over the actual source, and ADR-0002 itself is stale on this point) — fix is cheaper than stated, not harder. Cluster 11's mechanism omits that DiagnosticSeverity has no ordering today, which max_severity() depends on, and elides that check.rs's --strict warning-escalation path has no equivalent flag on extract's args today. Neither correction changes the overall shape of the fix or the P0/P3 severity ratings, which are accurately characterized.\"\n118\t      },\n119\t      {\n120\t        \"groupName\": \"Standalone findings (7 entries)\",\n121\t        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n122\t          {\n123\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"Generic alias .zip() silently truncates trailing type params (substitute.rs:33-43)\",\n124\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n125\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n126\t            \"issues\": \"build_substitution (substitute.rs:33-43) does exactly what's claimed: params.iter().zip(args.iter()...) silently drops any params beyond args.len(), with no diagnostic. Verified against the real signature and both call sites (apply_generic_args:57-73, generic_alias_with_structured_args:83-107). No existing test references build_substitution or exercises partial application, so there's no risk of a 'fix' breaking current behavior-dependent tests -- but note the doc doesn't actually propose a mechanism for this one (it's correctly left as an open standalone ticket in the table, not a false claim of a ready fix), so there's nothing to validate for soundness beyond the root-cause claim itself.\",\n127\t            \"correction\": \"\"\n128\t          },\n129\t          {\n130\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"watch --out write failure discarded via `let _ =` (watch.rs:107-112)\",\n131\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n132\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n133\t            \"issues\": \"watch.rs:110 is exactly `let _ = std::fs::write(p, json);` inside the Watchexec::new(move |action| {...}) closure -- confirmed verbatim. The non-atomic fs::write claim is also correct: unlike cache.rs::try_save (which does tmp-write + rename), this is a direct truncating write. However the proposed mechanism ('the real fix is routing through the CLI's own miette/output.rs error path') glosses over a real obstacle: this code runs inside a Watchexec callback closure with signature `move |action| { ...; action }` that returns `Action`, not `Result<_, _>` -- there's no `?` or return-propagation path available here. The actual fix has to print/log the failure inline (e.g. via output.rs's diagnostic printer or eprintln!) rather than 'propagate through miette', since propagation out of this closure isn't structurally possible without restructuring the watch loop.\",\n134\t            \"correction\": \"State explicitly that the fix must surface the error inline within the closure (print via output.rs, not miette propagation), since the Watchexec callback closure returns Action, not Result, and can't propagate an error upward the way the rest of the CLI does.\"\n135\t          },\n136\t          {\n137\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"ParsedProp allows contradictory required:true + default_value:Some(_) (types/output.rs:79-99)\",\n138\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n139\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"unsound\",\n140\t            \"issues\": \"The contradictory-state claim is accurate: ParsedProp (output.rs:81-99) has fully pub fields, no constructor, Default not derived, and nothing in the type prevents required:true + default_value:Some(_). Real bare-literal construction sites found by direct grep (not the ~10-sites-across-6-files figure sometimes cited): known.rs:297 (inside simple_prop, already a de-facto private constructor), resolver/mod.rs:297 (notable_inherited synthesis), resolver/chain.rs:244, resolver/alias.rs:181 -- 4 production sites across 4 files -- plus 3 test-only literals (resolver/mod.rs:827, 840; toon.rs:167) across 2 more files. Total 7 sites / 5 files, not ~10/6. More importantly, the proposed mechanism ('make fields pub(crate)') is actually broken: (1) crates/cli is a *separate crate* from crates/core and reads ParsedProp's fields directly and extensively -- extract.rs:107-112,140-144 (prop.name, prop.prop_type, prop.required, prop.default_value, prop.description, prop.parent) and inspect.rs:52-58 (prop.prop_type, prop.required, prop.default_value, prop.parent, prop.name). Making the fields pub(crate) would be a hard compile error in crates/cli. (2) Even setting that aside, pub(crate) grants visibility to the *entire defining crate* -- and every one of the offending construction sites (chain.rs, alias.rs, mod.rs, known.rs) is inside that same crate (oxc-react-docgen-core). pub(crate) would not stop any of the misuse it's meant to prevent; it only blocks *other crates* from bare-constructing, which none of the actual violators are.\",\n141\t            \"correction\": \"The fix needs field privacy scoped to the defining module (types::output), not pub(crate) -- e.g. keep fields effectively private via a private marker/sealed field so only ParsedProp::new(...) can construct one even from other core modules, while adding public accessor methods (or keeping fields pub for read but removing all direct positional/struct-literal construction ability another way) so crates/cli's existing field reads keep compiling. Simply flipping visibility to pub(crate) solves neither the cross-crate compile break nor the same-crate misuse it's meant to fix.\"\n142\t          },\n143\t          {\n144\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"DiagnosticCode::Unknown never constructed (types/diagnostic.rs:61)\",\n145\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n146\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n147\t            \"issues\": \"Grepped `DiagnosticCode::Unknown` across all of crates/ (rg with no --include filter issue this time) -- zero matches anywhere, confirming it is genuinely never constructed in the repo. The enum is confirmed #[non_exhaustive] at diagnostic.rs:54, directly above the enum definition that includes the Unknown variant at line 61 (actually 'Unknown' isn't even a listed variant in the current enum body I read -- rechecking: the variants present are UnresolvableImport, OpaqueType, MaxDepthExceeded, Unknown, JsDocDefaultMismatch, ComputedDefault, IndexedAccessOpaque, TemplateLiteralOpaque, DiscriminatedUnion, IoError, ExcessiveNesting, ParseError -- Unknown is indeed present at line 61). The doc's 'no fix needed, intentional headroom on a non_exhaustive enum' framing is a reasonable and low-risk call; nothing contradicts it.\",\n148\t            \"correction\": \"\"\n149\t          },\n150\t          {\n151\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"Cache staleness on same-tick, same-size edits (cache.rs:181-191, P0-2)\",\n152\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n153\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n154\t            \"issues\": \"key_for (cache.rs:181-191) keys on (path, size, mtime as full nanosecond SystemTime duration) -- confirmed this already extracts the maximum timestamp precision the OS API exposes; there's no cheaper within-the-current-mechanism precision gain left to claim. The doc's framing ('genuine filesystem-timestamp-resolution limitation... already does the best available thing') holds up: the residual staleness case truly requires an independent signal (content hash) the current key doesn't carry, not a logic bug. Minor imprecision: the doc says 'exactly one call site' but key_for is actually invoked from two places (get at line 121, insert at line 129) -- immaterial to the conclusion since both call the same helper consistently, but the phrasing is loose. The task prompt asked specifically whether a cheaper mitigation than full content-hashing was dismissed too quickly: since cache entries are scoped to .d.ts files only (typically small), a fast non-cryptographic hash (e.g. a single pass xxhash/CRC over the bytes already being read for size) would be materially cheaper than what 'content hash' implies and still close most of the gap -- the doc's blanket 'scoped performance/complexity tradeoff' framing slightly overstates the cost of the mitigation it defers, though it doesn't misstate the root cause or claim the wrong fix, just under-specifies how cheap a decent fix could be.\",\n155\t            \"correction\": \"\"\n156\t          },\n157\t          {\n158\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity round-trips to 0.0 (types/output.rs:314,423)\",\n159\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"partially-wrong\",\n160\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n161\t            \"issues\": \"The serialization mechanics are confirmed exactly as described: PropType::NumberLiteral(n) at output.rs:314 goes through serde_json::json!({...,\\\"value\\\": n}) where serde_json's From<f64> maps non-finite floats to Value::Null (Number::from_f64 returns None for NaN/Infinity), and the 'numberLiteral' deserialize arm at output.rs:423 does `v[\\\"value\\\"].as_f64().unwrap_or(0.0)`, silently turning that null back into 0.0. But the doc's severity claim -- 'Likely unreachable via normal TS literal syntax, but present' -- is wrong, not just optimistic. extractor/mod.rs:328 does `TSLiteral::NumericLiteral(n) => CollectedType::NumberLiteral(n.value)` with n.value being the f64 OXC parses directly from source text, no bounds check. A completely ordinary numeric-literal type like `type X = 1e400;` is valid TS/JS numeric-literal syntax and parses to `f64::INFINITY` under IEEE-754 overflow -- this is reachable through normal, not adversarial, source text, contradicting the 'no realistic reachable input' framing.\",\n162\t            \"correction\": \"Change 'no realistic reachable input' to acknowledge that an overflowing exponent literal (e.g. `1e400`) reaches this path through ordinary TS numeric-literal-type syntax, extracted verbatim at extractor/mod.rs:328 with no range check -- raise this from cosmetic-low-priority to at least worth a guard/diagnostic at the extraction boundary, since 'realistic' understates it.\"\n163\t          },\n164\t          {\n165\t            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"Zero-member LiteralUnion serializes as an empty RDT enum (cli/commands/extract.rs rdt_type_json)\",\n166\t            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n167\t            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n168\t            \"issues\": \"Confirmed: PropType::is_literal_union() (output.rs:212-220) returns true vacuously for an empty Union(vec![]) (iter().all() on empty is true) and unconditionally true for any PropType::LiteralUnion{..} regardless of member count. rdt_type_json (extract.rs:81-95) then emits {\\\"name\\\":\\\"enum\\\",\\\"value\\\":[]} for either shape with zero members. PropType::LiteralUnion is constructed from several live paths that could plausibly produce zero members from a malformed/edge-case source input (known.rs:208 cva variant extraction, resolver/primitives.rs:124, resolver/alias.rs:426, resolver/mod.rs:673, resolver/template.rs:26) -- not a purely theoretical shape. The 'low-priority, cosmetic' severity call is fair; nothing found suggests this is more serious than claimed.\",\n169\t            \"correction\": \"\"\n170\t          }\n171\t        ],\n172\t        \"overallVerdict\": \"Five of the seven standalone findings hold up well: substitute.rs's silent truncation, DiagnosticCode::Unknown's dead-code status, cache.rs's mtime-resolution limitation, and the zero-member LiteralUnion cosmetic gap are all accurately root-caused with correctly-scoped (or correctly deferred) fixes. Two need real correction before a plan is built on top of them. First, the ParsedProp fix ('make fields pub(crate)') is actively broken -- it would fail to compile against crates/cli's existing direct field reads (extract.rs, inspect.rs), and even where it would compile, pub(crate) grants access to the exact same-crate call sites (chain.rs, alias.rs, mod.rs, known.rs) that are the whole problem, so it wouldn't stop the misuse it's meant to stop; this needs a module-private-plus-constructor (sealed-field) approach instead, not a visibility-level tweak. Second, the NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity finding's severity is understated, not just optimistic: an ordinary numeric-literal type like `type X = 1e400;` reaches the exact null-round-trips-to-0.0 path through normal TS syntax (verified at extractor/mod.rs:328), not just a hypothetical. The watch.rs --out fix also needs a scoping correction (the write happens inside a Watchexec closure that returns Action, not Result, so 'propagate through miette' isn't literally available -- it needs an inline print instead). None of these invalidate the underlying edge-case catalog, but the root-cause-analysis.md prose should be corrected on these three points before Phase 1 tasks are scoped from it.\"\n173\t      }\n174\t    ],\n175\t    \"synthesis\": \"# Verdict: root-cause-analysis.md Adversarial Verification Synthesis\\n\\n## Overall Verdict\\n\\nRoot-cause-analysis.md is **largely trustworthy on diagnosis** — every skeptic independently confirmed the empirical root-cause claims down to line numbers, with zero fabricated citations across all 7 groups. It is **not safe to build an implementation plan on as-is**, however: three mechanisms are outright broken (won't compile or won't stop the misuse they target), and several more are underspecified in ways that would trip up an implementer. The fix is targeted prose edits to the Mechanism sections of clusters 1, 3, 6, 10, 11, plus the ParsedProp and watch.rs standalone findings — the diagnoses themselves need no rework.\\n\\n## Cluster Table\\n\\n| Cluster/Finding | Root Cause | Mechanism | Required Correction |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 4. No panic-containment boundary | confirmed | needs-adjustment | `&mut` captures in plugin hooks are `!UnwindSafe`; must specify `AssertUnwindSafe` + rationale, or discard mutated value on catch |\\n| ADR-0005 | confirmed | needs-adjustment | Note napi-rs's opt-in `#[napi(catch_unwind)]`; soften \\\"one sanctioned helper covers all three call sites\\\" |\\n| 8. Resolver precedence (P0-1) | confirmed | needs-adjustment | Note two further undiagnosed divergences (react-builtin order, ts-utility-type order) not fixed by the proposed shared function |\\n| 1. Resolver give-up constructor discipline | partially-wrong | unsound | Narrow to 3 confirmed sites (func.rs:61, chain.rs:40, alias.rs:116); drop/redesign `ResolvedChain` privacy proposal; known.rs and Deserialize impl can't use `give_up()` |\\n| 2. Extractor missing give-up signal | confirmed | sound | Minor: visit.rs:262-293 isn't an `.or_else()` chain, it's a single detector — cosmetic only |\\n| 7. Extractor depth-tracking proxy mismatch | confirmed | sound | none |\\n| 3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps | confirmed | needs-adjustment | `insert()`-discard fix doesn't catch 2-way src_dir overlap (different keys); needs separate dedup fix; empty-src_dirs guard needs distinct message, not a boolean flip |\\n| 5. Unbounded allocation (template/LSP) | confirmed | sound | none |\\n| 6. LSP scaffold immaturity | confirmed | needs-adjustment | Resync fix as stated (\\\"consume to next blank line\\\") is a no-op — blank line already consumed; needs real strategy for unknown body length |\\n| 9. TOON truncation indicator dropped | confirmed | sound | none |\\n| 10. Schema hand-maintained separately | confirmed | needs-adjustment | `OpaqueReason` is NOT recursive/hand-written Serialize (ADR-0002 itself is stale); derive `JsonSchema` normally on it, reserve manual treatment for `PropType`/`CollectedType` only |\\n| 11. CLI exit-code contract | confirmed | needs-adjustment | `DiagnosticSeverity` has no `Ord`; `max_severity()` needs explicit ranking added; note check.rs's `--strict` has no extract.rs equivalent |\\n| Generic alias `.zip()` truncation | confirmed | sound (no mechanism proposed) | none |\\n| watch --out write failure discarded | confirmed | needs-adjustment | Fix must print inline in the Watchexec closure (`Action`-returning, not `Result`) — \\\"propagate through miette\\\" isn't reachable |\\n| ParsedProp contradictory required+default | confirmed | **unsound** | `pub(crate)` fails to compile (crates/cli reads fields directly) and doesn't stop same-crate misuse; needs sealed-field + constructor + accessors instead |\\n| DiagnosticCode::Unknown never constructed | confirmed | sound | none |\\n| Cache staleness same-tick/same-size | confirmed | sound | none (minor: \\\"one call site\\\" is actually two, immaterial) |\\n| NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity → 0.0 | **partially-wrong** (severity) | sound | Reachable via ordinary `type X = 1e400;`, not just adversarial input — raise priority |\\n| Zero-member LiteralUnion → empty enum | confirmed | sound | none |\\n\\n## Must-Fix-Before-Planning\\n\\n1. **Cluster 1 (Resolver give-up constructors) — mechanism is unsound.** Rewrite: scope the fix to exactly 3 sites (func.rs:61, chain.rs:40, alias.rs:116) using a helper callable where `ResolveState` is already in scope. Drop the \\\"make `ResolvedChain`'s bare construction private, `give_up()` the only public entry point\\\" proposal as written — Rust's privacy model can't distinguish give-up call sites from the ~9 legitimate accumulator-init sites at the same module tier, and known.rs's ~7 Opaque-construction sites are architecturally barred from touching `ResolveState` per the no-reverse-deps rule, plus `types/output.rs`'s Deserialize impl constructs `Opaque` with no `ResolveState` in scope at all. Leave known.rs's construct-then-caller-diagnoses split as-is (it's correct and tested).\\n\\n2. **ParsedProp fix — mechanism is unsound.** `pub(crate)` will not compile: `crates/cli` (extract.rs, inspect.rs) reads `ParsedProp`'s fields directly across a crate boundary. Even ignoring that, `pub(crate)` grants access to the exact same-crate sites (chain.rs, alias.rs, mod.rs, known.rs) causing the problem, so it wouldn't stop the misuse. Replace with a module-private-plus-constructor (sealed field) approach: force construction through `ParsedProp::new(...)`, keep or add public read accessors so `crates/cli` keeps compiling.\\n\\n3. **Cluster 4 mechanism — plugin hook wrapping won't compile.** `contain_panic<T: FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe>` cannot wrap `plugin.on_file_extracted(path, &mut data)` / `plugin.on_component_resolved(&mut entry)` as literally written — `&mut T` is unconditionally `!UnwindSafe`. Add an explicit decision: use `AssertUnwindSafe` with a stated rationale for tolerating partially-mutated `SourceData`/`ComponentEntry` after a caught panic, or redesign so a caught panic discards the mutated value instead of letting the caller (mod.rs:292-297, :386) trust it.\\n\\n4. **Cluster 6 (LSP resync) — mechanism is ineffective as stated.** \\\"Make the `content_length: None` branch resync by consuming to the next blank line\\\" is a no-op — the header loop (lsp.rs:19-34) already consumes through the blank line before that branch runs. The real problem is an unknown, unconsumable body length with no LSP-level resync signal. Replace with: treat a no-Content-Length message as unrecoverable and close/reset the connection with a diagnostic, rather than attempting to skip-consume it.\\n\\n## Worth Noting But Not Blocking\\n\\n- **Cluster 8 (resolver precedence):** fix is sound and zero-regression-risk (no fixture exercises known-pattern-named interfaces), but note in the text that `chain.rs`/`named.rs` will still diverge on react-builtin-check and ts-utility-type-check ordering after the P0-1 fix — don't imply full unification.\\n- **Cluster 3 (pipeline gaps):** the `insert()`-discard fix only catches 3+-way same-key collisions, not the realistic 2-directory overlap case (which produces two differently-keyed entries). Add a separate dedup fix (e.g., `files.dedup()` after sort, or dedup `component_mappings` by `(name, file_path)`). The empty-`src_dirs` guard fix needs a distinct diagnostic message for \\\"no dirs configured\\\" vs. \\\"configured dirs missing,\\\" not a plain `&&` flip. Also note mod.rs:542/562 tests need updating for the new diagnostics parameter on `discover_files`.\\n- **Cluster 10 (schema drift):** cheaper than stated — `OpaqueReason` already derives ordinary `Serialize`/`Deserialize` and isn't recursive, so it can derive `JsonSchema` normally. ADR-0002's own text is stale on this point and should be corrected too.\\n- **Cluster 11 (exit codes):** `DiagnosticSeverity` needs an explicit ranking (no derived `Ord` today; watch declaration-order pitfalls) before `max_severity()` can be implemented as described. Also flag that `check.rs`'s `--strict` warning-escalation has no equivalent flag on `extract`'s args yet.\\n- **watch.rs --out write failure:** correct diagnosis, but state explicitly the fix must print inline within the Watchexec closure (returns `Action`, not `Result` — can't propagate via `?`/miette).\\n- **NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity:** severity is understated, not wrong in mechanism — `type X = 1e400;` is ordinary TS syntax that reaches the null-round-trips-to-0.0 path via extractor/mod.rs:328 with no bounds check. Bump from cosmetic to worth a guard/diagnostic at extraction time.\\n- **ADR-0005:** mention napi-rs's opt-in `#[napi(catch_unwind)]` as a complementary FFI-boundary mechanism; soften the \\\"one sanctioned helper\\\" framing given the plugin-hook `UnwindSafe` issue above.\"\n176\t  },\n177\t  \"workflowProgress\": [\n178\t    {\n179\t      \"type\": \"workflow_phase\","}]},"uuid":"de9f9383-c8b2-4cec-a009-4bdd3738cf48","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:47:31.492Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-oxc-react-codegen/0ae3953f-9438-46c4-a76a-88fce9eed529/tasks/w33aybccq.output","content":"{\n  \"summary\": \"Adversarially stress-test root-cause-analysis.md: is each root cause actually correct, and would each mechanism proposal actually work\",\n  \"agentCount\": 8,\n  \"logs\": [],\n  \"result\": {\n    \"verdicts\": [\n      {\n        \"groupName\": \"4. No panic-containment boundary / ADR 0005\",\n        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"4. No panic-containment boundary (root cause)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n            \"issues\": \"Root-cause claims verified accurate: (1) `grep -rn catch_unwind` across the repo (excluding target/) returns zero hits in any .rs file — only doc mentions. (2) crates/binding/src/lib.rs:118-127 (extract_all), 144-166 (extract_file_incremental), 171-189 (initialize_session) all wrap their body in `tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {...}).await.map_err(...)?`, and a panic inside the closure produces `JoinError` on `.await` rather than propagating the unwind — accurate. (3) create_session (lib.rs:131-138) and close_session (lib.rs:192-195) are plain sync `#[napi] pub fn` with no spawn_blocking — accurate, matches doc's cited lines almost exactly. (4) pipeline/mod.rs:260 (parse rayon `.map()`) and :357 (resolve rayon `.map()`) have no per-item catch_unwind — accurate. (5) plugin.rs's `run_on_file_extracted`/`run_on_component_resolved` loops (lines 47-56) call `plugin.on_file_extracted(file_path, data)` / `plugin.on_component_resolved(entry)` directly with no isolation between plugins — accurate; these are called sequentially from pipeline/mod.rs:293 and :385 (not inside the rayon closures — both plugin hook call sites are in the sequential post-collect loops, so the doc's mechanism of wrapping 'each individual call' is targeting the right, already-sequential granularity). (6) watch.rs:93 `self.initialized.lock().expect(\\\"init lock poisoned\\\")` holds the guard across the entire body of `initialize()`, which calls `extract_with_global` (the same rayon+plugin pipeline) — so a panic during that call while the guard is held does poison the mutex permanently, exactly as claimed. All root-cause claims check out.\\n\\nHowever the Mechanism paragraph (line 45) has a real, verified gap: it proposes wrapping `plugin.on_x(...)` calls individually via `contain_panic<T>(label, f: impl FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe)`, but `plugin.rs:49` (`plugin.on_file_extracted(file_path, data)` where `data: &mut SourceData`) and `plugin.rs:55` (`plugin.on_component_resolved(entry)` where `entry: &mut ComponentEntry`) each capture a `&mut` reference. I verified empirically (rustc E0277) that `&mut T` unconditionally does NOT implement `UnwindSafe` in std, regardless of T — this is a hard compile error, not a style nit. The doc never mentions this and gives no indication the fix needs `AssertUnwindSafe`. Using `AssertUnwindSafe` here isn't just plumbing either: it papers over the real hazard std's UnwindSafe bound exists to flag — if a plugin panics mid-mutation of `data`/`entry`, the caller (pipeline/mod.rs:293/385) unconditionally continues using that possibly half-mutated value afterward (`diagnostics.append(&mut data.diagnostics)`, `global.merge(&path, data)` at mod.rs:292/297; `components.insert(key, entry)` at mod.rs:386) with no rollback or corruption check. The mechanism needs to explicitly address this — either specify AssertUnwindSafe with a documented rationale for why partial mutation is acceptable here, or change the design so a caught panic discards the mutated value rather than trusting it. As written, 'wrap each individual plugin.on_x(...) call' is not directly buildable against the stated `contain_panic` signature.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"Proposed ADR-0005\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n            \"issues\": \"Context/Decision/Consequences text is consistent with the verified root cause and doesn't overstate anything — 'accident of spawn_blocking's JoinError' is accurate per my read of lib.rs. One thing worth noting: the ADR (and root-cause doc) present create_session/close_session's lack of spawn_blocking as purely an oversight relative to their siblings, without checking whether napi-rs itself has any opt-in panic-safety knob that would change the fix's shape. I checked: napi-derive-backend 5.1.1 (matches pinned napi=\\\"3\\\"/napi-derive=\\\"3\\\") has an opt-in `#[napi(catch_unwind)]` attribute (napi-derive-backend/src/ast.rs:35, codegen/fn.rs:142-145) that wraps the generated function body in `std::panic::catch_unwind` — but it is opt-in (bool, unset by default) and none of the four `#[napi]` fns in lib.rs use it. So napi-rs does NOT auto-protect create_session/close_session (confirms edge-cases.md's P1-9 'needs verification' item in the direction the doc assumed), but this also surfaces a second, simpler sanctioned tool already available at the FFI boundary specifically (`#[napi(catch_unwind)]`) that the ADR doesn't mention as an alternative/complement to the custom `panic_guard::contain_panic` helper — worth a line in the ADR's reasoning since it changes the 'one sanctioned helper' framing at that boundary.\\n\\nSeparately: the ADR's Decision line says panics from 'a rayon `.map()`, a DocgenPlugin hook, or a NAPI entry point' are contained through 'one sanctioned helper' — but per the finding above, the plugin-hook case can't cleanly use that same helper without the AssertUnwindSafe caveat, so the ADR's claim of one uniform mechanism for all three call-site classes is slightly optimistic; the plugin case needs an explicit, documented exception/wrapper convention, not just a drop-in call.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"overallVerdict\": \"The empirical root-cause claims for cluster 4 hold up well under direct verification — zero catch_unwind in the repo, all three async NAPI entry points genuinely rely on spawn_blocking's JoinError as accidental protection, create_session/close_session genuinely skip it, the rayon closures at pipeline/mod.rs:260 and :357 genuinely have no per-item boundary, and watch.rs:93's mutex genuinely stays locked across the exact pipeline call that would panic. This section is safe to build a plan on as far as diagnosis goes. But the proposed mechanism has one concrete, verified defect that must be corrected before implementation: wrapping the plugin hook calls (plugin.rs:49,55) in `contain_panic<T: FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe>` will not compile as literally described, because both calls capture a `&mut` reference and `&mut T` is unconditionally !UnwindSafe in std (verified via rustc reproduction). The plan needs an explicit decision — AssertUnwindSafe plus a stated rationale for tolerating partially-mutated SourceData/ComponentEntry after a caught panic, or a design that discards/validates the mutated value on panic rather than trusting it — before this task is handed to an implementer. The rayon closures at mod.rs:260/357 are comparatively low-risk (no &mut captures observed; ResolutionContext at resolver/mod.rs:118-143 doesn't carry the PluginRegistry/trait-object fields that would create their own RefUnwindSafe problems), though DashMap/oxc_resolver::Resolver's exact RefUnwindSafe status wasn't exhaustively verified. The ADR draft is reasonable but should note napi-rs's own opt-in `#[napi(catch_unwind)]` (confirmed present in napi-derive-backend 5.1.1, opt-in only) as an available complementary mechanism at the FFI boundary specifically, and should soften its 'one sanctioned helper covers all three call-site classes' framing given the plugin-hook UnwindSafe wrinkle. No existing test locks in the current panic-crashes-everything behavior (no should_panic tests found touching pipeline/plugin/binding code), so fixing this is not a breaking-test-change concern.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"groupName\": \"Resolver give-up constructor discipline (cluster 1) + Resolver precedence ordering (cluster 8)\",\n        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"8. Resolver precedence: two hand-copied orderings (P0-1)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n            \"issues\": \"Verified directly: chain.rs's resolve_props_chain checks known-pattern shortcuts (Step 2, chain.rs:92-141, calling resolve_known) BEFORE canonical/type-alias/interface lookup (Steps 3-5, chain.rs:142-158). named.rs's resolve_named checks canonical resolution -> type-alias (named.rs:41-58) -> interface (named.rs:60-65) BEFORE the known-pattern fallback (Step 5, named.rs:67-84), with an explicit comment at named.rs:41-43 stating this order is intentional. The doc's core claim and cited line ranges (chain.rs:90-106, named.rs:41-72) are accurate. I searched every fixture under crates/core/tests and the top-level fixtures/ tree, and grepped every known.rs pattern name (SxProps, SlotProps, VariantProps, StylesApiProps, ThemingProps, RecipeVariantProps, RecipeVariants) against 'extends' clauses -- zero hits anywhere. So fixing the precedence bug will NOT change any current snapshot/test output; regression risk is effectively nil (worth stating positively rather than leaving it an open question). Where the doc's mechanism needs adjustment: it frames the situation as 'two hand-copied orderings' of one shared 4-step sequence, but the two functions actually diverge in at least two further, unmentioned ways beyond the P0-1 step: (1) named.rs checks react-builtin FIRST (named.rs:31-34, before canonical resolution) while chain.rs checks it AFTER the known-pattern block (chain.rs Step 2.5, ~line 142); (2) chain.rs checks is_ts_utility_type very early (Step 1, chain.rs:86-89, before known-pattern) while named.rs's analogous silent-no-op check happens last (Step 6, named.rs:88-113, after known-pattern AND after interface/alias). The proposed `resolve_source_defined_or_known(...)` shared function (fixed order resolve_to_canonical -> lookup_type_alias -> lookup_interface -> resolve_known) is scoped correctly to fix P0-1 specifically and is compileable as a plan (both callers already have the necessary state/ctx/depth in scope, and closures for the differing terminal type (Named vs ResolvedChain) are a standard generic-return pattern), so 'Resolves: P0-1' is not overstated for that narrow claim. But calling it 'the same sequence' independently drifting is an incomplete diagnosis -- there are two more silent ordering divergences the doc doesn't catch, so a reviewer implementing only the P0-1 fix should not assume chain.rs and named.rs are now unified; they will still diverge on react-builtin and ts-utility-type ordering.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"1. Resolver give-up constructor discipline\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"partially-wrong\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"unsound\",\n            \"issues\": \"The premise that opaque/give-up construction is inconsistent is real, but only for exactly 3 sites, not '6 of ~10': func.rs:61 (MultiParamFunction) has zero diagnostic push anywhere nearby -- confirmed silent give-up. chain.rs:40 (cycle-detected `return ResolvedChain::default();`) has zero diagnostic push -- confirmed silent give-up. alias.rs:116 (`CollectedTypeAlias::LiteralUnion => ResolvedChain::default()`) has zero diagnostic, unlike the parallel diagnosed path at alias.rs:234-253 -- confirmed silent give-up. These three exactly match edge-cases.md's own three listed findings, so the *scope* of the real bug is 3 sites, not '6 of ~10 Opaque construction sites... invisible to a reviewer scanning the happy path' as root-cause-analysis.md states at line 13. The two other sites the doc explicitly names as bypassing -- primitives.rs:181 and template.rs:39 -- both already push a full, correctly-shaped Diagnostic immediately before constructing the Opaque (primitives.rs:171-179, template.rs:29-37); they just don't call collected.rs's private `push_opaque_diagnostic` helper (which is `fn`, not `pub`/`pub(crate)`, so it can't even be called from primitives.rs/template.rs today without a visibility change the doc never mentions). That's a duplication/style issue, not a give-up-with-no-diagnostic bug, and conflating it with func.rs:61 overstates severity. Mechanism is unsound as literally described, for two independent reasons: (1) Reverse-dependency violation: known.rs owns ~7 of the real PropType::Opaque construction sites (known.rs:102,108,126,133,193,221,228) and is explicitly documented (known.rs:74) and required by CLAUDE.md:30 ('No reverse dependencies') to never depend on `resolver::ResolveState`. The proposed `OpaqueDetail::give_up(state: &mut ResolveState, ...)` as the *only* public constructor cannot be called from known.rs at all -- the plan doesn't address this, and it's not a minor gap: it's ~7 of the ~18 real crate-wide Opaque construction sites (the '~10' count itself undercounts; resolver/ alone has ~9, plus known.rs's ~7, plus types/output.rs's hand-written Deserialize impl at lines 502-533 which constructs `PropType::Opaque{raw,reason}` twice (529, 531) from wire JSON with no ResolveState in scope at all -- deserialization isn't a give-up event and the plan never accounts for it). Existing tests (mod.rs:2117-2137 `test_known_opaque_result_emits_diagnostic`, mod.rs:2139-2160 `test_known_opaque_result_emits_diagnostic_at_chain_level`) confirm this two-step design is intentional and already covered: known.rs constructs the bare Opaque value, the *caller* (chain.rs:122-124, named.rs:74-76) diagnoses it via `push_known_opaque_diagnostic` using context (type_name_bare, consuming_file) that known.rs doesn't have. Collapsing construction+diagnosis into one constructor is incompatible with this tested split. (2) ResolvedChain privacy is close to a non-issue as stated, and actively harmful if implemented literally: the struct (`struct ResolvedChain` at mod.rs:346, no `pub`) and every field are already private to the resolver module tree -- unreachable from pipeline/ or anywhere outside resolver/ today. Within resolver/, Rust's privacy model gives chain.rs, alias.rs, named.rs, and extends.rs (all descendant modules of `resolver`) *equal* access to resolver::mod.rs's private items -- there is no Rust mechanism to let alias.rs use `ResolvedChain::default()` for legitimate accumulator initialization while blocking chain.rs's give-up call, since both are the same privacy tier. And the bare/Default construction is overwhelmingly NOT give-up usage: of the 11 `ResolvedChain::default()`/bare-literal sites I found (chain.rs:40,89,108,120,191; extends.rs:65; alias.rs:106,168,178,291,380), only chain.rs:40 is a true silent give-up. The rest are legitimate accumulator seeds later filled by `chain.merge_parent(...)` in a loop (alias.rs:106,168,178,291,380; chain.rs:191), a deliberately-silent no-op for a non-error case (chain.rs:89's TS-utility-type short-circuit, and the intentional `HtmlAttributes`-as-inheritance-layer case at extends.rs:65), or a success-path struct-update literal (chain.rs:108,120). Forcing all of these through a single diagnostic-emitting `give_up()` as 'the only public entry point' would either spuriously diagnose non-error paths or require a second, undocumented constructor -- the doc doesn't distinguish these categories at all, so 'make bare/Default construction private, add `give_up` as the only public entry point' does not describe an implementable, compiling change without significant redesign beyond what's written. Correction needed: scope the mechanism down to the 3 confirmed silent sites (func.rs:61, chain.rs:40, alias.rs:116) using a small helper that's callable where ResolveState is already in scope (all three qualify), leave known.rs's construction+caller-diagnoses split as-is (it's correct and tested), fix types/output.rs's Deserialize impl to use whatever crate-visible raw constructor is needed for wire reconstruction (not give_up), and drop the ResolvedChain-privacy half of the proposal or redesign it to keep a separate non-diagnosing `ResolvedChain::new_accumulator()`/similar alongside `give_up()`, explicitly acknowledging both are needed.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"overallVerdict\": \"P0-1 (cluster 8) is real and correctly cited down to line numbers; the fix is soundly scoped for that narrow claim, carries zero regression risk against current fixtures/snapshots (verified by exhaustive grep, no fixture extends a known-pattern-named interface), though the doc understates that chain.rs and named.rs diverge in two further ordering respects (react-builtin position, ts-utility-type position) it doesn't flag or fix. Cluster 1 is the weaker of the two: the underlying premise (some give-up paths silently drop diagnostics) is real but the true blast radius is 3 call sites, not '6 of ~10' -- most of the sites the doc cites as bypassing the pattern already diagnose correctly, just via duplicated inline code rather than a shared helper. Worse, the proposed mechanism as written does not compile/does not work: it ignores that known.rs's ~7 Opaque-construction sites are architecturally barred from touching resolver::ResolveState (CLAUDE.md's no-reverse-deps rule), ignores types/output.rs's Deserialize impl which also constructs Opaque with no ResolveState available, and proposes making ResolvedChain's bare construction 'private' when Rust's privacy model can't actually distinguish resolver's give-up call sites from its (far more numerous) legitimate accumulator-init call sites at the same module tier. Before a plan is built on cluster 1, its mechanism section needs to be rewritten: narrow the fix to the 3 confirmed silent sites, and either drop or substantially redesign the ResolvedChain/PropType::Opaque constructor-privacy proposal so it accounts for known.rs, the Deserialize impl, and the accumulator-vs-give-up split. Cluster 8 can proceed largely as written, with a note added about the two additional ordering divergences so the fix isn't assumed to fully unify the two functions.\\\"\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"groupName\": \"Extractor give-up signal channel (cluster 2) and depth-tracking proxy mismatch (cluster 7)\",\n        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"2. Extractor missing give-up signal channel\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"Verified every arm of classify_type_alias (crates/core/src/extractor/alias.rs:12-133): Omit (line 19, `tr.type_arguments.as_ref()?`), Pick (line 32, same), Partial (line 45-46, `tp.params.first()?` then `extract_type_name_from_type(...)?`), Required (line 54-55), Readonly (line 63-64) all silently `return None` via `?` on missing/malformed type arguments — confirmed for all five, not just a sample. classify_type_alias is `pub(super) fn classify_type_alias<'a>(&mut self, ...)` (alias.rs:12) — it does have `&mut self`, and SourceDataCollector holds `data: SourceData` (mod.rs:174) whose `.diagnostics` is already pushed into elsewhere (mod.rs:108, 134), so the doc's claim that the diagnostics channel is reachable with &mut self from this exact call context is accurate — no larger signature change is needed, contrary to what a skeptical read might expect. Also confirmed: grep across all of extractor/*.rs shows diagnostics.push only occurs at mod.rs:108 and mod.rs:134 (nesting guard + parse errors) — nowhere else — which supports the 'design gap, not helper drift' framing exactly as claimed. extract_type_name_from_type's `_ => None` fallback (mod.rs:650) is verified exactly as described — any TSType variant not covered by the TSTypeReference/Parenthesized/Union/Intersection/TypeLiteral arms silently produces no ComponentMapping. Minor imprecision only: the doc's 'component-detector .or_else() chains' framing, when applied to the second cited line range (visit.rs:262-293, visit_function), doesn't quite fit — that block is a single nested if-let detector (Pattern 4, function components), not an .or_else() chain across multiple named detectors like visit_variable_declaration's block at visit.rs:236-247 actually is. The 'end of each detector chain' phrasing slightly overgeneralizes what's actually two different failure shapes (multi-detector or_else vs. single nested if-let), but the underlying silent-failure claim holds for both. New DiagnosticCode::SkippedCandidate variant is safe to add — DiagnosticCode is #[non_exhaustive] with derived (not hand-written) Serialize (types/diagnostic.rs:54-56), so no serde match-arm updates are needed, unlike the PropType/CollectedType recursive-enum caveat elsewhere in the document.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"7. Extractor depth-tracking proxy mismatch\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"Verified ts_type_to_collected (extractor/mod.rs:311-471) has no depth parameter and recurses freely through TSConditionalType (line 416-421: check/extends_type/true_type/false_type all recurse via self.ts_type_to_collected with no counter), while every resolver resolve_* function threads an explicit `depth: u8` and bails via `if depth > MAX_DEPTH` (resolver/collected.rs:23-26, MAX_DEPTH=20 at resolver/mod.rs:46) — the asymmetry is real and exactly as described. Constructed the concrete counter-example the doc gestures at: `A extends B ? C extends D ? E extends F ? ... : X : X : X` requires literally zero brackets (no `(`, `{`, or `[`) at any nesting depth — max_bracket_nesting_depth (mod.rs:54-94) only counts those three bracket types, so a long chained-conditional file would report max_depth=0 regardless of how many levels deep the conditional nesting goes, sailing straight through the MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH=2000 guard undetected. This is actually a stronger case than the doc's 'proportionally fewer brackets per level' phrasing suggests (which undersells it — it's not just fewer, it's potentially none). Notably self-undermining: the guard's own doc comment (mod.rs:36-39) explicitly claims to guard against 'nested parenthesized types, object type literals, or conditional types' causing parser stack overflow, yet the implementation only scans brackets and has no way to see conditional-type nesting at all — a direct contradiction between the guard's stated purpose and its actual mechanism, strengthening the finding. Confirmed the only test for this guard (test_excessive_nesting_guard, mod.rs:1464-1478) uses pure paren nesting only, exactly as claimed — no conditional-type-shaped test exists. Mechanism check: call sites of ts_type_to_collected/ts_tuple_element_to_collected/ts_signature_to_object_field outside mod.rs are limited to alias.rs and component.rs:236 — all of them are recursion *entry points* (self.ts_type_to_collected(ty) called once, depth starts at 0), not participants in the internal recursion, so threading depth: u8 through 'ts_type_to_collected and sibling functions in extractor/mod.rs' as the doc scopes it is correctly bounded and does not require touching alias.rs/component.rs call-site signatures, contrary to what I initially suspected. The doc's illustrative 'depth 500' threshold is arbitrary relative to the resolver's MAX_DEPTH=20, but that's not an inconsistency — extraction-time structural recursion depth and resolution-time depth serve different purposes and don't need to share a constant.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"overallVerdict\": \"Both clusters in this section hold up well under direct code verification — no fabricated claims, no stale line numbers, and the two most checkable technical assertions (that all five classify_type_alias arms silently vanish via `?`, and that a chained-conditional-type example defeats the bracket-counting guard) are not just plausible but concretely demonstrable in the current code, in the depth-tracking case even more damning than the doc states (zero brackets required, not just fewer). The only defect worth a light touch-up before planning is cluster 2's slightly loose description of visit.rs:262-293 as part of an '.or_else() chain' when it's actually a distinct single-detector failure shape — cosmetic, doesn't change the fix or its scope. Safe to build an implementation plan on both clusters as written.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"groupName\": \"3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps\",\n        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n            \"issues\": \"Line-level claims check out exactly: discover.rs:6 `pub(super) fn discover_files(...) -> Vec<Utf8PathBuf>` with no diagnostic channel, `walker.flatten()` at discover.rs:17 drops ignore::Walk Err values, and the non-UTF8 fallback silently drops files at discover.rs:41 (`if let Ok(utf8) = Utf8PathBuf::from_path_buf(...)`). pipeline/mod.rs:220's guard `if !options.src_dirs.is_empty() && missing_src_dirs.len() == options.src_dirs.len()` and pipeline/mod.rs:386's `components.insert(key, entry);` are exactly as described, both discarding relevant information. Reachability confirmed, not upstream-blocked: crates/cli/src/config.rs:35-36 does `if let Some(dirs) = self.src_dirs { opts.src_dirs = dirs.into_iter().map(Into::into).collect(); }` with no non-empty check, so an explicit `srcDirs: []` in docgen.config.ts reaches extract_with_global with a genuinely empty Vec (PipelineOptions::default() only supplies `./src` as a fallback, which this override replaces). No existing test exercises this path — test_extract_empty_src (mod.rs:574) uses a real-but-empty tmp *directory*, not an empty src_dirs list, so 'fixing' the guard doesn't break it.\\n\\nTwo real problems with the mechanism as written:\\n\\n1. The claim that fixing components.insert's discarded Option<V> return 'fixes both the same-name-same-file collision AND the overlapping-src_dirs collision at once' does not hold for the common 2-way overlap case. Tracing the actual key logic (mod.rs:363-381): the disambiguation key is `format!(\\\"{} ({})\\\", base_name, entry.file_path)` for every occurrence after the first, regardless of count — so with exactly two duplicate ComponentMappings from the same file (the realistic overlapping-src_dirs case, e.g. [\\\"./src\\\", \\\"./src/components\\\"], produces exactly a 2x duplicate via `component_mappings.extend()` in types/global.rs:97 with no dedup anywhere, and discover.rs:54 sorts but never dedups src_files), the first duplicate gets the plain key ('Button') and the second gets the disambiguated key ('Button (path)') — two *different* keys, so `components.insert` never sees a collision and its discarded Option<V> would be None both times. The insert-return check only catches a true key collision, which requires 3+ duplicate mappings sharing the same name+file (e.g. 3-way overlapping dirs, or a literal duplicate declaration in one file colliding with a pre-existing 2nd-occurrence key) — a narrower and rarer case than 'overlapping src_dirs' as generally described. The mechanism section should call out that overlapping-src_dirs additionally needs its own targeted fix (e.g. `files.dedup()` after `files.sort()` in discover.rs, or deduping component_mappings/src_files by canonical path) rather than assuming the insert-return check alone resolves it.\\n\\n2. The 220 guard is not really a 'plain boolean logic error' fixable by a one-line operator swap, and the described fix ('fix the && guard') is underspecified: simply dropping `!options.src_dirs.is_empty() &&` makes the empty-src_dirs case fall into the Error branch, but that branch's message does `options.src_dirs.iter().map(...).join(\\\", \\\")` (empty string) and `options.src_dirs.first()` (None) — producing a diagnostic reading 'None of the configured source directories exist: ' with no file attached, which is confusing/wrong wording for 'no src dirs were configured at all' vs 'the configured dirs don't exist on disk'. The real fix needs a distinct branch/message for the empty-vec case, not just a boolean flip.\\n\\nMinor scope gap: mod.rs:542 and mod.rs:562 (`test_discover_files`, `test_exclude_stories`) call `discover_files(&[dir], &[])` directly and would need updating for the new `&mut Vec<Diagnostic>` parameter — not fatal, but the mechanism section's task list doesn't mention these call sites.\",\n            \"correction\": \"In the Mechanism paragraph, replace 'this one change fixes both the same-name-same-file collision AND the overlapping-src_dirs collision at once' with something like: 'the insert-return check catches genuine 3+-way same-key collisions; overlapping-src_dirs additionally needs its own fix (dedup src_files after sort in discover.rs, or dedup component_mappings by (name, file_path) before the keying loop) since a simple 2-directory overlap produces two differently-keyed entries that never collide at the BTreeMap level.' Also specify that the 220 fix needs a distinct diagnostic message/branch for 'src_dirs is empty' vs 'all configured src_dirs are missing', not just removing the `!is_empty()` conjunct. Add mod.rs:542/562 to the list of call sites needing updates when discover_files gains a diagnostics parameter.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"overallVerdict\": \"The root-cause claims for cluster 3 are accurate at the line level and genuinely reachable (verified via config.rs, not blocked upstream) — this part of the document is trustworthy. However the proposed mechanism has a real gap worth fixing before planning: the 'insert() discard fixes both collisions at once' claim is false for the realistic 2-directory overlapping-src_dirs case (traced through the actual keying logic, it produces two differently-keyed entries, not a same-key overwrite), so the plan needs a second, explicit fix for that case (e.g. dedup in discover.rs) rather than relying solely on the insert-return check. The empty-src_dirs guard fix is also underspecified regarding what diagnostic message the fixed branch should produce. Revise root-cause-analysis.md's mechanism section for cluster 3 before building an implementation plan on top of it.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"groupName\": \"root-cause-analysis.md sections 5 & 6 (Unbounded allocation / LSP scaffold immaturity)\",\n        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"5. Unbounded allocation from input-derived size\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"Verified against actual code. resolver/template.rs:94-104 has the exact Cartesian-product loop the doc describes (`result = next` accumulating per_part alternatives with zero size cap), and it does fall through to the Opaque+diagnostic path via the caller at template.rs:29-39 when try_expand_template_literal returns None (though a cap-triggered None looks identical to an ordinary unexpandable-template None to the diagnostic message text — minor, not incorrect). Checked for an implicit bound the analysis might have missed: resolver/mod.rs:46 defines MAX_DEPTH=20 and it is threaded through chain.rs/named.rs/collected.rs/primitives.rs (all confirmed at cited lines), and template.rs's own resolve_named_to_string_literals/try_expand_template_literal also thread depth+1 — but that only bounds *recursion depth* (how many nested type aliases get chased), not the *width* of any single union or the number of template-literal parts. A 6-part template literal where each part resolves to a 50-member LiteralUnion produces 50^6 (~15.6B) strings while depth stays at 1-2, nowhere near MAX_DEPTH=20. No union-member truncation exists anywhere in extractor or resolver (grepped, found nothing). So the claimed absence of an implicit bound is correct — the depth guard genuinely does not save this case. cli/commands/lsp.rs:36 confirmed: `vec![0u8; len]` where len comes straight from `header.trim().parse().ok()` at line 28, no upstream range/sanity check of any kind before the allocation. Only failure mode caught is non-numeric/negative-for-usize text (parse() returns None -> handled via content_length None branch), not large-but-valid values. Both root causes and the const-cap mechanism are sound and would compile (Opaque fallback path and diagnostic machinery already exist and are reused, not invented).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"6. LSP scaffold immaturity\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n            \"issues\": \"hoverProvider claim verified exactly: lsp.rs:56 advertises `\\\"hoverProvider\\\": true`, and a repo-wide grep for 'hover' turns up zero handler code anywhere in crates/ — only the doc comment (lsp.rs:3), the CLI help string (main.rs:60), and an unrelated `onHoverStart`/`onHoverEnd` HTML-attribute name in resolver/html.rs:8-9 (a synthetic-event name string, not LSP hover logic). match method at lsp.rs:47-79 has arms only for initialize/shutdown/exit, with `_ => {}` as the catch-all (line 78) — confirms a `textDocument/hover` request with an id gets no response, matching P1-6 exactly. Zero-test claim also confirmed: no #[test] or mod tests in lsp.rs, and `find crates/cli/tests -iname '*lsp*'` returns nothing. The desync/resync claim (P1-7) is real but the proposed fix's framing is imprecise: the doc says the mechanism is to 'make the content_length: None branch actually resync (consume to the next blank line) instead of silently continuing past an unconsumed body' — but tracing the actual control flow (lsp.rs:19-34), the inner header-reading loop already reads and breaks on the blank line (line 24-26) *before* the `let Some(len) = content_length else { continue }` check at line 32. So by the time that branch is reached, everything up to and including the blank line has already been consumed; 'consume to the next blank line' as stated would be a no-op, not a fix. The actual unrecoverable problem is that with no Content-Length there is no way to know how many bytes the (headerless) message body occupies, so it truly cannot be skip-consumed at all — the next read_line call will misinterpret body bytes as headers of a new message, which is the real desync mechanism. A correct fix needs a different strategy than 'consume to the next blank line' (e.g., treating any non-blank line that doesn't match a known header format, once inside a message with no declared length, as unrecoverable and closing/resetting the connection with a diagnostic, since LSP framing gives no other resync signal). This is a mechanism-soundness gap worth flagging before implementation, not a rejection of the overall diagnosis, which is otherwise accurate.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"overallVerdict\": \"Both clusters in this section hold up under direct code verification — no fabricated claims, no overstated severity, and the code at every cited line matches the doc's description almost exactly (template.rs:94-104 Cartesian loop, lsp.rs:36 vec![0u8; len], lsp.rs:56 hoverProvider:true with a confirmed-empty grep for any hover handler, zero LSP tests). Cluster 5's mechanism (const caps falling through to the existing Opaque+diagnostic path) is sound and low-risk. Cluster 6's hoverProvider/no-handler and zero-test findings are solid and its 'don't advertise hoverProvider until implemented' fix is trivially correct, but the proposed resync mechanism for the content_length:None branch is imprecise/likely ineffective as literally described (the blank line is already consumed by the time that branch executes; the real problem — an unknown, unconsumable body length — needs a different fix than 'consume to the next blank line'). This section is safe to build a plan on provided the resync fix gets rethought during implementation rather than implemented as literally written.</overallVerdict>\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"groupName\": \"TOON truncation, schema drift, exit-code contract\",\n        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"9. TOON truncation indicator dropped in one branch\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"Verified against crates/core/src/toon.rs:95-144. LiteralUnion (line 109-116) truncates to 6 members and correctly appends `format!(\\\"...(+{})\\\", members.len() - 6)` when truncated. Union (125-128) and Intersection (129-132), in the same format_type_compact function, truncate to 4 members via `.take(4)` with no indicator appended at all when members.len() > 4 — exactly as claimed. No existing test exercises Union/Intersection with >4 members (only test_format_type_compact_complex_types at line ~237 and the LiteralUnion truncation test at ~245), so adding the indicator would not break any test. One minor unaddressed nuance: Union/Intersection currently only format the first 4 members (cheap), while a straightforward `truncate_with_indicator(&[String], limit, sep)` helper as proposed would require formatting all members first then truncating (slightly more eager), which is a harmless implementation detail the doc doesn't call out but doesn't invalidate the mechanism.\",\n            \"correction\": \"\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"10. Schema hand-maintained separately from the real structs\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n            \"issues\": \"Field-drift claims verified exactly: schema.rs (crates/cli/src/commands/schema.rs:1-83) is a hand-written serde_json::json!() literal with only one test (test_cmd_schema_valid_json, line 79-82) checking .is_ok(), never diffing against real serialized output. ComponentEntry (core/types/output.rs:47-68) really has `.methods` (line 67) and `.tags` (line 65) absent from schema.rs. ExtractionStats (output.rs:609-619) has exactly 9 fields; schema.rs's stats block (schema.rs:58-67) documents only 4 (componentsExtracted, filesParsed, dtsCacheHits, durationMs) — the other 5 (componentsSkipped, dtsFilesParsed, tier1Count, tier3Count, opaqueCount) are missing, matching the '5 of 9' claim exactly. Diagnostic (core/types/diagnostic.rs:8-16) really has line/column/help absent from schema.rs's diagnostics block (schema.rs:45-57). schemars is confirmed absent from the workspace (no hits in Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock for any crate), so the 'new dependency' framing is accurate.\\n\\nHowever the mechanism's claim that OpaqueReason is one of 'the three recursive enums ADR-0002 already exempted from derive machinery for Serialize' is FALSE against the actual current code. OpaqueReason at core/types/output.rs:565-567 has `#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]` — an ordinary derive, not a hand-written impl — and it is not recursive (no field of type OpaqueReason nests into itself). Only PropType (output.rs:280, hand-written Serialize) and CollectedType (types/collected.rs:421, hand-written Serialize, genuinely recursive via Vec<CollectedType>/Box<CollectedType>) actually match that description. Interestingly ADR-0002 itself (docs/adr/0002-manual-serialize-for-prop-type.md line 11) asserts OpaqueReason also has a hand-written impl and doesn't derive — that claim in the ADR is stale/wrong relative to current code, and root-cause-analysis.md propagated the ADR's claim without checking the actual source, which is exactly the kind of unverified inference this audit is meant to catch.\",\n            \"correction\": \"The mechanism should say: derive schemars::JsonSchema normally on OpaqueReason (it already derives Serialize/Deserialize with no recursion problem) alongside the other plain structs; reserve the hand-written JsonSchema treatment for PropType and CollectedType only, the two enums that are actually recursive and actually hand-write Serialize today. Also worth flagging separately (not part of this cluster's fix) that ADR-0002's own text is inaccurate about OpaqueReason and should be corrected or annotated.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"11. CLI exit-code contract has no shared type\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n            \"issues\": \"Verified extract.rs:68-73 (`output.diagnostics.iter().any(|d| matches!(d.severity, ...Error))` -> Ok(if has_errors {2} else {0})) and check.rs:21-46 (separate `.filter().collect()` for errors and warnings, then `if !errors.is_empty() { return Ok(2) }`, `if args.strict && !warnings.is_empty() { return Ok(1) }`, else Ok(0)) — syntactically different, semantically overlapping but not identical: check.rs additionally supports a --strict warnings-escalate-to-1 path that extract.rs has no equivalent flag for (ExtractArgs at main.rs:68-108 has no `strict` field). Confirmed main.rs:181-198 exactly: Command::Watch discards cmd_watch's return and hardcodes exit code 0 (line 182-185), Command::Inspect does the same (187-190). Confirmed cmd_watch (crates/cli/src/commands/watch.rs:6) returns Result<()>, and cmd_inspect (crates/cli/src/commands/inspect.rs:5) returns Result<()> and never reads output.diagnostics anywhere in the file (grep for 'diagnostic' in inspect.rs returns zero hits) — it only errors on a missing-component lookup, exactly as claimed.\\n\\nGap in the mechanism: the proposed `max_severity(&self) -> Option<DiagnosticSeverity>` requires an ordering over DiagnosticSeverity (Error worse than Warning worse than Info) to compute a 'max', but DiagnosticSeverity (core/types/diagnostic.rs:44-51) currently derives only `Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize` — no Ord/PartialOrd, and no manual Ord impl exists anywhere in the file. The doc's one-line method signature doesn't mention that either DiagnosticSeverity needs an Ord/rank added (with attention to derived-Ord's declaration-order semantics being the *inverse* of severity-worst-first) or that max_severity needs a hand-written comparison, e.g. matching to an integer rank. This is a small but real gap someone implementing the mechanism as literally stated would trip on.\",\n            \"correction\": \"Add either `#[derive(PartialOrd, Ord, Eq)]` to DiagnosticSeverity with variants reordered so Error < Warning < Info reads correctly for whichever direction 'max' is meant to go (or add a private rank() -> u8 helper and implement max_severity via that instead of relying on derived Ord), and state that explicitly as part of this cluster's mechanism rather than leaving it implicit in the method signature.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"overallVerdict\": \"All three clusters' factual root-cause claims check out precisely against current source (toon.rs line-level truncation behavior, schema.rs field-by-field drift counts, and the extract/check/watch/inspect exit-code asymmetry are all exactly as described, down to line numbers and specific missing field names). Cluster 9 is safe to implement as-is. Clusters 10 and 11 are safe to build a plan on but need small corrections before implementation: cluster 10's mechanism incorrectly extends ADR-0002's hand-written-Serialize/recursion-limit rationale to OpaqueReason, which is not actually recursive and already uses a normal derive in current code (the analysis trusted ADR-0002's prose over the actual source, and ADR-0002 itself is stale on this point) — fix is cheaper than stated, not harder. Cluster 11's mechanism omits that DiagnosticSeverity has no ordering today, which max_severity() depends on, and elides that check.rs's --strict warning-escalation path has no equivalent flag on extract's args today. Neither correction changes the overall shape of the fix or the P0/P3 severity ratings, which are accurately characterized.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"groupName\": \"Standalone findings (7 entries)\",\n        \"perClusterVerdicts\": [\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"Generic alias .zip() silently truncates trailing type params (substitute.rs:33-43)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"build_substitution (substitute.rs:33-43) does exactly what's claimed: params.iter().zip(args.iter()...) silently drops any params beyond args.len(), with no diagnostic. Verified against the real signature and both call sites (apply_generic_args:57-73, generic_alias_with_structured_args:83-107). No existing test references build_substitution or exercises partial application, so there's no risk of a 'fix' breaking current behavior-dependent tests -- but note the doc doesn't actually propose a mechanism for this one (it's correctly left as an open standalone ticket in the table, not a false claim of a ready fix), so there's nothing to validate for soundness beyond the root-cause claim itself.\",\n            \"correction\": \"\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"watch --out write failure discarded via `let _ =` (watch.rs:107-112)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"needs-adjustment\",\n            \"issues\": \"watch.rs:110 is exactly `let _ = std::fs::write(p, json);` inside the Watchexec::new(move |action| {...}) closure -- confirmed verbatim. The non-atomic fs::write claim is also correct: unlike cache.rs::try_save (which does tmp-write + rename), this is a direct truncating write. However the proposed mechanism ('the real fix is routing through the CLI's own miette/output.rs error path') glosses over a real obstacle: this code runs inside a Watchexec callback closure with signature `move |action| { ...; action }` that returns `Action`, not `Result<_, _>` -- there's no `?` or return-propagation path available here. The actual fix has to print/log the failure inline (e.g. via output.rs's diagnostic printer or eprintln!) rather than 'propagate through miette', since propagation out of this closure isn't structurally possible without restructuring the watch loop.\",\n            \"correction\": \"State explicitly that the fix must surface the error inline within the closure (print via output.rs, not miette propagation), since the Watchexec callback closure returns Action, not Result, and can't propagate an error upward the way the rest of the CLI does.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"ParsedProp allows contradictory required:true + default_value:Some(_) (types/output.rs:79-99)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"unsound\",\n            \"issues\": \"The contradictory-state claim is accurate: ParsedProp (output.rs:81-99) has fully pub fields, no constructor, Default not derived, and nothing in the type prevents required:true + default_value:Some(_). Real bare-literal construction sites found by direct grep (not the ~10-sites-across-6-files figure sometimes cited): known.rs:297 (inside simple_prop, already a de-facto private constructor), resolver/mod.rs:297 (notable_inherited synthesis), resolver/chain.rs:244, resolver/alias.rs:181 -- 4 production sites across 4 files -- plus 3 test-only literals (resolver/mod.rs:827, 840; toon.rs:167) across 2 more files. Total 7 sites / 5 files, not ~10/6. More importantly, the proposed mechanism ('make fields pub(crate)') is actually broken: (1) crates/cli is a *separate crate* from crates/core and reads ParsedProp's fields directly and extensively -- extract.rs:107-112,140-144 (prop.name, prop.prop_type, prop.required, prop.default_value, prop.description, prop.parent) and inspect.rs:52-58 (prop.prop_type, prop.required, prop.default_value, prop.parent, prop.name). Making the fields pub(crate) would be a hard compile error in crates/cli. (2) Even setting that aside, pub(crate) grants visibility to the *entire defining crate* -- and every one of the offending construction sites (chain.rs, alias.rs, mod.rs, known.rs) is inside that same crate (oxc-react-docgen-core). pub(crate) would not stop any of the misuse it's meant to prevent; it only blocks *other crates* from bare-constructing, which none of the actual violators are.\",\n            \"correction\": \"The fix needs field privacy scoped to the defining module (types::output), not pub(crate) -- e.g. keep fields effectively private via a private marker/sealed field so only ParsedProp::new(...) can construct one even from other core modules, while adding public accessor methods (or keeping fields pub for read but removing all direct positional/struct-literal construction ability another way) so crates/cli's existing field reads keep compiling. Simply flipping visibility to pub(crate) solves neither the cross-crate compile break nor the same-crate misuse it's meant to fix.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"DiagnosticCode::Unknown never constructed (types/diagnostic.rs:61)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"Grepped `DiagnosticCode::Unknown` across all of crates/ (rg with no --include filter issue this time) -- zero matches anywhere, confirming it is genuinely never constructed in the repo. The enum is confirmed #[non_exhaustive] at diagnostic.rs:54, directly above the enum definition that includes the Unknown variant at line 61 (actually 'Unknown' isn't even a listed variant in the current enum body I read -- rechecking: the variants present are UnresolvableImport, OpaqueType, MaxDepthExceeded, Unknown, JsDocDefaultMismatch, ComputedDefault, IndexedAccessOpaque, TemplateLiteralOpaque, DiscriminatedUnion, IoError, ExcessiveNesting, ParseError -- Unknown is indeed present at line 61). The doc's 'no fix needed, intentional headroom on a non_exhaustive enum' framing is a reasonable and low-risk call; nothing contradicts it.\",\n            \"correction\": \"\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"Cache staleness on same-tick, same-size edits (cache.rs:181-191, P0-2)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"key_for (cache.rs:181-191) keys on (path, size, mtime as full nanosecond SystemTime duration) -- confirmed this already extracts the maximum timestamp precision the OS API exposes; there's no cheaper within-the-current-mechanism precision gain left to claim. The doc's framing ('genuine filesystem-timestamp-resolution limitation... already does the best available thing') holds up: the residual staleness case truly requires an independent signal (content hash) the current key doesn't carry, not a logic bug. Minor imprecision: the doc says 'exactly one call site' but key_for is actually invoked from two places (get at line 121, insert at line 129) -- immaterial to the conclusion since both call the same helper consistently, but the phrasing is loose. The task prompt asked specifically whether a cheaper mitigation than full content-hashing was dismissed too quickly: since cache entries are scoped to .d.ts files only (typically small), a fast non-cryptographic hash (e.g. a single pass xxhash/CRC over the bytes already being read for size) would be materially cheaper than what 'content hash' implies and still close most of the gap -- the doc's blanket 'scoped performance/complexity tradeoff' framing slightly overstates the cost of the mitigation it defers, though it doesn't misstate the root cause or claim the wrong fix, just under-specifies how cheap a decent fix could be.\",\n            \"correction\": \"\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity round-trips to 0.0 (types/output.rs:314,423)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"partially-wrong\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"The serialization mechanics are confirmed exactly as described: PropType::NumberLiteral(n) at output.rs:314 goes through serde_json::json!({...,\\\"value\\\": n}) where serde_json's From<f64> maps non-finite floats to Value::Null (Number::from_f64 returns None for NaN/Infinity), and the 'numberLiteral' deserialize arm at output.rs:423 does `v[\\\"value\\\"].as_f64().unwrap_or(0.0)`, silently turning that null back into 0.0. But the doc's severity claim -- 'Likely unreachable via normal TS literal syntax, but present' -- is wrong, not just optimistic. extractor/mod.rs:328 does `TSLiteral::NumericLiteral(n) => CollectedType::NumberLiteral(n.value)` with n.value being the f64 OXC parses directly from source text, no bounds check. A completely ordinary numeric-literal type like `type X = 1e400;` is valid TS/JS numeric-literal syntax and parses to `f64::INFINITY` under IEEE-754 overflow -- this is reachable through normal, not adversarial, source text, contradicting the 'no realistic reachable input' framing.\",\n            \"correction\": \"Change 'no realistic reachable input' to acknowledge that an overflowing exponent literal (e.g. `1e400`) reaches this path through ordinary TS numeric-literal-type syntax, extracted verbatim at extractor/mod.rs:328 with no range check -- raise this from cosmetic-low-priority to at least worth a guard/diagnostic at the extraction boundary, since 'realistic' understates it.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"clusterOrFindingName\": \"Zero-member LiteralUnion serializes as an empty RDT enum (cli/commands/extract.rs rdt_type_json)\",\n            \"rootCauseVerdict\": \"confirmed\",\n            \"mechanismVerdict\": \"sound\",\n            \"issues\": \"Confirmed: PropType::is_literal_union() (output.rs:212-220) returns true vacuously for an empty Union(vec![]) (iter().all() on empty is true) and unconditionally true for any PropType::LiteralUnion{..} regardless of member count. rdt_type_json (extract.rs:81-95) then emits {\\\"name\\\":\\\"enum\\\",\\\"value\\\":[]} for either shape with zero members. PropType::LiteralUnion is constructed from several live paths that could plausibly produce zero members from a malformed/edge-case source input (known.rs:208 cva variant extraction, resolver/primitives.rs:124, resolver/alias.rs:426, resolver/mod.rs:673, resolver/template.rs:26) -- not a purely theoretical shape. The 'low-priority, cosmetic' severity call is fair; nothing found suggests this is more serious than claimed.\",\n            \"correction\": \"\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"overallVerdict\": \"Five of the seven standalone findings hold up well: substitute.rs's silent truncation, DiagnosticCode::Unknown's dead-code status, cache.rs's mtime-resolution limitation, and the zero-member LiteralUnion cosmetic gap are all accurately root-caused with correctly-scoped (or correctly deferred) fixes. Two need real correction before a plan is built on top of them. First, the ParsedProp fix ('make fields pub(crate)') is actively broken -- it would fail to compile against crates/cli's existing direct field reads (extract.rs, inspect.rs), and even where it would compile, pub(crate) grants access to the exact same-crate call sites (chain.rs, alias.rs, mod.rs, known.rs) that are the whole problem, so it wouldn't stop the misuse it's meant to stop; this needs a module-private-plus-constructor (sealed-field) approach instead, not a visibility-level tweak. Second, the NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity finding's severity is understated, not just optimistic: an ordinary numeric-literal type like `type X = 1e400;` reaches the exact null-round-trips-to-0.0 path through normal TS syntax (verified at extractor/mod.rs:328), not just a hypothetical. The watch.rs --out fix also needs a scoping correction (the write happens inside a Watchexec closure that returns Action, not Result, so 'propagate through miette' isn't literally available -- it needs an inline print instead). None of these invalidate the underlying edge-case catalog, but the root-cause-analysis.md prose should be corrected on these three points before Phase 1 tasks are scoped from it.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"synthesis\": \"# Verdict: root-cause-analysis.md Adversarial Verification Synthesis\\n\\n## Overall Verdict\\n\\nRoot-cause-analysis.md is **largely trustworthy on diagnosis** — every skeptic independently confirmed the empirical root-cause claims down to line numbers, with zero fabricated citations across all 7 groups. It is **not safe to build an implementation plan on as-is**, however: three mechanisms are outright broken (won't compile or won't stop the misuse they target), and several more are underspecified in ways that would trip up an implementer. The fix is targeted prose edits to the Mechanism sections of clusters 1, 3, 6, 10, 11, plus the ParsedProp and watch.rs standalone findings — the diagnoses themselves need no rework.\\n\\n## Cluster Table\\n\\n| Cluster/Finding | Root Cause | Mechanism | Required Correction |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 4. No panic-containment boundary | confirmed | needs-adjustment | `&mut` captures in plugin hooks are `!UnwindSafe`; must specify `AssertUnwindSafe` + rationale, or discard mutated value on catch |\\n| ADR-0005 | confirmed | needs-adjustment | Note napi-rs's opt-in `#[napi(catch_unwind)]`; soften \\\"one sanctioned helper covers all three call sites\\\" |\\n| 8. Resolver precedence (P0-1) | confirmed | needs-adjustment | Note two further undiagnosed divergences (react-builtin order, ts-utility-type order) not fixed by the proposed shared function |\\n| 1. Resolver give-up constructor discipline | partially-wrong | unsound | Narrow to 3 confirmed sites (func.rs:61, chain.rs:40, alias.rs:116); drop/redesign `ResolvedChain` privacy proposal; known.rs and Deserialize impl can't use `give_up()` |\\n| 2. Extractor missing give-up signal | confirmed | sound | Minor: visit.rs:262-293 isn't an `.or_else()` chain, it's a single detector — cosmetic only |\\n| 7. Extractor depth-tracking proxy mismatch | confirmed | sound | none |\\n| 3. Pipeline discovery/merge diagnostic gaps | confirmed | needs-adjustment | `insert()`-discard fix doesn't catch 2-way src_dir overlap (different keys); needs separate dedup fix; empty-src_dirs guard needs distinct message, not a boolean flip |\\n| 5. Unbounded allocation (template/LSP) | confirmed | sound | none |\\n| 6. LSP scaffold immaturity | confirmed | needs-adjustment | Resync fix as stated (\\\"consume to next blank line\\\") is a no-op — blank line already consumed; needs real strategy for unknown body length |\\n| 9. TOON truncation indicator dropped | confirmed | sound | none |\\n| 10. Schema hand-maintained separately | confirmed | needs-adjustment | `OpaqueReason` is NOT recursive/hand-written Serialize (ADR-0002 itself is stale); derive `JsonSchema` normally on it, reserve manual treatment for `PropType`/`CollectedType` only |\\n| 11. CLI exit-code contract | confirmed | needs-adjustment | `DiagnosticSeverity` has no `Ord`; `max_severity()` needs explicit ranking added; note check.rs's `--strict` has no extract.rs equivalent |\\n| Generic alias `.zip()` truncation | confirmed | sound (no mechanism proposed) | none |\\n| watch --out write failure discarded | confirmed | needs-adjustment | Fix must print inline in the Watchexec closure (`Action`-returning, not `Result`) — \\\"propagate through miette\\\" isn't reachable |\\n| ParsedProp contradictory required+default | confirmed | **unsound** | `pub(crate)` fails to compile (crates/cli reads fields directly) and doesn't stop same-crate misuse; needs sealed-field + constructor + accessors instead |\\n| DiagnosticCode::Unknown never constructed | confirmed | sound | none |\\n| Cache staleness same-tick/same-size | confirmed | sound | none (minor: \\\"one call site\\\" is actually two, immaterial) |\\n| NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity → 0.0 | **partially-wrong** (severity) | sound | Reachable via ordinary `type X = 1e400;`, not just adversarial input — raise priority |\\n| Zero-member LiteralUnion → empty enum | confirmed | sound | none |\\n\\n## Must-Fix-Before-Planning\\n\\n1. **Cluster 1 (Resolver give-up constructors) — mechanism is unsound.** Rewrite: scope the fix to exactly 3 sites (func.rs:61, chain.rs:40, alias.rs:116) using a helper callable where `ResolveState` is already in scope. Drop the \\\"make `ResolvedChain`'s bare construction private, `give_up()` the only public entry point\\\" proposal as written — Rust's privacy model can't distinguish give-up call sites from the ~9 legitimate accumulator-init sites at the same module tier, and known.rs's ~7 Opaque-construction sites are architecturally barred from touching `ResolveState` per the no-reverse-deps rule, plus `types/output.rs`'s Deserialize impl constructs `Opaque` with no `ResolveState` in scope at all. Leave known.rs's construct-then-caller-diagnoses split as-is (it's correct and tested).\\n\\n2. **ParsedProp fix — mechanism is unsound.** `pub(crate)` will not compile: `crates/cli` (extract.rs, inspect.rs) reads `ParsedProp`'s fields directly across a crate boundary. Even ignoring that, `pub(crate)` grants access to the exact same-crate sites (chain.rs, alias.rs, mod.rs, known.rs) causing the problem, so it wouldn't stop the misuse. Replace with a module-private-plus-constructor (sealed field) approach: force construction through `ParsedProp::new(...)`, keep or add public read accessors so `crates/cli` keeps compiling.\\n\\n3. **Cluster 4 mechanism — plugin hook wrapping won't compile.** `contain_panic<T: FnOnce() -> T + UnwindSafe>` cannot wrap `plugin.on_file_extracted(path, &mut data)` / `plugin.on_component_resolved(&mut entry)` as literally written — `&mut T` is unconditionally `!UnwindSafe`. Add an explicit decision: use `AssertUnwindSafe` with a stated rationale for tolerating partially-mutated `SourceData`/`ComponentEntry` after a caught panic, or redesign so a caught panic discards the mutated value instead of letting the caller (mod.rs:292-297, :386) trust it.\\n\\n4. **Cluster 6 (LSP resync) — mechanism is ineffective as stated.** \\\"Make the `content_length: None` branch resync by consuming to the next blank line\\\" is a no-op — the header loop (lsp.rs:19-34) already consumes through the blank line before that branch runs. The real problem is an unknown, unconsumable body length with no LSP-level resync signal. Replace with: treat a no-Content-Length message as unrecoverable and close/reset the connection with a diagnostic, rather than attempting to skip-consume it.\\n\\n## Worth Noting But Not Blocking\\n\\n- **Cluster 8 (resolver precedence):** fix is sound and zero-regression-risk (no fixture exercises known-pattern-named interfaces), but note in the text that `chain.rs`/`named.rs` will still diverge on react-builtin-check and ts-utility-type-check ordering after the P0-1 fix — don't imply full unification.\\n- **Cluster 3 (pipeline gaps):** the `insert()`-discard fix only catches 3+-way same-key collisions, not the realistic 2-directory overlap case (which produces two differently-keyed entries). Add a separate dedup fix (e.g., `files.dedup()` after sort, or dedup `component_mappings` by `(name, file_path)`). The empty-`src_dirs` guard fix needs a distinct diagnostic message for \\\"no dirs configured\\\" vs. \\\"configured dirs missing,\\\" not a plain `&&` flip. Also note mod.rs:542/562 tests need updating for the new diagnostics parameter on `discover_files`.\\n- **Cluster 10 (schema drift):** cheaper than stated — `OpaqueReason` already derives ordinary `Serialize`/`Deserialize` and isn't recursive, so it can derive `JsonSchema` normally. ADR-0002's own text is stale on this point and should be corrected too.\\n- **Cluster 11 (exit codes):** `DiagnosticSeverity` needs an explicit ranking (no derived `Ord` today; watch declaration-order pitfalls) before `max_severity()` can be implemented as described. Also flag that `check.rs`'s `--strict` warning-escalation has no equivalent flag on `extract`'s args yet.\\n- **watch.rs --out write failure:** correct diagnosis, but state explicitly the fix must print inline within the Watchexec closure (returns `Action`, not `Result` — can't propagate via `?`/miette).\\n- **NumberLiteral NaN/Infinity:** severity is understated, not wrong in mechanism — `type X = 1e400;` is ordinary TS syntax that reaches the null-round-trips-to-0.0 path via extractor/mod.rs:328 with no bounds check. Bump from cosmetic to worth a guard/diagnostic at extraction time.\\n- **ADR-0005:** mention napi-rs's opt-in `#[napi(catch_unwind)]` as a complementary FFI-boundary mechanism; soften the \\\"one sanctioned helper\\\" framing given the plugin-hook `UnwindSafe` issue above.\"\n  },\n  \"workflowProgress\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"workflow_phase\",","numLines":179,"startLine":1,"totalLines":357,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ea40a096-bda1-482d-9126-d6d621464740","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
