{"parentUuid":"fadbfd77-4ade-4cb3-9de3-87132daa8e12","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"8cfd87e5-e489-465b-a51f-4926a8373775","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0111ZrGxN11ayXYGrzMD3Taq","type":"tool_result","content":"99\t\n100\t**ADR:** No.\n101\t\n102\t### 9. TOON truncation indicator dropped in one branch\n103\t\n104\t**Root cause:** `LiteralUnion`'s truncate-with-`\"...(+N)\"` pattern was never factored into a helper. When `Union`/`Intersection` needed the same truncate-with-indicator behavior — in the *same function*, `format_type_compact` — the indicator half was dropped. This isn't code aging apart over time; it's proof the codebase has no mechanism that makes \"format a truncated list\" a single call site, even within one function.\n105\t\n106\t**Mechanism:** Add `fn truncate_with_indicator(parts: &[String], limit: usize, sep: &str) -> String` in `toon.rs`. Route `LiteralUnion`, `Union`, and `Intersection` through it.\n107\t\n108\t**Resolves:** P0-4. Cheapest fix in this document — all three call sites are already colocated.\n109\t\n110\t**ADR:** No.\n111\t\n112\t### 10. Schema hand-maintained separately from the real structs\n113\t\n114\t**Root cause:** `cli/commands/schema.rs` hand-writes a JSON Schema as a `serde_json::json!()` literal instead of deriving it from `ComponentEntry`/`ExtractionStats`/`Diagnostic`. Every new field on those structs requires someone to remember to edit a second, structurally unrelated file — and the only existing test checks that the hand-written JSON is syntactically valid, never that it matches real serialized output, so drift is invisible to CI. Concrete drift already present: `.methods`/`.tags`, 5 of 9 `ExtractionStats` fields, and `.line`/`.column`/`.help` are all undocumented in the schema today.\n115\t\n116\t**Mechanism:** Derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on the plain structs (`ComponentEntry`, `ParsedProp`, `ExtractionStats`, `Diagnostic`, `InheritedLayer`); call `schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput)` from `cmd_schema()`. Hand-write `JsonSchema` impls for `PropType`/`CollectedType`/`OpaqueReason` — the three recursive enums ADR-0002 already exempted from derive machinery for `Serialize` — following that same precedent so the recursion-limit problem isn't reintroduced. If `schemars` as a new dependency is undesirable, the floor-level fix is a CI test that serializes a real fixture and validates it against `schema.rs`'s hand-written output.\n117\t\n118\t**Resolves:** P0-5.\n119\t\n120\t**ADR: Yes — see draft below.**\n121\t\n122\t### 11. CLI exit-code contract has no shared type\n123\t\n124\t**Root cause:** The 0/1/2 exit-code mapping is a doc-comment convention re-typed per command, not a shared function — `extract.rs:68-71` and `check.rs:21-30` already implement the same check with syntactically different code. `ExtractionOutput` has no `exit_code()` method, plausibly because `crates/core/CLAUDE.md`'s \"no terminal/display code in `crates/core`\" rule got over-applied to mean \"no exit-code logic belongs in core either.\" `cmd_watch` returns `()`, not `Result<i32>` (main.rs hardcodes exit 0 for that arm); `cmd_inspect` never touches `output.diagnostics` at all. Both were plausibly written by copying the general command-handler shape without copying the exit-code block, because nothing — return type, trait, lint — forces a new command handler to remember it.\n125\t\n126\t**Mechanism:** Add `impl ExtractionOutput { pub fn max_severity(&self) -> Option<DiagnosticSeverity>; pub fn exit_code(&self, strict: bool) -> i32 }` in `core/types/output.rs` — pure data-in/data-out, same category as the existing `html_element()` helper, so it doesn't violate the core/no-terminal-code rule. Route `extract.rs` and `check.rs` through it (collapsing their inline checks). Change `cmd_watch` to return `Result<i32>` and call `exit_code(false)` after each pass, or explicitly gate propagation behind a `--ci` flag if always-0 is intentional for interactive use — document the choice either way instead of leaving it silent. Call `exit_code(false)` in `cmd_inspect` after a successful lookup so an Error diagnostic elsewhere in the tree surfaces even when the inspected component itself is fine.\n127\t\n128\t**Resolves:** P3-1.\n129\t\n130\t**ADR:** No — a doc comment on `exit_code()` documenting the mapping is sufficient; this formalizes an already-informal contract, it doesn't create a new one.\n131\t\n132\t---\n133\t\n134\t## Standalone findings — no shared mechanism, treat as independent tasks\n135\t\n136\t| Finding | Location | Why it doesn't cluster |\n137\t| --- | --- | --- |\n138\t| Generic alias `.zip()` silently truncates unsubstituted trailing type params | `resolver/substitute.rs:33-43` | Silent *truncation* of a substitution map, not a give-up-and-return-nothing path — closer to a P0 correctness bug than the give-up cluster |\n139\t| `watch --out` write failure discarded via `let _ =` | `cli/commands/watch.rs:107-112` | CLI-layer, not extraction-pipeline degradation; non-negotiable #6 is scoped to `crates/core` — the real fix is routing through the CLI's own miette/`output.rs` error path, a different mechanism than the core-side clusters. Same file's non-atomic `fs::write` (no temp+rename) should be fixed alongside it. |\n140\t| Cache staleness on same-tick, same-size edits | `cache.rs:181-191` (P0-2) | Genuine filesystem-timestamp-resolution limitation, not a logic bug or missing abstraction — there's exactly one call site and it already does the best available thing. A content hash would close the gap but is a scoped performance/complexity tradeoff, not a missing shared check. |\n141\t| `ParsedProp` allows contradictory `required: true` + `default_value: Some(_)` | `types/output.rs:79-99` (P0-3) | Missing type invariant, not duplicated logic — there was never a shared constructor to unify. Fix: add `ParsedProp::new(...)`, make fields `pub(crate)`, and add a line to the `rust-types` skill checklist: \"if a struct has two fields whose combination can be contradictory, provide a validating constructor.\" |\n142\t| `DiagnosticCode::Unknown` never constructed | `types/diagnostic.rs:61` (P3-2) | Genuinely dead code but intentional headroom on a `#[non_exhaustive]` enum for external consumers — no fix needed. |\n143\t| `NumberLiteral` NaN/Infinity round-trips to `0.0` | `types/output.rs:314,423` | Serialization-boundary edge case with no realistic reachable input (TS literal syntax can't spell NaN directly) — low-priority standalone ticket. |\n144\t| Zero-member `LiteralUnion` serializes as an empty RDT enum | `cli/commands/extract.rs` (`rdt_type_json`) | Same tier as above — low-priority, cosmetic. |\n145\t\n146\t---\n147\t\n148\t## Draft ADRs\n149\t\n150\tTwo clusters are genuine new architectural decisions, not enforcement of an existing pattern. Drafted here; write the real `docs/adr/000N-*.md` file as part of implementing that cluster, per the ADR guide's \"write it when the decision is made\" convention — these are proposals, not yet accepted.\n151\t\n152\t### Proposed: 0005. Contain panics at a single, per-item boundary\n153\t\n154\t**Context:** No existing ADR covers panic/unwind policy. Panic safety today is an accident of `tokio::spawn_blocking`'s `JoinError`, inconsistent across NAPI entry points, plugin hooks, and rayon batches — `create_session`/`close_session` don't even get that accidental protection, and `watch.rs`'s `std::sync::Mutex` can poison permanently as a direct consequence. Picking the wrong granularity now (per-batch vs. per-file vs. per-plugin) is expensive to fix retroactively once call sites depend on it.\n155\t\n156\t**Decision:** Panics reachable from a rayon `.map()`, a `DocgenPlugin` hook, or a NAPI entry point are contained at per-file/per-plugin/per-call granularity through one sanctioned helper, `panic_guard::contain_panic`, converting the payload into a `Diagnostic` (or `napi::Error`) instead of aborting a batch, killing the whole pipeline, or poisoning a session lock.\n157\t\n158\t**Consequences:**\n159\t- One bad file, plugin, or session call degrades to a diagnostic instead of taking down everything sharing its batch/pipeline/session.\n160\t- Every future concurrent, plugin, or FFI entry point has one obvious place to route through, instead of re-deriving the answer.\n161\t- `watch.rs`'s poisoned-mutex trap becomes structurally much less likely, since nothing panics while the lock is held anymore.\n162\t\n163\t### Proposed: 0006. Derive JSON Schema instead of hand-writing it\n164\t\n165\t**Context:** `schema.rs`'s hand-written schema has already drifted from `ComponentEntry`/`ExtractionStats`/`Diagnostic`'s real fields, undetected because the only test checks JSON syntax validity, not field-set parity. ADR-0002 already established the precedent of hand-writing `Serialize` for the three recursive enums (`PropType`, `CollectedType`, `OpaqueReason`) to avoid a recursion-limit blowup.\n166\t\n167\t**Decision:** Derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on the plain output structs; hand-write matching `JsonSchema` impls for the three recursive enums, mirroring their existing hand-written `Serialize` impls. `cmd_schema()` calls `schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput)` instead of building a `json!()` literal by hand.\n168\t"}]},"uuid":"50ce421e-11aa-4c12-925d-224ed6b16bae","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:49:32.108Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen/docs/root-cause-analysis.md","content":"\n**ADR:** No.\n\n### 9. TOON truncation indicator dropped in one branch\n\n**Root cause:** `LiteralUnion`'s truncate-with-`\"...(+N)\"` pattern was never factored into a helper. When `Union`/`Intersection` needed the same truncate-with-indicator behavior — in the *same function*, `format_type_compact` — the indicator half was dropped. This isn't code aging apart over time; it's proof the codebase has no mechanism that makes \"format a truncated list\" a single call site, even within one function.\n\n**Mechanism:** Add `fn truncate_with_indicator(parts: &[String], limit: usize, sep: &str) -> String` in `toon.rs`. Route `LiteralUnion`, `Union`, and `Intersection` through it.\n\n**Resolves:** P0-4. Cheapest fix in this document — all three call sites are already colocated.\n\n**ADR:** No.\n\n### 10. Schema hand-maintained separately from the real structs\n\n**Root cause:** `cli/commands/schema.rs` hand-writes a JSON Schema as a `serde_json::json!()` literal instead of deriving it from `ComponentEntry`/`ExtractionStats`/`Diagnostic`. Every new field on those structs requires someone to remember to edit a second, structurally unrelated file — and the only existing test checks that the hand-written JSON is syntactically valid, never that it matches real serialized output, so drift is invisible to CI. Concrete drift already present: `.methods`/`.tags`, 5 of 9 `ExtractionStats` fields, and `.line`/`.column`/`.help` are all undocumented in the schema today.\n\n**Mechanism:** Derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on the plain structs (`ComponentEntry`, `ParsedProp`, `ExtractionStats`, `Diagnostic`, `InheritedLayer`); call `schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput)` from `cmd_schema()`. Hand-write `JsonSchema` impls for `PropType`/`CollectedType`/`OpaqueReason` — the three recursive enums ADR-0002 already exempted from derive machinery for `Serialize` — following that same precedent so the recursion-limit problem isn't reintroduced. If `schemars` as a new dependency is undesirable, the floor-level fix is a CI test that serializes a real fixture and validates it against `schema.rs`'s hand-written output.\n\n**Resolves:** P0-5.\n\n**ADR: Yes — see draft below.**\n\n### 11. CLI exit-code contract has no shared type\n\n**Root cause:** The 0/1/2 exit-code mapping is a doc-comment convention re-typed per command, not a shared function — `extract.rs:68-71` and `check.rs:21-30` already implement the same check with syntactically different code. `ExtractionOutput` has no `exit_code()` method, plausibly because `crates/core/CLAUDE.md`'s \"no terminal/display code in `crates/core`\" rule got over-applied to mean \"no exit-code logic belongs in core either.\" `cmd_watch` returns `()`, not `Result<i32>` (main.rs hardcodes exit 0 for that arm); `cmd_inspect` never touches `output.diagnostics` at all. Both were plausibly written by copying the general command-handler shape without copying the exit-code block, because nothing — return type, trait, lint — forces a new command handler to remember it.\n\n**Mechanism:** Add `impl ExtractionOutput { pub fn max_severity(&self) -> Option<DiagnosticSeverity>; pub fn exit_code(&self, strict: bool) -> i32 }` in `core/types/output.rs` — pure data-in/data-out, same category as the existing `html_element()` helper, so it doesn't violate the core/no-terminal-code rule. Route `extract.rs` and `check.rs` through it (collapsing their inline checks). Change `cmd_watch` to return `Result<i32>` and call `exit_code(false)` after each pass, or explicitly gate propagation behind a `--ci` flag if always-0 is intentional for interactive use — document the choice either way instead of leaving it silent. Call `exit_code(false)` in `cmd_inspect` after a successful lookup so an Error diagnostic elsewhere in the tree surfaces even when the inspected component itself is fine.\n\n**Resolves:** P3-1.\n\n**ADR:** No — a doc comment on `exit_code()` documenting the mapping is sufficient; this formalizes an already-informal contract, it doesn't create a new one.\n\n---\n\n## Standalone findings — no shared mechanism, treat as independent tasks\n\n| Finding | Location | Why it doesn't cluster |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Generic alias `.zip()` silently truncates unsubstituted trailing type params | `resolver/substitute.rs:33-43` | Silent *truncation* of a substitution map, not a give-up-and-return-nothing path — closer to a P0 correctness bug than the give-up cluster |\n| `watch --out` write failure discarded via `let _ =` | `cli/commands/watch.rs:107-112` | CLI-layer, not extraction-pipeline degradation; non-negotiable #6 is scoped to `crates/core` — the real fix is routing through the CLI's own miette/`output.rs` error path, a different mechanism than the core-side clusters. Same file's non-atomic `fs::write` (no temp+rename) should be fixed alongside it. |\n| Cache staleness on same-tick, same-size edits | `cache.rs:181-191` (P0-2) | Genuine filesystem-timestamp-resolution limitation, not a logic bug or missing abstraction — there's exactly one call site and it already does the best available thing. A content hash would close the gap but is a scoped performance/complexity tradeoff, not a missing shared check. |\n| `ParsedProp` allows contradictory `required: true` + `default_value: Some(_)` | `types/output.rs:79-99` (P0-3) | Missing type invariant, not duplicated logic — there was never a shared constructor to unify. Fix: add `ParsedProp::new(...)`, make fields `pub(crate)`, and add a line to the `rust-types` skill checklist: \"if a struct has two fields whose combination can be contradictory, provide a validating constructor.\" |\n| `DiagnosticCode::Unknown` never constructed | `types/diagnostic.rs:61` (P3-2) | Genuinely dead code but intentional headroom on a `#[non_exhaustive]` enum for external consumers — no fix needed. |\n| `NumberLiteral` NaN/Infinity round-trips to `0.0` | `types/output.rs:314,423` | Serialization-boundary edge case with no realistic reachable input (TS literal syntax can't spell NaN directly) — low-priority standalone ticket. |\n| Zero-member `LiteralUnion` serializes as an empty RDT enum | `cli/commands/extract.rs` (`rdt_type_json`) | Same tier as above — low-priority, cosmetic. |\n\n---\n\n## Draft ADRs\n\nTwo clusters are genuine new architectural decisions, not enforcement of an existing pattern. Drafted here; write the real `docs/adr/000N-*.md` file as part of implementing that cluster, per the ADR guide's \"write it when the decision is made\" convention — these are proposals, not yet accepted.\n\n### Proposed: 0005. Contain panics at a single, per-item boundary\n\n**Context:** No existing ADR covers panic/unwind policy. Panic safety today is an accident of `tokio::spawn_blocking`'s `JoinError`, inconsistent across NAPI entry points, plugin hooks, and rayon batches — `create_session`/`close_session` don't even get that accidental protection, and `watch.rs`'s `std::sync::Mutex` can poison permanently as a direct consequence. Picking the wrong granularity now (per-batch vs. per-file vs. per-plugin) is expensive to fix retroactively once call sites depend on it.\n\n**Decision:** Panics reachable from a rayon `.map()`, a `DocgenPlugin` hook, or a NAPI entry point are contained at per-file/per-plugin/per-call granularity through one sanctioned helper, `panic_guard::contain_panic`, converting the payload into a `Diagnostic` (or `napi::Error`) instead of aborting a batch, killing the whole pipeline, or poisoning a session lock.\n\n**Consequences:**\n- One bad file, plugin, or session call degrades to a diagnostic instead of taking down everything sharing its batch/pipeline/session.\n- Every future concurrent, plugin, or FFI entry point has one obvious place to route through, instead of re-deriving the answer.\n- `watch.rs`'s poisoned-mutex trap becomes structurally much less likely, since nothing panics while the lock is held anymore.\n\n### Proposed: 0006. Derive JSON Schema instead of hand-writing it\n\n**Context:** `schema.rs`'s hand-written schema has already drifted from `ComponentEntry`/`ExtractionStats`/`Diagnostic`'s real fields, undetected because the only test checks JSON syntax validity, not field-set parity. ADR-0002 already established the precedent of hand-writing `Serialize` for the three recursive enums (`PropType`, `CollectedType`, `OpaqueReason`) to avoid a recursion-limit blowup.\n\n**Decision:** Derive `schemars::JsonSchema` on the plain output structs; hand-write matching `JsonSchema` impls for the three recursive enums, mirroring their existing hand-written `Serialize` impls. `cmd_schema()` calls `schemars::schema_for!(ExtractionOutput)` instead of building a `json!()` literal by hand.\n","numLines":70,"startLine":99,"totalLines":196}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fadbfd77-4ade-4cb3-9de3-87132daa8e12","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
{"parentUuid":"50ce421e-11aa-4c12-925d-224ed6b16bae","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Read","toolUseID":"toolu_0111ZrGxN11ayXYGrzMD3Taq","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"oxc-react-codegen\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Read\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.1.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":57},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8983be7f-a53d-4016-afe1-51a5edb13720","timestamp":"2026-08-04T17:49:32.108Z","session_id":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/oxc-react-codegen","sessionId":"d83be67f-8b07-459c-989b-529791ef5ca7","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"delightful-booping-fern"}
